<node id="Random Forest">
  <data key="d0">Random Forest</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Random Forest (RF) is an ensemble machine learning algorithm for statistical learning and pattern recognition. Leo Breiman introduced it in "Random Forests" and developed it with Adele Cutler. It aggregates many decision trees, each trained with bagging on random subsets of attributes and grown to maximum depth, and combines their outputs, typically by averaging, to reduce overfitting. Out-of-bag data provides performance assessment. Performance generally improves with more trees, and the method yields feature-importance measures. RF is considered cost-effective and can be paired with the Boruta algorithm for feature selection. Across three datasets, feature selection generally improved its precision and F1-score.

RF is widely used for classification and prediction, including sales, stock price, and solar power forecasting. It is often compared with support vector machines (SVM). In injection molding machine selection, studies report different figures, likely from different studies or setups:
- One configuration (700 estimators, maximum depth 30, bootstrap sampling) outperformed SVM, with 81% accuracy, 78% F1-score, 85% precision, and 81% recall.
- Other reports name RF the best-performing supervised model, with 93% accuracy. Table 3 lists 96% precision, 95% recall, and a 95% F1-score. The narrative instead cites 95% weighted-average precision (about a 5% machine-selection error rate), 93% recall, and a 93% F1-score, an unresolved inconsistency.
- Alongside decision trees and SVM, RF reportedly predicted the correct machine with roughly 93% and 87% accuracy using limited training data.
- It misclassified only one class 4 machine (320 t) as class 3 (250 t). It predicted 200 t for Part_1 and 250 t for Part_2, assigned high probability to the actual class 3 (mold weight coefficient 0.13), and showed a learning curve that rose steadily before plateauing in the [70:100] interval.

In customer churn prediction, RF (labelled "FR" in Table 1) was one of two top models, with 91.21% accuracy. Its confusion matrix showed 461 true negatives, 8 false positives, 42 false negatives, and 58 true positives:
- Non-churn class: 92% precision, 98% recall, 95% F1-score.
- Churn class: 88% precision, 58% recall, 70% F1-score, indicating weaker detection of churners.

Tree SHAP and LIME explained this model (SHAP values in Figure 5(b)). Ullah et al. also applied RF to telecom churn prediction.

In healthcare and occupational research, RF appears in studies on musculoskeletal disorders, rehabilitation monitoring, and drivers. These include Raza et al.'s MSD prevalence study and an analysis of mining truck drivers' discomfort. Reference [33] reports it as the most accurate of four pressure ulcer prediction models.

In other comparisons, RF reached the highest accuracy (99.35%) against a neural network, SVM, Naive Bayes, and Adam. It was also compared with a Deep Q-Network by Panjasuchat and Limpiyakorn and evaluated by Momin et al. Its reported accuracies (81%, 91.21%, 93%, 99.35%) come from different studies, datasets, and domains, so they reflect context rather than contradiction.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard machine learning baseline planned for comparison with the proposed FBN.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine learning model whose fraud detection performance improved with cGAN-generated data.&lt;SEP&gt;A model used among the statistical metrics for evaluating how closely synthetic data mimics real financial time series.</data>
