<node id="Multi-Criteria Decision-Making">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Criteria Decision-Making</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM, sometimes written MDCM) is a field of decision-making methods and techniques for ranking and selecting the best alternative among several options. It addresses a class of decision problems in which alternatives are evaluated against multiple weighted criteria, which may be both quantitative and qualitative. By analyzing multiple criteria together, MCDM gives a comprehensive representation of decision-maker preferences and helps resolve complex decision situations.

MCDM approaches are generally grouped into subjective, objective and combined methods for weighting and evaluating criteria. The family includes well-known techniques such as the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), TOPSIS, fuzzy TOPSIS and CODAS, as well as integrated methods such as entropy-TOPSIS and hybrid fuzzy MCDM approaches, all of which appear in the cited literature.

MCDM has been applied widely to machine and equipment selection. A hybrid fuzzy approach for evaluating construction equipment, for example, is built on MCDM principles. In manufacturing, MCDM serves as a framework for selecting injection molding machines, where it handles multiple criteria, sub-criteria and conflicting objectives. It also characterizes the assignment of products to injection molding machines in profitability optimization, which positions MCDM as a structured basis for both machine selection and production allocation decisions.</data>
