<node id="Reference [1]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [1]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [1] is a citation label that appears across multiple distinct source documents, so it most likely refers to several different cited works rather than one publication. Each document uses "[1]" as its first reference, and the descriptions group into several thematic areas, summarized below.

In the context of injection molding and plastic manufacturing, Reference [1] is cited to establish that injection molding is widely used to produce complex plastic parts with precision and efficiency. It is also cited for the importance of injection molding within plastic manufacturing more broadly. In a related context, possibly the same work or a closely connected one, Reference [1] supports the claim that selecting the appropriate machine in the injection molding industry is difficult.

In the area of procurement and operations management, Reference [1] is cited for the importance of supplier selection. This topic connects thematically to the machine-selection problem, which suggests a decision-making or multi-criteria selection framework, although the two citations may come from different documents.

In data science, Reference [1] is cited in discussions of big data and the curse of dimensionality, the challenges that arise when analyzing high-dimensional datasets.

Regarding Industry 4.0 and emerging technologies, Reference [1] is cited for the observation that the fusion of Internet of Things (IoT) technologies with digital concepts gave rise to the Smart Industry paradigm. A separate citation labeled Reference [1] supports the adoption of blockchain technology for decentralized record keeping.

In the field of information technology management, Reference [1] is cited for its treatment of the maintenance of IT systems and digital infrastructures.

Because these topics range from manufacturing processes to data analytics, blockchain, and IT infrastructure, Reference [1] should be understood as a shared citation identifier that points to different works depending on the source document.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [1], describing the Zenzeleni community network and its upgrade to an external internet connection via a 3G modem. Full bibliographic details are not given in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [1] regarding security problems arising from lack of centralized management in ad hoc networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [1] on the role of signatures in permissioned blockchains and on Hyperledger Fabric's modular architecture, identity model, endorsement policies and ordering service.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [1]; work on Hyperledger Fabric describing its execute-order-validate pipeline and its performance.</data>
