<node id="Reference [2]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [2]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [2] is a citation label that appears in several different documents. Because the descriptions cover unrelated subjects, they most likely refer to multiple distinct works that share the same citation number rather than to a single source. The contexts in which Reference [2] is cited are summarized below.

In the context of manufacturing, Reference [2] is cited on injection molding. It highlights the importance of injection molding in the automotive, consumer electronics, and medical devices industries. It is also cited for the challenges injection molding faces, particularly rising costs and increasingly demanding quality standards. These two descriptions likely refer to the same work or to closely related citations within injection molding research.

In machine learning, Reference [2] is cited on data dispersion, generalization, and overfitting. It is also cited for comparing the accuracy of individual decision trees with the performance of Random Forest ensembles, a comparison that typically shows how ensembles reduce overfitting and improve generalization. These two usages may belong to the same body of work.

In decision science, Reference [2] is cited as supporting the need for Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) approaches in supplier selection.

In industrial digitalization, Reference [2] is cited for describing Digital Twins as virtual mirrors of physical systems.

In distributed ledger technology, Reference [2] is cited in support of adopting blockchain for decentralized record keeping.

In information technology management, Reference [2] is cited on the maintenance of IT systems and digital infrastructures.

Overall, Reference [2] does not denote one identifiable publication. It serves as a supporting citation across diverse fields, including manufacturing, machine learning, supplier selection, Digital Twins, blockchain, and IT infrastructure maintenance, and its exact identity depends on the document in which it appears.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [2]. A paper on applications whose title is only partially visible, published in the International Journal of Application or Innovation in Engineering &amp; Management (IJAIEM), vol. 2, no. 9, pp. 249–251, 2013.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [2] regarding adversaries exploiting MANET characteristics to read or modify data or stop services.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [2]; one of the seminal works on code generation tools selected for detailed analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [2]; work on migration from ECDSA toward standardized post-quantum signatures such as ML-DSA/Dilithium.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [2] in the first research stream on quantum threats, post-quantum cryptographic standardization and hybrid migration strategies. It supports the need to migrate from ECDSA toward standardized post-quantum signatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [2]. Source on the vulnerability of ECDSA to Shor's algorithm.</data>
