<node id="Reference [3]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [3]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">"Reference [3]" is a citation label that appears in several distinct documents, so it refers to multiple different cited works rather than one source. The descriptions fall into several thematic groups, summarized separately below.

In the context of injection molding and manufacturing, Reference [3] is cited for its treatment of machine characteristics, such as maximum shot size, injection pressure and screw type. A work cited under the same label also discusses the benefits of Digital Twins in injection molding, emphasizing real-time data analytics. Related manufacturing-oriented citations of Reference [3] address the classification of maintenance strategies.

In data science and decision-making contexts, Reference [3] is cited for showing that feature selection improves model efficiency and for outlining the categories of selection strategies. Separately, a work labeled Reference [3] is cited for the advantages of Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM) methods.

In security, Reference [3] is cited for describing the three general forms of authentication: something we have, something we know, and something we are.

In blockchain and cryptography research, Reference [3] is cited for Hyperledger Fabric's permissioned model and modular design. It also describes how ECDSA is used in Hyperledger Fabric through the Membership Service Provider (MSP) and the Blockchain Cryptographic Service Provider (BCCSP). The same citation covers migration from ECDSA toward standardized post-quantum signature schemes such as ML-DSA (Dilithium). It is cited within the range [2]–[4], which represents a research stream on quantum threats, post-quantum standardization and hybrid migration strategies. These blockchain and post-quantum citations likely belong to a single paper.</data>
