<node id="Reference [4]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [4]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [4] is a citation label that appears across several distinct scholarly documents, so it does not denote a single work. The descriptions point to several separate cited sources, each tied to a different research context. They are summarized individually below.

In manufacturing and materials engineering, Reference [4] is cited for mold specifications and material properties such as viscosity and density. In industrial operations, Reference [4] is a work on the classification of maintenance strategies. In Digital Twin research, Reference [4] addresses the challenges of integrating Digital Twins with IoT infrastructure, including edge and cloud computing layers.

In data science and machine learning, Reference [4] is cited for feature selection as a prominent research field. In decision science, Reference [4] is cited for the Decision-Making Trial and Evaluation Laboratory (DEMATEL), which is described as a subjective approach to weighting criteria.

In blockchain and distributed ledger research, Reference [4] is cited for Hyperledger Fabric's permissioned model and modular design. In information security, Reference [4] is cited for the claim that Multi-factor Authentication provides greater safety and protection.

In post-quantum cryptography, Reference [4] concerns the migration from ECDSA toward standardized post-quantum signature schemes such as ML-DSA (Dilithium). It is also cited within the range [2]–[4], which belongs to a research stream on quantum threats, post-quantum standardization, and hybrid migration strategies. These two cryptography-related descriptions are consistent and likely refer to the same work or to closely related works in a single document.</data>
