<node id="TOPSIS">
  <data key="d0">TOPSIS</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">TOPSIS (Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution, also written as Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution) is a classical multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) method for evaluating and ranking alternatives. It identifies the best alternative as the one closest to the positive ideal solution and farthest from the negative ideal (anti-ideal) solution. It does this by comparing each alternative's performance against both reference points. Although the method is systematic, it involves subjective decisions, particularly in assigning criteria weights. Many cited works discuss TOPSIS, including references [3], [6] and [7], comparative studies, group decision-making studies, and research on its weighting and applications. It has also been reviewed as an MCDM approach in the literature.

The TOPSIS procedure has five main steps. First, an initial decision matrix is built, sometimes using a 1–9 scale, and normalized using the Euclidean norm. Second, the normalized values are weighted by the importance of each criterion or sub-criterion, often using global weights. Third, the positive (best) and negative (worst) ideal solutions are determined. Fourth, the Euclidean separation measures of each alternative from both ideal solutions, denoted S+ and S−, are calculated. Fifth, a relative closeness or proximity coefficient (denoted Si or Pi) is computed for each alternative, and the alternatives are ranked by this value.

TOPSIS is most often applied to supplier selection, where it ranks suppliers according to weighted criteria. In one such application it was used to evaluate and rank four suppliers. It is also widely used for machine selection. In one study, TOPSIS was applied after AHP, using AHP-derived weights, to choose among three injection molding machines. The results ranked ARB250T first (0.62318), ARB200T second (0.54278) and HAIT 320T third (0.37503). Other applications include maintenance system evaluation and process optimization through hybrid approaches such as GRA-TOPSIS and Taguchi-AHP-TOPSIS.

TOPSIS can be used on its own but is frequently combined with other techniques, especially those that determine criteria weights. Common pairings include the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), fuzzy AHP, entropy weighting and fuzzy logic. It is also used in fuzzy form alongside CODAS and AHP for machine selection. Such hybrid combinations appear in several supplier selection studies, including those cited as references [17] and [21].</data>
