<node id="Precision">
  <data key="d0">Precision</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Precision is a standard classification evaluation metric that measures how accurate positive predictions are. It is the proportion of correctly predicted positive instances out of all instances predicted as positive, computed as TP / (TP + FP). TP denotes true positives and FP denotes false positives, and one source presents the formula as Eq. 2. Precision is derived from the confusion matrix and reflects how correctly a model identifies relevant instances. It is commonly reported alongside accuracy, recall, and F1-score, and it is combined with recall to form the F1 score.

Precision has been used to assess and compare machine learning and classification models in many studies and performance comparisons. In churn prediction research, it was reported per class, separating the non-churn class (class 0) from the churn class (class 1) for each model. In those results, the Decision Tree (DT) model achieved the highest precision for the non-churn class at 95%. The Support Vector Machine (SVM) model led for the churn class with 100% precision. More generally, Precision served as a per-class metric for model assessment.

Beyond churn prediction, Precision was used to evaluate road tile classification models, Random Forest classifiers, and a CNN classification model. In the CNN-based work, Precision rose from 94.24% without the Canny filter to 99.44% with it. This significant improvement indicates that the Canny filter reduced false positives.

Other studies also reported gains in Precision. A cGAN-based fraud detection approach improved the metric. Feature selection also notably increased Precision when evaluating classification models, which highlights the metric's sensitivity to the choice of input features.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5f0352d149ce07cb8b4fdf8db0076c7d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">978-3-031-75329-9_31.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;GANsinsyntheticfinancialdata_donalsonwilson_versionpublie.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467116</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recall">
  <data key="d0">Recall</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Recall, also known as sensitivity, is a standard classification evaluation metric that measures the proportion of actual positive instances a model correctly identifies. It is calculated as TP/(TP+FN), where TP is true positives and FN is false negatives, making it the ratio of correct positive predictions to the total number of actual positives. Recall is derived from the confusion matrix and is formally defined in the source literature as Eq. 3. Because recall depends on false negatives, it is directly affected by misclassifications.

Recall is widely used to assess and compare machine learning and classification models, and it typically appears in performance comparisons alongside accuracy, precision, and F1-score. It is closely linked to precision, since the two are combined to form the F1 score. Recall is often reported per class, which gives a more detailed view of how well a model handles each category rather than only its overall performance. One source characterizes high recall as reflecting a strong ability to predict both true positives and true negatives, and low recall as a weaker capability with true positive and false negative instances. Under the standard TP/(TP+FN) definition, however, recall reflects only the identification of actual positives and does not account for true negatives, so that characterization should be treated with caution.

Recall has been applied in several evaluation contexts. In churn classification, it was reported per class for each churn prediction model. The Support Vector Machine (SVM) achieved the highest non-churn recall at 100% but the lowest churn recall at only 5%, while the Decision Tree (DT) model led in churn recall at 75%. This contrast shows the trade-offs between models in identifying the minority churn class. In another comparison, the Naive Bayes (NB) model reached a recall of 63%. Recall was also used to evaluate road tile classification models and Random Forest classifiers, in combination with precision through the F1 score.

Recall was further used to evaluate a CNN classification model, both with and without a Canny filter applied before the CNN. In both configurations recall remained at 100%, meaning all actual positive cases were correctly identified. Applying the Canny filter therefore did not compromise recall.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5f0352d149ce07cb8b4fdf8db0076c7d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">978-3-031-75329-9_31.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467117</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="F1-Score">
  <data key="d0">F1-Score</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The F1-Score is a standard classification evaluation metric defined as the harmonic mean of precision and recall. It is favored over the arithmetic mean because the harmonic mean balances the two components and penalizes extreme values, so a model cannot score well by excelling in only one of them. The F1-Score is derived from the confusion matrix and is commonly reported alongside accuracy, precision, and recall. It is widely used to evaluate machine learning models and compare their performance.

In a study on customer churn prediction, the F1-Score was used to evaluate and compare several churn classification models. It was reported per class, separately for the churn and non-churn categories. On the non-churn class, the Artificial Neural Network (ANN) and Random Forest (RF) models both reached an F1-Score of 95%. On the churn class, the ANN led with 74%, while the Support Vector Machine (SVM) had the lowest score at 10%. This gap shows how class-specific F1-Scores can reveal differences in a model's ability to identify the minority churn class. The F1-Score also served as a key indicator of the effect of feature selection, which notably improved the models' F1-Scores.

In a fraud detection context, the F1-Score was among the evaluation metrics improved by a cGAN-based fraud detection approach.

In a study using a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) classification model, the F1-Score was used to measure the impact of applying a Canny filter. The CNN alone achieved an F1-Score of 97.03%, while the CNN combined with the Canny filter reached 99.72%, showing that edge-detection preprocessing improved classification performance.&lt;SEP&gt;An evaluation metric used alongside Accuracy, Precision and Recall to assess the deep learning IDS of Sbai and El Boukhari. A higher F1-score indicates a better machine learning model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5f0352d149ce07cb8b4fdf8db0076c7d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">978-3-031-75329-9_31.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;GANsinsyntheticfinancialdata_donalsonwilson_versionpublie.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;MVML_120 (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501031</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SHAP">
  <data key="d0">SHAP</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) is an explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) and interpretable machine learning technique for understanding how individual features contribute to model predictions. It is grounded in game theory and additive feature attribution. SHAP values quantify the difference between a model's actual prediction for a given instance and the model's average prediction, attributing that difference across the input features. SHAP serves as both a local interpretation method, explaining individual predictions, and a global explainability technique, revealing overall feature importance across a model.

SHAP is also available as an explainability toolkit, with documentation at https://shap.readthedocs.io. It includes several variants suited to different model types: Kernel SHAP, Tree SHAP, Deep SHAP, Gradient SHAP, Linear SHAP, and Partition SHAP.

SHAP has been applied across many domains, including analysis of customer behavior, stock trends, and credit risk.
- **Customer churn prediction:** SHAP was used to explain the two most accurate churn models, an Artificial Neural Network (ANN) and a Random Forest (RF). It computed feature contribution values for both models and identified "Complain" (complaint) as the most influential feature. The analysis also showed that this feature had both positive and negative SHAP values.
- **Manufacturing:** As a feature attribution method, SHAP showed that mold weight, height, length, and width were the primary contributors to Random Forest predictions.
- **Gradient boosting models:** SHAP has been used together with XGBoost to interpret model predictions.
- **Spatial analysis:** Reference 13 uses SHAP to extract spatial effects.
- **Credit risk:** Reference [33] uses SHAP for personal credit risk assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;SHapley Additive exPlanations values used for model explainability analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5f0352d149ce07cb8b4fdf8db0076c7d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5f0352d149ce07cb8b4fdf8db0076c7d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1920bb99aa33c3c871f9dc257956e3ca-chunk-005</data>
