<node id="Deep Learning">
  <data key="d0">Deep Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deep Learning (DL) is a family of machine learning models and an advanced learning paradigm central to contemporary artificial intelligence research and applications. As a subset of machine learning, it has driven the growing application of AI and revolutionized computer vision. DL methods are effective at analyzing large datasets and handling nonlinear, non-stationary problems. The family includes Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks and convolutional neural networks (CNNs), which are deep learning models designed for visual data analysis, although conventional models such as CNNs operate mainly on single-modal data. Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) derive from foundational deep learning architectures for unsupervised learning, and DL has played a crucial role in advancing data generation in domains with data accessibility issues, such as healthcare. Deep Learning has been integrated with other methods, such as the Extreme Learning Machine in the ICS-ELM approach, and combined with classical time series analysis in hybrid models.

Deep Learning has been applied across many domains. In transportation and remote sensing, it supports detection and classification of road anomalies in Vehicular Ad Hoc Networks (VANET) and road extraction from satellite images. In medicine, it has supported shoulder MRI segmentation, diagnosis of malignant bone tumors, multicenter medical models, prediction of states in epilepsy patients, and fusion of CT images with clinical variables and of medical imaging with electronic health records. In finance and business, it has been used for stock market analysis and prediction, and Pondel et al. used it for customer churn prediction in e-commerce. It is also a keyword of a paper comparing SARIMA and LSTM for sales forecasting. In IT systems, it supports predictive maintenance, such as estimating the remaining useful life of storage devices and detecting early GPU failures. However, DL models often lack interpretability, which reduces trust and adoption in critical infrastructures.

In manufacturing, Deep Learning has been applied to anomaly detection and quality prediction in injection molding, including LSTM-based forecasting of the melt cushion parameter. Its advancements have been used to make Statistical Process Control (SPC) control charts more intelligent and adaptable. Numerous studies have explored its integration into visual quality inspection, and it has been surveyed for automatic vision-based recognition of industrial surface defects. DL is also named as a direction for future work: improving injection machine selection, progressively extending a generated database, and extending road extraction to other domains. One paper lists it as a planned future addition, while a referenced work already uses it through an autoencoder.

In bibliometric and thematic analyses, Deep Learning is a prominent and well-developed theme. It appears as an AI technique in oncology bibliometric studies and is covered by Karger &amp; Kureljusic (2023). In feature selection research, it plays a central role in both feature selection and dimensionality reduction. It is the most common keyword in these publications, appearing 11% of the time, and is recognized as a fundamental theme in the thematic map. It emerged as a notable shift in 2023, rapidly intertwining with themes such as Bayesian networks and learning systems with increasing term frequency. It has also served as an AI-related search term in bibliometric queries, including a refined Scopus query, feature selection analysis, and the collection of articles on AI in Human Resource Management (HRM).&lt;SEP&gt;Machine learning approach using multiple layers, each providing a distinct interpretation of its input data.&lt;SEP&gt;Subclass of machine learning with multiple layers, each giving a distinct interpretation of its input data.&lt;SEP&gt;Approach used by Sbai and El Boukhari for intrusion detection of data flooding and UDP flooding attacks. It achieves high accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine learning approach applied in reference [17] to traffic accident analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Methods planned in future work to enhance predictions.&lt;SEP&gt;Field (DL) whose high-throughput workloads are particularly suited to GPU parallelism, as noted in the reviewed work.</data>
