<node id="Decision Tree">
  <data key="d0">Decision Tree</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Decision Tree (DT) is a supervised inductive machine learning algorithm used mainly for classification and outcome prediction. It splits a dataset step by step, based on the answers to questions about the features, into a tree-like structure that leads to outcomes. It is popular because it is easy to interpret, and reference [36] discusses it as a classification algorithm. Decision Tree is also the base learner of Random Forest. In that role, each tree is grown to maximum depth on a subset of features, and many trees are averaged to reduce overfitting and give more robust predictions. Decision Tree has also been suggested as a model for future use of Boruta feature selection.

Decision Tree has been applied in several domains. In occupational health, it has been used for ergonomic risk assessment and for predicting musculoskeletal disorders (MSDs); Raza et al., for example, used it to study MSD prevalence. In customer analytics, Shi et al. found it the most effective model for churn analysis on live stream e-commerce platforms, and Kanwal et al. reported that it reached 90% accuracy.

In one churn prediction study, Decision Tree was one of seven proposed classification models. The text reports an accuracy of 89%, while Table 1 lists 0.8981. Its confusion matrix (TN 436, FP 33, FN 25, TP 75) gives 511 correct predictions out of 569, which matches 0.8981, so the 89% figure is a truncation rather than a real discrepancy. For non-churn customers, it achieved 95% precision (the highest among the models), 93% recall and a 94% F1-score. For churners, it achieved 69% precision, 75% recall and a 72% F1-score. Its 75% churn recall was the best of all seven models, which shows its strength in identifying customers likely to churn.

In manufacturing, Decision Tree was among the preferred supervised models for predicting machine selection, and it performed favorably. It predicted 200 t for Part_1 and 250 t for Part_2. In its predictions, it assigned the highest probability to actual class 3, with a mold weight coefficient of 0.43 and a mold length coefficient of 0.01. Its confusion results were identical to those of SVM, and its learning curve kept improving, which suggests that more training data could improve its performance further. The reported metrics for this study are partly inconsistent. Table 3 gives 87% accuracy and 90% precision, recall and F1-score, while the narrative text gives 87% weighted-average precision and recall. The source does not resolve which figures are correct.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5f0352d149ce07cb8b4fdf8db0076c7d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-014</data>
