<node id="IEEE Access">
  <data key="d0">IEEE Access</data>
  <data key="d1">venue</data>
  <data key="d2">IEEE Access is a peer-reviewed, open-access journal published by the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE). It is widely cited for research on vehicular networking, blockchain security, machine learning, data-driven prediction, predictive maintenance, anomaly detection, microservices, supplier selection, and the intersection of artificial intelligence and behavioral economics. Volume 6 appeared in 2018, volume 8 in 2020, volume 11 in 2023, and volume 12 in 2024, and one source document references the journal's Volume 4, 2016 template.

Many source documents identify IEEE Access as the journal that accepted the article being read. Some of these texts are author versions not yet fully edited, and one carries a Volume 11 header. These attributions cover several distinct papers. "Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric," which proposes hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA signatures, is repeatedly described as accepted, which the ACCESS DOI confirms. "Intelligent Predictive Maintenance Using Fuzzy Bayesian Networks for Critical IT Components" applies fuzzy Bayesian networks to critical IT components. A microservices article presents and evaluates FSMicroGenerator. A 2024 article covers hybrid LSTM-AE and statistical process control (SPC) anomaly detection. "Comprehensive Framework for Supplier Selection" is attributed to the journal by inference from its ACCESS DOI and page headers showing "VOLUME 12, 2024," pages 145566–145567.

In blockchain research, IEEE Access published "Vehicle Lifecycle Management Using Blockchain" (2021) and papers [25], [27] and [36] on blockchain security and Hyperledger Fabric. It also published an empirical study on configuration-driven performance analysis of Hyperledger Fabric in 2026. In vehicular networking, it published a traceable blockchain-based VANET authentication paper (2019) and a study on VANET connectivity probability under adverse weather. That study is cited as volume 8, 2019, but volume 8 corresponds to 2020, so either the year or the volume in the citation may be inaccurate. The journal also published "A comparative analysis" in volume 8 (2020).

In predictive maintenance and analytics, IEEE Access published Manchadi et al.'s (2023) healthcare predictive maintenance survey, Ullah et al.'s 2019 random-forest customer churn prediction paper, a 2019 stock market trend prediction paper, Sun et al. (2017), and Eerapu et al. (2019). In microservices, it published several of the papers cited by the FSMicroGenerator work, including Tesoriero et al. (2022). Other attributed works include Sanchez-Nunez et al. (2020) and Aoujil et al. (2023), the latter on AI and behavioral economics.

IEEE Access also appears in many bibliographies: reference 6 (2020), reference [6] (2023), references [15] and [16], references [16] and [22] (2022), reference [45] (2020), reference [49] (2018), and reference 2 (2019). Several of these numbers recur across lists, which suggests they come from different papers' reference lists rather than conflicting entries.

In bibliometric analyses, IEEE Access is a prominent source. In feature selection research it ranks as the most relevant source, with 60 documents, and in another dataset it ranks second, with 120 documents. These figures most likely reflect studies with different scopes rather than conflicting measurements of the same dataset.</data>
