  <data key="d0">A. Azmani</data>
  <data key="d1">author</data>
  <data key="d2">A. Azmani is a multidisciplinary researcher affiliated with the Intelligent Automation and BioMedGenomics Laboratory at Abdelmalek Essaâdi University. Their work spans organizational behavior, decision support systems, and applied artificial intelligence. A frequently noted contribution is the 2024 article "Burnout: A Pervasive Challenge Threatening Workplace Well-Being and Organizational Success," co-authored with L. Bouhsaien and published in the International Journal of Professional Business Review. The article frames burnout as a widespread problem threatening both employee well-being and organizational success.

A. Azmani has a substantial record in probabilistic and fuzzy-logic modeling for risk assessment and decision-making. They co-authored "Using a Fuzzy-Bayesian Approach for Predictive Analysis of Delivery Delay Risk," one of two Fuzzy-Bayesian risk evaluation papers published in the International Journal of Advanced Computer Science and Applications (IJACSA) in 2022 and 2023. Their cited work from roughly 2013 to 2023 combines Bayesian networks, fuzzy logic, and multi-agent decision support for risk and quality-of-service (QoS) analysis. It includes studies on fuzzy-logic-based tender evaluation, expert systems for vocational guidance, and a Bayesian approach to assessing nephropathy risk. They have also published several papers applying the AHP-TOPSIS multi-criteria decision-making method.

Their applied machine learning research covers commerce, computer vision, and industry. A. Azmani co-authored a hybrid sales forecasting paper, a road detection pipeline paper, and a 2024 bibliometric study of AI applications in e-commerce based on merged data sources.

Manufacturing, especially plastic injection molding, is a prominent strand of this work. Working with F. Tayalati on at least two studies, A. Azmani contributed to research on:
- an AHP-TOPSIS approach to injection molding machine selection;
- machine learning and decision models for injection molding;
- predicting cooling time in injection molding;
- AI-based process parameter setting.

They also co-authored a 2022 review of AI-based plastic injection process parameter setting and monitoring.

Different source documents cite A. Azmani under different reference numbers, so the same number can point to different works. Across these sources they appear as a co-author of references [1], [2], [3], [5], [8], [11], [17], [18], [19], [28], [41], [43], [44] and [45]. For example, references [17] and [19] are described in one source as Bayesian-fuzzy risk and QoS work and in another as machine learning and decision models for injection molding. Likewise, the 2022 injection-process review is cited as reference [2] in one source and [5] in another.

A. Azmani's most recent noted work is a systematic review of Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) for synthetic financial data generation and market modeling. It was co-authored with D. Wilson, among others, and published in IJE Transactions B: Applications, Vol. 39, No. 02 (February 2026), pp. 395–406. One source names Azmani as the article's author, but most identify them as one of several co-authors.

Together, these contributions connect intelligent systems and uncertainty modeling with practical problems in management, healthcare, logistics, education, e-commerce, finance, computer vision, and industrial manufacturing.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the 2024 burnout paper in J Professional Business Review.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of reference [39].&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of "Configuration Driven Performance Analysis of Hyperledger Fabric: An Empirical Study" [36].</data>
