<node id="M. Azmani">
  <data key="d0">M. Azmani</data>
  <data key="d1">author</data>
  <data key="d2">M. Azmani is a researcher and frequent co-author in a body of literature applying artificial intelligence, machine learning, and multi-criteria decision-making methods to industrial and supply chain problems. The descriptions refer to a single individual whose work appears in multiple reference lists under different citation numbers, such as references 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, [1], [2], [3], [8], [11], [17], [19], 41, [43], [44], and [45], depending on the citing document.

A major part of M. Azmani's research concerns plastic injection molding. M. Azmani co-authored several papers on applying machine learning and artificial intelligence to this process. These include a 2022 review of AI-based approaches to the plastic injection process and a study on predicting cooling time in injection molding. The work also covers AI-based setting of process parameters and the selection of injection molding machines. In one citing source, references [17] and [19] are described as covering machine learning and decision models for injection molding. Together, these papers show a sustained focus on the intelligent optimization of manufacturing operations.

M. Azmani has also applied the AHP-TOPSIS multi-criteria decision-making methodology, cited as references [43], [44], and [45]. This includes co-authorship of a paper on selecting injection molding machines with AHP-TOPSIS, which links this decision-science work directly to the injection molding research, where structured ranking of alternatives is central.

M. Azmani has also contributed to probabilistic and fuzzy-logic approaches to risk analysis in logistics and supply chains. This includes co-authorship of "Using a Fuzzy-Bayesian Approach for Predictive Analysis of Delivery Delay Risk," as well as the Fuzzy-Bayesian papers by Bouhadi et al. (2022) and Benallou et al. (2023). These works combine Bayesian networks with fuzzy logic to predict delivery delay and other logistics risks. In another citing source, references [17] and [19] are described as covering this Bayesian-fuzzy logistics research. The differing descriptions of the same reference numbers most likely reflect different citing documents rather than a factual inconsistency.

Overall, M. Azmani is a prolific collaborator at the intersection of artificial intelligence, decision science, and industrial engineering.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of references [1], [2] and [5].&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the 2022 review on AI-based plastic injection process parameter setting and monitoring (reference [5]).&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of reference [39].</data>
