<node id="AHP">
  <data key="d0">AHP</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">AHP, the Analytic Hierarchy Process, is a multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) method developed by Saaty, with one source dating its introduction to 1971. It provides a systematic, structured approach to complex decision problems with a hierarchical structure organized into four levels: the overall goal, criteria, sub-criteria, and alternatives. AHP combines quantitative and qualitative evaluation and has been described as a six-step procedure that includes building the hierarchy and constructing pairwise comparison matrices.

AHP is primarily a subjective criteria-weighting method based on expert judgment, and it captures decision-makers' preferences. Decision-makers compare elements in pairs on a 1–9 scale. Criteria weights are then derived from the resulting matrices, for example by normalizing each matrix by its column sums and averaging the rows. Consistency ratios (CR) check the reliability of these judgments, with values below 0.1 considered acceptable. AHP is used both to build the hierarchical model of a problem and to derive and validate the weight of each criterion.

In one framework, pairwise comparison matrices were built from three experts' survey responses and aggregated using the geometric mean. The criteria-level CR was 0.09, and all ratios fell below 0.1. Criterion C1 received the highest weight (0.3749) and C6 the lowest (0.0375). In this framework, AHP served as the subjective weighting component alongside the objective entropy method. In a related evaluation, AHP identified Quality as the most important criterion and Reputation as the least influential. It produced the ranking A1 &gt; A4 &gt; A3 &gt; A2, which agreed with the most probable overall ranking only for alternative A1. Another application combined expert validation, pairwise comparison, and a weighted normalized matrix to weight eleven sub-criteria. Scrap rate received 34.5% of the total weight and breakdown rate 20.9%, while installation cost was least important at 1.6%.

AHP is widely applied and frequently integrated with other techniques. Cited works combine it with TOPSIS and CODAS or compare it with fuzzy AHP. It is particularly prominent in equipment and machine selection, where it is used to establish selection criteria, and References [1], [3], [4], and [5] apply it for this purpose. In injection molding, AHP is widely used for machine and process selection. One referenced study (Reference 5) used it to select optimal injection molding equipment, illustrating its role in industrial equipment selection.</data>
