<node id="Multi-Criteria Decision Making">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Criteria Decision Making</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Multi-Criteria Decision Making (MCDM) is both a class of decision problems and a family of decision analysis techniques. It addresses scenarios with multiple, often conflicting, objectives or criteria and aims to select the best option by weighing several factors together. MCDM is applied widely across engineering, management, and operations research.

When used with subjective models, an MCDM problem requires three elements: a set of criteria, a set of alternatives, and a panel of experts who evaluate them. Well-known MCDM methods include the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) and the Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS). These methods have been applied to supplier selection in the fashion industry and in the aerospace and defense industries.

In manufacturing, machine selection, including machine selection for injection molding, is recognized as an MCDM problem. References 9 and 11 used MCDM-based decision analysis for molding machine selection and injection moulding analysis. The family of MCDM techniques also underpins the five-phase structured approach proposed in This Paper. The field's methods are covered in the books and chapters by Triantaphyllou and by Pavan and Todeschini.

MCDM is valued for improving decision-making quality, risk management, and operational performance. A noted limitation is the difficulty of reaching consensus among the experts whose judgments feed into the evaluation.</data>
