<node id="Bayesian Network">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Network</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A Bayesian Network (BN) is a probabilistic graphical model for reasoning under uncertainty, developed by Judea Pearl and also covered in works by Nielsen and Jensen. It blends artificial intelligence and statistics to acquire, represent, and exploit knowledge under uncertainty. Formally, a BN is defined by a pair (G, O): a directed acyclic graph (DAG) over categorical random variables and a set of parameters. Nodes represent random variables and directed edges represent conditional dependencies, with edge direction indicating causal relationships. Each node has a conditional probability table (CPT), and node states may be expressed as linguistic values. The architecture can be built objectively, through structure learning from a database, or subjectively, from the expertise of field specialists. The choice depends on context and available resources, and causal relations may likewise be evaluated through expert estimation or learning databases. The BN supports belief updating, and its inference engine propagates probabilities from input parameters through intermediate effects to final outcomes, producing posterior probabilities and uncertainty intervals from discretized evidence.

The Bayesian Network appears in many cited works on prediction, risk assessment, and decision-making in transportation, safety, medicine, and other fields. Applications include warehouse planning and modeling the causal factors of overstocking, hospital service quality, and maintenance strategy selection and planning. It is also used for risk prediction and discovery, environmental risk assessment, collapse risk analysis, QoS quantification, DEMATEL-based decision problems, spatial agent-based learning, and multi-fatality crash factor analysis. Further uses include marine accident severity analysis, maritime cybersecurity risk assessment, and credit card churn prediction, where Najjar et al. compared it with other models. In organizational research, it models employee burnout and its influencing variables using the OpenMarkov tool.

In Vehicular Ad hoc Networks (VANET), the BN serves as a probabilistic causal model for analyzing and quantifying risk to Quality of Service (QoS). It represents dependencies among events such as mobility, density, congestion, connectivity, and routing. It also uses input nodes such as road condition, security problems, and vehicle resources. It predicts disturbances such as the impact of mobility and density on end-to-end delay (E2ED) and routing quality. In the proposed approach, the graph model has three levels. Input parameters form the input nodes. Intermediate effects break down into the input parameters that cause them. Final impacts directly affect the risk studied. The network is fed with conditional probabilities generated by Fuzzy Logic, and inference propagates these over the intermediate effects and final impacts to quantify QoS.

In road safety, the same three-level structure models the risk of an accident. Its nodes, all with linguistic states, include Driver Style, Driver Condition, Vehicle Condition, Road Design, Road Surface Condition, Traffic Flow, Kinetic Energy, Stopping Distance, and Accident on the Road. Conditional probabilities are propagated across the causal graph to estimate accident likelihood.

The combined Fuzzy-Bayesian approach, in which a fuzzy system generates the CPTs, is also applied to IT system reliability. There, the BN captures causal relationships among critical components and performs adaptive belief updating through exact inference. A unified network integrates CPU, GPU, RAM, and Hard_Disk subnetworks with a System_State child node conditioned on all component states. Nodes take linguistic values such as Low/Medium/High or Normal/Degraded/Critical, with Gaussian membership function parameters listed in TABLE 3. A classical BN without fuzzification is planned as a comparison baseline.</data>
