<node id="Fuzzy Logic">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Logic</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy Logic (FL) is a reasoning, inference, and control approach based on fuzzy sets. It handles uncertainty, imprecision, and vagueness through degrees of truth rather than strict binary values, and it is regarded as a robust mathematical tool for defining intermediate values between conventional true/false assessments. FL serves as a decision-making tool for uncertain situations that lack precise mathematical probabilities. It represents variable states in linguistic terms through linguistic variables, defined by the quadruplet (X, U, T(X), µx), and membership functions. Because it resembles human reasoning and natural language, FL is effective for imprecise data, linguistic problems, and uncertainty in expert judgement. It is especially useful when relationships between variables are poorly understood or when data are noisy, scarce, or incomplete.

FL is implemented in three principal steps: fuzzification, inference, and defuzzification. Applying it requires selecting fuzzy sets and membership degrees, defining the linguistic values and membership functions of the inputs, intermediate inputs, and outputs, and specifying the fuzzy IF–THEN rules and activated rules. These rules encode expert knowledge, such as linguistic health states. Related techniques include fuzzy logic controllers and Takagi–Sugeno fuzzy systems, which are used in several referenced works.

A central use of Fuzzy Logic is its symbiotic combination with Bayesian Networks and other probabilistic graphical models in the fuzzy Bayesian approach, which supports transparent, uncertainty-aware decision-making. In this approach, fuzzy inference computes the conditional probabilities and conditional probabilistic coefficients of Bayesian Network variables. It automatically generates the required conditional probability tables (CPTs) from expert judgments, a process illustrated as the Fuzzy Logic Process for Constructing CPTs (Figure 4). The method is particularly valuable when failure data are scarce or imprecise. It also minimizes the number of questions asked of experts and reduces the effort of building probability tables. This combination is applied both in related works, including references [37]–[40], and in This Paper.

Fuzzy-Bayesian applications span several domains. In burnout risk assessment, FL models burnout levels from communication, overload, and obstacle levels. In inventory management, it computes conditional probabilities that incorporate uncertainty into inventory decisions. In predictive maintenance, it is combined with Bayesian inference to handle uncertainty and incomplete observations, and it is suggested for root cause analysis of detected anomalies. In vehicular ad hoc networks (VANETs), it is combined with a Bayesian network to calculate quality of service (QoS) as a function of multiple criteria.

In multi-criteria decision-making, the FAHP-Entropy-TOPSIS approach uses FL to manage uncertainty in expert judgement. Trivedi et al. combined FL, as a membership-function-based approach, with an optimized TOPSIS method to make machine selection more autonomous. FL is also proposed as a future extension for pairwise comparisons to capture vagueness in decision-making. In addition, FL has been used to improve DBSCAN clustering.

FL has also proven effective in intrusion detection systems (IDSs). It is applied in control systems, risk management, energy consumption, image processing, natural language processing, medical diagnosis, and artificial intelligence. Cited works use it to study fire-spreading vulnerability, tender evaluation, and remotely operated vehicle (ROV) control, as well as in other fuzzy Bayesian analyses. FL is the subject of references [72] and [73] and is applied in references [74] and [76].</data>
