<node id="Intelligent Transportation Systems">
  <data key="d0">Intelligent Transportation Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">ITSs integrate modern technologies into existing transportation systems to monitor traffic.&lt;SEP&gt;Systems using new technologies to improve road safety, in which vehicles are equipped with wireless communication systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quality of Service">
  <data key="d0">Quality of Service</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Quality of Service (QoS) measures the degree of satisfaction with a service as presented to the end user. In the context of Vehicular Ad Hoc Networks (VANETs), QoS is regarded as the primary problem, because even small service failures can endanger human lives and harm the economy. Providing QoS support to VANETs is therefore an active area of research. The QoS of VANET communications can be degraded by various risks, and it is influenced by factors related to routing, security, and data processing.

In the study, QoS in VANET is the central risk or factor modeled by the proposed approach and the target of prediction. It is quantified and predicted by a fuzzy Bayesian (FB) model that considers multiple parameters. Within the Bayesian Network, QoS is the target variable and the performance property under analysis. It is directly influenced by the network's final impact nodes and is expressed through the linguistic values "bad," "medium," and "good."

The Bayesian Network predicts QoS under three environmental scenarios. The analysis finds that QoS is more vulnerable in urban areas, owing to high accessibility, congestion, and increased traffic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501546</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Risk Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Risk Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Analysis of risks in VANET that disrupt network functioning, used as the basis of the Fuzzy-Bayesian prediction tool.&lt;SEP&gt;Analysis and prediction of risks that can degrade QoS or block network functioning, causing material and human losses.&lt;SEP&gt;Analysis of the risk of degrading QoS in the network, supported by the database the article provides.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="VANET Routing Problems">
  <data key="d0">VANET Routing Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Routing in VANET is challenging due to dynamic topology, high vehicle mobility, variable network density and frequent path disruption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref [1]">
  <data key="d0">Ref [1]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Ref [1] is a citation label that appears across multiple distinct documents, each using it to refer to a different source work. The descriptions therefore represent several separate references that share the same numbering rather than a single unified work. Each is summarized below.

In the context of vehicular networking, Ref [1] is cited regarding the objectives of VANET (Vehicular Ad Hoc Network) communication systems. In a separate transportation-related document, Ref [1] is cited for the definition of professional driving. Another document cites Ref [1] as a work reporting regional road traffic death rates, providing statistical grounding for road safety discussions.

In the domain of business and technology, Ref [1] refers to a work describing electronic commerce as an internet-based field of business transactions. In a software engineering context, Ref [1] is cited regarding rising expectations for software functionality. In a further technology-focused document, Ref [1] is a study demonstrating blockchain's potential to enhance trust and data traceability across complex ecosystems.

In manufacturing, Ref [1] is cited regarding the widespread use of injection molding as a production process. Finally, in a legal and governance context, Ref [1] is a work cited for the role of legal services in ensuring compliance and access to public information.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work (reference [1]) on the growth of freight transport and the importance of logistics and transportation.</data>
