<node id="Ref [2]">
  <data key="d0">Ref [2]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Ref [2] is a citation label that appears across multiple, unrelated documents. The descriptions point to several distinct cited works that happen to share the same reference number, not to a single source. Each is summarized separately below.

In the context of vehicular networking, Ref [2] is cited regarding the objectives of Vehicular Ad Hoc Network (VANET) communication systems. A separate work, also on networking, is cited for situations that require instant, temporary multimedia networks, such as conferences, classrooms and stadiums. That use highlights the need for spontaneous ad hoc connectivity.

In occupational health research, Ref [2] is cited to support the claim that harsh working conditions make professional drivers vulnerable to musculoskeletal disorders (MSDs). In business and management literature, a different work cited as [2] discusses how successful 21st-century companies engage with their customers.

In manufacturing and operations research, Ref [2] appears in two distinct roles. One is a work on machine breakdown in the context of assigning products to machines, relevant to scheduling and production planning. The other is a work cited regarding injection pressures in injection molding processes.

In software engineering, Ref [2] is cited regarding the overwhelming amount of code that must be developed and maintained daily. Finally, in the domain of legal technology, a work cited as [2] addresses the benefits of integrating artificial intelligence (AI) into legal services.

Taken together, Ref [2] is a reused reference identifier spanning vehicular and multimedia networking, occupational health, business strategy, manufacturing, software engineering and legal AI. Each instance should be treated as a separate bibliographic source tied to its own citing document.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work reporting the contribution of two- and three-wheelers to road deaths in South-East Asia.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [2]; a study demonstrating blockchain's potential to enhance trust and data traceability across complex ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work (reference [2]) on balancing economic growth and environmental sustainability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26549e3ff493405b55b221c1d804516e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-000</data>
