<node id="Ref [4]">
  <data key="d0">Ref [4]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Ref [4] is a citation label that appears in several distinct documents. Each instance refers to a different cited work, so the descriptions below represent separate references that share the same label rather than a single source.

**Vehicles and occupational health.** In one context, Ref [4] is cited regarding vehicle positioning devices and sensors. In another, it is cited for identifying prolonged sitting, vehicle ergonomics, and vibrations as major risk factors for musculoskeletal disorders (MSDs). Both concern vehicles, so these may be the same work, though this cannot be confirmed.

**Business and economics.** Ref [4] is cited as a work on the role of customer loyalty in achieving economic success and generating continuous revenue.

**Industrial engineering and quality.** Ref [4] is cited as a work that applies the analytical hierarchy process (AHP) to machine selection. In a separate context, it is cited regarding the importance of addressing quality defects.

**Networking and security.** Ref [4] is cited as a work proposing clustering-based authentication in mobile ad hoc networks (MANETs). In another document, it is cited in the context of access. That work may relate to access control or network access, but the description gives no further detail.

**Software engineering.** Ref [4] is cited regarding model-driven paradigms as a fundamental approach to improving development efficiency.

Because "Ref [4]" is a positional citation marker, the label does not identify one specific publication. Each description should be read within the document where it appears.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [4] for AI solutions streamlining processes and for chatbots giving detailed conversational responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work on the multitude of factors contributing to accidents.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work on bad vehicle physical condition causing accidents.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [4]; a study demonstrating blockchain's potential to enhance trust and data traceability across complex ecosystems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ccc87fb659a572e9c9db17272d2f6955-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26549e3ff493405b55b221c1d804516e-chunk-001</data>
