<node id="VANET Routing Protocols">
  <data key="d0">VANET Routing Protocols</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Routing protocols for VANETs. They face issues such as route maintenance, selfish nodes, scalability, limited bandwidth, signal attenuation and security threats.&lt;SEP&gt;Routing protocols for vehicular ad-hoc networks. Their performance, issues and challenges are studied by cited works [7] and [9].&lt;SEP&gt;Routing protocols for VANETs. Their issues and challenges and their behaviour in rural, highway and urban settings are discussed in the cited works.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Frequent Path Disruption">
  <data key="d0">Frequent Path Disruption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Routes break often because cars join and leave the network quickly.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Selfish Nodes">
  <data key="d0">Selfish Nodes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nodes that refuse to take part in routing in order to save resources such as storage and processing time.&lt;SEP&gt;Nodes inside the network that usually carry out internal attacks. Their attacks are difficult to predict.&lt;SEP&gt;Highway VANET nodes that deny participation in routing to save their own resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scalability">
  <data key="d0">Scalability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scalability is a system property describing the ability of a network or system to maintain performance as it grows in size, number of participants, or workload. The descriptions apply the concept in several distinct technical contexts.

In Hyperledger Fabric networks, scalability refers to the network's ability to maintain performance as the number of peers per organization increases. It is influenced by the number of peers, the endorsement policies applied, and the choice of consensus mechanism. Evaluations tested configurations of 1, 2, 4, 6, and 8 peers per organization to observe how network performance changes as the peer count rises. The results showed that scalability issues emerge beyond 4 peers per organization. More broadly, scalability is identified as an open issue for blockchain systems, whose scalability problems limit their use as the number of nodes increases.

In Vehicular Ad Hoc Networks (VANETs), scalability is described as a common issue for routing protocols. Poor scalability in this setting leads to congestion and interference as the network grows.

In software engineering, scalability denotes the ability of generated applications to handle growing workloads without compromising performance. In this context, it was assessed through load testing of both implementations using Apache JMeter.&lt;SEP&gt;Ability of the proposed architecture to scale, which the architecture is designed to support.&lt;SEP&gt;Ability to generate large-scale datasets efficiently, an objective of the framework demonstrated by the GPU-accelerated approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Property of the framework further evaluated through additional case studies across different domains, summarized in Table 7.&lt;SEP&gt;Property confirmed by the Table 7 results across domains and complexity levels; also a potential weakness under more complex dependency structures and larger condition sets.</data>
