<node id="Reference [9]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [9]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [9] is a citation label that appears in several unrelated source documents. The descriptions do not share a subject, so they most likely refer to distinct works that happen to carry the same reference number. Each is summarized separately below.

In the context of vehicular ad hoc networks (VANETs), Reference [9] is cited as a source on diverse VANET attacks, including black hole, eavesdropping, wormhole and rushing attacks. In a related security context, Reference [9] is cited for a scheme that combines authentication and confidential communication using symmetric key cryptography. That approach is described as simple and efficient, but it suffers from the problem of sharing keys securely. These two security-related citations may come from the same document, but this cannot be confirmed.

In the domain of blockchain technology, Reference [9] is cited to explain how a blockchain operates. Miners validate blocks, and each node keeps its own copy of the blockchain, with all copies updated and verified simultaneously. In a separate, Hyperledger Fabric-related context, Reference [9] is cited as indicating that systematic performance tuning of Fabric remains relatively unexplored. This may belong to the same blockchain-focused document.

In medical and data science research, Reference [9] is cited for feature selection techniques applied to oncology and cancer detection.

In operations and supply chain management, Reference [9] is cited for TOPSIS (Technique for Order of Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution), a multi-criteria decision-making method used to rank suppliers.

In manufacturing, specifically injection molding, Reference [9] supports the description of the injection unit. In that unit, a screw mechanism pushes molten material through a heated barrel and into the mold.</data>
