</node>
<node id="Reference [12]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [12]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [12] is a citation label that appears in several distinct scholarly works. Because the descriptions cover unrelated subject areas, the label most likely refers to different cited sources in different documents rather than a single publication. Each usage is summarized separately below.

In vehicular and network security contexts, Reference [12] is cited for authentication attacks, such as an attacker impersonating an emergency vehicle to deceive other network participants. In a related networking context, Reference [12] supports the claim that Blockchain offers a decentralized and distributed framework for Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANET). These two citations may share a source focused on securing ad hoc and vehicular networks, but the descriptions do not confirm this.

In blockchain platform literature, Reference [12] is cited as work on Hyperledger Fabric. It describes Fabric's private, permissioned networks, its membership services for identity management, and its use of endorsement policies to validate transactions. Two descriptions address Fabric's membership services and endorsement-policy validation, which suggests a single consistent source in this context.

In business and telecommunications research, Reference [12] is cited for defining customer churn as a customer's transition from one service provider to another.

In decision-making and optimization literature, Reference [12] is cited for VIKOR, a multi-criteria optimization and compromise solution ranking technique used to rank alternatives under conflicting criteria.

In manufacturing and machine selection research, Reference [12] supports the finding that part characteristics, including dimensions, weight, and thickness, are the most significant parameters when selecting machines. The VIKOR citation may come from the same machine-selection study, since multi-criteria methods are commonly applied there, but this link is uncertain.

In Digital Twin research, Reference [12] is cited for describing the five essential components of the Digital Twin model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ccc87fb659a572e9c9db17272d2f6955-chunk-002</data>
