<node id="Reference [13]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [13]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [13] is a citation label that appears across several distinct scholarly documents, so it refers to multiple different cited works rather than a single source. Each is summarized below by subject area.

In the context of Vehicular Ad Hoc Networks (VANET), Reference [13] is cited for two points. It supports the claim that VANET improves transportation security and helps reduce accidents. It is also cited for the privacy risks that arise when vehicle messages in VANET are transmitted unencrypted. These two citations may come from the same work or from separate documents that discuss VANET.

In the domain of blockchain technology, Reference [13] describes how blockchain transactions are grouped into blocks, validated by network nodes, and then made visible to the entire network. A related citation, which may be the same work or a different one, is a source on Fabric (likely Hyperledger Fabric) and its suitability for industries such as finance, supply chain, and healthcare.

In the field of Digital Twins, Reference [13] is cited for describing how Digital Twins make machine and system operations visible to authorized personnel.

In business analytics, Reference [13] is a cited work that defines customer churn prediction as evaluating the proportion of customers who stop using a product or service.

In decision-making methodology, Reference [13] is cited for PROMETHEE, a multi-criteria ranking technique.

In manufacturing and materials engineering, Reference [13] supports the claim that material characteristics, including diverse chemical compositions, are pivotal in the injection molding process.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [13]; work on migration from ECDSA toward standardized post-quantum signatures such as ML-DSA/Dilithium.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [13] in the research stream establishing the need to migrate from ECDSA toward standardized post-quantum digital signatures such as ML-DSA/Dilithium.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [13]. Source on the NIST post-quantum cryptography standardization process and CRYSTALS-Dilithium.</data>
