<node id="Cloud Computing">
  <data key="d0">Cloud Computing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">External cloud servers to which complex computations are migrated. Cloud computing struggles with scalability, time and energy costs.&lt;SEP&gt;Computing paradigm used as the basis of a breast cancer diagnosis framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Cloud management resources combined with edge computing in the paper's hybrid approach for data acquisition and simulation. Integrating digital twins with cloud management is cited as a significant challenge.&lt;SEP&gt;Computing paradigm that fog computing extends.&lt;SEP&gt;A computing paradigm that fog computing extends.&lt;SEP&gt;Computing paradigm in which data is sent to the cloud for processing; fog computing is described as an extension of it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;acs_25_66701.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_171Edited_FV.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791466824</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Passive Attacks">
  <data key="d0">Passive Attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">VANET attacks in which an attacker listens to communication and uses the secret data to carry out malicious activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Attacks that intercept data in transit without noticeable perturbation, making them hard to detect; examples include traffic analysis, traffic monitoring and eavesdropping.&lt;SEP&gt;Category of MANET attacks classified according to the attack's behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Attacks that intercept data in transit without noticeable perturbation, hard to detect; e.g., traffic analysis, traffic monitoring, eavesdropping.&lt;SEP&gt;Attacks that intercept data in transit without noticeable perturbation, e.g., traffic analysis, traffic monitoring and eavesdropping; difficult to detect.&lt;SEP&gt;Attacks that gain access to network resources and monitor connections to gather traffic information without altering content.&lt;SEP&gt;Attacks that access network resources without altering content, monitoring connections to gather traffic information; compromise confidentiality.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active Attacks">
  <data key="d0">Active Attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Active Attacks are one of the two general categories of attacks on wireless ad hoc networks such as Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs) and Vehicular Ad Hoc Networks (VANETs). Classification into this category depends on the attack's behavior, and Active Attacks are usually set against passive attacks. Unlike passive attacks, which only observe, Active Attacks directly interfere with network operations and the data being transmitted.

In an Active Attack, malicious nodes disrupt network traffic and transmissions by generating congestion and spreading false routing information. They also manipulate or alter data in transit. This can involve changing or injecting packets, removing, modifying or replaying messages, and limiting normal network operations. In the VANET context specifically, an attacker may remove, modify or repeat messages to disrupt communication between vehicles.

Common examples of Active Attacks include modification, impersonation and fabrication. Because these attacks are dynamic and disruptive, they are generally considered fairly easy to detect and prevent compared with passive attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501056</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Black Hole Attack">
  <data key="d0">Black Hole Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the diverse attacks on VANET cited in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;An active attack that sends incorrect routing information to victim node tables, causing fraudulent routes and dropped packets.&lt;SEP&gt;Attack in MANETs targeted by several AI-based IDS methods, including cooperative black hole attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Type of Byzantine attack in which a malicious node gains access to the wireless network and drops received packets; cited with reference [30].&lt;SEP&gt;Smart blackhole attack in VANET that is detected using dynamic time warping.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
