<node id="QMM-VANET">
  <data key="d0">QMM-VANET</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Clustering routing protocol that considers QoS requirements and mobility constraints to maintain security and connectivity. It selects a trustworthy cluster head by QoS value, selects gateways and uses gateway recovery. It showed efficacy in packet delivery ratio and E2ED in a highway scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficient clustering algorithm for vehicular ad hoc networks based on QoS and monitoring of malicious vehicles, proposed in [24].&lt;SEP&gt;Clustering routing protocol aiming to maintain security and connectivity in the network.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gateway Recovery Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Gateway Recovery Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Algorithm used by QMM-VANET to select another gateway when a link fails.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dijkstra's Dedicated Short Range Communication (DDSRC)">
  <data key="d0">Dijkstra's Dedicated Short Range Communication (DDSRC)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Algorithm that finds the shortest route to a destination based on the cost of each transmission path, using Road Side Units.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Side Units (RSUs)">
  <data key="d0">Road Side Units (RSUs)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Infrastructure units exploited to accumulate and transmit traffic data in VANET.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MIMO">
  <data key="d0">MIMO</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Multiple-input multiple-output technique used to improve QoS parameters in VANET.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adaptive Modulation and Coding (AMC)">
  <data key="d0">Adaptive Modulation and Coding (AMC)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Technique used alongside routing algorithms to improve QoS parameters in VANET.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="End-to-End Delay (E2ED)">
  <data key="d0">End-to-End Delay (E2ED)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">QoS metric; it must be minimal for safety applications. It is influenced by mobility and density and in turn affects routing quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Packet Delivery Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Packet Delivery Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">QoS metric on which QMM-VANET demonstrated efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Network performance metric improved by the FADA-based flooding prevention approach relative to standard AODV.&lt;SEP&gt;QoS metric used by many of the related works.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clustering Routing">
  <data key="d0">Clustering Routing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Routing approach that groups vehicles into clusters with cluster heads and gateways.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Safety Applications Require Minimal Delay and Packet Loss">
  <data key="d0">Safety Applications Require Minimal Delay and Packet Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In VANET, each application has specific QoS requirements; for example, a safety application should have a minimum End-to-End Delay (E2ED) and packet loss because if a warning message does not arrive at a destination quickly, it will not help prevent an accident [23].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="QoS Aims at Correct Routing and Optimal Resource Use">
  <data key="d0">QoS Aims at Correct Routing and Optimal Resource Use</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In telecommunication networks, QoS aims at achieving better communication behaviour by routing the packets correctly and using resources optimally [22].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian Network Aids Reasoning Under Uncertainty">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Network Aids Reasoning Under Uncertainty</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">A Bayesian Network (BN) is a probabilistic graphical model developed by Pearl to aid in reasoning under uncertainty [30].&lt;SEP&gt;Statement in the text: "A Bayesian network (BN) is a probabilistic graphical model developed by Pearl to aid in reasoning under uncertainty [17]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BN Is a DAG Encoding a Joint Probability Distribution">
  <data key="d0">BN Is a DAG Encoding a Joint Probability Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">BN is defined by a pair (G, O) with G = V, A is a directed acyclic graph that encodes a joint probability distribution over a finite set of categorical variables V, and the arcs A represent direct relationships between them [31].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Packet Queues Decrease Service Rate">
  <data key="d0">Packet Queues Decrease Service Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">According to the authors of [29], if the number of packets transmitted in the network increases, a queue of packets may be created in the vehicle, and the response time of some packets may expire before being processed, causing a decreased service rate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Risk of QoS in VANET">
  <data key="d0">Risk of QoS in VANET</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Risks to QoS in VANET identified by the article being read: data routing risks (mobility, node density, security attacks), data security risks (authentication, confidentiality, privacy, integrity) and data processing risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref [30]">
  <data key="d0">Ref [30]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [30]. A work attributed to Pearl that develops the Bayesian Network as a probabilistic graphical model for reasoning under uncertainty. The input text gives no publication year.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [30] positioning RAG as a major advancement in generative models.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_113-Accurate_AI_Assistance_in_Contract_Law1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791500933</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Routing Protocols Minimize Delay and Increase QoS in VANET">
  <data key="d0">Routing Protocols Minimize Delay and Increase QoS in VANET</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In [25] and [26], the authors conduct brief analyses on QoS, which include some types of routing protocols that help minimize delay and increase overall QoS in VANET.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="QMM-VANET Improves Packet Delivery Ratio and E2ED">
  <data key="d0">QMM-VANET Improves Packet Delivery Ratio and E2ED</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"In comparison to other protocols, this one demonstrated its efficacy in terms of packet delivery ratio and E2ED in the highway scenario." This statement refers to QMM-VANET, proposed in [24].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Previous QoS Solutions Did Not Meet All QoS Criteria">
  <data key="d0">Previous QoS Solutions Did Not Meet All QoS Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"In overall, it can be concluded that none of the previous QoS solutions met all of the QoS criteria." The article draws this conclusion from its review of works [24]-[29].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mobility">
  <data key="d0">Mobility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A route condition in VANET. In the Bayesian model it is a parent of E2ED, and it influences data routing risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Routing input parameter of the causal graph. It describes the movement of wirelessly communicating nodes, which varies with their speed.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network variable with linguistic values low, medium, and high.&lt;SEP&gt;Vehicle movement level. It is low in urban environments, which supports good connectivity, and high on highways, which shortens link lifetime.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Node Density">
  <data key="d0">Node Density</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The density of nodes in VANET. In the Bayesian model it is a parent of E2ED, and it influences data routing risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Input variable of the Bayesian model giving the maximum number of vehicles in radio range. It is very high in congested cities and low in rural areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Packet Loss">
  <data key="d0">Packet Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A QoS metric. Safety applications in VANET require it to be minimal. Ref [27] improved the packet loss ratio.&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate effect in the causal graph. It is the number of packets that fail to reach their destination.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network variable with linguistic values low, medium, and high.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jitter">
  <data key="d0">Jitter</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A QoS parameter improved by Ref [27].&lt;SEP&gt;QoS metric used by related works [9], [11] and [12].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501527</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Throughput">
  <data key="d0">Throughput</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Throughput is a key performance and Quality of Service (QoS) metric that measures the rate at which a system processes work. It is usually expressed in transactions per second (tx/s or TPS) and is calculated as the number of successfully committed transactions divided by the total test duration. It is used as a QoS parameter in related works [7], [9] and [11], and Ref [27] is cited as having improved it. Throughput is reported by benchmarking tools such as Caliper and serves as a primary metric for evaluating Hyperledger Fabric configurations.

In Hyperledger Fabric, throughput depends on the endorsement policy, block size, batch size and batch-timeout settings. Configuration experiments report values from about 150 to 450 tx/s, with individual plots spanning roughly 210–280 tx/s and 150–220 tx/s. The best configuration reached 448.50 tx/s (±11.55). Specific results include 206.18 tx/s at (50, 50 ms), 226.13 tx/s at (50, 200 ms) and 171.19 tx/s at (200, 200 ms). A baseline with block size 100 achieved 280.03 tx/s under modest timeout changes, but with a higher P95 latency of 1485 ms.

A scalability analysis across peer configurations (Fig. 9, Fig. 10(b) "Throughput Comparison" and Table 5) reports much lower values that decline as peers per organization increase, especially beyond four. One account of the OR endorsement policy gives 1.9 tx/s for one peer, 1.8 tx/s for two to four peers and 0.2 tx/s at eight peers, with 1.4 tx/s at eight peers under the AND policy. Another OR panel plots 1.8, 1.8, 0.8, 0.7 and 0.6 tx/s. Fig. 10(b) compares OR and AND on a 0.0–0.8 tx/s axis, with values of roughly 0.5–0.8 tx/s. These figures differ somewhat, likely because they come from different panels or setups, but all show throughput falling as the network scales.

Throughput is also the primary metric for comparing classical ECDSA with post-quantum (PQC) and hybrid configurations. These comparisons use Welch's t-test, Cohen's d and TOST equivalence testing across scenarios S1–S5, and no significant differences were found. In one panel, values of 18.51 and 18.76 TPS were not significantly different (t=0.498, p=0.620). A further panel compares ECDSA, PQC and Hybrid on a 0.0–2.5 axis, but its values are not recoverable. ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 achieved 13.24, 14.42 and 12.02 TPS respectively. A two-instance configuration reached 9.84 TPS at a 10-TPS target and 22.78 TPS at a 50-TPS target.

In a separate microservice-generation context, throughput is a scalability-under-load metric measured in requests per second during load testing. The manual implementation scored 25.44 requests/sec raw, which became 5.12 when adjusted downward by Rf × Rnfr (Equation 4). FSMicroGenerator scored 21.75 requests/sec.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502232</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Directed Acyclic Graph">
  <data key="d0">Directed Acyclic Graph</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A graph G = (V, A) that encodes a joint probability distribution over categorical variables. It forms the structure of a Bayesian Network.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph structure whose nodes represent variables and whose directed edges represent the dependencies between them. It is used to represent a Bayesian Network.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph structure representing a Bayesian Network, over which the joint probability distribution is factorized.&lt;SEP&gt;DAG structure of the Bayesian Network through which evidence is propagated during inference.&lt;SEP&gt;Directed graph with no directed cycles whose vertices can be arranged in linear order; used as the structure of Bayesian networks and applied in biology, genealogy, epidemiology and sociology.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph G = (V, A) in a BN. It encodes a joint probability distribution over a finite set of categorical variables V, and its arcs A represent direct relationships between the variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501527</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Routing Risk">
  <data key="d0">Data Routing Risk</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A VANET risk related to data routing. It is influenced by route conditions such as mobility, node density and security attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Security Risk">
  <data key="d0">Data Security Risk</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A VANET risk to circulated data that arises from a lack of robust authentication, confidentiality, privacy and integrity mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Processing Risk">
  <data key="d0">Data Processing Risk</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A VANET risk to the data processing used for decision making. It is affected by the quality and quantity of data collected from network entities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Routing Quality">
  <data key="d0">Routing Quality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The quality of routing in the network. In the Bayesian model it is the child of E2ED.&lt;SEP&gt;Final impact (Routing) in the causal graph. It represents the quality of routing, which strongly affects data transmission between nodes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Causal Graph">
  <data key="d0">Causal Graph</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Architecture of the Bayesian Network defined in Step 1, developed from Table I and Table II after studying causal dependencies between nodes (Fig. 2).&lt;SEP&gt;Graph model linking security, routing and data processing input parameters to intermediate effects and to final impacts in VANET. The final impacts are routing, quality of data and QoS.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph model used in the proposed approach. Future work will generate the conditional probabilities (CPs) of all its nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph of causal dependencies between sensor, component and system nodes. Generalizing the fuzzy-based method to all of its nodes produced the conditional probabilities that parameterize the Bayesian Network.&lt;SEP&gt;A graph model whose input parameters are the identified root causes of vehicle accidents, as described in Table I.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph of causal relationships between nodes (road, traffic, driver, planning, vehicle condition) on which the Bayesian Network is built.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Probabilities">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Probabilities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Conditional probabilities (CPs) computed for each variable in Step 2 after constructing the BN graph, covering intermediate effects and final impacts.&lt;SEP&gt;Probability tables (CPs) of Bayesian Network nodes generated through fuzzy logic, considering all combinations of a node's parents.&lt;SEP&gt;CPs of the causal graph. This Paper generates them systematically with Fuzzy Logic.&lt;SEP&gt;Probabilities generated through fuzzy logic for the Bayesian network; with the Sugeno method, they are generated using only fuzzification and inference.&lt;SEP&gt;Probabilities (CPs) of the Bayesian network, generated using Fuzzy Logic.&lt;SEP&gt;The conditional probabilities (CPs) of the Bayesian network, generated by the fuzzy system; 540 CPs were produced.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501545</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Parameters">
  <data key="d0">Input Parameters</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input nodes of the Bayesian Network graph, described in Table I.&lt;SEP&gt;Input nodes of the network graph; they are the origins of the causes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501545</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intermediate Effects">
  <data key="d0">Intermediate Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Direct effects broken down into input parameters that are the origin of causes, shown in Table II.&lt;SEP&gt;Direct effects in the network that are broken down into the input parameters that are the origins of the causes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501545</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Final Impacts">
  <data key="d0">Final Impacts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Factors that directly influence QoS in VANET, shown in Table II.&lt;SEP&gt;Factors in the network that have a direct effect on the risk studied.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501545</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Traffic And Attack Databases">
  <data key="d0">Road Traffic And Attack Databases</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Databases containing road traffic and attacks, sources of probabilistic information; available ones are private and insufficiently rich for reliable examination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Risk Parameter Identification">
  <data key="d0">Risk Parameter Identification</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Identification of all parameters that could influence the occurrence degree of risks among different entities of the network; it led to conceiving the nature of dependencies between events and defining the causal architecture of the Bayesian model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Causal Dependencies">
  <data key="d0">Causal Dependencies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The nature of dependencies between different events, studied to define the causal architecture of the Bayesian model and to develop the causal graph from Table I and Table II.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Causal Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Causal Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A causal link between variables, illustrated by an example in Fig. 1 of the paper.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="QoS in VANET">
  <data key="d0">QoS in VANET</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Final impact measuring the degree of service satisfaction as presented to the end user in the VANET.&lt;SEP&gt;Quality of service in vehicular ad hoc networks, a variable with states bad, medium and good depending on data quality and routing.&lt;SEP&gt;Quality of Service in vehicular ad-hoc networks. It is influenced by multiple factors, and the absence of some variables can affect the efficacy of QoS prediction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
