</node>
<node id="Fuzzification">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzification</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy logic step converting input variables into a fuzzy subset using fuzzy linguistic values and membership functions.&lt;SEP&gt;First step of fuzzy logic, converting input variables into fuzzy variables using linguistic values and membership functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage that converts crisp inputs into fuzzy subsets defined by linguistic values and membership functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy conversion step translating input values into fuzzy representations using membership functions and assessing membership degrees of each fuzzy subset.&lt;SEP&gt;First step of Fuzzy Logic. It converts input variables into a fuzzy subset using fuzzy linguistic values and membership functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501527</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Inference">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Inference</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy Inference is the second step of the fuzzy logic process, following fuzzification. In this stage, the system applies fuzzy If–Then rules, evaluating and combining them to draw conclusions that produce the fuzzy outputs and determine the overall system output. Rule aggregation in this step is performed using the max operator, which merges the conclusions of the activated rules into a single fuzzy output.

Fuzzy Inference has been applied in several analytical contexts. It was used to derive the "QoS in VANET" output variable from activated fuzzy rules, as illustrated in Fig. 4. It was also applied to the variable "Occurrence of Accident" across four scenarios (S1–S4), with the results shown in Figs. 5–8. In addition, it was implemented as a method for estimating how variations in input parameters affect the nodes of a graph.

Within a probabilistic graphical modeling framework, Fuzzy Inference serves as rule-based fuzzy reasoning evaluated for each combination of parent states. The resulting membership degrees are normalized to generate conditional probability table (CPT) entries, expressed as P(X=s|c). This links fuzzy reasoning with probabilistic network modeling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defuzzification">
  <data key="d0">Defuzzification</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Defuzzification is the stage of a fuzzy logic system that converts the fuzzy outputs or conclusions produced by the inference engine into crisp numerical values. These values represent the final response of the fuzzy system and put its output into a numerical format that can be used in practical applications. Defuzzification is described both as the third step and as the final step of fuzzy logic, which suggests it is the third and concluding stage of the fuzzy reasoning process.

The role of defuzzification depends on the inference method. It is described as a required step only in the Mamdani method. The Sugeno method is said to avoid a separate defuzzification step, because its outputs are already expressed in numerical form, typically combined through a weighted average. At the same time, the Sugeno inference method is credited with an effective defuzzification system. The two accounts can be reconciled: Sugeno's weighted-average calculation acts as a simple, built-in form of defuzzification rather than a distinct, computationally demanding stage. As an example, the FisPro tool displays a standard weighted-average crisp value of 0.784 for the CPU State output variable in Fig. 6.

Choosing an appropriate defuzzification method has also been studied in applied settings, notably in the assessment of dam performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501558</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mamdani Inference Method">
  <data key="d0">Mamdani Inference Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Widely used fuzzy inference method that obtains crisp output through defuzzification of fuzzy outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Popular fuzzy inference method that uses a defuzzification phase to produce crisp outputs from fuzzy outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501545</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sugeno Inference Method">
  <data key="d0">Sugeno Inference Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Widely used fuzzy inference method using a weighted average to calculate result values; chosen by the study for its better processing time.&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy inference method selected for its rapid processing speed and effective defuzzification system.&lt;SEP&gt;Popular fuzzy inference method that computes result values using a weighted average. The study prefers it for its faster processing time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501545</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gaussian Membership Function">
  <data key="d0">Gaussian Membership Function</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The Gaussian Membership Function is a fuzzy membership function chosen for all nodes of the Bayesian Network (graph) models described. It was preferred over triangular and trapezoidal membership functions because it is smoother, produces fewer prediction errors, and has lower approximation error and inaccuracy, which makes it better at minimizing errors.

The function is defined by two parameters: a center and a spread. These are given for each linguistic term as c_i and σ_i, or equivalently as the mean (m) and standard deviation (k). Its form is given by Eq. (2): µA(x) = exp(−(x−m)² / (2k²)).

In one application, Gaussian membership functions are defined over the domain x∈[0,1] using three equidistant linguistic terms centered at 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8, each with a spread (σ) of 0.1. This configuration is specifically noted for the CPU State node.

The Gaussian function also serves as the membership function µx for other variables. Examples include the "congestion" variable and the variable "Driver reaction time," which is illustrated in Fig. 3. Beyond these uses, reference [29] employs the Gaussian membership function for voice recognition, showing its applicability in other fuzzy-logic domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501565</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triangular And Trapezoidal Membership Functions">
  <data key="d0">Triangular And Trapezoidal Membership Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Alternative membership function forms that produce more prediction errors than the Gaussian type.&lt;SEP&gt;Alternative membership functions reported to produce more inaccuracy than the Gaussian membership function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Membership Function">
  <data key="d0">Membership Function</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy membership function defined for the routing variable, shown in Fig. 3.&lt;SEP&gt;Graph representing the amplitude of each input participation; rules use input membership values to determine impact on final outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Function that, together with a linguistic variable, defines each fuzzy set; used during fuzzification to convert inputs into fuzzy variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy logic component that maps the numerical values of each variable onto linguistic values. Specifying it is one of the steps in applying fuzzy logic. Gaussian, triangular and trapezoidal functions are alternative forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fispro">
  <data key="d0">Fispro</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Open-source software (https://www.fispro.org/en/) used to implement fuzzy inference, providing exact values from different subsets of output variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421303</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="QoS In VANET">
  <data key="d0">QoS In VANET</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Output linguistic variable representing quality of service in vehicular ad hoc networks, evaluated through fuzzy inference; activated rules R2, R3 and R8 yield 'medium' and R9 yields 'good'.&lt;SEP&gt;Measure of the service degree of satisfaction presented to the end user in a VANET; Bayesian Network node with states good, medium, bad and parents routing and quality of data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-007</data>
