  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Probability Table">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Probability Table</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A Conditional Probability Table (CPT) is a table of probabilities that defines the complete probabilistic behavior of a node in a Bayesian Network (BN) across all combinations of its parent states, thereby encoding the dependencies between nodes. Reference [27] addresses methods for constructing these tables.

In the described framework, the CPTs of the Bayesian network nodes are generated from the outputs of a fuzzy inference system. The per-label aggregated firing strengths, or membership degrees, are normalized into valid probability distributions, and each row is checked to ensure it sums to unity. Expert input also informs the construction, so the CPTs are partly expert-elicited. One example is the CP table for the variable QoS in a VANET (Table VI). It is computed by normalizing fuzzy rule outputs over the states bad, medium and good, conditioned on data quality and routing.

The CPT parameters are subject to robustness and sensitivity analysis, during which the epistemic uncertainty of the expert-elicited tables is quantified. Static CPTs are noted to limit the network's long-term adaptability. They should therefore be periodically re-evaluated and learned from observed logs.&lt;SEP&gt;Tables of conditional probabilities assigned to BN nodes; the developed network requires 56,739,144 conditional probabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Table of conditional probabilities for a node, obtained by normalizing each state's aggregated value by the sum over all states; e.g. P(Reaction-time = Too Long | Condition = medium, not distracted, Visibility = medium) = 0.98.&lt;SEP&gt;CP tables computed by normalizing fuzzy rule conclusions so that the probabilities of each variable's states sum to 1, e.g. for the variable congestion given density and flooding attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">10.2478_acss-2022-0011.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vehicular Ad Hoc Network">
  <data key="d0">Vehicular Ad Hoc Network</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">VANET, the vehicular network whose QoS and risks are analysed.&lt;SEP&gt;VANET; a MANET application forming a network of cars to inform drivers about accidents and traffic, where confidentiality and authentication are vital issues.&lt;SEP&gt;VANET; a wireless network in which vehicles equipped with on-board units communicate with each other and with infrastructure.&lt;SEP&gt;VANET. A network of vehicles that is the central topic of many cited works on security, attacks and routing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-db5423cfc6b0239a805a929baa02d1e9-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ccc87fb659a572e9c9db17272d2f6955-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-010</data>
