<node id="arXiv">
  <data key="d0">arXiv</data>
  <data key="d1">venue</data>
  <data key="d2">arXiv is an open-access preprint server and database that hosts research papers across many scientific fields and publication years. It is frequently cited as the hosting venue for numerous referenced works. These include reference [30] from 2021, reference 29 posted in 2023 as arXiv:2303.01178, reference [9] from 2025, and reference [20]. It also hosts references 7, 12, 13, 14, 16 and 17, along with a further group comprising references 53, 54, 57, 58, 59, 60, 62, 64 and 65.

In machine learning and generative modeling, arXiv hosts several foundational and survey papers. Among them is the seminal paper on Generative Adversarial Networks (abs/1406.2661), along with other cited GAN papers such as Radford et al. (2015) and Choi et al. (2017), and studies applying GANs to synthetic financial data generation. For tabular data synthesis it hosts TabDiff, AutoDiff, a survey on tabular data generation, and the Conditional GAN paper on tabular data modeling. It also hosts related works by Kamthe et al. (2021), Kotelnikov et al. (2022) and Li et al. (2025), as well as Barr et al. (2025), Cardoso et al. (2021) and Del Gobbo (2025).

In probabilistic modeling, arXiv hosts "A tutorial on learning with Bayesian networks" (2022) and a 2021 survey on Bayesian network structure learning. It also hosts "Making Sensitivity Analysis Computationally Efficient," posted as arXiv:1301.3868 in January 2013.

In retrieval and language-model research, arXiv hosts the original Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) paper from 2020 and the Atlas paper from 2022. It also hosts cited works on FAISS, the similarity search library, and RAGAS, the RAG evaluation framework.

arXiv also hosts applied research across diverse domains. Examples include a 2019 survey on VANET (vehicular ad hoc network) security, a 2021 paper by Shugran, and a 2024 paper on sentiment analysis of drug reviews. It further hosts cited works on predictive maintenance and failure prediction, and the Truss and Boehm paper on support ticket classification (arXiv:2406.01789).

Beyond hosting individual cited works, arXiv serves as a literature source for systematic reviews. One such review identified it as one of four major databases from which relevant papers were collected.&lt;SEP&gt;Preprint server hosting a version of paper [35].</data>
