<node id="Scopus">
  <data key="d0">Scopus</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Scopus is a comprehensive, multidisciplinary bibliographic literature database launched by Elsevier in 2004 and available by subscription. It is widely used across disciplines and has served as a primary data source in many bibliometric studies. Researchers often choose it for its relevance, broad coverage, and high number of indexed articles. Its records are commonly searched, exported (frequently in CSV format), and analyzed to map publication trends, subject-area distributions, citation and publication metrics, and other bibliographic characteristics across research fields.

Scopus has been used both as a sole data source and as one of several merged sources. In burnout research, it was the database for a bibliometric analysis of burnout publications. One query there returned 2,088 documents, and the results were used to show how burnout documents are distributed by subject area. In other studies, Scopus data were merged with records from Web of Science, and in some cases also with PubMed, to create combined datasets for analysis. Several cited works compare Scopus with other databases, including comparisons of its journal coverage with that of Web of Science, or merge it with them for bibliometric purposes.

Scopus has also supported research on explainable artificial intelligence (XAI). It was used to analyze yearly XAI publications from 2018 to 2023, with the search conducted on 2 February 2024. In another study, a search covering 2019 to 2023 yielded 132 articles, which were exported as CSV files for further processing.

In a study of artificial intelligence and market analysis, Scopus was the sole data source and was selected for its broader coverage. An initial keyword search yielded 206 papers, and a refined query yielded 4,051 relevant documents published over 35 years. One description gives this figure as 4,015 documents, which most likely reflects a transcription discrepancy for the same dataset, so the exact count is uncertain. That study acknowledged that relying solely on Scopus, and on the specific keywords chosen, may have influenced its results. Scopus has also been cited as the source of the bibliometric data for an article under examination.

The differing document counts and time frames across these uses come from separate studies with distinct topics, queries, and periods, not from inconsistencies about the database itself. Together, these uses show Scopus as a versatile and widely relied-upon resource for bibliometric research on subjects ranging from occupational burnout to explainable AI and AI-driven market analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Bibliographic database used as a source of papers for the systematic review.&lt;SEP&gt;Bibliographic database developed by Elsevier in 2004. It is considered highly reliable and is used in the review.&lt;SEP&gt;Database from which papers analyzed in the systematic review on GANs in finance were drawn.&lt;SEP&gt;Bibliographic database that served as one of the four sources of the 30 papers analyzed in the review.&lt;SEP&gt;One of four major databases from which relevant papers were collected for the review.</data>
