</node>
<node id="Bayesian networks combine probability theory and graph theory">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian networks combine probability theory and graph theory</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">It is a combination between both probability theory (Fellaji et al., 2014; Lamaakchaoui et al., 2014) and graphs theory to make a probabilistic graphical model (Kitson et al., 2023).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian networks allow explicit representation under uncertainty based on logical relationships">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian networks allow explicit representation under uncertainty based on logical relationships</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Bayesian networks are considered as a probabilistic study and the power of using this type of methods is in the possibility of including an explicit representation, even with some uncertainties, and that it is based on the logical relationships between variables (Krause &amp; Bokinala, 2023).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy sets are defined through linguistic variables and membership functions">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy sets are defined through linguistic variables and membership functions</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">It achieves this by defining each fuzzy set through a linguistic variable and a membership function, offering a more nuanced way to represent data (Diabagate et al., 2014).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy logic generates conditional probabilities via fuzzification, inference, and defuzzification">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy logic generates conditional probabilities via fuzzification, inference, and defuzzification</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Due to it relevance for qualitative variables, it was implemented to generate conditional probabilities following three steps: fuzzification, inference, and defuzzification (Pop et al., 2020; Sattar et al., 2019).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mamdani and Sugeno differ in deducing crisp output from fuzzy inputs">
  <data key="d0">Mamdani and Sugeno differ in deducing crisp output from fuzzy inputs</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">When Mamdani relies on the defuzzification process for fuzzy outputs, Sugeno calculates the result values through a weighted average. (Bian &amp; Zhang, 2009; Khalfaoui et al., 2023).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Bayesian networks tackle uncertain variables and causal dependencies">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Bayesian networks tackle uncertain variables and causal dependencies</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy Bayesian networks result from the fusion of Bayesian networks and fuzzy logic theory, a strategy commonly used to tackle problems involving uncertain variables or to represent causal dependencies among these variables (Benallou et al., 2023; Zarei et al., 2019).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Probability Theory">
  <data key="d0">Probability Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical theory that, combined with graph theory, forms the basis of Bayesian networks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Graph Theory">
  <data key="d0">Graph Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mathematical theory that, combined with probability theory, allows Bayesian networks to form a probabilistic graphical model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Probabilistic Graphical Model">
  <data key="d0">Probabilistic Graphical Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Graph-based model built from dependence relations between variables (nodes); Bayesian networks are this type of model.&lt;SEP&gt;Class of models to which Bayesian networks belong; described as powerful for learner modeling under uncertainty.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linguistic Variable">
  <data key="d0">Linguistic Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variable whose values are fuzzy linguistic terms; used with membership functions to define fuzzy sets and to fuzzify input variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Variable whose values are expressed qualitatively in natural language, such as the degree of 'fatal injury' for the 'occurrence of accident' node.&lt;SEP&gt;A fuzzy variable defined by a quadruplet: a name X, a universe of discourse U, a set of linguistic values T(X), and membership functions µx.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Risk Prediction">
  <data key="d0">Risk Prediction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prediction of risk in complex systems, facilitated by the fuzzy Bayesian approach combining fuzzy logic and expert knowledge.&lt;SEP&gt;Prediction of risk in complex systems, which the FB approach is designed to facilitate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expert Knowledge">
  <data key="d0">Expert Knowledge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Knowledge from experts that the fuzzy Bayesian approach combines with fuzzy logic through Bayesian network modeling.&lt;SEP&gt;Knowledge gathered through literature review, expert consultations, interviews and brainstorming sessions, combined with fuzzy theory to formulate rules and derive conditional probabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary source of model parameters due to limited historical failure data; inaccuracies may influence risk estimates.&lt;SEP&gt;Knowledge and insights held by field specialists that may not be present in the data. Subjective Bayesian network construction methods can incorporate it, and numerous studies in the literature have relied on it.&lt;SEP&gt;Knowledge from experts, who identify the parameters influencing risk, define the causal architecture of the Bayesian model, and determine the fuzzy rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501527</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Computational Complexity">
  <data key="d0">Computational Complexity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource-intensive inference in fuzzy Bayesian networks, in computation time and resources, which becomes pronounced for large networks or those with many states and limits real-time responses or frequent updates.&lt;SEP&gt;Limitation of the hybrid model: integrating multiple models is computationally demanding, especially with large datasets. Future work aims to optimize efficiency, explore cloud-based solutions and apply model compression.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423547</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Bayesian network inference is computationally resource-intensive">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Bayesian network inference is computationally resource-intensive</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "While fuzzy Bayesian networks are well-suited for addressing the specified problem, conducting inference in these networks can be resource-intensive, particularly in terms of computation time and computational resources."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Overload">
  <data key="d0">Overload</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Situation in which employees are burdened by excessive work and tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the causes of the CPU reaching a critical state. A maintained overload is the failure outcome of load balancing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obstacles Level">
  <data key="d0">Obstacles Level</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Level of barriers such as workloads, office temptations and time constraints. When this level is high, it can reduce engagement in healthy lifestyles.&lt;SEP&gt;An input variable in the fuzzy burnout model, with linguistic values Low, Medium and High.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anxiety">
  <data key="d0">Anxiety</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nervousness related to future uncertainties or stressful situations.&lt;SEP&gt;An intermediate variable with linguistic values Minimal, Moderate and Severe.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the interconnected variables in the burnout landscape.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pression">
  <data key="d0">Pression</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pressure resulting from long working hours, large numbers of tasks and negative relations.&lt;SEP&gt;An intermediate variable in the fuzzy burnout model, with linguistic values Low, Medium and High.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Work Environment Conflicts">
  <data key="d0">Work Environment Conflicts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Disputes and disagreements between employees or groups of employees in companies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Frustration">
  <data key="d0">Frustration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Feeling of dissatisfaction or irritation that an employee faces when obstacles or challenges are high. It is also used to describe the tension variable.&lt;SEP&gt;An intermediate variable (Minimal, Occasional, Frequent) used as an input for the burnout rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Personal Problems">
  <data key="d0">Personal Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Individual challenges or personal issues that can significantly impact an employee's well-being and performance.&lt;SEP&gt;An intermediate variable in the fuzzy burnout model, with linguistic values Rare, Occasional and Frequent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
