<node id="Fuzzy Rules">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy Rules are IF-THEN statements that form the core of a fuzzy inference system. They are a powerful tool for modeling complex systems and for decision-making within fuzzy logic. Each rule captures a fuzzy relationship between input and output variables and guides the system in converting inputs into outputs. During inference, the rules are evaluated and combined to reach conclusions, which become the fuzzy outputs of the system. A typical example is: "IF road is dangerous, driver's performance is bad and Physical Vehicle condition is bad, THEN occurrence of accident will have a fatal injury."

Fuzzy Rules are generally elicited from experts, sometimes combined with numerical data, and experts calibrate them iteratively. In the fuzzy-Bayesian (FB) approach, the fuzzy logic system uses Fuzzy Rules to generate conditional probabilities for the fuzzy nodes of a Bayesian Network (BN). The size of the rule base depends on the node, for example 3, 9, 27 or 81 rules; the User Activity node has 9 rules. Rule firing strengths are normalized into probabilities. For instance, strengths of Normal 0.002, Degraded 0.044 and Critical 0.882 give P=(0.002, 0.048, 0.950). Ref [15] also uses Fuzzy Rules to process network and application information.

Several specific rule bases appear across the applications described:

- **Driver Reaction Time:** a set of 27 rules (Table IV) maps Driver Condition (Bad/Medium/Good), Distracted (Distracted/Half-Distracted/Not-Distracted) and Visibility (Good/Medium/Bad) to reaction-time values of 0.2, 0.5 or 0.8.
- **Network congestion:** a system with 180 rules in total uses nine rules (Table 4) for congestion alone, combining density and flooding attacks. After inference, the activated rules R5, R6, R8 and R9 produce general conclusions of 0.044 for medium and 0.607 for high congestion. The low state has zero possibility, but a minimum value of 0.001 is tolerated for it.
- **Burnout and work-life balance:** a set of 9 rules (Figure 7) defines burnout from frustration and the achievement of work-life balance. Four of these rules were activated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501554</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
