<node id="Burnout Prevention">
  <data key="d0">Burnout Prevention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Comprehensive approach advocated by the study, including organizational culture initiatives, work-life balance, psychological support, employee assistance programs, flexible work arrangements and mental health resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communication effectiveness moderates the relationship between communication overload and burnout">
  <data key="d0">Communication effectiveness moderates the relationship between communication overload and burnout</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "It also shows that communication effectiveness can moderate the relationship between communication overload and burnout. In the scenarios with weak communication, burnout is higher than in the scenarios with medium or high communication."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communication overload and obstacle level positively correlate with burnout">
  <data key="d0">Communication overload and obstacle level positively correlate with burnout</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Table 6 shows that there is a positive correlation between communication overload, obstacle level, and burnout. As communication overload and obstacle level increase, burnout also increases. This is consistent with previous research that has shown that these factors can contribute to burnout."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload alone does not determine burnout levels">
  <data key="d0">Workload alone does not determine burnout levels</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Conclusion stated in the text: "The study challenges the simplistic view that workload alone determines burnout levels, shedding light on the complex interplay of elements such as communication effectiveness, workload patterns, and the presence of obstacles."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 6 Burnout Scenario Results">
  <data key="d0">Table 6 Burnout Scenario Results</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Set of 27 scenarios reported in Table 6 of the burnout study. Each scenario combines communication (Weak, Medium, High), overload (Rare, Occasional, Frequent) and obstacles level (Low, Medium, High) with a resulting burnout level (Medium or High). The scenarios show that burnout rises with communication overload and obstacle level.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Burnout is lowest with strong communication and low obstacles">
  <data key="d0">Burnout is lowest with strong communication and low obstacles</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Key finding stated in the text: "burnout is lowest when communication is strong, and obstacles are low, it is highest when communication is weak, and obstacles are high, and it is more likely to occur when communication is occasional, and obstacles are medium."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Open and effective communication reduces burnout risk">
  <data key="d0">Open and effective communication reduces burnout risk</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Implication stated in the text: "organizations should strive to maintain open and effective communication with their employees. This can help to reduce the risk of burnout by ensuring that employees feel heard and understood."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supportive work environments reduce burnout and increase productivity">
  <data key="d0">Supportive work environments reduce burnout and increase productivity</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "By taking these steps, organizations can help to create a more supportive and healthy work environment for their employees. This can lead to reduced burnout, increased productivity, and improved employee morale."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Workload Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Element in the complex interplay of burnout factors, considered together with communication effectiveness and the presence of obstacles rather than as the sole determinant of burnout.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421472</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Organizational Culture">
  <data key="d0">Organizational Culture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Area of initiatives included in the comprehensive burnout prevention approach advocated by the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Area proposed for expanded AI applications to provide more holistic HR management.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7f659f89172b81a0adff01963801bdcb-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">4597JPB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;ACS_10_THEPOTENTIALOFARTIFICIALINTELLIGENCEINHUMANRESOURCEMANAGEMENT.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422721</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
