<node id="Burnout">
  <data key="d0">Burnout</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Burnout is a psychological syndrome and workplace phenomenon defined as a state of physical, emotional, mental, physiological and psychological fatigue and suffering. It is caused by prolonged, intense and excessive stress, particularly from workplace stressors, and is frequently encountered in demanding work environments. Burnout, often described as occupational exhaustion, is increasingly prevalent among employees and is recognized as a significant and pervasive workplace challenge that threatens both individual well-being and organizational success. It is closely associated with professional burnout and job stress, and it featured prominently in a factorial analysis.

The literature characterizes Burnout as multifactorial. Its development is influenced by demographics, organizational elements and personality traits, as well as a network of interconnected factors. These include communication, workload or overload, obstacle levels, anxiety, pressure, work environment, tension, stress, satisfaction, personal problems, frustration and work-life balance. Psychological aspects also contribute to its emergence.

Burnout is studied across many disciplines and is supported by a growing body of literature. Medicine accounts for 25.8% of the research, Psychology for 17.5%, Business and Management for 15.7%, and Social Sciences for 13.0%. Burnout is the central topic of a 2024 article, which argues that it should not be worn as a badge of honor. That article cites several related works, including studies by Kumareswaran and by Lee et al. The cited works also cover job burnout, activist burnout, limbic brain structures, mindfulness interventions, neurophysiology and computational modeling.

Within the 2024 study, a fuzzy Bayesian network methodology is applied to Burnout as a workplace well-being problem. Burnout serves as the output variable of the fuzzy system, with the linguistic values Low, Medium and High. The resulting values were Low at 0.001, Medium at 0.135 and High at 0.607, which indicates a predominance of the high Burnout level.&lt;SEP&gt;HR risk studied in bibliometric analyses, alongside turnover.&lt;SEP&gt;A pervasive challenge that threatens workplace well-being and organizational success.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bed29169806bd2571a0eff67abe0e549-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7f659f89172b81a0adff01963801bdcb-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-020</data>
