<node id="Prolonged sitting, vehicle ergonomics and vibrations are major MSD risk factors">
  <data key="d0">Prolonged sitting, vehicle ergonomics and vibrations are major MSD risk factors</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Prolonged sitting, vehicle ergonomics, and vibrations are major risk factors [4].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSD risk increases with age and career years">
  <data key="d0">MSD risk increases with age and career years</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In addition, the risk of MSDs increases with age and career years [5].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Whole-body vibration on uneven roads amplifies seat wobbles">
  <data key="d0">Whole-body vibration on uneven roads amplifies seat wobbles</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-term exposure to vibration throughout the body [6], especially on uneven roads, amplifies seat wobbles when accelerating [7].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Long journeys without regular stops increase muscle tension">
  <data key="d0">Long journeys without regular stops increase muscle tension</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Another important risk factor is the length of the journey and the lack of regular stops, which increases muscle tension [8].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High BMI and lack of exercise contribute to MSDs">
  <data key="d0">High BMI and lack of exercise contribute to MSDs</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Additionally, a high body mass index (BMI) and a lack of regular exercise contribute more to the development of musculoskeletal disorders [9].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSDs are the leading cause of permanent disability in 60% of reported cases">
  <data key="d0">MSDs are the leading cause of permanent disability in 60% of reported cases</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In addition, MSDs are the leading cause of permanent disability in 60% of reported cases [10].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="More than half of European workers affected by MSDs report work absence">
  <data key="d0">More than half of European workers affected by MSDs report work absence</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In European companies, more than half of workers affected by MSDs report absence from work, and these employees are generally absent for more extended periods than those with other health problems. In addition, MSDs are the leading cause of permanent disability in 60% of reported cases [10].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professional Drivers">
  <data key="d0">Professional Drivers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Truck, bus, taxi and delivery vehicle drivers who transport goods and people. Prolonged sitting, vibration, repetitive manual activities, psychosocial factors and individual characteristics expose them to musculoskeletal disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;Occupational population studied: 277 drivers of taxis, trucks, buses and service vehicles. The drivers were grouped by age, BMI, driving experience and weekly driving time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Whole-Body Vibration">
  <data key="d0">Whole-Body Vibration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-term exposure to vibration throughout the body during driving, especially on uneven roads. It is identified as a major risk factor for musculoskeletal disorders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Body Mass Index">
  <data key="d0">Body Mass Index</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">BMI variable in the dataset specifying the driver's body mass index.&lt;SEP&gt;BMI; calculated from height and weight estimates and classified as underweight/normal (&lt;25), overweight (25–30) or obese (≥30).&lt;SEP&gt;Body mass index (BMI), a personal variable in the driver profiling model. A high BMI contributes to the development of musculoskeletal disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;BMI; one of the most influential factors in cluster formation (cluster means 29.26, 32.51, 21.97).&lt;SEP&gt;BMI, a key variable used in the driver segmentation model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Only 21% of drivers are at low risk of developing MSDs">
  <data key="d0">Only 21% of drivers are at low risk of developing MSDs</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of the paper: "The model estimates suggest that only 21% of drivers are at low risk of developing MSDs, highlighting the high prevalence of these disorders within this occupation."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Matos et al. [11]">
  <data key="d0">Matos et al. [11]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [11]; proposes a system monitoring textile machinery workers to detect high-risk MSD movements, combining motion capture, time series forecasting with machine learning (SVM, XG, MLP, deep LSTM) and a rule-based risk detection module.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Su et al. [12]">
  <data key="d0">Su et al. [12]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [12]; investigates decision trees for assessing ergonomic risks of musculoskeletal disorders among sewing machine operators, highlighting relationships between body segments and risk patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
