</node>
<node id="Convolutional LSTM">
  <data key="d0">Convolutional LSTM</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Deep learning model used to recognize awkward worker postures from inertial sensor data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Convolutional Neural Network">
  <data key="d0">Convolutional Neural Network</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is a class of deep learning architecture designed primarily for visual data analysis. It learns hierarchical features through convolutional filters and is typically composed of convolutional, activation, pooling, fully connected, and softmax layers. CNNs are supervised learning methods trained using backpropagation. Their ability to learn intricate patterns from data has made them successful in both image processing and natural language processing. They are widely used for image recognition, classification, object detection, and segmentation, and are recognized as a current and trending research topic across multiple domains, especially cancer research.

In medicine, CNNs are commonly used to analyze medical images and have been applied to various cancers. These include prostate cancer since 2023, where Ao et al. (2023) used CNNs for Gleason scoring. CNNs have also been used to detect bone tumor malignancy from MRI scans and, in several cited works, to classify brain tumors.

Beyond medicine, CNNs support a broad range of applications:

- **Remote sensing and vision:** road extraction and face attribute classification. In the pipeline described in This Paper, CNNs perform road-presence classification, and eight CNN architectures are evaluated for road quality classification.
- **Business analytics:** De Caigny et al. use CNNs to incorporate textual information into churn prediction models.
- **Forecasting:** Ensafi et al. employ a CNN model for furniture sales forecasting.
- **Finance:** Kumar et al. use a CNN as the discriminator of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) in their stock prediction method.

In manufacturing and quality control, CNNs are used to classify parts as good or bad, and they appear in several cited defect detection studies. One study applied a CNN to defect detection in injection molded parts and evaluated it in two configurations. On raw images without Canny filtering, the model achieved 95.31% accuracy, 94.24% precision, 100% recall, and a 97.03% F1 score. With Canny edge-detection pre-processing, performance improved to 99.57% accuracy, 99.44% precision, 100% recall, and a 99.72% F1 score.

Despite these strengths, CNNs have noted limitations. They are characterized as conventional deep learning models that mainly operate on single-modal data, which limits their understanding of complex relationships. In addition, in the work of Amin et al., a CNN served as a reference classifier that was outperformed by their adaptive Naive Bayes (NB) approach.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;acs_25_66701.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;GANsinsyntheticfinancialdata_donalsonwilson_versionpublie.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467129</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linear Regression">
  <data key="d0">Linear Regression</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical method compared against Random Forest for mining truck driver musculoskeletal discomfort.&lt;SEP&gt;Linear regression, a reference classifier in Amin et al.&lt;SEP&gt;Forecasting technique that Deepa and Raghuram used among their time series forecasting methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Time series forecasting technique used by Deepa and Raghuram (9) on daily sales records of 1115 stores.&lt;SEP&gt;Regression model that uses independent variables to understand how changes in them relate to changes in the dependent variable. The text uses it as an example in the logistic regression section.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf</data>
