<node id="K-Means">
  <data key="d0">K-Means</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">K-Means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm that partitions data into a predefined number of groups. Its main disadvantage is that the number of clusters (k) must be specified in advance, so the optimal value of k has to be determined before the algorithm can be applied effectively.

In a study of drivers, K-Means was used to segment the driver population according to their susceptibility to developing musculoskeletal disorders (MSDs). After the optimal k was determined, the algorithm produced three clusters containing 527, 575 and 285 drivers. Among the approaches considered, K-Means gave the most interpretable and well-separated clusters for this dataset, and it proved effective for driver segmentation.

K-Means is also widely used in customer churn prediction research, often as a segmentation step before predictive modeling. It is applied to group customers into segments prior to churn prediction. Xiahou and Harada combined K-Means with Support Vector Machines (SVM) for churn prediction in B2C e-commerce. In banking, K-Means was used in reference [37] for churn prediction, and it has also been applied alongside the anomaly detection techniques LOF (Local Outlier Factor) and CBLOF (Cluster-Based Local Outlier Factor) to predict churn in banking systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423768</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
