</node>
<node id="Ergonomics">
  <data key="d0">Ergonomics</data>
  <data key="d1">venue</data>
  <data key="d2">Workplace and vehicle ergonomic conditions, including seating position, adjustable seats and steering wheels, and armrests, considered as risk factors for MSDs.&lt;SEP&gt;Journal that published reference [19] in 2001.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unsupervised Machine Learning">
  <data key="d0">Unsupervised Machine Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Category of machine learning operating on unlabeled data, to which k-means belongs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lack of Objective Clinical Validation">
  <data key="d0">Lack of Objective Clinical Validation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Research gap found in previous studies: the reported musculoskeletal disorders were not objectively validated in a clinical setting. The text says this limits the validity of the collected data and the external validity of the results.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Omission of Decisive Risk Variables">
  <data key="d0">Omission of Decisive Risk Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Research gap found in most previous studies: potentially decisive variables were not taken into account. Examples are seating-position ergonomics, road surface quality and effort intensity. The text says their exclusion limits the explanatory value of the models and may lead to under- or overestimation of risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Number of Clusters Selection">
  <data key="d0">Number of Clusters Selection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The main disadvantage of k-means is the need to predict the number of clusters accurately. The Silhouette and Elbow methods are used to address it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Cleaning">
  <data key="d0">Data Cleaning</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Preprocessing step in which the professional drivers dataset was cleaned to detect and correct inaccurate, incomplete or erroneous entries.&lt;SEP&gt;Preprocessing step that assigns zero sales to unrecorded days so that every day of the analyzed period is included, giving a continuous and reliable temporal analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Python">
  <data key="d0">Python</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Programming language used to generate numerical values for age, BMI, weekly driving time and driving experience so that clustering algorithms could be applied.&lt;SEP&gt;Programming language whose libraries the paper uses to implement the forecasting models.&lt;SEP&gt;Programming language (v3.10.12) used to implement fuzzy-set definition, rule processing, CPT generation and Monte Carlo robustness analysis, with NumPy, Pandas, SciPy and Matplotlib.&lt;SEP&gt;Open-source, object-oriented programming language widely used in data science and high-performance computing; used to implement the framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Programming language offering extensive libraries for data manipulation and numerical analysis with readability and modularity, used to develop the synthetic data generation system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transport Sector">
  <data key="d0">Transport Sector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sector for which the study aims to support specific preventive measures and a more efficient ergonomic design policy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Segmentation Process">
  <data key="d0">Driver Segmentation Process</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Process illustrated in Figure 2 of the paper for segmenting drivers based on personal, ergonomic and driving-condition attributes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Dataset">
  <data key="d0">Driver Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset described in Table 1 containing driver attributes: Age, BMI, weekly driving time (WHW), driving experience (EXP), frequency of heavy lifting (FLHO), weekly physical activity hours (WEH), seat adjustability (ST), steering wheel adjustability (STR), armrest presence (ARM), journey nature (TRP) and road condition (RD).&lt;SEP&gt;Standardized data on drivers with features Age, BMI, WHW, EXP, FLHO, WEH and WCS; 1387 drivers grouped into three clusters (527, 575, 285).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weekly Driving Time">
  <data key="d0">Weekly Driving Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">WHW variable indicating the driver's weekly driving time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driving Experience">
  <data key="d0">Driving Experience</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">EXP variable reflecting the driver's total years of driving experience.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency of Heavy Lifting">
  <data key="d0">Frequency of Heavy Lifting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">FLHO variable containing the frequency of heavy lifting per day, ranging from 0 to 8.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequency with which drivers lift heavy objects, a key variable integrated into the driver segmentation model for MSD risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weekly Physical Activity">
  <data key="d0">Weekly Physical Activity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">WEH variable giving the number of hours of physical activity per week, ranging from 0 to 8.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Seat Adjustment Options">
  <data key="d0">Seat Adjustment Options</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">ST criterion indicating whether the vehicle seat is adjustable in height, depth or tilt; scored 0 if not adjustable, otherwise 1 point per available option, summed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Steering Wheel Adjustment Options">
  <data key="d0">Steering Wheel Adjustment Options</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">STR criterion specifying whether the steering wheel is adjustable; scored 1 if adjustment options exist and 0 otherwise.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Armrest Presence">
  <data key="d0">Armrest Presence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">ARM criterion specifying whether armrests are present; scored 1 if present and 0 if not.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Journey Nature">
  <data key="d0">Journey Nature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">TRP variable indicating the nature of journeys: short, medium or long.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Condition">
  <data key="d0">Road Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">RD variable indicating the condition of roads: poor, average or good.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Numerical Criteria Representation">
  <data key="d0">Non-Numerical Criteria Representation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scoring scheme described in Table 2 for converting non-numerical criteria (seat, steering wheel and armrest adjustability) into numerical scores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Age">
  <data key="d0">Driver Age</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Age variable in the dataset described in Table 1, giving the driver's age.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Musculoskeletal Disorder Susceptibility">
  <data key="d0">Musculoskeletal Disorder Susceptibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The research problem of assessing how susceptible professional drivers are to musculoskeletal disorders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Working Conditions Score (WCS)">
  <data key="d0">Working Conditions Score (WCS)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Composite score replacing columns ST, STR, ARM, TRP and RD, obtained by adding coefficients related to vehicle ergonomics, journey length and road state to reflect drivers' working conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nature of Trips (TRP)">
  <data key="d0">Nature of Trips (TRP)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset variable describing the nature of the drivers' trips, scored 0 for long trips, 1 for medium trips and 2 for short trips. TRP is one of the columns removed and merged into the Working Conditions Score (WCS).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Conditions (RD)">
  <data key="d0">Road Conditions (RD)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset variable describing road conditions, scored 0 for roads in poor condition, 1 for average condition and 2 for good condition. RD is one of the columns removed and merged into the Working Conditions Score (WCS).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synthetic Data Augmentation">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Data Augmentation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Algorithmic approach that expanded the original 277 observations to 1,385 synthetic individuals by controlled reproduction of existing profiles, generating random values within ranges derived from observed distributions while preserving correlations and observed frequencies, followed by validation checks for consistency and representativeness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Standardization">
  <data key="d0">Data Standardization</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Normalization of the dataset, essential because the model relies on measuring distances between data points where dimensions play a crucial role.&lt;SEP&gt;Preprocessing step in which driver features (Age, BMI, WHW, EXP, FLHO, WEH, WCS) were scaled to standardized values, as shown by the centered, unit-scaled values in the data table preceding clustering.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distance-Based Model">
  <data key="d0">Distance-Based Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Model used in the study that is based on measuring distances between data, requiring standardized inputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Body Mass Index (BMI)">
  <data key="d0">Body Mass Index (BMI)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Driver attribute included in the dataset; BMI categories were preserved during data augmentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vehicle Ergonomics">
  <data key="d0">Vehicle Ergonomics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Working-condition factor whose coefficients are summed into the Working Conditions Score. It is presumably represented by the ST, STR and ARM columns of the original dataset.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Validation Checks">
  <data key="d0">Validation Checks</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Checks performed on the expanded dataset of 1,385 synthetic individuals to ensure internal consistency and representativeness, so that analyses on the expanded data are reliable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driving Experience (EXP)">
  <data key="d0">Driving Experience (EXP)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset variable (EXP) recording drivers' driving experience. Driving experience levels were preserved during data augmentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weekly Driving Hours">
  <data key="d0">Weekly Driving Hours</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Driver attribute whose levels were maintained when generating synthetic instances. It likely corresponds to one of the dataset's working-hour columns (e.g. WHW).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Age">
  <data key="d0">Age</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset variable recording driver age. Age groups were maintained during data augmentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Driver age, one of the most influential factors in K-Means cluster formation, with significant differences between cluster means (41.28, 56.72 and 26.77 years for clusters 0, 1 and 2).&lt;SEP&gt;Drivers' age, a key variable integrated into the driver segmentation model for MSD risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node 15 with linguistic values Adolescent, Adulthood, Middle-Age.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principal Component Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Principal Component Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Dimensionality reduction technique (PCA) used solely for visualization, projecting high-dimensional driver data onto two principal components to plot clusters on a 2D chart (Figure 4).&lt;SEP&gt;PCA, used to visualize the K-Means clusters.&lt;SEP&gt;Dimensionality reduction technique. It is the second most common keyword and is used by Babenko et al. for feature extraction.&lt;SEP&gt;A dimensionality reduction method (PCA) included in the search query.&lt;SEP&gt;Foundational topic still in development; plays a prominent role in feature selection and dimensionality reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Preprocessing technique used in article (14) for aerial image road extraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_bibliometric_analysis_of_feature_selection_techn.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Clustering">
  <data key="d0">Driver Clustering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Grouping of drivers into clusters based on their characteristics; three clusters were obtained.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Optimal Number Of Clusters">
  <data key="d0">Optimal Number Of Clusters</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The value of k needed to implement K-means; found to be 3 via the Elbow method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cluster Visualization">
  <data key="d0">Cluster Visualization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">2D visualization of cluster separation and internal organization obtained by projecting data onto two principal components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dimensionality Reduction">
  <data key="d0">Dimensionality Reduction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Technique category to which PCA belongs; in this work PCA was not used to reduce features for modeling but only to project data into two principal components for visualization.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequent keyword in feature selection publications.&lt;SEP&gt;A topic closely related to feature selection. It is used as a search term in the bibliometric query.&lt;SEP&gt;Area in which deep learning and principal component analysis play prominent roles.&lt;SEP&gt;Techniques for managing high-dimensional data in machine learning, reducing complexity and overfitting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_bibliometric_analysis_of_feature_selection_techn.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Silhouette Score">
  <data key="d0">Silhouette Score</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Internal validation metric used to compare the clustering algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Internal clustering validation metric; yielded 0.218, indicating moderate cohesion and separation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calinski-Harabasz Index">
  <data key="d0">Calinski-Harabasz Index</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Internal validation metric used to compare the clustering algorithms.&lt;SEP&gt;Internal clustering validation metric; yielded 538.93, indicating strong inter-cluster distinction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weekly Hours Worked">
  <data key="d0">Weekly Hours Worked</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">WHW, weekly driving time; influential factor in cluster formation (means 50.09, 64.17, 38.78 hours).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professional Experience">
  <data key="d0">Professional Experience</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">EXP; years of driving experience, an influential clustering factor (means 12.58, 25.55, 2.72 years).&lt;SEP&gt;Years of experience of drivers (EXP), a key variable integrated into the driver segmentation model for MSD risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Working Conditions Score">
  <data key="d0">Working Conditions Score</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">WCS; perceived working conditions score with less influence on cluster formation (means 4.11, 3.97, 3.84).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cluster 0">
  <data key="d0">Cluster 0</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">38% of drivers; middle-aged (about 41), BMI 29.26, about 50 hours/week, about 12 years experience, best working conditions (4.11); labeled average risk of MSDs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cluster 1">
  <data key="d0">Cluster 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">41% of drivers; older (about 56), BMI 32.51, 64.17 hours/week, about 25 years experience, working conditions 3.97; classified as high risk of MSDs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cluster 2">
  <data key="d0">Cluster 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">21% of drivers; young (about 27), BMI 21.97, lowest workload (about 39 hours/week) and least experience (2.72 years).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Older drivers with high workload are at high risk of MSDs">
  <data key="d0">Older drivers with high workload are at high risk of MSDs</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding in the text: "It follows, therefore, that this group could be classified as drivers who are at high risk of developing MSDs."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FLHO">
  <data key="d0">FLHO</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clustering variable linked to the frequency of lifting or carrying heavy loads (up to about 4 times a day). It has less influence on cluster formation because its means are similar across clusters (3.53, 3.50, 3.69).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="WEH">
  <data key="d0">WEH</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clustering variable linked to drivers' weekly physical activity. It has less influence on cluster formation because its means are similar across clusters (2.55, 2.52, 2.30). Physical activity remains low in clusters 0 and 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preventive Measures for High-Risk Driver Groups">
  <data key="d0">Preventive Measures for High-Risk Driver Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Targeted interventions made possible by analyzing each driver cluster's characteristics, aimed more precisely at high-risk groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Age, BMI, WHW and EXP are the most influential factors in cluster formation">
  <data key="d0">Age, BMI, WHW and EXP are the most influential factors in cluster formation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding in the text: "Age, BMI, WHW, and EXP appear to be the most influential factors in cluster formation, with significant differences between the averages of the clusters."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clustering structure is reasonably coherent and robust">
  <data key="d0">Clustering structure is reasonably coherent and robust</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding in the text: "These values suggest that the clustering structure is reasonably coherent, with moderate cohesion and separation (as indicated by the Silhouette Score), and strong inter-cluster distinction (as reflected by the high Calinski-Harabasz Index)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K-means">
  <data key="d0">K-means</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Clustering algorithm used with k = 3. It achieved the highest scores (Silhouette Score = 0.218, Calinski-Harabasz Index = 538.93) and was judged the most suitable method for the driver dataset.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GMM">
  <data key="d0">GMM</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Gaussian Mixture Model clustering tested with n_components = 3, yielding Silhouette Score 0.163 and Calinski-Harabasz Index 440.86.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PCA">
  <data key="d0">PCA</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Component Analysis, used to visualize the clusters generated by each clustering method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Risk Profiling">
  <data key="d0">Driver Risk Profiling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Identification of distinct driver profiles from clusters to guide targeted preventive measures against MSDs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K-means is the most suitable clustering method for the driver dataset">
  <data key="d0">K-means is the most suitable clustering method for the driver dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "These results suggest that K-means is the most suitable method for this dataset, providing a balance between simplicity, interpretability, and clustering performance."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Low-Risk Driver Cluster">
  <data key="d0">Low-Risk Driver Cluster</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the three K-means driver clusters, labeled in the text as drivers at low risk of developing MSDs. Its drivers have about three years of experience and a lower workload than the other groups. Their heavy lifting frequency is slightly higher, their weekly physical activity is the lowest, and their working conditions score is about 4, the weakest of the three groups. The text notes that these factors give the impression of less favourable working conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Validation Metrics">
  <data key="d0">Internal Validation Metrics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cluster quality measures, namely the Silhouette Score and the Calinski-Harabasz Index, used in Table 8 to compare the clustering algorithms without ground-truth labels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DBSCAN failed to produce meaningful clusters on the driver dataset">
  <data key="d0">DBSCAN failed to produce meaningful clusters on the driver dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "DBSCAN, while known for its robustness to noise and ability to detect clusters of arbitrary shape, did not yield usable results under the tested parameter settings, as it failed to produce a sufficient number of meaningful clusters without classifying a majority of the points as noise."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K-means yields the most compact and well-defined clusters">
  <data key="d0">K-means yields the most compact and well-defined clusters</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Among the tested methods, K-means yielded the highest scores (Silhouette Score = 0.218, Calinski-Harabasz Index = 538.93), indicating relatively well-defined and compact clusters."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assessing Musculoskeletal Disorder Susceptibility in Professional…">
  <data key="d0">Assessing Musculoskeletal Disorder Susceptibility in Professional…</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">The article being read, by I. Benallou et al., published in Informatica 49 (2025) 801–812. It uses K-means clustering to segment professional drivers into low, medium and high risk groups for musculoskeletal disorders based on age, BMI, weekly work hours, experience and frequency of heavy lifting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Segmentation Model">
  <data key="d0">Driver Segmentation Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Model segmenting drivers by MSD risk profile using age, experience, BMI, weekly work hours and frequency of heavy lifting; adaptable to other datasets for transportation companies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reinforcement Learning">
  <data key="d0">Reinforcement Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed as future research for real-time recommendations of activity or posture adjustments based on data from movement tracking devices and posture sensors.&lt;SEP&gt;Learning paradigm in which the deep Q network (DQN) model is applied for churn prediction by Panjasuchat and Limpiyakorn.&lt;SEP&gt;Learning paradigm applied to customer churn prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimization model suggested as future work for executing actions on physical systems directly from the Digital Twin.&lt;SEP&gt;Learning paradigm proposed for integration with GANs to enable dynamic market simulations with adaptive agents.&lt;SEP&gt;Learning paradigm in which agents adapt their strategies over time. It is proposed for integration with GANs to enable dynamic market simulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_171Edited_FV.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;GANsinsyntheticfinancialdata_donalsonwilson_versionpublie.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425841</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Only 21% of professional drivers have a low risk of developing MSDs">
  <data key="d0">Only 21% of professional drivers have a low risk of developing MSDs</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "These results estimate that only 21% of professional drivers have a low risk of developing MSDs, which confirms the relatively high prevalence of these disorders in this category of professionals."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference [24]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [24]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [24] for MSDs being a major global health problem that causes injuries and economic losses due to absenteeism and medical expenses.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work describing information entropy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [24]. A source on information entropy, the entropy weight method, and the combination of AHP/FAHP with entropy, including the combined weight formula.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work (bibliography entry not visible) on stochastic gradient descent minimizing a cost function.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work on microservices enabling shorter time-to-market and introducing complexity for new developers.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [24] in support of the importance of monitoring in distributed architectures.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [24] regarding VANET applications such as traffic control, platooning, vehicle tracking, comfort applications and notification services.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501546</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSDs are a major global health problem causing economic losses">
  <data key="d0">MSDs are a major global health problem causing economic losses</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Musculoskeletal disorders (MSDs) are a major global health problem. They impact the quality of life and cause injuries among staff, leading to economic losses due to absenteeism and medical expenses [24]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference [25]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [25]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [25] for preventive measures such as periodic MSD screening and training programs that reduce MSDs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work (bibliography entry not visible) on regression and dependent variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited study on the impact of consensus mechanisms on performance, limited by small-scale simulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work on synchronous RPC and asynchronous message-broker communication and on RabbitMQ's suitability for transactional, low-latency systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [25] regarding adverse weather causing signal attenuation in wireless communications.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [25]. Earlier work exploring anti-quantum authentication models and lattice-based transaction authentication mechanisms for blockchain systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Screening and training programs effectively reduce MSDs">
  <data key="d0">Screening and training programs effectively reduce MSDs</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "These measures may include periodic MSD screening programs and training programs on behaviors and exercises that effectively reduce MSDs [25]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moroccan Law No. 09-08">
  <data key="d0">Moroccan Law No. 09-08</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Moroccan law on the protection of personal data; the study involved no sensitive data as defined by this law.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weekly Work Hours">
  <data key="d0">Weekly Work Hours</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Number of hours worked per week by drivers, one of the key variables integrated into the driver segmentation model for MSD risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver MSD Risk Clusters">
  <data key="d0">Driver MSD Risk Clusters</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Three clusters produced by K-means: Cluster 0 groups drivers at average (medium) risk of MSDs, Cluster 1 groups drivers at high risk, and Cluster 2 groups drivers at low risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K-means gave the most interpretable and well-separated clusters">
  <data key="d0">K-means gave the most interpretable and well-separated clusters</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Although alternative clustering techniques were explored, K-means proved to offer the most interpretable and well-separated clusters for the dataset, supporting its use in the final driver segmentation analysis."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Real-Time Posture Recommendation System">
  <data key="d0">Real-Time Posture Recommendation System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Envisioned future system that would recommend adjustments to activity or posture based on real-time data from movement tracking devices and posture sensors, reducing risky behaviours before problems arise.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSD Prevention Measures">
  <data key="d0">MSD Prevention Measures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Preventive measures to preserve drivers' musculoskeletal systems, including periodic MSD screening programs, training programs on behaviors and exercises, ergonomic modifications and workload reductions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Verbal Informed Consent">
  <data key="d0">Verbal Informed Consent</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Ethical procedure in which participants were verbally informed of the study's objectives, data collected and their right to decline or withdraw, and gave verbal consent prior to participation; responses were fully anonymized.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-c6cf0e7751b1667f02411ba9fa2e2995-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">7561-ArticleText-31951-37131-10-20251228.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791421575</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
