<node id="Support Vector Machines">
  <data key="d0">Support Vector Machines</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Support Vector Machines (SVM) are a supervised machine learning technique developed by Cortes and Vapnik in 1995. They are particularly effective for binary classification, which they perform by finding the optimal hyperplane separating two classes. Because of this, SVMs are widely applied to two-outcome prediction problems.

SVMs have been used in several domains. They have been applied to churn prediction and to predicting ticket recurrence, both binary classification tasks. In financial analysis, Huang et al. (2004) compared SVMs with neural networks for credit rating analysis. In fraud detection, SVM performance improved when the model was trained with data generated by a conditional Generative Adversarial Network (cGAN). SVMs have also been suggested as a candidate model for future work using Boruta feature selection.

The name "Support Vector Machines" also refers to a bibliographic reference, cited as [26]. This is a chapter by A. Shmilovici in the *Data Mining and Knowledge Discovery Handbook*, published by Springer-Verlag, New York, spanning pages 257–276. The chapter describes support vector machines. One version of this bibliography entry appears truncated, but the other provides the complete citation details, including the page range.&lt;SEP&gt;Listed by study [9] as a method used for road traffic accident forecasting, cited with [16].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4e26e4b19a21a1bf5f3daec0bcb0830c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ef486f441df72175f968e398ffdd23d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;acs_AIapplicationinmarketanalysis.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;ICRAMCS2025F.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;GANsinsyntheticfinancialdata_donalsonwilson_versionpublie.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artificial Neural Networks">
  <data key="d0">Artificial Neural Networks</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Supervised learning technique (ANN) used for churn prediction. Neural networks were also used in Baghla and Gupta [5].&lt;SEP&gt;Interconnected layers of neurons acting as processing units similar to biological neurons, using activation functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine learning models evaluated for tunneling-induced settlement prediction in reference 43.&lt;SEP&gt;Computer systems inspired by biological neural networks. ANNs are used to develop clustering algorithms for selecting cluster heads.&lt;SEP&gt;ANNs, computing systems inspired by biological neural networks. They are used for cluster head selection and black hole node detection in MANETs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501031</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adam">
  <data key="d0">Adam</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique referred to as a deep learning technique and used by Baghla and Gupta [5] for churn prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep learning technique compared alongside neural network, SVM, NB and RF in a churn prediction study where RF achieved the highest accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimizer used for the classification models (lr=1×10⁻⁴, decayed on plateau) and for DeepLabV3+ (lr=8×10⁻⁵).&lt;SEP&gt;Optimizer used in Tune1, Tune2 and Tune3 of the LSTM hyperparameter tuning.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimizer used across the LSTM tuning iterations.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimizer considered as an option during hyperparameter tuning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1920bb99aa33c3c871f9dc257956e3ca-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_171Edited_FV.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid_Approach_Integrating_Deep_Learning-Autoencoder_With_Statistical_Process_Control_Chart_for_Anomaly_Detection_Case_Study_in_Injection_Molding_Process1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426328</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LIME">
  <data key="d0">LIME</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">LIME (Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic Explanations) is a post-hoc, model-agnostic explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) method that provides localized explanations for specific predictions. It treats machine learning models as black boxes and generates local explanations of how individual features contribute to particular predictions. This lets it assess the influence of individual features on a model's output for single data points.

LIME has been widely applied in customer churn prediction. In this context, it has been used to explain the two most accurate churn models and to understand the reasons behind customer departure. One account describes it as applied to both the artificial neural network (ANN) and random forest (RF) models. Another states that it was applied only to the ANN. This difference may reflect separate studies or analyses.

When applied to the ANN, LIME identified the key features that drive the churn versus non-churn classification of individual data points:
- **Features that raised the likelihood of a churn classification:** complaint, tenure, marital status, and preferred order category.
- **Features that lowered it:** city tier, gender, coupon used, and warehouse-to-home distance.

Beyond churn analysis, LIME has also been used as a local explanation technique alongside SHAP to explain stock market trend predictions. This use shows its versatility across predictive domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425074</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Customer churn is the transition of a customer between service providers">
  <data key="d0">Customer churn is the transition of a customer between service providers</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">However, businesses, irrespective of their industry, cannot completely avoid the phenomenon of customer churn, which refers to the transition of a customer from one service provider to another [12].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Churn prediction evaluates the proportion of customers terminating service use">
  <data key="d0">Churn prediction evaluates the proportion of customers terminating service use</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">This is where the significance of predicting customer churn becomes apparent, aiming to evaluate the proportion of customers terminating or potentially terminating their use or subscription to a product or service provided by an organization or company [13].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AI replicates human intelligence in computer systems">
  <data key="d0">AI replicates human intelligence in computer systems</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Artificial intelligence (AI) is the development of computer systems that replicate human intelligence, encompassing natural language processing, visual perception, speech recognition, and decision-making [14].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AI techniques enhance e-commerce operations and understanding of purchasing behavior">
  <data key="d0">AI techniques enhance e-commerce operations and understanding of purchasing behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In e-commerce, AI techniques enhance network marketing, electronic payments, and logistics management, while also allowing platforms to understand the factors influencing the purchasing behaviors of current and potential clients, enabling the development of innovative strategies to address the evolving needs and preferences of consumers [15].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XAI fosters trustworthiness, fairness and interpretability of AI predictions">
  <data key="d0">XAI fosters trustworthiness, fairness and interpretability of AI predictions</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">XAI aims to provide clarity on the processes behind these predictions, thereby fostering trustworthiness, ensuring causal relationships, enhancing transferability, building confidence, promoting fairness, facilitating accessibility, and encouraging interactivity [16].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Customer relationship management relies on proactive understanding and fitting strategies">
  <data key="d0">Customer relationship management relies on proactive understanding and fitting strategies</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">...the proactive understanding and management of customer relations [10], achieved through the implementation of customer relationship strategies that fit their business [11].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Random forest achieves highest accuracy of 99.35% for e-commerce churn prediction">
  <data key="d0">Random forest achieves highest accuracy of 99.35% for e-commerce churn prediction</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"The results reveal that the RF classifier achieves the highest prediction accuracy at 99.35%, surpassing other methods in the study." This finding is attributed to Baghla and Gupta [5].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Model Interpretability">
  <data key="d0">Model Interpretability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The understandability of a model's predictions. The study aims to bridge the gap between machine learning accuracy and model interpretability in churn prediction.&lt;SEP&gt;The topic of explaining and interpreting predictions of machine learning models, addressed by SHAP- and LIME-related references.&lt;SEP&gt;Property improved when the Boruta algorithm simplifies models by retaining only relevant features, also making them more computationally efficient.&lt;SEP&gt;Property of models that is enhanced when feature selection simplifies them by retaining relevant features.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423726</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Customer Retention">
  <data key="d0">Customer Retention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strategies and targeted efforts by e-commerce platforms to retain customers and reduce churn. The aim is to improve customer satisfaction and revenue stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
