<node id="Ensemble of trees improves accuracy and reliability">
  <data key="d0">Ensemble of trees improves accuracy and reliability</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">...accuracy [14]. This approach combines the strengths of different trees to enhance model reliability, thereby contributing to improved predictive performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Naive Bayes relies on a priori and conditional probabilities">
  <data key="d0">Naive Bayes relies on a priori and conditional probabilities</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">NB, introduced by Reverend Thomas Bayes in 1,760, relies on two types of probabilities: i) the a priori probability of each class and ii) the conditional probability of each class label given the value x of the variable [23].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logistic regression is an alternative to least squares for multivariate models">
  <data key="d0">Logistic regression is an alternative to least squares for multivariate models</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">LR serves as an alternative to the least squares method, particularly when dealing with multivariate models that involve a distinction between dependent and independent variables [35].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="K-NN assumes the most similar data points belong to the same class">
  <data key="d0">K-NN assumes the most similar data points belong to the same class</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">K-NN, introduced by Fix and Hodges in 1951, is based on the fundamental principle that the most similar data points belong to the same class [35].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logistic regression assigns classes using a 0.5 probability threshold">
  <data key="d0">Logistic regression assigns classes using a 0.5 probability threshold</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The prediction rule states that if the predicted probability is above 0.5, the output is assigned to class 0; otherwise, it is assigned to class 1 [40].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SVM seeks the optimal hyperplane for class separation">
  <data key="d0">SVM seeks the optimal hyperplane for class separation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Its objective is to define the optimal hyperplane for a perfect separation of classes [41].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ANN consists of interconnected layers of neuron-like units">
  <data key="d0">ANN consists of interconnected layers of neuron-like units</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">ANN consists of interconnected layers of neurons, functioning as basic processing units similar to biological neurons [44].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interpretable machine learning identifies input-output relationships and influential features">
  <data key="d0">Interpretable machine learning identifies input-output relationships and influential features</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">It seeks to address questions about the relationships between input and output, as well as identify the most influential features driving predictions [46].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SHAP uses additive feature attribution to make models interpretable">
  <data key="d0">SHAP uses additive feature attribution to make models interpretable</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Using an additive feature attribution method, SHAP seeks to make the model interpretable [47].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LIME is a post-hoc model-agnostic local explanation method">
  <data key="d0">LIME is a post-hoc model-agnostic local explanation method</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">LIME falls into the category of Post-Hoc and Model-Agnostic methods, offering localized explanations for specific predictions [48].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
