<node id="Post-Hoc Model-Agnostic Explainability">
  <data key="d0">Post-Hoc Model-Agnostic Explainability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Category of explanation methods applied after training and independent of model type, to which LIME belongs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Binary Classification">
  <data key="d0">Binary Classification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Classification task with class 0 and class 1 on which the seven models were evaluated.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification task with two classes, illustrated by the binary classification confusion matrix in Fig. 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification task with two classes. All three datasets have 2-class targets, and the confusion matrix is shown for binary classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification task with two outcomes. Ticket recurrence prediction is framed as binary classification, for which SVM is described as a suitable algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification into two classes, performed by the CNN with a sigmoid output neuron.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ef486f441df72175f968e398ffdd23d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_174.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;ICRAMCS2025F.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467113</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ANN achieves the highest accuracy among the seven models">
  <data key="d0">ANN achieves the highest accuracy among the seven models</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The ANN model achieved a higher accuracy at 92%, with precision, recall, and F1 score metrics of 93%, 98%, and 95% for class 0, and 87%, 65%, and 74% for class 1."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Class Imbalance">
  <data key="d0">Class Imbalance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class 0 has 469 samples and class 1 has 100 samples in the evaluation set (from the confusion matrix totals). All models show much lower class 1 recall and F1 than class 0.&lt;SEP&gt;Significant imbalance between road pixels and background pixels in aerial images, which hinders segmentation in background-dominated situations.&lt;SEP&gt;Problem of imbalanced datasets, handled by combining focal Tversky loss and weighted cross-entropy loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenomenon in road segmentation where road pixels may be significantly fewer than non-road pixels. Dice Loss is described as particularly effective for such imbalanced datasets.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-equitable distribution of records among machines; Machine 200 t (Class 2) is the majority class with 20 records while Machines 4, 5 and 1 are minority classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Model Explainability">
  <data key="d0">Model Explainability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The goal of explaining model predictions, which the paper pursues with SHAP techniques and LIME.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Global Feature Importance">
  <data key="d0">Global Feature Importance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ranking of features by the absolute sum of their effect magnitudes on the model output, shown for the ANN and RF models in Figure 4.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complain Feature">
  <data key="d0">Complain Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The 'Complain' feature, which emerges as the most influential feature in both the ANN and RF models. This indicates that the presence or absence of complaints strongly affects churn predictions.&lt;SEP&gt;Customer attribute indicating complaints; showed non-linear relationship with churn in SHAP and a LIME probability value of 0.20 contributing strongly to churn prediction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precision-Recall Balance">
  <data key="d0">Precision-Recall Balance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The goal of balancing precision and recall. It motivated the choice of ANN and RF as the high-performing models to explain with SHAP and LIME.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Explainable Machine Learning">
  <data key="d0">Explainable Machine Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interpretability of machine learning models, addressed via SHAP and LIME to understand model decision-making.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local Feature Importance">
  <data key="d0">Local Feature Importance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Instance-level feature contributions generated by LIME, expressed as probability values indicating each feature's impact on a specific prediction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tenure Feature">
  <data key="d0">Tenure Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute with significant positive impact on churn classification per LIME and a probability value of 0.15 for non-churn prediction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complain feature has a non-linear relationship with churn">
  <data key="d0">Complain feature has a non-linear relationship with churn</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Notably, the 'Complain' feature exhibits both positive and negative SHAP values in both models, indicating a non-linear relationship with the target variable."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marital Status Feature">
  <data key="d0">Marital Status Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute that LIME found to have a significant positive impact on classifying a data point as churn. For the non-churn prediction it has a probability value of 0.10.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preferred Order Category Feature">
  <data key="d0">Preferred Order Category Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute (PreferredOrderCat) that LIME found to have a significant positive impact on churn classification. It has a probability value of 0.08 for the non-churn prediction, and in the SHAP analysis PreferredOrderCat=3 shows a positive influence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="City Tier Feature">
  <data key="d0">City Tier Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute that LIME found to have a negative effect on classifying the data point as churn.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gender Feature">
  <data key="d0">Gender Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute that LIME found to have a negative effect on classifying the data point as churn.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coupon Used Feature">
  <data key="d0">Coupon Used Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute that LIME found to have a negative effect on classifying the data point as churn.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Warehouse To Home Feature">
  <data key="d0">Warehouse To Home Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute (distance from warehouse to home) that LIME found to have a negative effect on classifying the data point as churn.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Order Count Feature">
  <data key="d0">Order Count Feature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer attribute (OrderCount). In the SHAP analysis, customers who have placed multiple orders (OrderCount=1) show a positive influence toward engagement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complain, tenure, marital status and preferred order category drive churn classification">
  <data key="d0">Complain, tenure, marital status and preferred order category drive churn classification</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "As illustrated in Figure 6(a), according to LIME's analysis, the features indicating complain, tenure, marital status and preferred order category have a significant positive impact on the classification of this data point as a churn customer."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">25293-55764-1-PB.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422010</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ANN model shows superior performance">
  <data key="d0">ANN model shows superior performance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "The LIME method was applied to the ANN model due to its superior performance, aiming to identify key features influencing the classification of data points into churn or non-churn categories."</data>
