<node id="Logistic Regression">
  <data key="d0">Logistic Regression</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Logistic Regression (LR) is a supervised statistical classification and regression analysis method. It is a probabilistic model that estimates the probability that an instance belongs to a particular class, and it is used to predict a dependent variable from independent variables. Methodologically, Logistic Regression serves as an alternative to least squares for multivariate modeling. It accepts both real-valued and categorical input variables and produces a probability for each observation. A 0.5 probability threshold is typically used to assign each observation to a class. The method is explained in the reference "Understanding Logistic Regression Analysis."

In customer churn prediction, including in the telecommunications sector, Logistic Regression is widely used and often serves as a baseline classifier. It was one of seven classification models compared in a churn prediction study, and Reference [40] also applied it to telecom churn prediction. In that evaluation, the model reached an overall accuracy of about 86% (0.8559). Its confusion matrix contained 461 true negatives, 8 false positives, 74 false negatives and 26 true positives. For the non-churn class (Class 0), it achieved 86% precision, 98% recall and a 92% F1-score. For the churn class (Class 1), it achieved 76% precision, 26% recall and a 39% F1-score. These results show a strong bias toward the majority non-churn class: the model identified almost all non-churning customers but only about a quarter of actual churners.

In a separate manufacturing study, Logistic Regression was one of six classification algorithms applied to machine selection prediction, specifically predicting the appropriate clamping force machine. There it performed poorly, with 53% accuracy, 42% precision, 50% recall and a 45% F1-score. Its performance was lower than that of Random Forest (RF), Decision Tree (DT) and Support Vector Machine (SVM). In the learning curve analysis, its trend resembled that of Naive Bayes (NB), indicating underfitting and limited generalization, which explains its weak results in this application.&lt;SEP&gt;Machine learning model whose fraud detection performance improved with cGAN-generated data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-005</data>
