<node id="Naive Bayes">
  <data key="d0">Naive Bayes</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Naive Bayes (NB) is a probabilistic, supervised classification method based on Bayes' theorem. It relies on a priori class probabilities and conditional probabilities to predict the likelihood that an object belongs to a given class, and it is considered efficient for large datasets. One source attributes the method to the Reverend Thomas Bayes and dates it to 1760. Bayes' essay containing the theorem was actually published posthumously in 1763, so the 1760 date should be read as approximate. Naive Bayes is used across many domains, often as a baseline or comparison model.

In customer churn prediction, Naive Bayes is widely applied and is cited for this purpose in references [38] and [39]. It forms the basis of the adaptive churn prediction approach proposed by Amin et al., and several studies use it as a comparison model. In one study comparing seven supervised models for churn prediction, Naive Bayes performed worst, with the lowest accuracy of about 75% (0.7504). Its confusion matrix contained 373 true negatives, 96 false positives, 46 false negatives and 54 true positives. Its class-level results were uneven:
- For non-churners (class 0), it achieved 89% precision, 80% recall and an 84% F1-score.
- For churners (class 1), it achieved only 36% precision, 54% recall and a 43% F1-score.

This shows that it identified non-churning customers reasonably well but struggled to predict churners accurately.

Naive Bayes was also one of the machine learning models Patre et al. compared for predicting product sales in e-commerce.

In industrial manufacturing, Naive Bayes was used for machine selection prediction as one of six classification algorithms applied to predict the clamping force machine. In this context it achieved 73% accuracy, 67% precision, 63% recall and a 64% F1-score. It misclassified instances in classes 2, 3 and 4. Learning curve analysis showed underfitting, with training accuracy around 70% and validation accuracy plateauing at 60%. This underperformance was attributed to the small dataset. Its overall performance was lower than that of Random Forest (RF), Decision Tree (DT) and Support Vector Machine (SVM).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3b6de35b4fc6654223999680ef1082ec-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
