<node id="Customer Churn Prediction">
  <data key="d0">Customer Churn Prediction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer Churn Prediction is the task of identifying which customers will stop using, cancel, or terminate their subscription to a business's product or service. It also involves estimating the proportion of customers who have ended, or may end, their relationship with the business. It is framed as a binary classification problem in which customers are labeled as churners (class 1) or non-churners (class 0). The aim is to forecast customer attrition before it happens.

Customer Churn Prediction is the central problem and target task of the study in question, and it is also the main subject of many of the works that study cites. Research on the problem spans several industries, most notably e-commerce, telecommunications, and banking, with some cited work focusing specifically on credit card settings. Its breadth across these sectors reflects how important customer retention is in subscription- and service-based businesses.&lt;SEP&gt;Task of predicting customer churn in e-commerce. It is the subject of the cited work by Boukrouh and Azmani.&lt;SEP&gt;Prediction of customer churn in the telecommunications industry.</data>
