<node id="Reference [8]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [8]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [8] is a citation label that appears across several distinct scholarly documents. Because each document numbers its bibliography independently, the label refers to different cited works covering unrelated topics, which are summarized separately below.

In the context of machine learning and data analysis, Reference [8] is cited for adaptive feature selection methods. In a decision-making or multi-criteria analysis context, Reference [8] is cited for the Criteria Importance Through Inter-criteria Correlation (CRITIC) method, an objective technique for weighting criteria. In a manufacturing and mechanical engineering context, Reference [8] supports the description of the clamping unit of an injection molding machine.

In research on system performance and configuration, Reference [8] is cited as noting a lack of research on the combined effects of parameters in large-scale environments. In a related context, it is cited as indicating that systematic performance tuning of Fabric (likely Hyperledger Fabric) remains less explored. These two descriptions may belong to the same cited work, since both identify gaps in parameter-tuning research for large-scale systems.

In the field of uncertainty modeling and artificial intelligence, Reference [8] is cited for its work integrating Bayesian networks with fuzzy logic to manage uncertainty.

In network security research, Reference [8] is cited for providing classifications of attacks in Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs). In a security context that may or may not be the same, Reference [8] describes an approach combining authentication and intrusion detection through four levels of security. That approach is noted as being limited by constraints associated with fingerprint recognition.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work on fuzzy logic enabling human-like reasoning in AI.</data>
