<node id="PubMed">
  <data key="d0">PubMed</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">PubMed is a biomedical bibliographic and literature database that is frequently used to collect bibliographic data. It is commonly cited alongside other major databases, particularly Web of Science (WoS) and Scopus, and is regarded as a valuable data source for bibliometric research. AlRyalat et al. (2019) compared PubMed with Scopus and Web of Science to assess their respective strengths for bibliometric analysis.

In several studies, PubMed served as a core data source and was merged with WoS and Scopus to build a more comprehensive dataset. In one documented workflow, PubMed was queried using the same search strategy applied to the other databases. The PubMed identifiers (PMIDs) retrieved from that search were then extracted and used to obtain equivalent plain-text records from WoS, which harmonized the data formats across sources and allowed the records to be integrated.

The literature also treats PubMed in other ways. In some contexts it is mentioned only as an alternative data source [22]. In at least one review, it was named as an additional academic database but was not selected for use. These differences most likely reflect varying methodological choices across separate studies rather than conflicting information about the database. Together, they show that PubMed is a widely recognized resource whose inclusion depends on the scope and design of each research project.</data>
