<node id="Lotka's Law">
  <data key="d0">Lotka's Law</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical principle proposed by Alfred James Lotka in 1926. It states that N(x)=N(1)/x^n, with n typically around 2, so a small proportion of authors produce most publications.&lt;SEP&gt;Bibliometric law of author productivity; the distribution showed only 0.6% of authors authored 5 articles while 83.5% had one publication.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical principle describing the distribution of scientific productivity among authors: a small percentage of authors account for a large proportion of published works, while a larger percentage contribute fewer articles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4e26e4b19a21a1bf5f3daec0bcb0830c-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">A_bibliometric_analysis_of_feature_selection_techn.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;acs_25_66701.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;acs_AIapplicationinmarketanalysis.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423294</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lotka 1926">
  <data key="d0">Lotka 1926</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [29]. The 1926 work by Alfred James Lotka that proposed Lotka's law on the distribution of scientific productivity among authors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">A_bibliometric_analysis_of_feature_selection_techn.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422137</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
