<node id="Generative Adversarial Networks">
  <data key="d0">Generative Adversarial Networks</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) are a deep learning architecture and generative modeling framework first introduced by Goodfellow, Pouget-Abadie, Mirza, Xu, Warde-Farley, Ozair, Courville and Bengio in 2014 (arXiv abs/1406.2661). The work was later published in Communications of the ACM in 2020 (63(11):139-44). GANs are built on an adversarial framework in which two networks are trained against each other. A generator learns the distribution of real data, while a discriminator estimates whether a given sample is real or generated. Through this process, GANs can produce highly realistic synthetic data that closely replicates the statistical distributions of real-world datasets.

GANs have been widely presented as a promising solution for machine learning and artificial intelligence applications constrained by limited data availability and access. By generating synthetic data that mirrors real datasets, they help address data scarcity and access restrictions. Numerous variants have been developed for different data types, including images, audio and tabular data. Many works cite or build on the framework, including Goodfellow's tutorial, Wasserstein GAN, StyleGAN, PATE-GAN, TabFairGAN, COSCI-GAN and Sig-Wasserstein GANs.

In finance, GAN variants have been extensively reviewed for synthetic financial data generation and market modeling, an application area that gained traction around 2019. In this domain they have been applied to modeling financial time series, asset prices and volatility. They have also been used to generate synthetic telecom data. Beyond these fields, GANs are cited among modern deep learning strategies for image analysis and remote sensing, and they are applied in oncology research.

Despite their strengths, GANs have notable limitations. They require substantial amounts of real data for training, and they are prone to training difficulties such as convergence issues and mode collapse.&lt;SEP&gt;Deep generative models that have been adapted to tabular data. Directly applying image-style GANs to tabular data causes mode collapse and poor handling of rare categories.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-7b9912ddab6105f96b8cd8cb51d46ee9-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4e26e4b19a21a1bf5f3daec0bcb0830c-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-001</data>
