  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Analytic Hierarchy Process">
  <data key="d0">Analytic Hierarchy Process</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) is a multi-criteria decision-making method developed by T. L. Saaty. It works by hierarchical priority scaling, structuring a complex decision problem into a hierarchy of criteria and determining their relative importance. AHP is classified as a subjective criteria-weighting approach because it relies on expert judgments to establish the weights assigned to each criterion.

AHP is widely applied to supplier selection, and several supplier selection studies in the reference literature use it. Other cited works cover its consistency ratio, a measure used to check the coherence of expert pairwise comparisons, as well as its applications in construction, project management and sorting problems.

In the context of machine selection, the initial phase of AHP structured the decision problem into eleven criteria, organized as five main criteria and eleven sub-criteria that consider both cost and performance. AHP was then used to assess and assign weights to these criteria according to their relative importance. The resulting weights served as inputs to the TOPSIS method, which carried out the subsequent ranking of alternatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426442</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy AHP">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy AHP</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">FAHP is a fuzzy extension of AHP for determining criteria weights.&lt;SEP&gt;FAHP, a subjective, fuzzy extension of AHP that relies on expert judgments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy extension of the Analytic Hierarchy Process for decision-making with subjective judgements.&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy variants of AHP for decision making with subjective judgements, reviewed in [35].&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy extension of the Analytic Hierarchy Process used for supplier selection in references [20] and [21].&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy variant of AHP listed among MCDM methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Fuzzy analytic hierarchy process (FAHP), a fuzzy extension of AHP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entropy">
  <data key="d0">Entropy</data>
  <data key="d1">venue</data>
  <data key="d2">Entropy is a concept introduced by R. Clausius in 1865 to quantify the degree of disorder in thermodynamic systems. It was later adapted to information theory, where it measures uncertainty and information content. This adaptation is the basis for its use in data analysis and decision-making.

Building on this information-theoretic foundation, Entropy also refers to the entropy weighting method, an objective weighting technique based on empirical data. It derives weights from the variability within the data rather than from subjective judgment. In multi-criteria analysis, the method is used to calculate the weights of criteria and sub-criteria.

Separately, Entropy is the name of a scientific journal. It published reference [24] in 2020 and reference [9] in 2023.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467665</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
