<node id="Reference [31]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [31]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [31] is a citation marker that appears in several distinct source documents. Each document uses it for a different work, so the descriptions most likely refer to separate cited works that share only the in-text label. Little bibliographic detail is available for any of them, and no authors, years, or titles are given. Each distinct usage is summarized below.

In one context, Reference [31] is cited for Saaty's scale, which is used to construct pairwise comparison matrices. This is typical of multi-criteria decision-making approaches such as the Analytic Hierarchy Process. In a related decision-oriented context, Reference [31] is described as a study that relied on expert insights. This usage may or may not refer to the same work.

In the field of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI), Reference [31] is cited for LIME being one of the field's prominent techniques. Only the in-text marker is visible for this citation. The entity replaces an earlier label, "Ribeiro 2016 LIME Reference [31]," which attributed an author and year not present in the source text. That attribution is therefore not confirmed.

Reference [31] also appears in work on fuzzy logic, where it is cited for describing the Mamdani and Sugeno fuzzy inference methods and the defuzzification process. In a machine learning context, it is cited for describing artificial neural networks (ANNs) as computing systems inspired by biological neural networks.

In a software and systems context, Reference [31] is cited for describing Docker as a containerization technology. Docker packages application components together with their dependencies into isolated environments.

Finally, Reference [31] is cited in work on network security and mobile ad hoc networks. One usage supports the statement that selfish node behavior degrades network performance in terms of battery power, available bandwidth, and computational ability. Another usage concerns flooding denial-of-service (DoS) attacks carried out with false route request (RREQ) packets. These two networking citations may come from the same source document and could refer to a single work on ad hoc network misbehavior and attacks, but this cannot be confirmed from the available information.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [31]; Hyperledger Fabric performance study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
