  <data key="d5">1791426446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yoon 1987">
  <data key="d0">Yoon 1987</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [9]. Work by Yoon in 1987 that further elaborated the TOPSIS method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Euclidean Distance">
  <data key="d0">Euclidean Distance</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A distance measure used in TOPSIS for normalization and for the distances to the ideal solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Metric used to compute distances between each method's scores and scenario scores, averaged to choose between MCDM methods producing similar rankings.&lt;SEP&gt;Distance measure used to normalize the TOPSIS decision matrix and to compute each alternative's distances (S+ and S-) from the ideal solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Distance measure that TOPSIS computes between each alternative and both the ideal and anti-ideal solutions in order to determine relative closeness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entropy was adapted to measure uncertainty in information sources">
  <data key="d0">Entropy was adapted to measure uncertainty in information sources</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "This concept was later adapted by Shannon in 1948 [7], to measure uncertainty in information sources, becoming known as information entropy [24]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entropy weight method weights criteria by information entropy">
  <data key="d0">Entropy weight method weights criteria by information entropy</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "The entropy weight method assesses a criterion's significance and weight by calculating its information entropy, which is based on the variation of the initial data matrix, through several steps [24], [42]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combining AHP/FAHP with entropy enhances decision analysis">
  <data key="d0">Combining AHP/FAHP with entropy enhances decision analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "Combining AHP, FAHP, and entropy into AHP-Entropy and FAHP-Entropy enhances decision analysis by blending subjective opinions with objective data, reducing variations and analyzing the dependability of the results [24]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TOPSIS was introduced by Hwang and Yoon and elaborated by Yoon">
  <data key="d0">TOPSIS was introduced by Hwang and Yoon and elaborated by Yoon</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "The TOPSIS approach was initially introduced by Hwang and Yoon in 1981 [10], and later elaborated by Yoon in 1987 [9], is a multi-criteria decision-making method designed to facilitate the ranking and selection of the best alternative among several options."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TOPSIS follows five steps">
  <data key="d0">TOPSIS follows five steps</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "This method follows five steps [43], [44], [45]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hwang">
  <data key="d0">Hwang</data>
  <data key="d1">author</data>
  <data key="d2">Co-author, with Yoon, of the 1981 work cited as [10], which first introduced the TOPSIS approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author, with Yoon, of the 1981 work from which TOPSIS originates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yoon">
  <data key="d0">Yoon</data>
  <data key="d1">author</data>
  <data key="d2">Co-author of the 1981 work that introduced TOPSIS [10]; also sole author of the 1987 work that elaborated TOPSIS [9].&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author, with Hwang, of the 1981 work from which TOPSIS originates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Positive Ideal Solution">
  <data key="d0">Positive Ideal Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The best value of each criterion in TOPSIS. It is the column maximum for favorable criteria and the column minimum for unfavorable criteria (equation 23).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Negative Ideal Solution">
  <data key="d0">Negative Ideal Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The worst value of each criterion in TOPSIS. It is the column minimum for favorable criteria and the column maximum for unfavorable criteria (equation 24).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proximity Coefficient">
  <data key="d0">Proximity Coefficient</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The TOPSIS score S* for each alternative, computed from its distances to the positive and negative ideal solutions (equation 27). It determines the alternative's rank.&lt;SEP&gt;TOPSIS measure computed from the distances to the ideal solutions and used to rank the four supplier alternatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decision Matrix Normalization">
  <data key="d0">Decision Matrix Normalization</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A standardization step that removes unit differences across indicators in the decision matrix. It is the first step of both the entropy weight method (equation 5) and TOPSIS (Euclidean normalization, equation 21).&lt;SEP&gt;Normalization of the decision matrix in Table 5 using Eq. (8), which is the first step of the entropy method; the results are shown in Table 17.&lt;SEP&gt;TOPSIS step that normalizes the decision matrix of alternative evaluations. Normalization is needed because the evaluations use diverse scales, and it ensures the same level of comparison.&lt;SEP&gt;Step converting decision matrix elements expressed in different dimensions to a comparable level using r_ij = x_ij / sqrt(sum x_ij^2) (Equation 3).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combined Weight">
  <data key="d0">Combined Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A weight that merges subjective weights (AHP/FAHP) and objective weights (entropy) across n criteria using equation 20.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TOPSIS ranks alternatives by combining positive criteria while minimizing negative criteria">
  <data key="d0">TOPSIS ranks alternatives by combining positive criteria while minimizing negative criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "It is based on finding the alternative that optimally combines positive criteria while minimizing negative criteria. This method follows five steps [43], [44], [45]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boukrouh Reference [46]">
  <data key="d0">Boukrouh Reference [46]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as [46] for the procedure of obtaining external weights of sub-criteria by multiplying internal weights by their corresponding criterion weight.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External weights obtained by multiplying internal weights by criterion weight">
  <data key="d0">External weights obtained by multiplying internal weights by criterion weight</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "The internal weights of the sub-criteria were also calculated using the same formula, while the external weights were obtained by multiplying the internal weights by their corresponding criterion [46]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MCDM">
  <data key="d0">MCDM</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Multi-criteria decision-making; five MCDM methods are used to compute criteria and sub-criteria weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-criteria decision-making techniques whose supplier rankings differ significantly across methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-criteria decision-making methods applied across various aspects of injection molding processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426446</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triangular Fuzzy Numbers">
  <data key="d0">Triangular Fuzzy Numbers</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">TFNs applied to construct fuzzy pairwise comparison matrices in the FAHP method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Geometric Mean Method">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Geometric Mean Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Aggregation method used in FAHP to compute fuzzy geometric means before determining fuzzy weights.&lt;SEP&gt;Aggregation method that computes the geometric mean of the lower, middle and upper values of fuzzy comparisons for each criterion; for example, criterion C2 has a fuzzy geometric mean of (1.641; 2.168; 2.737).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expert Survey">
  <data key="d0">Expert Survey</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Survey responses from three experts, collected with a questionnaire in Appendix A, used to construct the pairwise comparison matrices for criteria and sub-criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supplier Ranking">
  <data key="d0">Supplier Ranking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Step of the case study in which the four suppliers A1, A2, A3 and A4 are ranked to select the single best supplier.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal And External Weights">
  <data key="d0">Internal And External Weights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Internal weights express the importance of a sub-criterion within its criterion; for example, C11 has an internal weight of 0.6651. External weights express the importance of a sub-criterion across the whole hierarchy and are obtained by multiplying the internal weight by the criterion weight; for example, C11 has an external weight of 0.2493.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C1 is the most significant criterion">
  <data key="d0">C1 is the most significant criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from the AHP results: "The results indicate that among the main criteria, the most significant factor is C1, with a weight of 0.3749, suggesting its essential role in the overall decision-making process. Conversely, C6, with the lowest weight of 0.0375, is identified as the least critical criterion."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Weights">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Weights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Triangular fuzzy weights obtained by multiplying each fuzzy geometric mean by the inverse of the sum of all fuzzy geometric means; for example, C2 has weight (0.135; 0.224; 0.366) and C21 has weight (0.427; 0.627; 0.893).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Center of Gravity Method">
  <data key="d0">Center of Gravity Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Defuzzification method used to convert fuzzy weights into crisp weights for criteria and sub-criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal and External Weights">
  <data key="d0">Internal and External Weights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Internal weights are the crisp weights of sub-criteria within their criterion; external weights are obtained by multiplying each internal weight by the weight of its parent criterion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C1 Is the Highest-Weighted Main Criterion">
  <data key="d0">C1 Is the Highest-Weighted Main Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "C1 emerges as the highest-weighted main criterion with a value of 0.3820, indicating its predominant importance in the decision-making process."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C6 Is the Lowest-Weighted Criterion">
  <data key="d0">C6 Is the Lowest-Weighted Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "C6 is identified as the criterion with the least weight, at 0.0401, highlighting its relatively minor influence."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C11 Is the Most Significant Sub-Criterion">
  <data key="d0">C11 Is the Most Significant Sub-Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "C11 stands out as the most significant with an external weight of 0.2554."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C42 Is the Least Impactful Sub-Criterion">
  <data key="d0">C42 Is the Least Impactful Sub-Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "C42 is noted as the least impactful sub-criterion, with the lowest weight of 0.0045."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entropy Value Calculation">
  <data key="d0">Entropy Value Calculation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Step of the entropy method that follows normalization. It computes an entropy value e_i for each of n criteria using Eq. (18), and the values e_i1, e_i2 and e_i3 for the three sub-criteria under a given criterion. The results are summarized in Table 18.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Crisp Weights">
  <data key="d0">Crisp Weights</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-fuzzy weights of criteria and sub-criteria, obtained by defuzzifying the fuzzy weights with the center of gravity method (Eq. 17). They are presented in Table 16, where C1 has the highest criterion weight (0.3820) and C6 the lowest (0.0401).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boukrouh Ref [46]">
  <data key="d0">Boukrouh Ref [46]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as [46] in "Comprehensive Framework for Supplier Selection". The label refers to the citation number only and does not mean Boukrouh wrote it, because its authors and title are not visible. The citing article draws on it for two points: variation in rankings across MCDM methods is a significant challenge, and [46] applied the scenario formulation methodology of [47].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boukrouh Ref [47]">
  <data key="d0">Boukrouh Ref [47]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as [47] in "Comprehensive Framework for Supplier Selection". The label refers to the citation number only and does not mean Boukrouh wrote it, because its authors and title are not visible. [47] provides the methodology for formulating sensitivity-analysis scenarios: one criterion is set as most important, another as least important, and the rest are ranked in between. The number of scenarios equals the number of criteria, and a normalization value s is used for the weighting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Variation in rankings across MCDM methods is a significant challenge">
  <data key="d0">Variation in rankings across MCDM methods is a significant challenge</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "This variation in rankings is considered a significant challenge within these methodologies [46], and underscores the necessity for a sensitivity analysis to understand these variations and to evaluate the robustness of the proposed rankings."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sensitivity scenarios assign criteria weights by ordering importance">
  <data key="d0">Sensitivity scenarios assign criteria weights by ordering importance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "In this approach, each scenario adjusts the importance of criteria by setting one as most important, another as least important, and ranking the rest in between [47], allowing for a thorough examination of varied configurations."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Number of scenarios equals number of criteria">
  <data key="d0">Number of scenarios equals number of criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "To evaluate each criterion under all potential weightings, the total number of scenarios created is equal to the number of criteria [47]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Positive And Negative Ideal Solutions">
  <data key="d0">Positive And Negative Ideal Solutions</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The reference points V+ and V- in TOPSIS. They are computed from the weighted normalized decision matrix, and each supplier's distance from them is measured.&lt;SEP&gt;The best value A_j+ (max of r_pij) and the worst value A_j- (min of r_pij) for each criterion, computed from the weighted normalized matrix with Equations 5 and 6.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426448</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scenario-Based Weighting">
  <data key="d0">Scenario-Based Weighting</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Sensitivity-analysis procedure from [47]. Each scenario reorders criteria importance, and weights are assigned as 1/s, 2/s, ..., n/s, where s is a normalization value. The number of scenarios equals the number of criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ranking Robustness">
  <data key="d0">Ranking Robustness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The stability of supplier rankings under changes in criteria weights. It is assessed in order to verify the reliability of the techniques and to identify the most suitable method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weight normalization value s equals n(n+1)/2">
  <data key="d0">Weight normalization value s equals n(n+1)/2</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Here, s represents the normalization value for weighting, calculated as [47]: s = \sum_{i=1}^{n} i = n(n + 1)/2". In the first scenario, the ith element is assigned the value i/s, up to the nth element, which equals n/s.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scenario-based weight distribution for n criteria">
  <data key="d0">Scenario-based weight distribution for n criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "TABLE 26. Weight distribution for n criteria in various scenarios [47]." Scenario weights assign i/s to the ith element.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scenario-Based Sensitivity Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Scenario-Based Sensitivity Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedure creating twenty-one weight scenarios according to the number of sub-criteria to rank four alternatives; the most probable ranking was A1 &gt; A2 &gt; A4 &gt; A3.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Most probable supplier ranking is A1 &gt; A2 &gt; A4 &gt; A3">
  <data key="d0">Most probable supplier ranking is A1 &gt; A2 &gt; A4 &gt; A3</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from the scenario analysis: "According to Figure 9, the most probable ranking of the alternatives across all considered scenarios emerges as A1 &gt; A2 &gt; A4 &gt; A3." A1 ranks first in scenarios 1-3 and 7-21. A2 overtakes A1 in scenarios 4-6. A4 overtakes A3 from scenario 7 onward.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FAHP-Entropy-TOPSIS best aligns with scenario requirements">
  <data key="d0">FAHP-Entropy-TOPSIS best aligns with scenario requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from the text: "the average distance results indicate that FAHP-Entropy-TOPSIS exhibits the shortest distance, while AHP-TOPSIS shows the longest distance. This finding suggests that FAHP-Entropy-TOPSIS may be better aligned with the scenario's requirements."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supplier Alternatives A1-A4">
  <data key="d0">Supplier Alternatives A1-A4</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The four supplier alternatives (A1, A2, A3, A4) that are ranked across twenty-one scenarios and by the five methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subjective, Objective and Combined Approaches">
  <data key="d0">Subjective, Objective and Combined Approaches</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The three types of approaches in multi-criteria decision-making. Subjective techniques depend on expert insight and judgment. Objective techniques rely on data. Combined approaches integrate both.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzified Pairwise Comparison Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzified Pairwise Comparison Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Matrix of fuzzy pairwise comparisons of criteria used as the input to FAHP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uncertainty of Knowledge">
  <data key="d0">Uncertainty of Knowledge</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inherent uncertainty and ambiguity in expert knowledge in many domains. This uncertainty has led experts to integrate fuzzy set theory into subjective methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ranking Methods Comparison">
  <data key="d0">Ranking Methods Comparison</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Comparison of supplier rankings from AHP, FAHP, Entropy, AHP-Entropy and FAHP-Entropy against the most probable scenario ranking. One method swapped A1 with A2 and A4 with A3.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM)">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (MCDM)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Decision-making involving multiple criteria, with subjective, objective, and combined approaches to weighting criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Financial Situation">
  <data key="d0">Financial Situation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplier selection criterion C6; less significant according to Entropy, AHP-Entropy and FAHP-Entropy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weighting technique impacts supplier ranking">
  <data key="d0">Weighting technique impacts supplier ranking</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "These findings highlight the impact of the weighting technique on supplier ranking."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combined approaches provide stable outcomes across conditions">
  <data key="d0">Combined approaches provide stable outcomes across conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: rankings derived using AHP-Entropy and FAHP-Entropy remained consistent, "suggesting that these combined approaches provide a stable outcome across a diverse range of conditions."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subjective Approaches">
  <data key="d0">Subjective Approaches</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">MCDM weighting approaches that depend on the insight and judgment of experts; often integrated with fuzzy set theory to represent uncertainty and ambiguity in human reasoning. AHP and FAHP are used as subjective approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Objective Approaches">
  <data key="d0">Objective Approaches</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">MCDM weighting approaches that seek to eliminate personal bias by relying on quantitative data, but that may overlook the insights of subjective judgment. Entropy is used as the objective approach.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combined Approaches">
  <data key="d0">Combined Approaches</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">MCDM weighting approaches that integrate expert opinions with empirical data, using the strengths of both subjective and objective methods. AHP-Entropy and FAHP-Entropy are used as combined approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quality and delivery are the most critical supplier selection criteria">
  <data key="d0">Quality and delivery are the most critical supplier selection criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The results highlight quality and delivery as the most critical criteria across all methods, significantly influencing the final rankings."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entropy weights match sensitivity scenarios 4, 5 and 6">
  <data key="d0">Entropy weights match sensitivity scenarios 4, 5 and 6</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "the weights based on the Entropy method yielded identical rankings to those observed in scenarios 4, 5, and 6, showcasing some consistency in how this method aligns with specific scenario conditions."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AHP and FAHP rankings match no sensitivity scenario">
  <data key="d0">AHP and FAHP rankings match no sensitivity scenario</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "the rankings obtained using weights from the AHP and FAHP methods did not match any of the scenarios, indicating a unique set of preferences these methods capture that wasn't directly replicated in the sensitivity analysis scenarios."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422477</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Criteria Decision Making (MCDM)">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Criteria Decision Making (MCDM)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family of subjective, objective and combined decision-making approaches used for supplier selection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
