</node>
<node id="Comprehensive Framework for Supplier Selection">
  <data key="d0">Comprehensive Framework for Supplier Selection</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Comprehensive Framework for Supplier Selection is an article authored by I. Boukrouh et al., published in 2024 in Volume 12 of a journal (page 145559) under a CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 license. The article proposes a multi-phase supplier selection framework based on multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) methods. It evaluates and compares subjective, objective, fuzzy, and combined approaches for determining criteria weights, ranks supplier alternatives with TOPSIS, and assesses the robustness of the results through sensitivity analysis.

The framework compares five weighting methods: the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), Fuzzy AHP (FAHP), the Entropy weight method, and the hybrid AHP-Entropy and FAHP-Entropy approaches. In the AHP procedure, experts provide pairwise comparisons, which are aggregated using the geometric mean. The resulting matrix is normalized, criterion weights are calculated, and consistency is validated. FAHP applies a fuzzy pairwise-comparison weighting approach. The Entropy method supplies objective weights, which are combined with the subjective AHP and FAHP weights to form the hybrid methods. These methods are used to compute weights for both criteria and sub-criteria.

The methodology is demonstrated through a case study in the online retail sector. The case study involves seven main criteria, twenty-one sub-criteria, and four supplier alternatives. Suppliers are ranked with TOPSIS under each weighting method. A scenario-based sensitivity analysis comprising 21 scenarios then compares the methods and tests the stability of the rankings. The study finds the combined FAHP-Entropy-TOPSIS approach particularly effective for supplier selection.

The article's reference list includes works on fuzzy AHP, TOPSIS, and other MCDM methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Comprehensive_Framework_for_Supplier_Selection_Using_Subjective_Objective_and_Hybrid_Multi-Criteria_Decision-Making_Techniques_With_Sensitivity_Analysis_F (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422490</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FAHP">
  <data key="d0">FAHP</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">FAHP, the Fuzzy Analytic Hierarchy Process (also referred to as Fuzzy AHP), is a subjective multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) weighting method. It combines fuzzy set theory with the traditional Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) to handle the uncertainty and imprecision of human opinions and expert judgments. It does this through fuzzy ratings, fuzzy scales and linguistic variables, which lets decision-makers state their subjective evaluations more flexibly than crisp numerical comparisons allow.

Methodologically, FAHP builds fuzzified pairwise comparison matrices using triangular fuzzy numbers. It then computes fuzzy weights with the fuzzy geometric mean, and these weights are used to derive the criteria weights. Because it draws on expert judgment while explicitly addressing uncertainty, FAHP is an enhancement of conventional AHP for subjective evaluations.

In the proposed methodological framework, FAHP appears as one of the weighting methods alongside AHP and Entropy in the methodology flowchart (Figure 1). Its role there is to calculate subjective criteria weights, and it is combined with the objective Entropy method to produce the framework's weighting scheme. The results obtained with FAHP matched those produced by traditional AHP, which indicates consistency between the fuzzy and crisp versions of the method within the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-54e26c32a6c6377d00b93d14e0ea3092-chunk-012</data>
