<node id="Cross-Dataset Generalization">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Dataset Generalization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Open issue acknowledged by the authors. Further evaluations on other data, such as SAR or lower-resolution sensors, remain necessary to assess the pipeline's generalization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422750</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="VGG19">
  <data key="d0">VGG19</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">VGG19 is a deep convolutional neural network (CNN) that extends the VGG16 architecture with three additional convolutional layers, labeled (a'), (b'), and (c'), giving it 19 learnable layers in total. Its greater depth allows it to capture more complex features and finer image details. The trade-off is higher computational requirements and longer training times than VGG16.

In the road-extraction context, VGG19 was used alongside VGG16 in the classification step of a satellite imagery pipeline. Its role was to filter out satellite image tiles that do not contain roads. The model was pre-trained on ImageNet and fine-tuned through transfer learning for binary road-presence classification, and its performance was assessed with image-level accuracy. VGG19 reached a training accuracy of 0.9227, a validation accuracy of 0.9136, a training loss of 0.2116, and a validation loss of 0.2312. Despite its deeper architecture, it consistently achieved lower accuracy than VGG16 for road tile classification and was outperformed by VGG16 in this task.

Separately, VGG19 is also described as a deep convolutional neural network applied to insect classification and to transfer learning on the Caltech-101 dataset. This shows that its use extends beyond road-presence detection to other image classification domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="VGG16">
  <data key="d0">VGG16</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">VGG16 is a convolutional neural network (CNN) with 16 learnable layers: 13 convolutional layers and 3 fully connected layers. Its relatively simple architecture trains faster and requires less computation than deeper networks while still achieving high image classification accuracy. Its convolutional and fully connected layers are illustrated in Figure 1 of the source work, labeled (a) through (e).

In the road-extraction context, VGG16 serves as the classifier in the classification phase of a satellite-image processing pipeline. Its role is to identify road-containing satellite image tiles and filter out images without roads before later processing steps. The model uses transfer learning: it starts from ImageNet-pretrained weights and is fine-tuned for binary road-presence classification. Its performance is assessed with image-level accuracy.

VGG16 was compared directly with VGG19 and outperformed it on every reported measure. It achieved a training accuracy of 99.29% (0.9929) and a validation accuracy of 99.09% (0.9909). It also had lower losses, with a training loss of 0.0336 and a validation loss of 0.0391. These results made it the preferred model for detecting road presence in satellite imagery.

Separately, VGG16 is described as a deep convolutional neural network compared with VGG19 for insect classification. This appears to be a different application of the same architecture, likely from another study, rather than part of the road-presence work.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-9a32e5ba6e7447add4fce7dbb90abe96-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">A_Deep_Learning_Pipeline_for_Accurate_Road_Detecti.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791422754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
