<node id="Complexity is the greatest challenge for scientific and engineering disciplines">
  <data key="d0">Complexity is the greatest challenge for scientific and engineering disciplines</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Complexity is undeniably the greatest challenge faced by numerous scientific and engineering disciplines (Tian et al., 2012).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">acs_25_66701.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423275</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cancer screening effectiveness is limited by public buy-in and financial factors">
  <data key="d0">Cancer screening effectiveness is limited by public buy-in and financial factors</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">While screening is considered promising, public buy-in, financial considerations, and other factors limit its effectiveness in covering all at-risk populations (Ahnen et al., 2014).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">acs_25_66701.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423275</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patients may fit several subgroups with heterogeneous treatment effects">
  <data key="d0">Patients may fit several subgroups with heterogeneous treatment effects</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Yet subpopulations are often defined by one or two characteristics, and any patient may fit into several subgroups with heterogeneous treatment effects (McAlister et al., 2000).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">acs_25_66701.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423275</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patients and clinicians assess treatment options differently">
  <data key="d0">Patients and clinicians assess treatment options differently</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"Decision-making remains a challenge, as patients and clinicians assess treatment options differently (Reyna et al., 2015)." Also: "Patients often struggle to interpret risk figures and probabilities (Redelmeier et al., 1993; Mazur &amp; Hickam, 1993)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">acs_25_66701.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423275</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breast cancer patients weigh treatment costs and benefits across multiple outcomes">
  <data key="d0">Breast cancer patients weigh treatment costs and benefits across multiple outcomes</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">For instance, breast cancer patients weigh treatment costs and benefits related to symptom-free survival, daily life quality, relapse time, and functional status (Lee et al., 2010).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-860e34bf4e631717387a6fc6684e1154-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">acs_25_66701.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423275</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
