<node id="LSTM enhances neural networks with memory of previous inputs">
  <data key="d0">LSTM enhances neural networks with memory of previous inputs</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">LSTM recurrent neural networks enhance traditional neural networks by processing sequential data with a memory mechanism for previous inputs (20).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LSTM cell uses input, forget and output gates">
  <data key="d0">LSTM cell uses input, forget and output gates</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">This is achieved through a cell with three gates: the input gate which determines if the input should modify the content of the cell, the forget which decides on the content's retention, and the output which determines whether the cell should eliminate its current content, essentially forgetting its previous knowledge (21).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LSTM unit is described by gate and state equations">
  <data key="d0">LSTM unit is described by gate and state equations</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"These elements are represented mathematically through the following equations (12)" - forget, input and output gates, cell input, cell state and unit output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Forget gate controls retention of past information">
  <data key="d0">Forget gate controls retention of past information</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Firstly, it filters the previous cell state c_{t-1} with the forget gate f_t, which determines how much of the past information is kept (ranging from 0, indicating complete rejecting, to 1, signifying complete keeping) (22).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Gate">
  <data key="d0">Input Gate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">LSTM gate (i_t) that determines whether the input should modify the content of the cell; together with the cell input activation it incorporates new information into the cell state.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM gate (i_t) that, together with the cell input activation, incorporates new information into the cell state.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM gate that generates a candidate memory state g_t via tanh and determines which portions are retained through the input state i_t.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM cell component that generates a new candidate memory state g_t via tanh. It also determines which portions to retain, producing input state i_t.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1920bb99aa33c3c871f9dc257956e3ca-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_171Edited_FV.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid_Approach_Integrating_Deep_Learning-Autoencoder_With_Statistical_Process_Control_Chart_for_Anomaly_Detection_Case_Study_in_Injection_Molding_Process1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426328</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Output Gate">
  <data key="d0">Output Gate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">LSTM gate (o_t) that determines whether the cell should eliminate its current content; its activation is used to generate a candidate value for the final LSTM output.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM gate whose activation generates a candidate value used in computing the LSTM's final output.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM gate producing the output state o_t; the hidden state is h_t = tanh(c_t) o_t.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM cell component producing output state o_t via sigmoid. o_t is used with tanh(c_t) to generate the updated hidden state h_t.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1920bb99aa33c3c871f9dc257956e3ca-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_171Edited_FV.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid_Approach_Integrating_Deep_Learning-Autoencoder_With_Statistical_Process_Control_Chart_for_Anomaly_Detection_Case_Study_in_Injection_Molding_Process1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426328</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cell Input Activation">
  <data key="d0">Cell Input Activation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Candidate cell input (c̃_t) computed with the hyperbolic tangent function from the current input and previous output; combined with the input gate to update the cell state.&lt;SEP&gt;LSTM component (c̃_t) that works with the input gate to incorporate new information into the cell state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hadamard Product">
  <data key="d0">Hadamard Product</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Element-wise multiplication (denoted ∗) used in the LSTM equations to combine gate activations with cell states.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sigmoid Function">
  <data key="d0">Sigmoid Function</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Activation function (σ) used to compute the forget, input and output gate activations in the LSTM unit.&lt;SEP&gt;Activation function σ(x) = e^x/(e^x + 1) used in LSTM gates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b861a9335e2f8bb52dad6132b0acac1d-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;CIST_171Edited_FV.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423714</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperbolic Tangent Function">
  <data key="d0">Hyperbolic Tangent Function</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Activation function (tanh) used to compute the cell input activation and to transform the cell state for the LSTM output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Six-Step Methodology">
  <data key="d0">Six-Step Methodology</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Research framework comprising six steps (illustrated in Figure 1) followed to identify the most effective model for forecasting daily retail sales, with each step detailed through a case study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sequence Prediction">
  <data key="d0">Sequence Prediction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Task of predicting elements of complex sequences in data science, for which LSTM networks have become the preferred method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No theoretical framework exists for selecting neural network hyperparameters">
  <data key="d0">No theoretical framework exists for selecting neural network hyperparameters</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Nowadays, there is no theoretical framework to identify these parameters to obtain a specific performance of the model; hence, it requires iterative training and parameter adjustment to minimize the chosen quality metric until an optimal model is identified (23)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ARMA">
  <data key="d0">ARMA</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Autoregressive moving average model of order (p, q), combining autoregressive and moving average components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Stationary Process">
  <data key="d0">Non-Stationary Process</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Time series process admitting a trend and seasonality, modeled by SARIMA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trend">
  <data key="d0">Trend</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-term movement in a non-stationary time series process. SARIMA models are designed to handle trend alongside seasonality.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-term component of time series data; in SARIMA it is addressed by the parameters p, d and q (autoregressive terms, degree of differencing and moving average terms).&lt;SEP&gt;Long-term component of a time series. It can be none, additive or multiplicative in exponential smoothing configurations, and its presence determines the choice of model.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-term directional movement present in the sales data. Together with seasonality, trends motivated the choice of time series models.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Moving Average Model">
  <data key="d0">Moving Average Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">MA(q) model in which each observation is a combination of the errors from the previous q observations and the observation's own error, with normally distributed error terms of mean zero and variance sigma squared.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ARMA Model">
  <data key="d0">ARMA Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Autoregressive Moving Average ARMA(p, q) model combining AR and MA polynomials via the backshift operation, with a constant term.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SARIMA Model">
  <data key="d0">SARIMA Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Seasonal Integrated Autoregressive Moving Average model built from seven parameters (p, d, q)(P, D, Q)m capturing trend and seasonal dynamics of time series data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backshift Operator">
  <data key="d0">Backshift Operator</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Operator beta used to rewrite autoregressive and moving average models in simplified polynomial form; seasonal and non-seasonal variants are used in SARIMA.&lt;SEP&gt;Operator used in the seasonal ARIMA formulation. The model has a non-seasonal form (β) and a seasonal form (β^m).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stationarity">
  <data key="d0">Stationarity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property required for time series analysis; achieved via differencing (degree d) and seasonal differencing (D) in SARIMA.&lt;SEP&gt;Property of a time series that must be achieved before ARIMA-type modeling. It is assessed with ADF/KPSS tests and achieved through differencing or logarithmic transformations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differencing">
  <data key="d0">Differencing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transformation applied to time series data to achieve stationarity; in SARIMA, the parameter d gives the degree of non-seasonal differencing and D gives the number of seasonal differences.&lt;SEP&gt;Transformation applied to non-stationary time series to achieve stationarity. Its non-seasonal and seasonal orders are given by the parameters d and D.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normally Distributed Error Terms">
  <data key="d0">Normally Distributed Error Terms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sequence of unobserved error terms epsilon_t following a normal distribution with mean zero and variance sigma squared, written as epsilon_t ~ N(0, sigma^2), used in the MA and ARMA models.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lag Order">
  <data key="d0">Lag Order</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Order specified by parameters p and q, giving the lag order of the time series observations x_t and the residuals epsilon_t, respectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 24">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 24</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (24) for the four primary steps of the Box-Jenkins time series modeling methodology.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 25">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 25</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (25) for assessing stationarity by visual inspection or with the ADF and KPSS tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 26">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 26</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (26) for how the ADF test detects non-stationarity through autoregressive polynomial roots close to 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 27">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 27</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (27) for the KPSS test, which evaluates whether ARMA residuals show white noise and therefore indicate stationarity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 28">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 28</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (28) for using differencing or logarithmic transformations to achieve stationarity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 7">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 7</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (7) for using ACF and PACF to determine model parameters, and for automating model selection by minimizing statistical criteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 29">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 29</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (29) for exponential smoothing as a univariate forecasting method with exponentially decreasing weights on older data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref. 30">
  <data key="d0">Ref. 30</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (30) on exponential smoothing. It covers exponentially decreasing weights and the additive or multiplicative arrangement of trend and seasonal factors, and is referenced for the other model configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
