<node id="Box-Jenkins methodology has four primary steps">
  <data key="d0">Box-Jenkins methodology has four primary steps</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The Box-Jenkins methodology for time series modeling progresses through four primary keys: model identification, parameter estimation, model validation, and forecasting (24).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stationarity can be assessed visually or with ADF and KPSS tests">
  <data key="d0">Stationarity can be assessed visually or with ADF and KPSS tests</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Initially, the process begins with assessing the stationarity of the time series under study, either through visual inspection or by employing statistical tests like the Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) or the Kwiatkowski-Phillips-Schmidt-Shin (KPSS) tests (25).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ADF test detects non-stationarity via autoregressive roots near 1">
  <data key="d0">ADF test detects non-stationarity via autoregressive roots near 1</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The ADF test checks if the time series is non-stationary by identifying if any autoregressive polynomial roots are close to 1 (26).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="KPSS test indicates stationarity via white noise residuals">
  <data key="d0">KPSS test indicates stationarity via white noise residuals</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Conversely, the KPSS test evaluates if the residuals from ARMA modeling display white noise characteristics, indicating stationarity (27).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Differencing or log transformations achieve stationarity">
  <data key="d0">Differencing or log transformations achieve stationarity</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In cases of non-stationarity, techniques like differencing or logarithmic transformations are applied to achieve stationarity (28).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ACF and PACF are used to determine model parameters">
  <data key="d0">ACF and PACF are used to determine model parameters</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">After verifying stationarity, the determination of model parameters proceeds by examining the Auto-Correlation Function (ACF)... and the Partial Auto-Correlation Function (PACF), which identifies the correlation between time series observations at specific lags (7).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Model selection can be automated by minimizing statistical criteria">
  <data key="d0">Model selection can be automated by minimizing statistical criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">For optimal predictive accuracy, model selection can be automated by identifying its hyperparameters (p, d, q, P, D, Q) that minimize statistical criteria (7), such as the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) or the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential smoothing weights recent observations more heavily">
  <data key="d0">Exponential smoothing weights recent observations more heavily</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Exponential smoothing serves as a prediction method for univariate time series data, focusing on forecasting future values by giving more significance to recent observations through exponentially diminishing weights on older data (29, 30).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simple exponential smoothing suits data without trend or seasonality">
  <data key="d0">Simple exponential smoothing suits data without trend or seasonality</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Simple exponential smoothing is applied to data lacking both trends and seasonality (31)</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holt's method suits time series with trends">
  <data key="d0">Holt's method suits time series with trends</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">double exponential smoothing, or Holt's method, is appropriate for time series with trends (32)</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holt-Winters method suits seasonal data">
  <data key="d0">Holt-Winters method suits seasonal data</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">triple exponential smoothing, or Holt-Winter's method, is designed for analyzing seasonal data (33).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential smoothing trend and seasonal factors can be additive or multiplicative">
  <data key="d0">Exponential smoothing trend and seasonal factors can be additive or multiplicative</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Exponential smoothing models integrate trend and seasonal factors, which can be arranged either additively or multiplicatively (30), offering twelve distinct configurations, as illustrated in Table 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holt-Winters additive model is defined by level, trend and seasonal equations">
  <data key="d0">Holt-Winters additive model is defined by level, trend and seasonal equations</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">For the Holt-Winters additive model (Add, Add), the level, trend, and seasonal equations, are formulated as follows (34)</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Augmented Dickey-Fuller Test">
  <data key="d0">Augmented Dickey-Fuller Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical stationarity test (ADF) that checks for non-stationarity by identifying autoregressive polynomial roots close to 1. It is sensitive to roots above or near one.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="KPSS Test">
  <data key="d0">KPSS Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Kwiatkowski-Phillips-Schmidt-Shin test, which evaluates whether ARMA residuals display white noise characteristics, indicating stationarity. It is recommended alongside the ADF test.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Auto-Correlation Function">
  <data key="d0">Auto-Correlation Function</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">ACF; quantifies the relationship between time series observations separated by specific lag times. It is used to determine model parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Auto-Correlation Function">
  <data key="d0">Partial Auto-Correlation Function</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">PACF; identifies the correlation between time series observations at specific lags. It is used for model parameter determination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Akaike Information Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Akaike Information Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">AIC = -2 log(L) + 2k; a statistical criterion minimized for automated model selection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian Information Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Information Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">BIC = -2 log(L) + k log(n); a statistical criterion minimized for automated model selection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holt's Method">
  <data key="d0">Holt's Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Double exponential smoothing, appropriate for time series with trends.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holt-Winters Method">
  <data key="d0">Holt-Winters Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Triple exponential smoothing designed for seasonal data. Its additive (Add, Add) form is defined by level, trend and seasonal equations.&lt;SEP&gt;Seasonal exponential smoothing forecasting model developed from the work of Holt and Winters; initialization of its multiple seasonal variants is studied in reference 30.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simple Exponential Smoothing">
  <data key="d0">Simple Exponential Smoothing</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Basic exponential smoothing model applied to time series data that lack both trend and seasonality.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Seasonal ARIMA Model">
  <data key="d0">Seasonal ARIMA Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Time series model with non-seasonal and seasonal backshift operators, autoregressive and moving-average polynomials of degrees p, P, q and Q, and orders d and D of non-seasonal and seasonal differencing. Its hyperparameters (p, d, q, P, D, Q) are chosen by minimizing AIC or BIC.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logarithmic Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Logarithmic Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Transformation applied to non-stationary time series to help achieve stationarity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="White Noise">
  <data key="d0">White Noise</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property of residuals with no autocorrelation and a normal distribution. The KPSS test uses it to indicate stationarity, and model diagnostics check residuals against it.&lt;SEP&gt;Property that the residuals of a validated SARIMA model are expected to exhibit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Residual Diagnostics">
  <data key="d0">Residual Diagnostics</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Model validation step of the Box-Jenkins methodology. It analyzes model residuals for autocorrelation and checks their distribution for normality to confirm white noise behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holt-Winters Additive Model">
  <data key="d0">Holt-Winters Additive Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Exponential smoothing model (Add, Add) with level, trend, and seasonal equations using smoothing parameters alpha, beta, and gamma between 0 and 1, and seasonal periodicity m.&lt;SEP&gt;The (Add, Add) configuration of exponential smoothing, with additive trend and additive seasonality. It is defined by level, trend and seasonal equations with smoothing parameters α and β.&lt;SEP&gt;Exponential smoothing forecasting model that gives less weight to older observations. Its coefficients are optimized by grid search to minimize MAPE. For the sales series, the optimal values are level 0.03, trend 0.2 and seasonal 0.01. For the residuals series, they are level 0.08, trend 0.2 and seasonal 0.08.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Other exponential smoothing configurations are described in the literature">
  <data key="d0">Other exponential smoothing configurations are described in the literature</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">To explore the other models, refer to literature (30, 34, 35).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference (34)">
  <data key="d0">Reference (34)</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited work (34) used as the source for the formulation of the Holt-Winters additive model level, trend, and seasonal equations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference (36)">
  <data key="d0">Reference (36)</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited work (36) describing choices for initial smoothing values, including Least Squares Estimates, Convenient initial estimates, and Zero Estimates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LSTM-SARIMA-Holt-Winters Integrated Approach">
  <data key="d0">LSTM-SARIMA-Holt-Winters Integrated Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed hybrid sales forecasting approach that sequentially applies LSTM, SARIMA on LSTM residuals, and Holt-Winters on adjusted residuals, with the final prediction obtained by adding the component predictions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Box-Jenkins Models">
  <data key="d0">Box-Jenkins Models</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Traditional time series models for which Holt-Winters is considered a strong alternative.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Least Squares Estimates">
  <data key="d0">Least Squares Estimates</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the available choices for selecting initial smoothing values l0, T0, and s0.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zero Estimates">
  <data key="d0">Zero Estimates</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the available choices for selecting initial smoothing values in exponential smoothing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exponential Smoothing Classification">
  <data key="d0">Exponential Smoothing Classification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Classification of exponential smoothing models by trend and seasonal components (None, Additive, Multiplicative), presented in Table 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retail Sales Forecasting">
  <data key="d0">Retail Sales Forecasting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problem of forecasting sales in the retail sector, addressed by the proposed integrated approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Forecasting of retail sales, addressed by references 33 and 44.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SARIMA can outperform LSTM in certain cases">
  <data key="d0">SARIMA can outperform LSTM in certain cases</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "The choice of employing this LSTM-SARIMA combination is informed by comparisons between these two models in existing literature, where SARIMA models have demonstrated superior performance compared to deep learning algorithms such as LSTM in certain cases."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Convenient Initial Estimates">
  <data key="d0">Convenient Initial Estimates</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the available choices for selecting initial smoothing values l0, T0, and s0 when t = 0 in exponential smoothing, as described in reference (36).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iterative Testing and Fine-Tuning">
  <data key="d0">Iterative Testing and Fine-Tuning</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedure used to address challenges in integrating LSTM, SARIMA, and Holt-Winters models, such as ensuring interoperability and aligning outputs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Residual Modeling">
  <data key="d0">Residual Modeling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Technique in the proposed approach where SARIMA models LSTM residuals Et = Yt − Ŷt and Holt-Winters models adjusted residuals Rt = Et − Êt; the final prediction adds the predictions from the models.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Linear Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Non-Linear Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex non-linear patterns in sales time series that LSTM is capable of learning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recent Observations Prioritization">
  <data key="d0">Recent Observations Prioritization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Emphasis on recent observations, provided by Holt-Winters, to improve the accuracy of future value prediction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Holt-Winters is a strong alternative to Box-Jenkins models">
  <data key="d0">Holt-Winters is a strong alternative to Box-Jenkins models</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "This integration is motivated by the recognition of the Holt-Winters method's effectiveness as a strong alternative to traditional Box-Jenkins models, aiming to enhance the accuracy of future value prediction by prioritizing recent observations."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="US Superstore Retail Sales Dataset">
  <data key="d0">US Superstore Retail Sales Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Retail sales dataset from a superstore in the USA covering January 2, 2011 to December 30, 2023, merged from four time frames; includes 35874 orders across technology, office supplies and furniture categories, aggregated into daily sales.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference (37)">
  <data key="d0">Reference (37)</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Article cited as (37) that offers detailed techniques on data preprocessing steps: cleaning, reduction, scaling, transformation and partitioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference (38)">
  <data key="d0">Reference (38)</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as (38) in relation to data scaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-285b07b80137f799fd1205a24fb418c4-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">AHybridApproachforSalesForecastingCombiningDeepLearningandTimeSeries.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423544</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
