<node id="AI has transformed multiple industries">
  <data key="d0">AI has transformed multiple industries</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Artificial intelligence (AI) has revolutionized numerous industries, bringing transformative changes to sectors such as marketing [1], manufacturing [2], logistic [3], healthcare [4], and more.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Feature selection and feature extraction are the key dimensionality reduction approaches">
  <data key="d0">Feature selection and feature extraction are the key dimensionality reduction approaches</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Feature selection and feature extraction represent the two key approaches used for this purpose [5].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Feature selection prevents overfitting and boosts classifier accuracy">
  <data key="d0">Feature selection prevents overfitting and boosts classifier accuracy</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">This research prioritizes feature selection to streamline high-dimensional data by removing irrelevant and redundant features, thereby preventing overfitting and boosting classifier accuracy [6], [7].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta is particularly effective for feature selection">
  <data key="d0">Boruta is particularly effective for feature selection</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The Boruta algorithm is particularly effective for feature selection [8], [9], [10], [11], [12], [13].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta can be integrated with ensemble learning algorithms">
  <data key="d0">Boruta can be integrated with ensemble learning algorithms</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">To further enhance its efficacy, Boruta can be integrated with ensemble learning algorithms like Random Forest [10], Boruta-ERT [11], and XGBoost [12].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Classification Performance">
  <data key="d0">Classification Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Effectiveness of classification models measured by accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. The article finds that Boruta-based feature selection improves it, especially in datasets with irrelevant features.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dataset 1">
  <data key="d0">Dataset 1</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">First of three datasets evaluated in the article. With feature selection, accuracy improved by 1.06%, precision by 3.23%, recall by 3.46%, and F1-score by 3.36%.&lt;SEP&gt;Dataset with 20 original features, 7 selected by Boruta; accuracy improved from 95.32% to 96.39% and F1-score from 85.01% to 88.47% with feature selection.&lt;SEP&gt;First of three datasets; feature selection led to significant improvement in accuracy, precision and recall.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dataset 2">
  <data key="d0">Dataset 2</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Second of three datasets evaluated in the article. With feature selection, accuracy increased by 0.46%, precision by 2.36%, recall by 4.82%, and F1-score by 5.42%.&lt;SEP&gt;Dataset with 29 original features, 15 selected by Boruta; accuracy rose from 87.05% to 87.50% and F1-score from 28.10% to 33.33% with feature selection.&lt;SEP&gt;Second of three datasets; feature selection resulted in notable enhancements in precision and F1-score.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dataset 3">
  <data key="d0">Dataset 3</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Third of three datasets evaluated in the article. It showed no significant changes with feature selection, with both configurations yielding similar performance metrics.&lt;SEP&gt;Dataset with 21 features, all retained by Boruta; performance stayed identical with and without feature selection (accuracy 94.31%, precision 97.87%, recall 17.10%, F1-score 29.11%).&lt;SEP&gt;Third of three datasets, in which all features were deemed relevant; feature selection had minimal impact.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wrapper Method">
  <data key="d0">Wrapper Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Feature selection approach used by Boruta to assess feature importance by comparing features to random noise features.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ensemble Learning">
  <data key="d0">Ensemble Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family of predictive algorithms whose predictive power, combined with Boruta's feature selection, yields superior results on complex datasets.&lt;SEP&gt;Family of learning algorithms, such as Random Forest and XGBoost, that can be combined with Boruta to leverage both its feature selection and the predictive power of ensemble methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Learning paradigm whose synergy with feature selection algorithms is suggested for future investigation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta enhances classification performance when irrelevant features are present">
  <data key="d0">Boruta enhances classification performance when irrelevant features are present</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of the article: "These findings confirm that the Boruta algorithm effectively enhances classification performance by reducing dataset dimensionality and retaining key features, especially in datasets with irrelevant features. However, when all features are relevant, the benefits of feature selection may be minimal."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta uses a wrapper method comparing features to random noise">
  <data key="d0">Boruta uses a wrapper method comparing features to random noise</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Developed by Miron B. Kursa et al in [14], Boruta uses a wrapper method to assess feature importance by comparing them to random noise features [22].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta categorizes features as selected, tentative, or rejected">
  <data key="d0">Boruta categorizes features as selected, tentative, or rejected</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">It categorizes features as 'selected', 'tentative', or 'rejected' based on their importance [41].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta">
  <data key="d0">Boruta</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Feature selection method that classifies features as 'selected', 'tentative', or 'rejected' based on their importance. It creates shadow features by randomly shuffling the original features, uses the highest shadow Z value (Z max) as a threshold, and repeats until all features are confirmed or rejected or an iteration limit is reached.&lt;SEP&gt;Feature selection algorithm. The paper suggests applying it to other ML models, such as Decision Tree, Gradient Boosting Machines, Support Vector Machines and Neural Networks.&lt;SEP&gt;A feature selection algorithm described in "Feature Selection with the Boruta Package". It is used for hot-rolled strip crown prediction and, in improved form with XGBoost, for PEMFC control.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shadow Features">
  <data key="d0">Shadow Features</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Features generated by randomly shuffling the original features. They are combined with the original dataset to assess feature importance.&lt;SEP&gt;Randomly generated features. The Boruta algorithm compares the importance of the original features against them, keeping only features that are significantly more important.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Z Max">
  <data key="d0">Z Max</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The highest Z value among the shadow features. Original features with Z above Z max are deemed important and selected, and those below are deemed unimportant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta classifies features as selected, tentative, or rejected by importance">
  <data key="d0">Boruta classifies features as selected, tentative, or rejected by importance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "...as 'selected', 'tentative', or 'rejected' based on their importance [41]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boruta can be integrated with ensemble algorithms like Random Forest, Boruta-ERT, and XGBoost">
  <data key="d0">Boruta can be integrated with ensemble algorithms like Random Forest, Boruta-ERT, and XGBoost</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "To further enhance its efficacy, Boruta can be integrated with ensemble learning algorithms like Random Forest [10], Boruta-ERT [11], and XGBoost [12]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed5a32c2476eb625d3377722542a5d95-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">CIST_174.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791423712</data>
