  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Feature Influence Assessment">
  <data key="d0">Feature Influence Assessment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Final verification step of the approach. It assesses how individual features influence the models, using LIME.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machine Learning-Assisted Decision Support in Industrial Plastic Molding">
  <data key="d0">Machine Learning-Assisted Decision Support in Industrial Plastic Molding</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">The article being read, by Faouzi Tayalati, published in Int J Artif Intell (ISSN 2252-8938). The running header truncates the title to "Machine learning-assisted decision support in industrial ...", so the full title is not given in the excerpt. The excerpt analyzes how mold, part and material features relate to machine clamping force. It also shows how machines are distributed across five clamping force classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Correlation Matrix">
  <data key="d0">Data Correlation Matrix</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Correlation analysis between machine clamping force and the dataset features. The coefficients include 0.156 for density, -0.346 for melt flow and 0.11 for material type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold And Part Features Are The Most Significant Contributors To Machine Selection">
  <data key="d0">Mold And Part Features Are The Most Significant Contributors To Machine Selection</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "This initial analysis confirms that mold and part features are the most significant contributors to machine selection. This is consistent with the actual plastic molding manufacturing."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smaller Molds Are Assigned To Smaller Machines">
  <data key="d0">Smaller Molds Are Assigned To Smaller Machines</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The trend observed indicates that smaller molds are more likely to be assigned to smaller machines." Molds under 400 mm in height go to Class 1 and 2 machines, while molds over 700 mm go to Class 5 machines.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold Width">
  <data key="d0">Mold Width</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mold dimension that significantly influences machine selection. Widths under 400 mm are assigned to Class 1 and 2 machines, and widths over 600 mm are assigned to 250 t, 320 t and 400 t machines.&lt;SEP&gt;Input feature. The RF model assigns it a LIME coefficient of 0.02.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold Height">
  <data key="d0">Mold Height</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mold dimension that affects machine allocation. Heights under 400 mm tend to be assigned to Class 1 and 2 machines, while heights over 700 mm are associated with Class 5 machines.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Part Weight">
  <data key="d0">Part Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A main part feature used, together with mold specification, to classify machine size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Melt Flow">
  <data key="d0">Melt Flow</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A material feature with a correlation coefficient of -0.346 with machine clamping force.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Molding Manufacturing">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Molding Manufacturing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The industrial domain of the study. Real plastic molding practice is consistent with the finding that mold and part features drive machine selection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold Specification">
  <data key="d0">Mold Specification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The set of mold characteristics, such as dimensions like height and width. Together with part weight, mold specification is a main feature used to classify machine size (Figure 5).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold Dimensions Impact Machine Allocation">
  <data key="d0">Mold Dimensions Impact Machine Allocation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "This correlation underscores the impact of mold dimensions on the allocation of machines."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machine Size Classification">
  <data key="d0">Machine Size Classification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Classification of machines into five clamping force classes based on mold specification and part weight (Figure 5). The classes are Class 1 (150 t), Class 2 (200 t), Class 3 (250 t), Class 4 (320 t) and Class 5 (400 t). The distribution is roughly normal and centered on the 200 t and 250 t machines.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
