<node id="Learning Curves">
  <data key="d0">Learning Curves</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation technique used to assess the training approaches of each model and to detect overfitting and underfitting.&lt;SEP&gt;Models of performance as a function of experience or training data. They are reviewed in [28] and used to improve the robustness of load forecasting in [30].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Molds And Parts Dataset">
  <data key="d0">Molds And Parts Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset of molds and parts with an 85%/15% split: models were trained on 85 molds and parts and tested on 15 additional parts. The 15 predicted machines are distributed across five classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425064</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Underfitting">
  <data key="d0">Underfitting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Situation in which NB and LR fail to capture the intricacies of the training dataset, with training accuracy around 70%.&lt;SEP&gt;Problem in models with lower scores, investigated through learning curves.&lt;SEP&gt;Phenomenon observed in the SGD, LR, and NB models, attributed to the small dataset size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference [28]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [28]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work cited as [28] in connection with the use of learning curves to assess model training.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [28] regarding fuzzy logic enabling linguistic representation of variable states and automatic CPT generation.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [28] supporting the choice of Sugeno method for generating CPTs of a Bayesian network.&lt;SEP&gt;Work cited as [28] on deep learning, in which multiple layers each provide a distinct interpretation of the data.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [28] regarding false data injection attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501546</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning curves assess model training approaches">
  <data key="d0">Learning curves assess model training approaches</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Finally, the evaluation includes the examination of learning curves [28] to assess the training approaches adopted by each model over the course of their training."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning curves reveal overfitting and underfitting">
  <data key="d0">Learning curves reveal overfitting and underfitting</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "This will be further investigated in this paper through learning curves to assess overfitting in models with good predictions and under fitting in models with lower scores [29]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Random Forest outperforms other models for machine selection">
  <data key="d0">Random Forest outperforms other models for machine selection</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from the results: "As evident from the results, all metrics consistently favor optimistic predictions for RF, DT, and SVM, with a preference for RF."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML can select machines with limited data">
  <data key="d0">ML can select machines with limited data</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from the results: "This suggests that ML exhibits the ability to select machines even with limited data."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bias">
  <data key="d0">Bias</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Potential issue noted in the text: the favorable predictions obtained with limited data may introduce bias, which is investigated through learning curves.&lt;SEP&gt;Model bias. The high accuracy of the training score suggests low bias for the better-performing models.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning Curves Analysis Reference [30]">
  <data key="d0">Learning Curves Analysis Reference [30]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [30]; the reference for the learning curves analysis used to evaluate the models.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning Curves Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Learning Curves Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A widely adopted diagnostic tool in machine learning, used for algorithms that progressively acquire knowledge from an expanding training dataset. The paper uses it to evaluate how each model gained proficiency, using accuracy on the training and validation sets.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stochastic Gradient Descent">
  <data key="d0">Stochastic Gradient Descent</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">SGD model. It has difficulty learning from the dataset and fails to improve performance, similar to NB and LR.&lt;SEP&gt;SGD model compared in the learning curve analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Classification model that gave the least favorable results, with 27% accuracy, due to underfitting.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine learning optimization method, applied to a contextual analysis of spam remarks.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimization algorithm used to minimize loss during CNN training.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning curves are a widely adopted diagnostic tool in ML">
  <data key="d0">Learning curves are a widely adopted diagnostic tool in ML</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Learning curves serve as a widely adopted diagnostic tool in the field of ML, particularly for algorithms that progressively acquire knowledge from an expanding training dataset." The approach is attributed to [30].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Industrial Machine Classification">
  <data key="d0">Industrial Machine Classification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The task of predicting machine categories (five classes, e.g. 250 t and 320 t machines) for industrial decision support.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Industrial Machine Dataset">
  <data key="d0">Industrial Machine Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset of industrial machines grouped into five classes, such as class 3 (250 t machines) and class 4 (320 t machines). It is used to train and evaluate the six models. The confusion matrices cover 15 predicted machines, and the learning curves track performance as training size grows from about 20 to 100 examples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Training Set">
  <data key="d0">Training Set</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The portion of data used to train the models. Accuracy on the training set is tracked in the learning curves. For NB and LR, training accuracy stays around 70%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Validation Set">
  <data key="d0">Validation Set</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Held-out data used to measure each model's generalization in the learning curves. Validation accuracy plateaus at 60% for NB and LR.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="True Positives, False Positives and False Negatives">
  <data key="d0">True Positives, False Positives and False Negatives</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Classification outcome categories read from the confusion matrix. The tree models distinguished them well. The NB model had limited capability to distinguish false negatives from false positives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Six Machine Learning Models">
  <data key="d0">Six Machine Learning Models</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The set of six algorithms evaluated in the paper: RF, SVM, DT, NB, LR and SGD. They are grouped into three learning-curve visualizations based on their trends.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ribeiro 2016 LIME Reference [31]">
  <data key="d0">Ribeiro 2016 LIME Reference [31]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [31]; reference describing LIME as one of the prominent techniques in explainable artificial intelligence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LIME is a prominent XAI technique">
  <data key="d0">LIME is a prominent XAI technique</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "To interpret the model's predictions, we explore the LIME method, which stands for local interpretable model-agnostic explanations, renowned as one of the prominent techniques in explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) [31]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Model Agnosticism">
  <data key="d0">Model Agnosticism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principle of LIME: ability to explain any supervised learning model by treating it as a black box.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local Explanations">
  <data key="d0">Local Explanations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principle of LIME: explanations faithful and relevant to the vicinity of the observation being explained.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning Curve Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Learning Curve Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Analysis comparing training and cross-validation scores as training size grows; revealed overfitting and that more training data improves performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Validation">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Validation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Validation scoring used in learning curves; scores increased gradually with training set size.&lt;SEP&gt;Model optimization technique recommended for future work to refine accuracy and reliability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold Weight">
  <data key="d0">Mold Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Feature receiving the highest LIME coefficients across RF (0.13), DT (0.43) and SVM (0.21).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Black Box Model">
  <data key="d0">Black Box Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">LIME assumes the explained ML model is a black box whose internal mechanisms are unknown, and it produces explanations under that premise.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Validation Dataset">
  <data key="d0">Validation Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset from which values are randomly sampled to apply LIME. Sample N°4 comes from it and actually belongs to machine number 3.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Part Weight">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Part Weight</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input feature. The RF model assigns it a LIME coefficient of 0.02.&lt;SEP&gt;Weight of the plastic part (Part_Weight). LIME analysis showed that it also influences the machine allocation decision.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold Length">
  <data key="d0">Mold Length</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input feature. It has the DT model's second-highest LIME coefficient, 0.01.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Material Density">
  <data key="d0">Material Density</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input feature. The SVM model assigns it a LIME weight of 0.06.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Variance">
  <data key="d0">High Variance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Model condition shown in the learning curves alongside overfitting. Cross-validation scores are comparatively lower and rise gradually as the training set grows.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LIME Analysis">
  <data key="d0">LIME Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Interpretability analysis that showed mold mechanical dimensions and plastic part weight as the most influential features for machine allocation predictions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precision, Accuracy, Recall and F1-Score">
  <data key="d0">Precision, Accuracy, Recall and F1-Score</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation metrics used to assess the performance of each model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Production Workshop Dataset">
  <data key="d0">Production Workshop Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Dataset gathered from an actual production workshop. It covers 70 different parts, five categories of machines, and 10 varieties of plastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Plastic Parts Validation Set">
  <data key="d0">New Plastic Parts Validation Set</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Two new plastic parts (Part_1 and Part_2) obtained from another workshop. They are described by ten features, including mold dimensions, weights, and material properties, and were used to validate the trained models.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decision Support">
  <data key="d0">Decision Support</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Use of ML model outputs as valuable indications and supportive information for decisions on machine selection.&lt;SEP&gt;Downstream application targeted for future evaluation of the framework's impact, alongside predictive maintenance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mold Mechanical Dimensions">
  <data key="d0">Mold Mechanical Dimensions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mold features (Mold_Width, Mold_Length, Mold_Height). LIME analysis confirmed that they play the most significant role in the models' machine allocation predictions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Class Probability">
  <data key="d0">Class Probability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Probability each algorithm computes for each machine class from feature values and feature weights. The class with the highest probability is predicted. Values differ across algorithms because each algorithm computes different feature weights.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machine Classes 2, 3 and 4">
  <data key="d0">Machine Classes 2, 3 and 4</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mid-size machine classes that all models consistently predicted for the new parts. The models systematically excluded large and small machines, which the paper views as a reasonable initial approximation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Random Search">
  <data key="d0">Random Search</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperparameter optimization algorithm cited as future work; it was not used in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machine Selection In Injection Molding">
  <data key="d0">Machine Selection In Injection Molding</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The challenging task of classifying the appropriate injection molding machine for a given product.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Other studies report high Random Forest performance metrics">
  <data key="d0">Other studies report high Random Forest performance metrics</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "For instance, other studies report RF metrics like an accuracy of 99.88%, recall of 99.88%, precision of 99.93%, and F1 score of 99.88% [32]; and precision of 95.86, recall of 95.71, and F1 score of 95.73 [33]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Grid search and random search can optimize hyperparameters">
  <data key="d0">Grid search and random search can optimize hyperparameters</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "The hyper parameters were not fine-tuned using optimization algorithms such as grid search or random search [20], [21]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decision-Making Process">
  <data key="d0">Decision-Making Process</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of choosing machines in a real production workshop. The ML models support it by predicting outcomes for unseen parts and by helping eliminate machines with oversized or undersized capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automotive Sector">
  <data key="d0">Automotive Sector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Industrial sector of the real-world company from which the injection molding dataset was collected.&lt;SEP&gt;Industrial sector in which the authors suggest adding specific criteria, such as environmental impact, to the evaluation framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-690b1785f1cbb35b37a4dcdbae1984e4-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;IJE_Volume38_Issue2_Pages488-502_Versionfinale.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791426457</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RF, DT and SVM outperform SGD, LR and NB for machine selection">
  <data key="d0">RF, DT and SVM outperform SGD, LR and NB for machine selection</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding in the text: "The RF, DT, and SVM models exhibited favorable performance compared to SGD, LR, and NB. Among these, the RF model demonstrated the highest performance, achieving an accuracy of 93% and an F1 score of 95%."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small dataset size causes underfitting in SGD, LR and NB models">
  <data key="d0">Small dataset size causes underfitting in SGD, LR and NB models</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding in the text: "The dataset's size significantly influences the training of models, leading to the under fitting phenomenon observed in SGD, logistic regression, and NB models."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unseen Test Parts">
  <data key="d0">Unseen Test Parts</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Two new parts used to simulate predictions on unseen data. Both have similar characteristics and are assigned to machines of 250 t and 320 t. Testing is limited to these two parts, which is a stated technical limitation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equipment selection based on the AHP and Yager's method">
  <data key="d0">Equipment selection based on the AHP and Yager's method</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [1]. Written by M. Yavuz and published in 2015 in the Journal of the Southern African Institute of Mining and Metallurgy, vol. 115, no. 5, pp. 425-433. It covers equipment selection using AHP and Yager's method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection molding manufacturing process: review of case-based reasoning applications">
  <data key="d0">Injection molding manufacturing process: review of case-based reasoning applications</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [2]. Written by M. R. Khosravani and S. Nasiri and published in April 2020 in the Journal of Intelligent Manufacturing, vol. 31, no. 4, pp. 847-864. It reviews case-based reasoning applications in injection molding.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design of combined AHP-TOPSIS model for optimizing the selection of injection molding machines">
  <data key="d0">Design of combined AHP-TOPSIS model for optimizing the selection of injection molding machines</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [3]. Written by F. Tayalati, I. Boukrouh, L. Bouhsaien, A. Azmani and M. Azmani and published in 2024 in the International Journal of Engineering &amp; Technology. It proposes a combined AHP-TOPSIS model for selecting injection molding machines.&lt;SEP&gt;Reference [2]. Tayalati, Boukrouh, Bouhsaien, Azmani and Azmani, International Journal of Engineering, 2024; proposes a combined AHP-TOPSIS model for selecting injection molding machines.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467113</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Criteria in AHP: a systematic review of literature">
  <data key="d0">Criteria in AHP: a systematic review of literature</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [4]. Written by R. D. F. S. M. Russo and R. Camanho and published in 2015 in Procedia Computer Science, vol. 55, pp. 1123-1132. It is a systematic literature review of criteria used in AHP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Evaluation of machine selection by the AHP method">
  <data key="d0">Evaluation of machine selection by the AHP method</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [5]. Written by Z.-C. Lin and C.-B. Yang and published in February 1996 in the Journal of Materials Processing Technology, vol. 57, no. 3-4, pp. 253-258. It evaluates machine selection using the AHP method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application of TOPSIS and PIV methods for multi-criteria decision making in hard turning process">
  <data key="d0">Application of TOPSIS and PIV methods for multi-criteria decision making in hard turning process</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [6]. Written by T. Do and published in December 2021 in the Journal of Machine Engineering, vol. 21, no. 4, pp. 57-71. It applies TOPSIS and PIV multi-criteria decision making methods to hard turning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="How to handling with uncertain data in the TOPSIS technique?">
  <data key="d0">How to handling with uncertain data in the TOPSIS technique?</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [7]. Written by J. Więckowski and W. Sałabun and published in 2020 in Procedia Computer Science, vol. 176, pp. 2232-2242. It addresses handling uncertain data in the TOPSIS technique.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-467b87e0a1eaa0377cb70477245debc3-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">document.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425067</data>
