<node id="Permissioned Blockchain">
  <data key="d0">Permissioned Blockchain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Permissioned Blockchain is a blockchain architecture that restricts participation to authorized parties and operates with controlled membership. Participants receive secure, permissioned access to a shared ledger, which supports verifiable transactions and immutable data records. Enterprises prefer these frameworks because they offer privacy, governance, and regulatory compliance. Permissioned blockchains serve as enterprise infrastructure that requires shared governance, auditability, access control, and tamper-evident transaction records.

In permissioned blockchains, digital signatures do more than secure transactions. They also support participant authentication, access control, endorsement collection, and policy enforcement. This expanded role of cryptographic signatures is a defining characteristic of the permissioned setting.

Hyperledger Fabric is the most prominent example of a permissioned blockchain. Restricted participation is cited as one of its key features, and reference [11] presents it as a distributed operating system for permissioned blockchains. In the associated research, permissioned blockchains exemplified by Hyperledger Fabric are the target of an incremental post-quantum migration. The paper connects performance analysis of these enterprise blockchain systems with post-quantum cryptographic benchmarking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26549e3ff493405b55b221c1d804516e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26549e3ff493405b55b221c1d804516e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;poster_nihad.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raft">
  <data key="d0">Raft</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Consensus mechanism selectable in Hyperledger Fabric's pluggable consensus architecture.&lt;SEP&gt;Consensus mechanism supported by Hyperledger Fabric.&lt;SEP&gt;Consensus protocol used by the three orderers in the evaluated Fabric network.&lt;SEP&gt;Consensus algorithm underlying Fabric ordering services. It is not a post-quantum mechanism but can be affected indirectly by larger payloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
