  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CouchDB">
  <data key="d0">CouchDB</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">CouchDB is the state database used by the peers in Hyperledger Fabric, where it typically stores the ledger's world state. It offers rich query capabilities along with efficient storage and retrieval of transaction states, which makes it well suited to Fabric deployments that need more than simple key-value lookups.

In the evaluated Fabric network, each peer maintains its own CouchDB instance. These instances store the world-state records for Document, HybridSignature, and PQCKeyRecord entries, which hold the application's documents, hybrid signatures, and post-quantum cryptographic key records.

In the Fabric testbed, the CouchDB instances run as Docker containers. They are treated as Fabric components whose resource consumption is monitored during the evaluation, so their performance overhead is measured alongside the other parts of the network.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaincode">
  <data key="d0">Chaincode</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode refers to smart contracts in Hyperledger Fabric that automate business logic. During Fabric's execution phase, each endorsing peer runs the chaincode independently against its local copy of the world state and produces a read-write set. In the experiments described, the chaincode has deterministic execution and a uniform transaction size, which keeps performance measurements consistent. One simple deployment is on the tokenchannel, where the chaincode manages token transactions through the Mint operation.

The paper also proposes a chaincode for document management on Hyperledger Fabric. It handles document metadata and signature collections and exposes six principal functions, including CreateDocument, RegisterPQCKey, RevokePQCKey, GetPQCKeyRecord and SignDocument. Through these functions it manages the lifecycle of post-quantum cryptographic (PQC) keys and the signing of documents.

A central security role of this chaincode is enforcing the binding between a participant's MSP identity and their registered PQC key. When a signing transaction is submitted, the chaincode checks whether the ML-DSA-65 public key it contains matches the active key registered for that identity. The chaincode does not perform ML-DSA-65 signature verification itself during transaction processing. Its role is limited to key-matching and binding enforcement, and verification of the actual post-quantum signatures happens outside the chaincode.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Channel Model">
  <data key="d0">Channel Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric mechanism isolating transaction data between organizations to ensure privacy.&lt;SEP&gt;The model of channels in Hyperledger Fabric, explained in the documentation reference [17].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-021</data>
