<node id="Peers, endorsement policies and consensus drive Fabric latency, throughput and scalability">
  <data key="d0">Peers, endorsement policies and consensus drive Fabric latency, throughput and scalability</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Factors such as the number of peers, endorsement policies, and consensus mechanisms play a key role in latency, throughput, and scalability [19].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency rises with peers and orderers while throughput depends on endorsement policy and block size">
  <data key="d0">Latency rises with peers and orderers while throughput depends on endorsement policy and block size</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">For example, studies have shown that network latency increases with the number of peers and orderers, while throughput can be significantly impacted by endorsement policy and block size settings [21], [22].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric latency and throughput are highly sensitive to endorsing peers, batch size and batch timeout">
  <data key="d0">Fabric latency and throughput are highly sensitive to endorsing peers, batch size and batch timeout</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In particular, Shalaby et al. demonstrated that latency and throughput in Hyperledger Fabric are highly sensitive to changes in these parameters... [23], [24].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Choice of consensus drastically affects latency and scalability">
  <data key="d0">Choice of consensus drastically affects latency and scalability</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Moreover, several studies have examined the impact of consensus mechanisms on system performance, illustrating that the choice of consensus can drastically affect both latency and scalability [25], [26].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recent work increasingly addresses Fabric performance metrics">
  <data key="d0">Recent work increasingly addresses Fabric performance metrics</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Early research primarily focused on the security and functionality of the platform, but recent work has increasingly addressed key performance metrics such as latency, throughput, and scalability, which are critical for large-scale enterprise use cases [20].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Comprehensive research on combined parameter effects in large-scale Fabric networks is lacking">
  <data key="d0">Comprehensive research on combined parameter effects in large-scale Fabric networks is lacking</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">As a result, there remains a lack of comprehensive research that evaluates the combined effect of multiple parameters on latency and throughput in large-scale, multi-peer environments [8], [24].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prior Fabric research is limited by small-scale simulations">
  <data key="d0">Prior Fabric research is limited by small-scale simulations</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Research has also been limited by small-scale simulations that do not fully capture the complexities of enterprise-level blockchain networks [25], [26].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prior studies analyzed endorsement policy, block size and timeout effects but lack large multi-peer analysis">
  <data key="d0">Prior studies analyzed endorsement policy, block size and timeout effects but lack large multi-peer analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">For example, studies have analyzed the impact of endorsement policies, block size, and timeout settings on performance [21], [22], but comprehensive analysis in larger, multi-peer networks is lacking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425158</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Parameter Configuration Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Parameter Configuration Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Systematic evaluation of the combined effects of multiple Hyperledger Fabric configuration parameters (endorsement policies, block sizes, timeouts, peer counts) on latency and throughput; the research gap This Paper fills.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001</data>
