<node id="10.1109/access.2026.3677633">
  <data key="d0">10.1109/access.2026.3677633</data>
  <data key="d1">identifier</data>
  <data key="d2">10.1109/access.2026.3677633 is the Digital Object Identifier (DOI) assigned to the article being read, a research paper published in IEEE Access. The DOI is listed in the paper's citation information. It serves as the persistent identifier for this specific article, referred to as "This Paper," after its acceptance in the journal.

The paper identified by 10.1109/access.2026.3677633 presents a configuration-driven performance analysis and evaluation of Hyperledger Fabric, the permissioned blockchain platform. The DOI therefore links directly to this Hyperledger Fabric performance evaluation study in IEEE Access and allows it to be cited and retrieved unambiguously.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425162</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperledger Fabric">
  <data key="d0">Hyperledger Fabric</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric is a permissioned, enterprise-grade blockchain framework developed under the Linux Foundation. It is one of the most widely adopted platforms for enterprise applications and is often described as a distributed operating system for permissioned blockchains. Its modular architecture includes pluggable consensus, membership-based identity through Membership Service Providers (MSPs), configurable endorsement policies, a configurable ordering service, private data exchange, fine-grained identity management, and chaincode (smart contract) support. Its core components are peers, orderers, channels, and a state database. Fabric follows an execute-order-validate architecture that separates execution, ordering, and validation. Its transaction pipeline covers endorsement collection, ordering (typically Raft consensus in version 2.5), block formation and propagation, validation, and ledger commit.

Cryptography is built into the platform at many points: X.509 certificates, MSP identities, the BCCSP, endorsement signatures, transaction envelopes, TLS channels, ordering services, and block validation. ECDSA is its native signature scheme and is used for identity authentication, endorsement signing, and transaction validation. Ledger blocks are serialized as protobuf-encoded files. They contain endorsement signatures, MSP credentials, transaction metadata, block headers, and read-write sets.

One empirical study used Fabric version 2.4 because it offers a robust and stable network. The study benchmarked latency and throughput while varying endorsement policies, ordering and batching settings (block size, batching timeout), and the number of peers per organization. It found good performance with one to four peers per organization. Beyond four peers, scalability problems appeared, with substantially higher latency and lower throughput. Many related works cover Fabric's endorsement policies, channel model, consensus, ordering services, transaction flow, performance, scalability, and post-quantum security. Fabric has also served as the foundation for a vehicle data management network.

Fabric is the target platform for research on hybrid post-quantum signatures, which are integrated at the application layer. The evaluated deployment used version 2.5.13 on a single host over a Docker bridge network. It had two organizations, four peers, three Raft orderers, a MAJORITY endorsement policy under which both organizations endorse transactions, CouchDB state databases, TLS and service discovery, and a Go chaincode deployed via Chaincode-as-a-Service. The hybrid scheme leaves Fabric's native cryptography unchanged, so MSP/X.509 identities, ECDSA endorsements, TLS, BCCSP, block metadata, and the ordering service all remain classical. The ledger stores ML-DSA-65 signature and public-key artifacts. The original Fabric baseline was used to quantify total block expansion when the post-quantum layer was added.

Benchmarks showed typical pipeline latency of 0.5–6 s. Under sustained 50 TPS submission, the network saturated at roughly 33–36 TPS, consistent with its ordering and commit capacity. CPU and memory monitoring of peers (e.g., peer1-org1), orderers, and chaincode containers showed memory differences below 2% between the ECDSA and hybrid PQC configurations. No statistically significant Fabric-level performance separation was found among ML-DSA parameter sets. Adopting hybrid ML-DSA-65 signatures required no changes to performance tuning, workload configuration, or hardware provisioning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26549e3ff493405b55b221c1d804516e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-26549e3ff493405b55b221c1d804516e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-02