  <data key="d0">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License is an open-access license that governs how scholarly works may be shared and reused. Its full terms are available at https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. In the provided records, it is the license under which at least two research articles are published, distributed and released.

The first is "Intelligent Predictive Maintenance Using Fuzzy Bayesian Networks for Critical IT Components," which applies Fuzzy Bayesian Networks to the predictive maintenance of critical IT components. The license covers this paper as accepted in and published by IEEE Access. The licensing terms appear in the article's footer and in the IEEE Access author's version notice, both of which confirm that the paper is distributed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 terms.

The second is "Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric." Multiple descriptions consistently identify the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License as the open license under which this article is published, released and distributed. This is stated explicitly in the article's licensing note, which also references the same license URL.

More generally, the license is described as the open license applied to articles accepted in IEEE Access, with further information available at the Creative Commons website. Across all descriptions, it serves as the legal framework enabling open dissemination and reuse of these works, provided the original authors receive appropriate attribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raft Consensus Protocol">
  <data key="d0">Raft Consensus Protocol</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Consensus protocol run by the three orderers. It guarantees fault tolerance and strong consistency across the system.&lt;SEP&gt;Consensus protocol typically used by the ordering service in Fabric 2.5, cited as [32].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric SDK">
  <data key="d0">Fabric SDK</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Software development kit used with custom scripts to submit transactions during benchmarking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mint Operation">
  <data key="d0">Mint Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode operation that creates new tokens and updates the ledger state. It is used as the benchmark transaction type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Optimization">
  <data key="d0">Performance Optimization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tuning of Hyperledger Fabric configuration parameters for high performance in enterprise deployments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Campaign C1">
  <data key="d0">Campaign C1</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">First test campaign, 'Impact of Endorsement Policy (Baseline)'. It compares the OR and AND endorsement policies using a baseline of 1 peer per organization, BLOCK=100 and TIMEOUT=100 ms, to assess their impact on latency and throughput.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Campaign C2">
  <data key="d0">Campaign C2</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Second test campaign, 'Tuning Block Size and Timeout'. It uses 4 peers per organization and the OR endorsement policy, and systematically varies block size (50, 100, 200) and timeout (50 ms, 100 ms, 200 ms).&lt;SEP&gt;Experimental campaign investigating the impact of block size (50, 100, 200 transactions per block) and batch timeout (50, 100, 200 ms) using 4 peers per organization under the OR endorsement policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Experimental evaluation campaign in This Paper that varies block size and timeout, showing block size = 100 and timeout = 50 ms is the best configuration for the setup.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peers Per Organization">
  <data key="d0">Peers Per Organization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The PEERS_PER_ORG parameter: the number of peers per organization, varied across 1, 2, 4, 6 and 8. The peers validate transactions and maintain the ledger state.&lt;SEP&gt;Network configuration parameter giving the number of peer nodes in each organization. It is set to 1 in C1 and 4 in C2, and varied across 1, 2, 4, 6, and 8 in C3.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperledger Fabric network parameter varied across 1, 2, 4, 6 and 8 in experiment C3. Increasing the parameter to 6–8 caused significant latency growth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Org1">
  <data key="d0">Org1</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the two organizations in the studied Hyperledger Fabric network. It hosts a varying number of peers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Org2">
  <data key="d0">Org2</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the two organizations in the studied Hyperledger Fabric network. It hosts a varying number of peers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tokenchannel">
  <data key="d0">Tokenchannel</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The single channel, named tokenchannel, used for communication between the peers of the two organizations.&lt;SEP&gt;Single application channel used for all transactions in the Fabric network.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Custom Benchmark Scripts">
  <data key="d0">Custom Benchmark Scripts</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Custom scripts that work with the Fabric SDK. They initiate Mint transactions, configure the test environment and collect performance metrics consistently across test configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Benchmark Workload Procedure">
  <data key="d0">Benchmark Workload Procedure</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Each test runs 300 transactions. A 5-minute warm-up period precedes the test and a 120-second waiting period follows it, so all transactions are validated before metrics are recorded.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C1 Impact of OR vs AND Policy">
  <data key="d0">C1 Impact of OR vs AND Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Baseline test campaign comparing the OR and AND endorsement policies, with 1 peer per organization, block size 100, and timeout 100 ms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C2 Tuning Block Size and Timeout">
  <data key="d0">C2 Tuning Block Size and Timeout</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Test campaign using 4 peers per organization and the OR policy. It varies block size (50, 100, 200) and timeout (50, 100, 200 ms) to find the configuration that minimizes latency and maximizes throughput.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C3 Scalability with Number of Peers">
  <data key="d0">C3 Scalability with Number of Peers</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Test campaign evaluating network scalability with 1, 2, 4, 6, and 8 peers per organization under the OR and AND policies, with block size 100 and timeout 100 ms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Performance vs Endorsement Strictness Trade-off">
  <data key="d0">Performance vs Endorsement Strictness Trade-off</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding that OR suits deployments prioritizing responsiveness and throughput, while AND suits applications needing broader endorsement coverage at the cost of higher latency and lower throughput.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AND policy nearly doubles median latency and halves throughput">
  <data key="d0">AND policy nearly doubles median latency and halves throughput</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Table 2 consolidates these outcomes ... confirming that AND nearly doubles median latency and halves throughput in the baseline."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency Percentiles (P50, P95, P99)">
  <data key="d0">Latency Percentiles (P50, P95, P99)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric used to report transaction latency at the 50th, 95th, and 99th percentiles. Table 2 summarizes the median latency percentiles, the P50 range across runs, and throughput.&lt;SEP&gt;Latency metrics reported in milliseconds; configuration (100, 50 ms) yields P50 = 619 ms, P95 = 731 ms and P99 = 765 ms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsement policy strongly affects blockchain performance">
  <data key="d0">Endorsement policy strongly affects blockchain performance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The baseline campaign (C1) shows that the endorsement policy strongly affects blockchain performance under identical settings (BLOCK = 100, TIMEOUT = 100 ms, 1 peer/org)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Size and Timeout Jointly Drive Latency and Throughput">
  <data key="d0">Block Size and Timeout Jointly Drive Latency and Throughput</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The results show that block size and timeout jointly drive latency and throughput, and that selecting a balanced configuration is necessary to avoid both excessive waiting and inefficient batching."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OR Policy Halves Latency and Doubles Throughput Versus AND Policy">
  <data key="d0">OR Policy Halves Latency and Doubles Throughput Versus AND Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Result from Table 2: the OR policy achieved P50 latency of 518 ms and 1.8 tx/s versus 1062 ms and 0.9 tx/s for the AND policy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Balanced Block Configuration Avoids Excessive Waiting and Inefficient Batching">
  <data key="d0">Balanced Block Configuration Avoids Excessive Waiting and Inefficient Batching</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The results show that block size and timeout jointly drive latency and throughput, and that selecting a balanced configuration is necessary to avoid both excessive waiting and inefficient batching."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Size 100 and Timeout 50 ms Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Block Size 100 and Timeout 50 ms Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Configuration from Table 3 with block size 100 and batch timeout 50 ms. It gives the lowest P50 latency (619 ms, std 15.6), P95 of 731 ms, P99 of 765 ms, throughput of 448.5 tx/s and efficiency of 1.6.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peers per Organization">
  <data key="d0">Peers per Organization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Number of peers deployed per organization. It was set to 4 in campaign C2.&lt;SEP&gt;Number of peers in each organization of the Hyperledger Fabric network, varied from 1 to 8 in the experiments. Performance stays stable for 1–4 peers per organization and degrades for 6–8, with substantial latency increases and reduced throughput.&lt;SEP&gt;Network scale parameter; 1–4 peers per organization is optimal for low latency and high throughput, with scalability challenges beyond this threshold, particularly under the AND policy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Efficiency Metric">
  <data key="d0">Efficiency Metric</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Efficiency (Eff.) column reported in Table 3 for each block size and timeout configuration. Values range from 1.6 to 8.8.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Timeout">
  <data key="d0">Timeout</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Configuration parameter (T), in milliseconds, varied at 50, 100 and 200 ms; an optimum is found around 50 ms.&lt;SEP&gt;An experimental parameter, measured in milliseconds (labelled "Timeout (ms)"), on a figure axis in the paper. The figure evaluates it at values of 50, 100 and 200 ms.&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration parameter evaluated in This Paper; timeout has a strong negative effect when combined with larger blocks, with 50 ms being optimal.&lt;SEP&gt;Timeout parameter measured in milliseconds and varied from about 40 to 200 ms. Specific settings T=50ms and T=100ms are compared.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Efficiency (P95/Throughput)">
  <data key="d0">Efficiency (P95/Throughput)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Efficiency metric defined as the ratio of P95 latency to throughput, used to compare configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Size 100 and Timeout 50 ms Is the Optimal Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Block Size 100 and Timeout 50 ms Is the Optimal Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental finding stated in the text: "Fig. 4 highlights a clear optimum around block size = 100 and timeout = 50 ms. This setting achieves the best throughput and the lowest latencies simultaneously. According to Table 3, the configuration (100, 50 ms) yields P50 = 619 ms (±15.6), P95 = 731 ms (±6.4), P99 = 765 ms, and a throughput of 448.50 tx/s (±11.55). This makes it the most efficient operating point among all tested configurations."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Timeout">
  <data key="d0">Block Timeout</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Time to wait before cutting a block. Values of 50 ms and 200 ms are evaluated; longer timeouts delay block cutting under lower or variable arrival rates and amplify delay when combined with larger blocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Batch timeout configuration parameter analyzed against block size; optimal value found was 50 ms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425167</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsement Policy">
  <data key="d0">Endorsement Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Endorsement Policy is a rule in Hyperledger Fabric that determines which peers, or which organizations, must endorse a transaction for it to be validated. It governs transaction validation in Fabric networks and is exposed as a configuration parameter, referred to in the study as POLICY_KIND. The setting has a strong influence on blockchain performance, particularly latency and throughput.

The study tested two variants of the Endorsement Policy. In the OR configuration, at least one peer from each organization must endorse a transaction. In the AND configuration, all peers from each organization must endorse it. These variants are compared throughout the paper, including in the scalability analysis presented in Figure 8. The OR policy consistently outperformed the AND policy in both latency and throughput.

The Endorsement Policy emerged as a dominant driver of performance across all baseline conditions examined. It accounts for a large share of the overhead on the critical path of transaction processing, which makes it one of the most consequential configuration choices for Hyperledger Fabric deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric policy governing transaction endorsement. OR policies generally provide lower latency and higher throughput than stricter AND policies.&lt;SEP&gt;Channel policy determining which endorsing peers execute and sign transaction proposals; kept consistent across experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric policy against which endorsement signatures are verified during the validation phase.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric policy that multi-organization transactions must satisfy; peers execute authorization and key-registry checks during endorsement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="P50 Latency">
  <data key="d0">P50 Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Median transaction latency metric in milliseconds, e.g. 1452 ms at (200, 200 ms).&lt;SEP&gt;Median latency metric measured in milliseconds. Plotted values range from about 600 to 1400 ms against throughput.&lt;SEP&gt;Median (50th percentile) latency metric used in the C3 scalability experiment. Under the OR policy it ranged from 498 ms to 522 ms with 1–4 peers per organization. It was reported as 1091 ms with 4 peers per organization and jumped to 4470 ms at 8 peers per organization.&lt;SEP&gt;Median latency percentile metric used to evaluate Hyperledger Fabric; it reaches 1141 ms with 6 peers per organization under the AND policy.&lt;SEP&gt;The median (50th percentile) transaction latency in milliseconds. It is the metric plotted in Figure 10(a) and reported in Table 5, which also gives a P50 range.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Efficiency Ratio">
  <data key="d0">Efficiency Ratio</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Ratio of P95 latency to throughput (P95/Throughput). The best configuration achieves 1.6, while (100, 200 ms) degrades to 15.3 and (200, 200 ms) to 13.6.&lt;SEP&gt;Metric used in This Paper; the best configuration achieves 1.6, while (100, 200 ms) degrades to 15.3 and (200, 200 ms) to 13.6.&lt;SEP&gt;Efficiency metric reported in the Optimal Configurations Summary (Table 4). The best efficiency ratio of 1.6 was achieved with block size 100 and a 50 ms timeout.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ordering/Validation Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Ordering/Validation Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Overhead incurred when the system commits more blocks for the same workload, which increases when blocks are small.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Size 100 With 50 ms Timeout Is The Optimal Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Block Size 100 With 50 ms Timeout Is The Optimal Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding reported in the text: "Fig. 6 further shows that (100, 50 ms) dominates other configurations: it provides the lowest latency for the highest throughput, which is also reflected in the efficiency ratio (P95/Throughput)." The best configuration achieves an efficiency ratio of 1.6.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small Blocks Increase Ordering/Validation Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Small Blocks Increase Ordering/Validation Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding reported in the text: "at (50, 50 ms), latency increases (P95 = 1532 ms) and throughput drops to 206.18 tx/s, indicating that smaller blocks increase ordering/validation overhead because the system commits more blocks for the same workload." Increasing timeout at block size 50 does not compensate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Larger Blocks With High Timeout Amplify Delay">
  <data key="d0">Larger Blocks With High Timeout Amplify Delay</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding reported in the text: "With (200, 200 ms), latency rises sharply (P95 = 2336 ms, P50 = 1452 ms) while throughput remains limited (171.19 tx/s)." Larger batches require more validation time and longer timeouts delay block cutting under lower or variable arrival rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Cutting">
  <data key="d0">Block Cutting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The process of finalizing a batch of transactions into a block; longer timeouts delay block cutting under lower or variable arrival rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Arrival Rate">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Arrival Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rate at which transactions arrive at the system; under lower or variable arrival rates, longer timeouts delay block cutting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 3">
  <data key="d0">Table 3</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Table in the paper reporting efficiency ratios: 1.6 for the best configuration (100, 50 ms), 15.3 for (100, 200 ms) and 13.6 for (200, 200 ms).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 7">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 7</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure in This Paper plotting block size and timeout (ms) against P95 latency (ms). It shows that timeout has a strong negative effect when combined with larger blocks, while block size = 100 tolerates modest timeout changes without severe throughput collapse.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
