</node>
<node id="Coordination Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Coordination Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Overhead from peers needing to validate transactions. It increases with more peers and under the AND policy, which limits the system's ability to process transactions at a high rate.&lt;SEP&gt;Overhead from endorsement, dissemination and validation work that increases with peers and endorsement set size, causing performance gains to saturate or reverse.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="P50, P95 and P99 Latency Percentiles">
  <data key="d0">P50, P95 and P99 Latency Percentiles</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Latency percentiles (P50, P95, P99) plotted against the number of peers per organization in the paper's figures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Number of Peers per Organization">
  <data key="d0">Number of Peers per Organization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental variable (peer configuration) ranging from 1 to 8 peers per organization, used to study Hyperledger Fabric performance.&lt;SEP&gt;The experimental variable in the scalability analysis, tested at values such as 4, 6 and 8 peers per organization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperledger Fabric performs well with up to 4 peers per organization">
  <data key="d0">Hyperledger Fabric performs well with up to 4 peers per organization</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "These results highlight that Hyperledger Fabric performs well with 1–4 peers per organization, but scalability issues emerge as the number of peers increases beyond this point. The network can handle up to 4 peers per organization without significant performance degradation."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scaling beyond 4 peers degrades Fabric performance">
  <data key="d0">Scaling beyond 4 peers degrades Fabric performance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "However, scaling beyond 4 peers introduces substantial latency and reduces" throughput.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scalability Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Scalability Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental evaluation shown in Figure 8. It measures how latency and throughput change as the number of peers per organization increases, under OR and AND endorsement policies.&lt;SEP&gt;Experimental analysis of throughput and latency versus number of peers per organization for OR and AND endorsement policies; finds 1–4 peers per organization optimal.&lt;SEP&gt;An experimental procedure in the paper that measures performance with a varying number of peers per organization. The results appear in Table 5 and include P50, P95 and P99 latency, P50 range and throughput.&lt;SEP&gt;Experimental analysis varying the number of peers per organization (1–8) under OR and AND endorsement policies, reported in Table 5 and Figure 10.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Number Of Peers Per Organization">
  <data key="d0">Number Of Peers Per Organization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The independent variable of the scalability analysis in Figure 8. It is varied across 1, 2, 4, 6 and 8 peers per organization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Percentile Latency (P50, P95, P99)">
  <data key="d0">Percentile Latency (P50, P95, P99)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Latency percentiles (P50, P95, P99) reported in the scalability analysis to characterize the latency distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ordering Layer">
  <data key="d0">Ordering Layer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric layer where block size is configured; a block size of 100 transactions amortizes ordering and validation costs across enough transactions to sustain throughput without excessive waiting time before block cut.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsement Coordination">
  <data key="d0">Endorsement Coordination</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coordination overhead among endorsing peers on the critical path; the OR policy limits it, lowering latency and improving throughput, while the AND policy increases endorsement requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recommended Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Recommended Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Practical baseline configuration for Hyperledger Fabric identified by This Paper: OR endorsement policy when application logic allows, block size of 100 transactions, batch timeout of 50–100 ms, and 1–4 peers per organization; it reduces end-to-end confirmation time and preserves transaction capacity under typical loads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Batching Timeout">
  <data key="d0">Batching Timeout</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ordering-layer batching parameter; a 50–100 ms timeout is recommended.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peer Scaling">
  <data key="d0">Peer Scaling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scale-out of the number of peers per organization; 1–4 peers per organization offers robust performance, while additional peers increase endorsement and dissemination work, degrading latency and flattening throughput gains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency Percentiles">
  <data key="d0">Latency Percentiles</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Performance metrics P50, P95 and P99 latency used to measure end-to-end behavior of Hyperledger Fabric configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Token-Based Mint Operation">
  <data key="d0">Token-Based Mint Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Single write-intensive workload type used in the evaluation, representative of common enterprise blockchain transactions but not of read-heavy or complex multi-state workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threats To Validity">
  <data key="d0">Threats To Validity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Limitations of the study: fixed homogeneous hardware, single workload type, single deterministic chaincode with uniform transaction size, constant number of organizations, and controlled conditions excluding network faults, workload fluctuations, or adversarial behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Considerations regarding construct validity (team composition, normalization approach) and external validity (context specificity, participant characteristics) of the findings.&lt;SEP&gt;Limitations the paper acknowledges: single-host deployment, synthetic ECDSA baseline delay, limited topology, single workload type, small secondary sample sizes, and post-hoc equivalence analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Private Data Collections">
  <data key="d0">Private Data Collections</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric feature that could affect endorsement latency and ordering behavior and is not covered in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OR Endorsement Reduces Latency And Increases Throughput Compared To AND">
  <data key="d0">OR Endorsement Reduces Latency And Increases Throughput Compared To AND</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of This Paper: "The OR policy consistently reduced latency and increased throughput compared to AND, which is explained by the smaller endorsement set and the lower coordination and validation work per transaction."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1–4 Peers Per Organization Offers Robust Performance">
  <data key="d0">1–4 Peers Per Organization Offers Robust Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of This Paper: "the experiments indicate that 1–4 peers per organization offers robust performance with manageable coordination overhead; beyond this range, additional peers tend to increase endorsement and dissemination work faster than they increase useful capacity."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recommended Configuration Achieves Balanced Latency-Throughput Trade-Off">
  <data key="d0">Recommended Configuration Achieves Balanced Latency-Throughput Trade-Off</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of This Paper: "Overall, the recommended configuration-OR endorsement, 100 transactions/block, 50–100 ms timeout, and 1–4 peers per organization-achieves a balanced trade-off between low latency and high throughput and remains scalable for moderate organizational growth."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsement Policy Is A Dominant Driver Of Performance">
  <data key="d0">Endorsement Policy Is A Dominant Driver Of Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of This Paper: "Across all baseline conditions, endorsement policy emerged as a dominant driver of performance."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Heterogeneous Hardware Environments">
  <data key="d0">Heterogeneous Hardware Environments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hardware or cloud-based deployments with resource contention and virtualization overhead. The study did not cover them because it used homogeneous nodes, and future work will extend the analysis to them.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Channel Interactions">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Channel Interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric network feature that could affect endorsement latency and ordering behavior. The study does not cover it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enterprise Blockchain Applications">
  <data key="d0">Enterprise Blockchain Applications</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application domain whose common write-intensive transactions are represented by the token-based Mint workload.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Organization Scaling">
  <data key="d0">Organization Scaling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scaling the number of organizations in the network. The study kept the number of organizations constant, so it does not evaluate this effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Size And Batch Timeout Tuning">
  <data key="d0">Block Size And Batch Timeout Tuning</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Tuning of parameters in the ordering and batching layer. A block size of 100 transactions combined with a 50–100 ms timeout achieves strong throughput without excessive queuing delays before block cut.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency-Throughput Trade-Off">
  <data key="d0">Latency-Throughput Trade-Off</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Trade-off between transaction latency and throughput that is materially changed by ordering and batching parameter tuning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peer Scale-Out Saturation">
  <data key="d0">Peer Scale-Out Saturation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Phenomenon in which Fabric maintains stable performance up to roughly 2–4 peers per organization. Beyond that range, additional peers raise tail latency and depress throughput, especially under stricter endorsement requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ordering Service">
  <data key="d0">Ordering Service</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric ordering layer. The paper proposes studying it further under alternative consensus configurations, fault models and recovery events.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperledger Fabric component that receives transaction envelopes from clients and groups them into blocks according to the MaxMessageCount and BatchTimeout batching parameters. The resulting blocks are distributed to all peers on the channel. In Fabric 2.5 it typically uses Raft.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric component whose consensus messages remain protected only by classical ECDSA in the hybrid model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adaptive Configuration Strategies">
  <data key="d0">Adaptive Configuration Strategies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed future research direction: online adjustment of endorsement selection, batching and resource allocation to maintain target latency and throughput under dynamic workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425172</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
