<node id="Block Size">
  <data key="d0">Block Size</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Block Size (also called BLOCK, BS, or batch size) is a Hyperledger Fabric configuration parameter at the ordering layer. It sets the number of transactions included in each block and is evaluated in This Paper as a key factor affecting network performance. Together with the batch timeout, it determines both throughput and latency, so it was one of the main parameters tuned in the experiments.

In the experiments, Block Size was set to 50, 100 and 200 transactions per block. It served as the independent variable on the axes of the throughput and latency plots. In the 3D performance surface analysis, it was varied over a continuous range from about 40 to 200.

The results show a clear trade-off. Small blocks (50) increase ordering and validation overhead, while large blocks (200) require more validation time. Larger blocks combined with longer timeouts also degrade efficiency. A block size of 100 is the optimum. It spreads ordering and validation costs efficiently without making transactions wait too long before a block is cut, and it serves as a robust baseline that tolerates modest changes in timeout.

The best overall configuration was a block size of 100 with a 50 ms batch timeout. This setting achieved the maximum throughput (448.5 tx/s), the minimum P95 latency (731 ms) and the best efficiency ratio (1.6). On this basis, 100 transactions per block is recommended as the operating point for balanced latency and throughput.&lt;SEP&gt;Metric (bytes): serialized size of protobuf-encoded blocks including transaction payloads, endorsement signatures and metadata.&lt;SEP&gt;Metric defined as the serialized size in bytes of protobuf-encoded blocks fetched from the ledger, reflecting total on-disk storage cost per block.</data>
