<node id="Blockchain">
  <data key="d0">Blockchain</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Blockchain is a decentralized, distributed ledger technology for storing cryptographically signed data. Transactions are grouped into blocks that are validated by miners through consensus. The blocks are linked to one another by timestamps and hashes, and the ledger is replicated at every node, which avoids a single point of failure. Its cryptographic features, unforgeability, reliability and fault tolerance make it well suited to authentication and access control in Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs). It has also been applied to authentication in VANET, FANET and IoT environments; for example, reference [9] uses blockchain as the basis of a decentralized authentication system for IoT.

Blockchain is the core concept behind the proposed decentralized authentication mechanism for MANETs, which relies on its distributed nature and cryptographic characteristics. In this mechanism, the blockchain is used to register clusters, admins and devices. It stores admin tokens, device tokens, lists of registered device nodes, cluster and device registration blocks, and hashed node credentials, along with hash values of registration and authentication transactions. Accepted transactions generate new blocks. The ledger is shared among admins via fog nodes. SHA-256 and Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) serve as cryptographic proof for data integrity and authentication, and admin nodes digitally sign and validate its transactions.

Blockchain also has notable limitations, including scalability problems, consensus delays and transaction fee costs. To mitigate these drawbacks, the proposed approach has only administrators maintain the blockchain rather than all network nodes.

Beyond this mechanism, blockchain is central to the research of Nihad Lechhab Ouadrassi. Her doctoral work addresses the secure management of digital transactions by combining blockchain with artificial intelligence.&lt;SEP&gt;Technology for storing and transmitting data in a transparent, secure, decentralized way; transactions are grouped in blocks validated by network nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Emerging decentralized technology in which every node takes part in authentication. It protects node identity privacy and data integrity, and it uses consensus algorithms such as Proof of Work and Proof of Stake.&lt;SEP&gt;Distributed ledger technology. It is applied to MANET routing, security and VANET authentication, and its advantages and disadvantages are analyzed.&lt;SEP&gt;Distributed database technology in which each node participates in authentication, ensuring node privacy and data integrity by signing transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ccc87fb659a572e9c9db17272d2f6955-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ccc87fb659a572e9c9db17272d2f6955-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ccc87fb659a572e9c9db17272d2f6955-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper (1).pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OR Endorsement Policy">
  <data key="d0">OR Endorsement Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The OR Endorsement Policy is a Hyperledger Fabric endorsement policy configuration evaluated in the paper and compared against the AND endorsement policy. It validates a transaction once the minimum endorsement condition is met and so requires a smaller endorsement set. One description defines it as a configuration in which a transaction is valid if at least one peer from each organization endorses it. That wording resembles a conjunctive requirement across organizations, so the exact semantics may depend on how the policy was configured in the study.

Across the paper's experiments, the OR Endorsement Policy consistently outperformed the AND policy in both latency and throughput. The authors attribute this to reduced coordination and validation work per transaction. They therefore recommend it whenever application logic allows, and include it in their recommended default configuration.

In campaign C1, the OR policy achieved a P50 latency of 518 ms, with P50 values ranging from 498 to 525 ms. It also recorded a P95 of 603 ms, a P99 of 638 ms, and a throughput of about 1.8 tx/s. Campaign C2 uses the OR policy as its baseline.

The C3 scalability tests, shown in panel (a) of Figure 8, were run under the OR policy to measure latency and throughput as the number of peers per organization increased:

* **1–4 peers per organization:** P50 latency ranged from 498 to 522 ms, P95 from 566 to 604 ms, and P99 from 592 to 769 ms. However, the text also reports a separate 4-peer result of P50 1091 ms, P95 1410 ms, and P99 1566 ms.
* **8 peers per organization:** performance degraded sharply, with P50 rising to 4470 ms, P95 to 13611 ms, and P99 to 27075 ms.
* **Throughput:** it was 1.9 tx/s with 1 peer, 1.8 tx/s with 2–4 peers, and fell to 0.2 tx/s at 8 peers.

Table 5 reports somewhat different figures. There, throughput was 1.8 tx/s with 1–2 peers per organization and fell to 0.6 tx/s with 8 peers, while P50 latency rose from 513 ms to 1385 ms. These differences from the C3 results, both at 4 and at 8 peers, may reflect separate experimental runs or reporting contexts. All sources agree, however, that the OR policy's performance degrades substantially as the number of peers per organization grows.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425186</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AND Endorsement Policy">
  <data key="d0">AND Endorsement Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The AND Endorsement Policy is a Hyperledger Fabric endorsement policy configuration evaluated in the paper against the OR endorsement policy. It is a stricter policy that requires endorsements from a broader set of peers: a transaction is valid only if all peers from each organization endorse it. Because it requires multiple endorsements, it consistently showed higher latency and lower throughput than the OR policy.

In configuration C1, the AND Endorsement Policy reached a median (P50) latency of 1062 ms, with P50 values ranging from 1051 to 1092 ms. Its P95 latency was 1494 ms, its P99 latency was 1602 ms, and its throughput was about 0.9 tx/s.

The policy was also used for scalability tests with 1, 2, 4, 6 and 8 peers per organization. Latency and throughput were measured for each peer configuration, and the latency results appear in panel (b) of Figure 8. Across 4, 6 and 8 peers per organization, P50 latency ranged from 1121 ms to 1431 ms. With 6 peers per organization, the policy yielded a P50 latency of 1141 ms and a P99 latency of 2163 ms. At 8 peers per organization, P99 latency reached 3095 ms.

Throughput under the AND policy was reported to fall from 0.8 tx/s to 0.5 tx/s as peers per organization increased. However, one description reports 1.4 tx/s at 8 peers per organization, so the throughput figure for that configuration is uncertain and may come from a different measurement or reporting context. In all cases, AND throughput remained lower than under the OR policy.

Because the higher endorsement requirements reduce throughput as the number of peers per organization grows, the AND Endorsement Policy is considered less suited for larger networks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425189</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency">
  <data key="d0">Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Latency is a key performance metric used to evaluate Hyperledger Fabric. It refers to the transaction processing delay, specifically the time needed to validate and commit transactions. In the study it is measured in milliseconds and reported at the P50, P95 and P99 percentiles. It is assessed alongside related measures such as end-to-end confirmation time, with P50 latency serving as a primary reference point, and is used to compare different Hyperledger Fabric configurations.

Latency rises as the network grows, increasing with the number of peers, orderers and endorsing peers. This happens because transaction endorsement requires more coordination among a larger set of peers. When latency is plotted against the number of peers per organization, it increases substantially once there are more than 4 peers per organization. The endorsement policy also matters: the AND policy, which requires endorsements from multiple parties, worsens the increase compared with the OR policy.

The scalability analysis in Figure 8 plots latency at the P50, P95 and P99 percentiles against the number of peers per organization for both the OR and AND endorsement policies, with the y-axis extending to 3000 ms. Figure 10(a), titled "P50 Latency Comparison," focuses on P50 latency for the OR and AND policies at 4, 6 and 8 peers per organization, and also reports a P50 range. The plotted values include approximately 1091, 1121, 1141, 1153, 1385 and 1431 ms. These values show that latency climbs as peer counts grow, and that the AND policy generally produces higher delays than the OR policy.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance metric recorded during the load test; lower for the manual implementation but reflecting fewer processing layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Scalability-under-load metric in ms: 15.21 manual raw, 75.56 normalized, 1,828.38 FSMicroGenerator.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance metric reported by Caliper.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="P95 Latency">
  <data key="d0">P95 Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">P95 Latency is a performance metric that captures the 95th-percentile transaction latency, measured in milliseconds. It is used in This Paper alongside the P50 and P99 latency metrics to assess network performance. It indicates the latency within which 95 percent of transactions complete, so it reflects tail-end behavior rather than the average.

Reported P95 values vary with configuration and network conditions. For example, P95 Latency was 1532 ms under a (50, 50 ms) configuration, 1690 ms under (50, 200 ms), and 2336 ms under (200, 200 ms). This shows that latency rises as both parameters increase. Another configuration recorded a relatively higher P95 of 1485 ms. Across the paper's figures, P95 Latency ranges roughly from 800 to 2400 ms.

The metric appears in several analyses. The figures plot it against throughput across different block sizes, showing the trade-off between transaction processing capacity and tail latency. In the Sensitivity and Performance Degradation Analysis (Figure 7), P95 Latency is plotted in milliseconds together with P95 degradation, expressed as a percentage, across configurations. The scalability analysis also reports P95 transaction latency values in Table 5.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-dfeceae54500e0aef047f46e66c1451b-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Configuration_Driven_Performance_Analysis_of_Hyper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425204</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="From Uncertainty to Decision: An Intelligent Predictive Maintenance Approach Leveraging Fuzzy Bayesian Networks for Critical IT Components">
  <data key="d0">From Uncertainty to Decision: An Intelligent Predictive Maintenance Approach Leveraging Fuzzy Bayesian Networks for Critical IT Components</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Article accepted for publication in IEEE Access by Boukhryss et al. It develops a hybrid predictive maintenance approach for critical IT systems. The approach combines Bayesian networks, which model causal dependencies, with fuzzy logic, which calculates conditional probabilities. The model is validated by verifying three axioms. The article then generates six degradation scenarios and performs partial and global sensitivity analyses. It applies event tree analysis to evaluate maintenance strategies for CPU, RAM, GPU and hard disk components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yousra Bouassab">
