<node id="Probabilistic graphical models with fuzzy logic enable uncertainty-aware decisions">
  <data key="d0">Probabilistic graphical models with fuzzy logic enable uncertainty-aware decisions</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Recent research addresses these limitations by integrating probabilistic graphical models with fuzzy logic (FL), providing transparent and uncertainty-aware decision-making [16].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Bayesian approaches predict supply chain and inventory risks">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Bayesian approaches predict supply chain and inventory risks</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy Bayesian approaches have been applied to predict disruption risks in warehouse planning [17], manage product unavailability in inventory systems [18], and assess delivery delay risks in supply chains [19].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contrastive pre-training reduces need for labeled failure data">
  <data key="d0">Contrastive pre-training reduces need for labeled failure data</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Recent work on semi-supervised cross-domain fault diagnosis has demonstrated that contrastive pre-training combined with annotation-efficient alignment strategies can significantly reduce the need for labeled failure data by leveraging unlabeled samples across domains [20].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian Network Inference">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Network Inference</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Probabilistic inference over Bayesian networks. The proposed framework combines it with fuzzy expert knowledge to obtain interpretable risk estimates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contrastive Pre-Training">
  <data key="d0">Contrastive Pre-Training</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Technique that, combined with annotation-efficient alignment strategies, reduces the need for labeled failure data in semi-supervised cross-domain fault diagnosis by leveraging unlabeled samples across domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Semi-Supervised Cross-Domain Fault Diagnosis">
  <data key="d0">Semi-Supervised Cross-Domain Fault Diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Research area that uses unlabeled samples across domains to diagnose faults with limited labeled failure data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Remaining Useful Life">
  <data key="d0">Remaining Useful Life</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Estimate of how long a component, such as a storage device, can keep operating. Deep learning methods including bidirectional LSTMs are used to estimate it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expert-Elicited Fuzzy Rules">
  <data key="d0">Expert-Elicited Fuzzy Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy rules derived from expert knowledge. The proposed framework uses them as a substitute for data-driven feature learning in cold-start scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scenario-Based Inference Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Scenario-Based Inference Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Analysis used to validate the model by examining inference results under various scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation procedure used to validate the knowledge-based Fuzzy Bayesian Network framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation component that analyzes Bayesian network inference under defined scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Robustness Assessment">
  <data key="d0">Robustness Assessment</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation of how robust the framework is under perturbations of its conditional probability tables (CPTs).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Precision, Recall, F1-Score and AUC">
  <data key="d0">Precision, Recall, F1-Score and AUC</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Classical supervised performance metrics. The article states they are not applicable to its framework because they require large labeled failure logs, which are rarely available in operational IT environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IT System Reliability">
  <data key="d0">IT System Reliability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reliability of IT systems modeled by the Bayesian network with color-coded nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Overall reliability of IT systems. The framework aims to improve it by identifying the critical factors that influence component degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability and stability of IT systems, shown to be strongly influenced by component degradation, cooling efficiency and administrator intervention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data-driven PdM models struggle with scarce data, uncertainty and interpretability">
  <data key="d0">Data-driven PdM models struggle with scarce data, uncertainty and interpretability</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Limitation stated by the authors: "Most models require large amounts of historical data, which may be incomplete or unavailable for critical components. They also struggle to handle uncertainty, noise, and missing information... deep learning models often lack interpretability... most approaches fail to capture complex causal interactions among interdependent components."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425274</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Variable Elimination">
  <data key="d0">Variable Elimination</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Efficient inference method enabled by Bayesian Network independence relations.&lt;SEP&gt;Exact Bayesian inference algorithm used by the GeNIe Academic engine to propagate evidence through the DAG.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Belief Propagation">
  <data key="d0">Belief Propagation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Efficient inference method enabled by Bayesian Network independence relations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GeNIe Academic">
  <data key="d0">GeNIe Academic</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Software used to create the Bayesian Network modeling factors influencing IT system reliability.&lt;SEP&gt;Inference engine that performed exact Bayesian inference via variable elimination using pre-calibrated CPTs.&lt;SEP&gt;Software by BayesFusion LLC whose built-in sensitivity module was used for the analysis; varies parameters within ±10% for global sensitivity.&lt;SEP&gt;Software tool (v4.0) used for Bayesian network modeling and exact inference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IT System Failure Risk">
  <data key="d0">IT System Failure Risk</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Risk of failures in critical IT system components, the target of the proposed PdM model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref [21]">
  <data key="d0">Ref [21]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited work (in-text [21]) related to model validation via structural/probabilistic validation, scenario-based inference and robustness under CPT uncertainty.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [21] for the statement that the cushion prevents short shots and reduces the risk of incomplete parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited work ([21]) on chatbots' uses and their reshaping of human-technology interaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [21] in support of chatbots reshaping human-technology interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Work spotlighting different types of Intrusion Detection Systems such as Host IDS, Application-based IDS and Network IDS.&lt;SEP&gt;Paper cited as [21] regarding the difference between the Mamdani and Sugeno inference methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1920bb99aa33c3c871f9dc257956e3ca-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid_Approach_Integrating_Deep_Learning-Autoencoder_With_Statistical_Process_Control_Chart_for_Anomaly_Detection_Case_Study_in_Injection_Molding_Process1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_113-Accurate_AI_Assistance_in_Contract_Law1.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501545</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FBN model validated through structural, probabilistic, scenario and robustness analyses">
  <data key="d0">FBN model validated through structural, probabilistic, scenario and robustness analyses</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "...validated through structural and probabilistic validation (three-axiom verification and monotonicity tests), scenario-based inference analysis, and robustness assessment under CPT uncertainty [21]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian Networks combine probability theory with graph theory for reasoning under uncertainty">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Networks combine probability theory with graph theory for reasoning under uncertainty</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "Bayesian Networks (BNs) are probabilistic graphical models that provide a principled framework for reasoning under uncertainty by combining probability theory with graph theory [22]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="A BN is a DAG whose edges denote conditional dependencies">
  <data key="d0">A BN is a DAG whose edges denote conditional dependencies</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "Formally, a BN is represented as a directed acyclic graph (DAG), where each node corresponds to a random variable and the directed edges denote conditional dependencies between variables [23]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BN quantitative aspect is defined by CPTs or parametric conditional distributions">
  <data key="d0">BN quantitative aspect is defined by CPTs or parametric conditional distributions</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "The quantitative aspect of BNs is defined through conditional probability tables (CPTs) for discrete variables, or parametric conditional distributions for continuous ones [6]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BN independence relations enable efficient inference">
  <data key="d0">BN independence relations enable efficient inference</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "...this formalism captures independence and conditional independence relations between variables, enabling efficient inference methods such as variable elimination and belief propagation [24]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BN construction can use literature reviews, expert interviews, brainstorming or data analysis">
  <data key="d0">BN construction can use literature reviews, expert interviews, brainstorming or data analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "This process can be achieved through literature reviews, expert interviews, brainstorming sessions, or data analysis [25]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Relying solely on expert knowledge for many CPTs is impractical">
  <data key="d0">Relying solely on expert knowledge for many CPTs is impractical</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "The study faced the challenge of handling a large number of conditional probability tables (CPTs), making it impractical to rely solely on expert knowledge [27]."&lt;SEP&gt;The study faced the challenge of handling a large number of conditional probability tables (CPTs), making it impractical to rely solely on expert knowledge [27].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy logic enables automatic CPT generation and improves modeling precision">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy logic enables automatic CPT generation and improves modeling precision</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "This method enables the linguistic representation of variable states and the automatic generation of CPTs, thus reducing the dependence on repeated expert consultations while improving precision in modeling [28]."&lt;SEP&gt;This method enables the linguistic representation of variable states and the automatic generation of CPTs, thus reducing the dependence on repeated expert consultations while improving precision in modeling [28].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Probability">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Probability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Individual conditional probability (CP) quantifying the likelihood of a specific state of a node given the states of its parent nodes; CPs are grouped into CPTs and were computed using fuzzy logic due to scarce historical failure data.&lt;SEP&gt;A way to measure the probability of an event A happening given that another event B has already happened, represented as P(A|B) or PB(A), computed as P(A∩B)/P(B) (Eq. (1)).&lt;SEP&gt;Probability of an event A given that event B has occurred, written P(A|B); fundamental to Bayesian models.&lt;SEP&gt;Conditional probabilities obtained by generalizing an elicitation strategy to all nodes in the causal graph, then used to supply the Bayesian Network.&lt;SEP&gt;CP, calculated by the FL system and fused with the causal relationship graph to create the FB network.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501527</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joint Probability Distribution Factorization">
  <data key="d0">Joint Probability Distribution Factorization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Factorization of the joint probability distribution over BN variables according to the DAG structure: P(X1,...,Xn) = product over i of P(Xi | Pa(Xi)), where Pa(Xi) denotes the parent nodes of Xi.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subjective Probability Elicitation">
  <data key="d0">Subjective Probability Elicitation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Subjective approach to assigning conditional probabilities that draws upon expert knowledge, often collected through brainstorming sessions; adopted because no database covering the BN parameters exists in the literature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Objective Probability Learning">
  <data key="d0">Objective Probability Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Objective approach to determining conditional probabilities relying on learning algorithms applied to existing databases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Robustness Assessment Under CPT Uncertainty">
  <data key="d0">Robustness Assessment Under CPT Uncertainty</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Assessment of model robustness with respect to uncertainty in conditional probability tables.&lt;SEP&gt;Third evaluation component of the paper. It assesses model robustness by quantifying epistemic uncertainty in the CPT parameters and reporting uncertainty intervals on key output probabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System Reliability">
  <data key="d0">System Reliability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Overall IT system reliability represented by the pink output node of the Bayesian Network built in GeNIe Academic.&lt;SEP&gt;Property quantified by ETA through branch probabilities, supporting decisions that reduce downtime and improve operational resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;Reliability of the IT system, which sensitivity analysis helps enhance through preventive measures.&lt;SEP&gt;Component-level and system-level reliability, whose most influential parameters were identified by sensitivity analyses and which the proposed approach aims to enhance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reliability Engineering">
  <data key="d0">Reliability Engineering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Field in which Bayesian Networks are highly effective for modeling complex systems with interdependent failure factors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IT System Health Monitoring">
  <data key="d0">IT System Health Monitoring</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Purpose of the causal graph whose input parameters are described in Table 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Probability Tables">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Probability Tables</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">CPTs that quantify dependencies in a Bayesian network; their large number makes expert elicitation impractical.&lt;SEP&gt;CPTs of the Bayesian network, constructed from calibrated fuzzy membership degrees.&lt;SEP&gt;CPTs parameterizing the Bayesian Network; epistemic uncertainty in their parameters is quantified.&lt;SEP&gt;Probability tables of Bayesian network nodes. In This Paper they are generated through fuzzy inference from expert evaluations.&lt;SEP&gt;CPTs of the Bayesian Network nodes, generated by fuzzy inference, illustrated for the Driver reaction time node.&lt;SEP&gt;Tables of the conditional probabilities of the Bayesian network variables. They are computed with fuzzy inferences to overcome the lack of data adapted to the study context.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501518</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzification converts crisp inputs into fuzzy subsets">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzification converts crisp inputs into fuzzy subsets</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzification: converts crisp inputs into fuzzy subsets defined by linguistic values and membership functions [29], [30].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mamdani outputs fuzzy sets while Sugeno outputs constants or linear functions">
  <data key="d0">Mamdani outputs fuzzy sets while Sugeno outputs constants or linear functions</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Two main approaches are commonly used: the Mamdani method, where the output is a fuzzy set, and the Sugeno method, where the output is expressed as a constant or a linear function of the inputs [31].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sugeno method offers higher computational efficiency than Mamdani">
  <data key="d0">Sugeno method offers higher computational efficiency than Mamdani</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Defuzzification: is only required in the Mamdani method to transform fuzzy results into crisp values, while the Sugeno method directly provides numerical outputs, offering higher computational efficiency [31].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gaussian membership functions minimize approximation errors">
  <data key="d0">Gaussian membership functions minimize approximation errors</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The choice of Gaussian membership functions for all nodes in the Bayesian Network is motivated by their superior smoothness and their ability to minimize approximation errors compared with triangular or trapezoidal alternatives [29], [30].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy If-Then Rules">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy If-Then Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Set of rules applied during the fuzzy inference stage to determine the system's output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expert Knowledge Elicitation">
  <data key="d0">Expert Knowledge Elicitation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reliance on repeated expert consultations to specify conditional probability tables. The paper calls this impractical for a large number of CPTs, and fuzzy logic reduces the dependence on it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Normalization">
  <data key="d0">Input Normalization</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Preprocessing step in which all input variables x are normalized to the domain [0,1] before each linguistic label is modeled with a Gaussian membership function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spread parameter sigma is determined using a controlled-overlap criterion">
  <data key="d0">Spread parameter sigma is determined using a controlled-overlap criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "The spread parameter σ was determined using a controlled-overlap criterion [32], [33], [34], [35]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha = 1/3 is the recommended overlap value for interpretable fuzzy systems">
  <data key="d0">Alpha = 1/3 is the recommended overlap value for interpretable fuzzy systems</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Using the recommended value α = 1/3 for interpretable fuzzy systems [36] results in σ = 0.10."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Controlled-Overlap Criterion">
  <data key="d0">Controlled-Overlap Criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Method that sets the spread of Gaussian membership functions from a desired overlap level α at the midpoint between adjacent membership functions, giving σ = Δ / (2·sqrt(2 ln(1/α))).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interpretable Fuzzy Systems">
  <data key="d0">Interpretable Fuzzy Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy systems designed for interpretability, for which an overlap level of α = 1/3 is recommended.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU State Node">
  <data key="d0">CPU State Node</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bayesian Network node whose state is described by three Gaussian membership functions (T1, T2, T3) centered at 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8 with σ = 0.1.&lt;SEP&gt;BN fuzzy node with 9 rules, used as an example of expert elicitation.&lt;SEP&gt;Node of the Bayesian network representing CPU condition (Normal / Degraded / Critical). Its states are determined by expert elicitation over 9 rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Causal Graph Input Parameters">
  <data key="d0">Causal Graph Input Parameters</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Input parameters of the causal graph, listed in TABLE 1. They include RAM metrics (Active Memory, Inactive Memory, Pages Swapped In, Fragmentation Rate), CPU metrics (User Space, Nice, Kernel Space, Hardware Interrupts, Software Interrupts, I/O Wait, Steal Time, Idle), hard disk metrics (UltraDMA CRC Error Count, Power-on Hours, Disk Usage), GPU Usage, and common system parameters (Ambient Temperature, Overclocking).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common System Parameters">
  <data key="d0">Common System Parameters</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Parameters common to all components, namely Ambient Temperature and Overclocking.&lt;SEP&gt;CSP: shared system parameters made up of ambient temperature and overclocking, varied across the degradation scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Overlap Level Alpha">
  <data key="d0">Overlap Level Alpha</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The desired overlap level α at the midpoint between two adjacent Gaussian membership functions. It is set to 1/3, and with a center spacing of Δ = 0.3 this gives a spread of σ = 0.10.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Linguistic Term">
  <data key="d0">Linguistic Term</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A fuzzy label, such as T1, T2 or T3, that represents a Bayesian Network node state. Each linguistic term has a Gaussian membership function with center c_i and spread σ_i.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sugeno-Type Fuzzy Inference System">
  <data key="d0">Sugeno-Type Fuzzy Inference System</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy inference system that evaluates degrees of membership for fuzzy subsets; a zero-order variant with constant rule consequents is used, avoiding defuzzification and enabling faster processing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gaussian Membership Functions">
  <data key="d0">Gaussian Membership Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Membership functions defined on a normalized domain for network nodes such as CPU State; input values are set at their peaks.&lt;SEP&gt;Gaussian distributions whose peaks are used to initialize the fuzzy system with input values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Rule Base">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Rule Base</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Set of fuzzy rules, defined with domain experts, describing how node states such as CPU State vary with parent node states.&lt;SEP&gt;Set of rules evaluating the variability of the Driver reaction time node based on the conditions of its parent nodes, detailed in Table IV.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zadeh Min Operator">
  <data key="d0">Zadeh Min Operator</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Operator used to combine rule antecedents (AND) to compute firing strengths.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normalized Aggregated Firing Strengths as Probability Generators">
  <data key="d0">Normalized Aggregated Firing Strengths as Probability Generators</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Approach in which aggregated firing strengths are normalized into a sum-one distribution satisfying Kolmogorov axioms, interpreting activation degrees as relative plausibility scores.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Possibility Theory">
  <data key="d0">Possibility Theory</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Zadeh's possibility theory, grounding the interpretation of activation degrees as plausibility scores.&lt;SEP&gt;Theory that Zadeh's 1978 paper founds on the basis of fuzzy sets.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kolmogorov Axioms">
  <data key="d0">Kolmogorov Axioms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Probability axioms of non-negativity and unit sum, satisfied by the normalized distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425296</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU State">
  <data key="d0">CPU State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Network node with states Normal, Degraded and Critical; inferred from CPU Temperature and System Load Average, e.g. P(Critical)=0.95 for Rule 6.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node with the values Normal, Degraded and Critical. It is inferred from CPU temperature and system load average using nine fuzzy rules.&lt;SEP&gt;Component-level state node (Normal, Degraded, Critical) whose parent nodes are CPU Temperature and System Load Average. It is used to illustrate CPT derivation, calibration and expert elicitation.&lt;SEP&gt;State classes of the CPU component whose predicted probabilities are evaluated for calibration in Figure 7.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sugeno method suits CPT generation for Bayesian networks">
  <data key="d0">Sugeno method suits CPT generation for Bayesian networks</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In this study, the Sugeno method was chosen because it avoids the defuzzification step and enables faster processing, making it suitable for generating the CPTs required to parameterize the BN [28].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normalized firing strengths can serve as probability generators">
  <data key="d0">Normalized firing strengths can serve as probability generators</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Our approach differs from standard Sugeno by using normalized aggregated firing strengths as probability generators [37], grounded in Zadeh's possibility theory [34].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zadeh">
  <data key="d0">Zadeh</data>
  <data key="d1">author</data>
  <data key="d2">Originator of the possibility theory cited as [34]. Zadeh's name is also attached to the min operator used to combine rule antecedents.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU Temperature">
  <data key="d0">CPU Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input variable of the CPU State node, with the values Normal, Warning and Critical.&lt;SEP&gt;Parent node, or causal factor, of the CPU State node in the Bayesian network. In Rule 6 of TABLE 5 it takes the state "warning".&lt;SEP&gt;Intermediate physical sensor node and parent of CPU State. It uses the label Warning, which reflects a sensor-level alert threshold.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System Load Average">
  <data key="d0">System Load Average</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input variable of the CPU State node, with the values Low, Medium and High.&lt;SEP&gt;Parent node, or causal factor, of the CPU State node in the Bayesian network. In Rule 6 of TABLE 5 it takes the state "high".&lt;SEP&gt;Parent node of CPU State in the causal graph. Table 5 presents the CPU State conditional probabilities as a function of its states.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="User Activity">
  <data key="d0">User Activity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy node in the network. Appendix B gives its rule base as a representative example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Domain Experts">
  <data key="d0">Domain Experts</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Experts who worked with the authors to define the fuzzy rules, so that the rule base reflects realistic operational behaviors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sugeno Fuzzy Inference">
  <data key="d0">Sugeno Fuzzy Inference</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy inference method whose standard output is a weighted average, y* = Σ w_i·c_i / Σ w_i, where c_i is the consequent constant of rule i. This crisp output is not used in the construction of the conditional probability tables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aggregated Firing Strengths">
  <data key="d0">Aggregated Firing Strengths</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Per-label aggregated firing strengths µ_j extracted directly from the fuzzy inference system. They are normalized into probabilities. For example, Rule 6 gives µ values of (0.002, 0.044, 0.882), which normalize to P=(0.002, 0.048, 0.950).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RAM State">
  <data key="d0">RAM State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bayesian network node with the values Normal, Degraded and Critical. It is related to memory variables, RAM temperature, overclocking and fragmentation rate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hard Disk State">
  <data key="d0">Hard Disk State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bayesian network node with the values Normal, Degraded and Critical. It is related to SMART attributes such as reallocated sectors, pending sectors and power-on hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU State">
  <data key="d0">GPU State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bayesian network node with the values Normal, Degraded and Critical. It is related to GPU temperature, usage, fan speed, VRAM usage, OOM errors and power consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System State">
  <data key="d0">System State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Top-level Bayesian network node representing overall system health, with the values Normal, Degraded and Critical.&lt;SEP&gt;System-level state node of the Bayesian Network that uses the Normal, Degraded and Critical labels.&lt;SEP&gt;Output variable (system_state) of the BN with Normal, Degraded and Critical states. Its marginal posterior probabilities are computed for each scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;System_State is a child node of the unified Bayesian network, conditioned on all component states. Setting System_State=Critical is used for system-level (global) sensitivity analysis. The highest sensitivity occurs when multiple components are in Critical or Degraded states.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Rules of the CPU State Node">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Rules of the CPU State Node</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Nine fuzzy rules that combine CPU Temperature (Normal, Warning, Critical) and System Load Average (Low, Medium, High) to infer CPU State. For example, Rule 6 (Warning, High) yields High.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weighted-Average Crisp Output">
  <data key="d0">Weighted-Average Crisp Output</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Crisp value y* = Σ w_i·c_i / Σ w_i (Equation 10) from Sugeno inference. The value 0.784 displayed by FisPro for Rule 6 lies close to the Critical label center (c=0.8) and is consistent with the probability distribution, but it serves only as a reference and is not used in CPT construction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RAM Temperature">
  <data key="d0">RAM Temperature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bayesian network node with the values Normal, Warning and Critical. It is related to RAM state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fragmentation Rate">
  <data key="d0">Fragmentation Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bayesian network node with the values Low, Medium and High. It is related to RAM state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-eafe46d984faf5ae72cd0cf550c6ec66-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">From_Uncertainty_to_Decision_An_Intelligent_Predic.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791425298</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Overclocking">
