<node id="Mode Collapse">
  <data key="d0">Mode Collapse</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mode Collapse is a well-known and persistent failure mode of Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs). It occurs when the generator produces a limited set of repetitive outputs instead of capturing the full diversity of the underlying data distribution. The review identifies it as a key limitation of GANs, and it is widely recognized as a central obstacle to reliable generative modeling.

Mode Collapse is a training issue inherent to the original GAN formulation. Later architectures were designed to address it. The Wasserstein GAN (WGAN) was introduced in part to resolve this problem, and IndexGAN is likewise described as mitigating it.

In financial data generation, Mode Collapse remains a significant challenge. Generators may fail to reproduce the full range of financial patterns, yielding homogeneous synthetic data. The problem persists alongside model stability issues, and it is cited as a key challenge together with data biases and the difficulty of defining appropriate evaluation metrics.

Mode Collapse also arises when GAN architectures originally designed for images are applied directly to mixed-type tabular data. In that setting, it tends to coincide with poor handling of rare categories. This shows how unsuitable model designs can worsen the loss of distributional diversity in synthetic data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ed200d7fb9035c5336533a6730c8671b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-001</data>
