<node id="Smart Contract">
  <data key="d0">Smart Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The Smart Contract is a program deployed on the Ethereum blockchain that encodes the contract rules governing the proposed security mechanism. Its core purpose is to register and authenticate devices and to calculate the access tokens used to identify them. It serves as the central on-chain component that implements admin creation, registration, token calculation and authentication checks.

The Smart Contract's constructor creates the first admin using the creator's address, the admin identifier (AID) and the cluster identifier (CID). It computes admin tokens using the Keccak256 hash function and checks by hash whether an admin is already registered before adding a new one. It accepts transactions and, when an admin is successfully added, emits the AdminAdded event.

For device management, the Smart Contract verifies admins and their cluster identifiers, checks whether a device already exists, and registers new devices through the addDevice function. During registration it creates tokens for the devices and stores them on the blockchain, emitting the DeviceAdded event when registration succeeds.

The Smart Contract also handles authentication and packet legitimacy verification. It compares a presented token with the token stored on the blockchain to decide whether a device is legitimate. Depending on the outcome, it triggers either the Authenticated or the NoAuthenticated event.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791466849</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ethereum">
  <data key="d0">Ethereum</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Ethereum is described as the most popular and widely adopted blockchain platform, and it was selected as the foundation for implementing the proposed system and its mechanism. Within the proposed system architecture, Ethereum serves as the underlying blockchain platform, with its operations managed and governed by smart contract rules.

Ethereum was chosen for several reasons. It simplifies the construction of decentralized applications (DApps) and supports the full lifecycle of smart contracts, including their creation, deployment, and testing. It secures transactions using Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC). It also offers test networks that use fake Ether, with accounts that can be reset, which allows development and experimentation without real financial cost. Finally, it benefits from a large, active community.

In the proposed system, each admin is assigned an Ethereum address associated with an asymmetric key pair. These addresses identify administrators on the blockchain and allow them to interact securely with the platform.&lt;SEP&gt;Public blockchain where signatures authorize asset transfers.&lt;SEP&gt;Decentralised generalised transaction ledger specified in Wood's Ethereum Yellow Paper [18].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-039</data>
  <data key="d4">InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Keccak256">
  <data key="d0">Keccak256</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Keccak256 is a cryptographic hash function used within the system to generate authentication tokens for both administrators and devices. Each token is derived from three inputs: the entity's address, the identifier of the cluster it belongs to, and its own unique identifier. This gives every administrator and device a distinct token tied to its identity and cluster membership.

For administrators, Keccak256 computes the admin token as keccak256(AIP, CID, AID). Here AIP is the administrator's address, CID is the cluster identifier, and AID is the administrator's unique identifier.

For devices, Keccak256 creates the device token when a new device is registered, computed as keccak256(DIP, CID, DID). Here DIP is the device's address, CID is the cluster identifier, and DID is the device's unique identifier. Generating the token at registration provides a verifiable credential for the device within its cluster.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-aaf2c8f641e35f769c72ec556a34ec4b-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">InfocomJ_2022_3_4_Khalfaoui.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791466859</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integrating Canny Filter and Convolutional Neural Networks for Quality Defect Detection in Injection Molding Process">
  <data key="d0">Integrating Canny Filter and Convolutional Neural Networks for Quality Defect Detection in Injection Molding Process</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">The article being read, published in the Proceedings of EECSS'24 (Barcelona, August 19-21, 2024) as Paper No. MVML 120. It combines the Canny filter for edge feature extraction with CNNs to classify injection-molded parts as good or defective. With the Canny filter the model reaches 99.57% accuracy, 99.44% precision, 100% recall and a 99.72% F1-score. Without the filter it reaches 95.31% accuracy, 94.24% precision, 100% recall and a 97.03% F1-score.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467085</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
