<node id="Quality defect detection in injection molding is particularly challenging">
  <data key="d0">Quality defect detection in injection molding is particularly challenging</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Quality defect detection remains a particularly challenging task in injection molding due to the intricate nature of the process and the high standards required for the final products[5].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467085</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traditional inspection methods fall short in accuracy and efficiency">
  <data key="d0">Traditional inspection methods fall short in accuracy and efficiency</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Traditional inspection methods often fall short in terms of accuracy and efficiency, prompting the need for more advanced solutions[6].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467085</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Canny-CNN Integrated Model">
  <data key="d0">Canny-CNN Integrated Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Two-step method proposed by the paper. It first applies the Canny filter to extract edge characteristics, then uses Convolutional Neural Networks to classify injection-molded parts as good or defective. It reaches 99.57% accuracy, 99.44% precision, 100% recall and a 99.72% F1-score. It outperforms CNN alone, which reaches 95.31% accuracy, 94.24% precision, 100% recall and a 97.03% F1-score. The authors state it can be implemented in online production systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467085</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Combined Canny filter and CNN method outperforms CNN alone">
  <data key="d0">Combined Canny filter and CNN method outperforms CNN alone</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of the paper: "The results demonstrate that the combined method outperforms the use of CNN alone, achieving an accuracy of 99.57%, a precision of 99.44%, a recall of 100%, and an F1-score of 99.72% with the Canny filter, compared to an accuracy of 95.31%, a precision of 94.24%, a recall of 100%, and an F1-score of 97.03% without the Canny filter."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467085</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Manual Visual Inspection">
  <data key="d0">Manual Visual Inspection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Human visual inspection of products, which has inherent limitations. Automated defect detection approaches aim to overcome these limitations.&lt;SEP&gt;Traditional human inspection of manufactured parts. Its limitations motivate automatic defect detection approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467085</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visual Quality Inspection">
  <data key="d0">Visual Quality Inspection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application area concerned with automated inspection of product quality from images, including in a human-free production environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467103</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Online Production Systems">
  <data key="d0">Online Production Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Production systems into which the integrated Canny-CNN model can be implemented to enhance defect detection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467103</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Liu et al. (2021) Knowledge Reuse for Defect Detection">
  <data key="d0">Liu et al. (2021) Knowledge Reuse for Defect Detection</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [8]. Liu et al., 2021; proposes a knowledge reuse strategy combining model-based transfer learning and data augmentation to train CNN models for automatic appearance defect detection, achieving about 99% accuracy on injection molding products with only 200 images per category.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yang et al. (2015) Edge Detection and Model-Free Optimization for Injection Molding">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al. (2015) Edge Detection and Model-Free Optimization for Injection Molding</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [9]. Yang et al., 2015; assesses Roberts, Sobel, Prewitt and Canny edge detection operators in digital image processing and integrates DIP with model-free optimization to eliminate defects in injection molded parts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reckert et al. (2024) CNN Quality Classification in Multi-Material Jetting">
  <data key="d0">Reckert et al. (2024) CNN Quality Classification in Multi-Material Jetting</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [10]. Reckert et al., 2024; uses surface measurements and CNNs to automate classification of process parameterization adequacy on green parts in Multi-Material Jetting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Im et al. (2023) Neural Network Quality Prediction for Injection Molding">
  <data key="d0">Im et al. (2023) Neural Network Quality Prediction for Injection Molding</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [11]. Im et al., 2023; develops a neural network algorithm predicting injection molding product quality from vibration, cavity temperature and pressure data, and image features graded with the Canny edge algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Canny Edge Detection">
  <data key="d0">Canny Edge Detection</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Popular computer vision and image processing algorithm for detecting edges, developed by John F. Canny in 1986; consists of noise reduction, gradient calculation, non-maximum suppression, double thresholding and edge tracking by hysteresis.&lt;SEP&gt;Edge detection operator used to extract edge characteristics from images of plastic parts and to determine quality indicators.&lt;SEP&gt;Edge detection algorithm. In these references it is modified, enhanced by scale multiplication, and given a quantum enhancement.&lt;SEP&gt;Edge detection algorithm used for fault detection in injection molding (reference [7]) and improved in reference [12].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Step Detection Approach">
  <data key="d0">Two-Step Detection Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Approach proposed by This Paper involving edge-detection filtering followed by CNN analysis to classify faulty injection-molded parts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Digital Image Processing">
  <data key="d0">Digital Image Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">DIP used to monitor surface defect magnitudes in injection molding.&lt;SEP&gt;Image analysis technique used for online quality optimization of the injection molding process (reference [9]).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Model-Free Optimization">
  <data key="d0">Model-Free Optimization</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">MFO uses online feedback to identify optimal injection molding settings.&lt;SEP&gt;Optimization approach used with digital image processing for online quality optimization of injection molding (reference [9]).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5436254d7328f14e8f79864da9fef604-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">MVML_120 (1).pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467109</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
