<node id="Microservices Architecture">
  <data key="d0">Microservices Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Microservices Architecture is a distributed software architectural style that splits an application into independent, modular services. It is favored for its modularity and scalability, and it improves fault isolation and deployment speed. However, it also introduces the complexity inherent to distributed systems. In this style, backend services operate independently and communicate with one another through mechanisms such as message queues.

Microservices Architecture is the target domain of FSMicroGenerator, a tool that generates full-stack web applications built on this architecture. FSMicroGenerator aims to simplify the adoption of microservices and optimize software development by automatically producing complex microservice architectures composed of modular backend services. The generated solutions rely on proven architectural patterns, including API Gateway, Database per Service and Event-Driven Architecture. The MEAN stack is considered especially well suited to Microservices Architecture, and FSMicroGenerator uses it to build its applications.

Microservices Architecture is often contrasted with monolithic architectures. Many prior code-generation tools are monolithic or lack service integration and inter-service communication mechanisms. In contrast, FSMicroGenerator and the work of Prasanthan et al. explicitly adopt the microservices approach.

The architectural style is also a recurring theme across the cited literature. These works address scalability, deployment, communication patterns and migration from monolithic systems to microservices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467631</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Selected works are seminal in code generation tool development">
  <data key="d0">Selected works are seminal in code generation tool development</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "We have selected a specific set of works [1], [2], [6], [17]–[20] for a detailed analysis, as these sources are seminal in the development of code generation tools, covering a broad spectrum of methodologies and application areas."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791467631</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FSMicroGenerator">
  <data key="d0">FSMicroGenerator</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">FSMicroGenerator is a low-code code generation tool, and the name also refers to the article that presents it, accepted for publication in IEEE Access (DOI 10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3589285). The tool uses UML class diagrams and templates to generate scalable, secure, maintainable, and multilingual full-stack web applications based on a microservices architecture. It aims to simplify and accelerate development from design to deployment through continuous delivery and deployment, lower technical barriers, and reduce technical debt. It is planned to be released as open source and has already been deployed in production.

Architecturally, FSMicroGenerator is organized around three parts: template management, a user interface, and a code generation engine. Following microservices principles, it structures applications into three logical units: packages, modules, and projects, with the package as the core unit. It also maintains a catalog of reusable packages, modules, and functional blocks. Users define business data models with UML class diagrams, including attribute types, constraints, and associations. The tool then turns these conceptual models into working applications through a structured generation process. It follows OMG standards, RESTful best practices, DevSecOps principles, and established microservice and software design patterns, and relies on generic functions and reusable components.

Built on a JavaScript-based MEAN stack (Node.js, Express.js, MongoDB, Angular with PrimeNG) together with Swagger and Docker, the tool generates a frontend, an API Gateway, and microservices. Inter-service communication uses a hybrid approach based on RabbitMQ. It automatically produces:
- CRUD, translation, and clone operations
- Entity relationships, including foreign keys, form bindings, API endpoints, and service scaffolding
- Filtering and search logic, plus customizable HTML tables and forms
- Error handling, logging, tests, and API documentation

Generated applications come with pre-configured services such as authentication, role-based access control, and dynamic menus. They also include dashboards, reporting, business statistics visualization, data import/export, and pre-configured entity documents.

For infrastructure, FSMicroGenerator generates Dockerfiles, .dockerignore files, and Docker Compose files for both development and production. It injects Prometheus-compatible measurement points, provides preconfigured Prometheus/Grafana monitoring and alerting, and creates a GitLab CI/CD pipeline template (.gitlab-ci.yml) with DevSecOps practices. It then pushes the complete source code to a designated repository on a private GitLab instance. Non-functional requirements can be applied globally or per module.

The tool was evaluated in an 8-hour experiment in which a team of two junior developers using it was compared with manual implementation on effort, productivity, and code quality:
- **Coverage:** The team reached 100% functional coverage (28/28) and 100% non-functional coverage (52/52, against 0.33 for the baseline) within 3 hours.
- **Effort:** This required 11 commits, no manually created files, and 148 manual file modifications.
- **Output size:** The generated system contained 904 files (about 192k lines of code) and ran 16 containers.
- **Quality:** It scored 5/5 on all qualitative criteria, including consistency and maintainability.
- **Load performance:** Under load it showed 1,828.38 ms latency, 21.75 requests per second, 136% CPU usage, and 1,292 MB of RAM.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a4aa31c8531fdbc66868e8d96c0e0d18-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Microservices-Driven_Automation_in_Full-Stack_Deve.pdf</data>
