<node id="Retrieval-Augmented Generation">
  <data key="d0">Retrieval-Augmented Generation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a technique and architectural framework that enhances large language models (LLMs) by integrating dynamic external knowledge retrieval with text generation. It was introduced by Lewis et al. (2020) for knowledge-intensive natural language processing tasks and later benchmarked with large language models by Chen et al. (2024). It has also been introduced and studied in other referenced works ([18], [16], and [17]). RAG addresses key limitations of LLMs, notably outdated knowledge and hallucinations, and thereby reduces the misinformation these models produce.

Structurally, Retrieval-Augmented Generation consists of two main components: a document retriever and a text generator. The retriever finds relevant texts in an external corpus, often stored in vector databases. These texts are then supplied to the language model, either through cross-attention mechanisms or by inserting the retrieved documents directly into the prompt. This adds pertinent information to the model's context, so that generation is grounded in retrieved knowledge rather than relying solely on the model's internal parameters.

In the legal domain, Retrieval-Augmented Generation is applied to deliver accurate and contextually relevant legal responses. It combines legal vector databases with large language models so that answers are grounded in official legal data. It serves as the core pipeline of a contract law assistance system, described as an architecture that combines search and generation with the help of generative AI. Within this system, the RAG process relies on legal texts as the primary source during the data augmentation phase that precedes answer generation. RAG also forms the foundation of the proposed chatbot architecture, which uses retrieval from vector databases to enrich the model's context and produce reliable, well-grounded responses.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-caa1a23d50101616a42f59c788898ea5-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_113-Accurate_AI_Assistance_in_Contract_Law1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791500968</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
