</node>
<node id="Proactive Routing Protocols">
  <data key="d0">Proactive Routing Protocols</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">MANET routing schemes focusing on preventing adversaries from invoking intruders.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Routing Protocols">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Routing Protocols</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">MANET routing protocols combining the best of reactive and proactive methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DSR">
  <data key="d0">DSR</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Dynamic Source Routing protocol, modified to protect its discovery phase from Man-In-The-Middle attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Man-In-The-Middle Attack">
  <data key="d0">Man-In-The-Middle Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Attack in which the attacker captures all packets and then removes or modifies them.&lt;SEP&gt;Attack through which malicious nodes can obtain keys in MANET key distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Passive attacks compromise confidentiality and enable other attacks">
  <data key="d0">Passive attacks compromise confidentiality and enable other attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">This type of assault is not severe, but it compromises confidentiality, aids the attacker in obtaining information and creates a bridge to other dangerous attacks [10].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal attacks by selfish nodes are difficult to predict">
  <data key="d0">Internal attacks by selfish nodes are difficult to predict</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">For the second type, attacks usually occur within the network by selfish nodes, and it is difficult to predict them [11].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MANET has two sorts of security measures">
  <data key="d0">MANET has two sorts of security measures</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">According to research, MANET has two sorts of security measures. The first type concerns protecting routing schemes, while the second concerns data security schemes [13].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MANET routing protocols must be adaptive and support multiple services">
  <data key="d0">MANET routing protocols must be adaptive and support multiple services</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">A routing protocol must also support various services for multiple applications in MANET environment, which is dynamic, flexible to changes in network architecture, and vulnerable to security attacks [14].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MANET routing protocols divide into reactive, proactive and hybrid">
  <data key="d0">MANET routing protocols divide into reactive, proactive and hybrid</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The majority of authors in the literature, like Abolhasan et al. [17], divided MANET routing protocols into three categories: reactive..., proactive..., and hybrid routing protocols.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Attacks are classified by source and behavior">
  <data key="d0">Attacks are classified by source and behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"The table 1 shows the classification of various attacks according to the source and behavior of the attacker." The table, Table 1, is attributed to [12]. It divides attacks into internal and external by source and into passive and active by behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Many surveys evaluate MANET routing processes and strategies">
  <data key="d0">Many surveys evaluate MANET routing processes and strategies</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Many surveys [15, 16] have been conducted to evaluate the routing process and strategies used by MANETs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Existing MANET routing protocols were discussed with one example per category">
  <data key="d0">Existing MANET routing protocols were discussed with one example per category</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In [14], for example, multiple existent MANETs routing protocols were discussed, with one example for each category of a routing technique being examined.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Authentication can be combined with symmetric-key confidentiality">
  <data key="d0">Authentication can be combined with symmetric-key confidentiality</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Some authors in [19] combine authentication with confidentiality mechanism. They are interested in encrypting data with a symmetric key after the node has been successfully authenticated because symmetric keys are simple, utilize less memory, consume less power, and occupy less memory.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Securing DSR route discovery provides data integrity against Man-In-The-Middle attacks">
  <data key="d0">Securing DSR route discovery provides data integrity against Man-In-The-Middle attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Other authors [20] developed a method for changing DSR routing protocol and gaining data integrity by protecting the discovery phase of the routing protocol from a Man-In-The-Middle attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IDS use cryptography and access control to detect and prevent attacks">
  <data key="d0">IDS use cryptography and access control to detect and prevent attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In [21], authors spotlight different types of Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) such as Host IDS (HIDS), Application-based IDS and Network IDS. They are mechanisms that use cryptography and access control technologies for detecting and preventing security attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MANET Routing Protocols">
  <data key="d0">MANET Routing Protocols</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Routing protocols for MANETs. Routing is one of the most difficult tasks in a MANET. The protocols should be efficient and adaptive to changing network conditions. They are categorized as reactive, proactive and hybrid, and also as Hierarchical, Geographical, Power-Aware and Multicast routing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501030</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Host IDS">
  <data key="d0">Host IDS</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Host-based Intrusion Detection System (HIDS). It is one of the IDS types spotlighted in [21].&lt;SEP&gt;HIDS; a type of Intrusion Detection System highlighted in the work cited as [21].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1e0beceeffe2b62352afd4291e0fd937-chunk-002</data>
