  <data key="d0">U.S. Department of Transportation</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The U.S. federal department under which NHTSA operates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Highway Safety Act of 1970">
  <data key="d0">Highway Safety Act of 1970</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The legislation that established NHTSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subjective Method">
  <data key="d0">Subjective Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A method that relies on the expertise of professionals in the field. It serves as the foundation for identifying accident parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vehicle Accidents">
  <data key="d0">Vehicle Accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Road traffic accidents. The root-cause parameters identified include road alignment and grade, traffic controls, weather, driver, stopping distance, traffic parameters, delivery planning and vehicle condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Most crashes occur on one-lane roads">
  <data key="d0">Most crashes occur on one-lane roads</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Furthermore, a greater number of crashes occur on one-lane roads (1606 times), in which inconsiderate drivers are a major cause of accidents (1910 times) [36].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Most accidents occur on straight road segments">
  <data key="d0">Most accidents occur on straight road segments</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Most of accident occurred on a straight segment [36].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Faulty traffic control devices lead to accidents">
  <data key="d0">Faulty traffic control devices lead to accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">If these devices are not properly placed, are malfunctioning, or are difficult to see or understand, they can lead to confusion and accidents [37].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lighting conditions affect road accident occurrence">
  <data key="d0">Lighting conditions affect road accident occurrence</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Lighting conditions have a considerable effect on the occurrence of road accidents, due to the adaptation of speed with visibility conditions [38].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uncleaned accident locations cause vehicles to skid">
  <data key="d0">Uncleaned accident locations cause vehicles to skid</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">If accident locations are not adequately cleaned, the remaining debris, body fluids, and fuel can cause vehicles to skid [8].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Most accidents occur in clear weather">
  <data key="d0">Most accidents occur in clear weather</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The largest number of accidents occurs in clear weather [36].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rain or fog reduce visibility of traffic controls causing accidents">
  <data key="d0">Rain or fog reduce visibility of traffic controls causing accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Rain or fog can make it difficult for drivers to see traffic signals and road signs, leading to accidents [39].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adverse weather impairs drivers, road grip and vehicle control">
  <data key="d0">Adverse weather impairs drivers, road grip and vehicle control</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Adverse weather conditions can negatively impact a driver's ability, the grip of the road, the state of road infrastructure, and the stability and control of the vehicle... [40].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adverse weather affects traffic demand, safety and flow">
  <data key="d0">Adverse weather affects traffic demand, safety and flow</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Consequently, these conditions impact traffic demand..., traffic safety..., and the dynamics of traffic flow... [41].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Slippery roads increase vehicle stopping distances">
  <data key="d0">Slippery roads increase vehicle stopping distances</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Slippery roads due to rain, snow or ice, as well as the ambient temperature in dry or wet weather, increase stopping distances of vehicles. [42]</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Routine driving style and fatigue contribute to traffic accidents">
  <data key="d0">Routine driving style and fatigue contribute to traffic accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"According to [43], [44], routine driving style contribute to traffic accidents." and "Fatigue and sleepiness, contributes to traffic accidents [43], [44]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alcohol consumption while driving leads to fatal accidents">
  <data key="d0">Alcohol consumption while driving leads to fatal accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The consumption of alcohol while driving is one of the dangerous factor of the driver's conditions, it often leads to fatal accidents [3].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Speeding driven by negative emotions causes fatal accidents">
  <data key="d0">Speeding driven by negative emotions causes fatal accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Negative emotions and stress can lead to increased speed, which is a major factor in accidents. This results in a significant number of injuries and deaths. In fact, one-third of total accidents involving driver speed result in fatalities [36], [45], [46]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver behavior is a major source of traffic accidents">
  <data key="d0">Driver behavior is a major source of traffic accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"Driver behavior includes failure to respect safety distance and speed limits, as well as sudden braking and hard acceleration [47]." and "According to the article [48] one of the major sources of traffic accidents is who Driver behavior while driving."&lt;SEP&gt;According to the article [48] one of the major sources of traffic accidents is who Driver behavior while driving.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic way is registered in all accident records">
  <data key="d0">Traffic way is registered in all accident records</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "In annual traffic crash data, released by in 2020 by 'U.S. Department of Transportation's National Highway Traffic Safety Administration' (NHTSA) [35], we find that traffic way, is one of the elements registered in all accidents records."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road surface condition affects accident occurrence">
  <data key="d0">Road surface condition affects accident occurrence</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "NHTSA in his report [35], has considered that the surface's road named on the report 'VSURCOND' is one of the parameter affecting the occurrence of the accident."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic control device functioning is recorded in crash data">
  <data key="d0">Traffic control device functioning is recorded in crash data</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "According to NHTSA's report Revised July 2022 [35], the traffic control is, not functioning, functioning improperly or functioning properly, the variable name was named 'VTCONT_F'."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weather Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Weather Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An accident prediction parameter covering rain, fog, snow, ice, extreme temperatures, strong winds and lightning. Weather conditions affect visibility, road grip, the effectiveness of traffic controls, stopping distance and traffic flow.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic Control Devices">
  <data key="d0">Traffic Control Devices</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Traffic signals, road signs and road markings that give information and instructions to road users. When these devices are misplaced or malfunctioning, they can cause accidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Driver Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Driver actions such as failing to respect safety distance and speed limits, sudden braking and hard acceleration. Driver behavior is a major source of traffic accidents.&lt;SEP&gt;Driving conduct including failure to respect safety distance and speed limits, sudden braking and hard acceleration; identified as a major source of traffic accidents.&lt;SEP&gt;Factor found to exert a significant influence on accident occurrences; monitoring and driver training are recommended.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Fatigue">
  <data key="d0">Driver Fatigue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fatigue and sleepiness of the driver, which contribute to traffic accidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alcohol Consumption">
  <data key="d0">Alcohol Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Drinking alcohol while driving, a dangerous driver condition that often leads to fatal accidents.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Speeding">
  <data key="d0">Speeding</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Increased driving speed, which can be caused by negative emotions and stress. Speeding is a major factor in accidents and fatalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Driving above speed limits or at excessive speed. It is associated with one-third of fatal accidents involving driver speed and with 6.9% of passenger vehicle drivers in 2020 fatal crashes. Speeding also lengthens stopping distance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stopping Distance">
  <data key="d0">Stopping Distance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The distance a vehicle travels after the driver brakes. Stopping distance increases on slippery roads and with ambient temperature.&lt;SEP&gt;Distance needed for a vehicle to stop, influenced by technical factors (road adhesion, road grade, kinetic energy, tire condition, weight) and human factors (reaction time, visibility); approximated by squaring the tens figure of speed (120 km/h gives 144 m).&lt;SEP&gt;Total distance to stop a vehicle; a final impact in the BN, increased by brake wear, vehicle weight and other factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node 13 with linguistic values Short, Normal, Long.&lt;SEP&gt;Node of the Bayesian network modeling road accident risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Vehicle stopping distance, studied with respect to pedestrian estimation and connected vehicles at highway-rail grade crossings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lighting Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Lighting Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Visibility conditions that affect accident occurrence because drivers adapt their speed to visibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Surface Condition">
  <data key="d0">Road Surface Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The state of the road surface, recorded as VSURCOND in NHTSA data, and considered a parameter affecting accident occurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node 6 with linguistic values Dry, Dirt, Wet.&lt;SEP&gt;Node of the Bayesian network modeling road accident risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BELDAM Survey">
  <data key="d0">BELDAM Survey</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [54]; Belgian mobility survey showing 2/3 of morning peak trips (65% between 6am and 9am) are home-to-work trips, and differences between school and non-school days.&lt;SEP&gt;Belgian mobility survey whose 2010 results are reported in the 2012 report by Cornelis et al.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
