  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One-third of speed-related accidents result in fatalities">
  <data key="d0">One-third of speed-related accidents result in fatalities</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In fact, one-third of total accidents involving driver speed result in fatalities [36], [45], [46].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver behavior includes failure to respect safety distance and speed limits, sudden braking and hard acceleration">
  <data key="d0">Driver behavior includes failure to respect safety distance and speed limits, sudden braking and hard acceleration</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Driver behavior includes failure to respect safety distance and speed limits, as well as sudden braking and hard acceleration [47].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Speeding caused 6.9% of passenger vehicle driver fatal crashes in 2020">
  <data key="d0">Speeding caused 6.9% of passenger vehicle driver fatal crashes in 2020</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">According to [49] study, from the 41144 passenger vehicle drivers involved in 2020 in fatal crashes, 2819 were due to speeding, this represent 6.9%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Higher speed results in longer stopping distance">
  <data key="d0">Higher speed results in longer stopping distance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"The speed is higher, the stopping distance of vehicle is longer [50]." Stopping distance is also affected by driver reflexes, weather and road grip [50].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Festive, social and unforeseen events interrupt road traffic">
  <data key="d0">Festive, social and unforeseen events interrupt road traffic</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Road Traffic can be interrupted by the presence of a number of events, such as festive events ... [8], [51], social events (political demonstrations, diplomatic visits [52], sports events) [8], [51] and unforeseen or occasional events ... [8], [52].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Urban delivery during peak hours generates mixed dense traffic">
  <data key="d0">Urban delivery during peak hours generates mixed dense traffic</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"Urban delivery during peak hours is very difficult, as private vehicles and goods vehicles overlap, generating a mixed and dense traffic flow [51]." Also: "Delivery period influences traffic, delivery during peak hours can slow down traffic [8]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Home-to-work and home-school trips drive peak congestion in Belgium">
  <data key="d0">Home-to-work and home-school trips drive peak congestion in Belgium</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">the BELDAM survey [54] showed that 2/3 of the morning peak trips were related to this reason ... This means that 'home-school' trips also play a role in congestion in Belgium [53], [54], [55], [56].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bad vehicle physical condition can cause dramatic accidents">
  <data key="d0">Bad vehicle physical condition can cause dramatic accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">A Vehicle having a bad physical condition can be a cause of a dramatic accident [4], [36].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reaction time ranges from 1.8 to 2.5 seconds and depends on fatigue, alcohol, drugs and distraction">
  <data key="d0">Reaction time ranges from 1.8 to 2.5 seconds and depends on fatigue, alcohol, drugs and distraction</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Various studies show that reaction time is in the range of 1.8 to 2.5 seconds, in France, it is generally estimated at 2 seconds. The reaction time depends on many factors ... [57].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weather and night reduce visibility and affect reaction time">
  <data key="d0">Weather and night reduce visibility and affect reaction time</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Fog, Rain, Snow, Hail, and Night, affect the visibility needed to interpret risks and hazards and influence the reaction time and distance travelled [58].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road surface and weather determine tire-road friction coefficient">
  <data key="d0">Road surface and weather determine tire-road friction coefficient</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Together with the weather, they constitute what is known as the coefficient of friction between the tires and the road [59].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road grade affects braking distance and steering grip">
  <data key="d0">Road grade affects braking distance and steering grip</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Braking distance is improved on uphill and reduced on downhill. Similarly, steering grip is improved on positive slopes and penalised on negative slopes. [60]</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accidents occur when braking distance exceeds available stopping distance">
  <data key="d0">Accidents occur when braking distance exceeds available stopping distance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Accidents often occur when the braking distance is longer than the distance available to stop safely. [61], [62], [63]</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic Congestion">
  <data key="d0">Traffic Congestion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Phenomenon of slowed or blocked traffic, linked to peak-hour trips, events and urban deliveries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traffic Accidents">
  <data key="d0">Traffic Accidents</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Road crashes causing injuries and deaths, linked to speed, driver behavior, poor vehicle physical condition and braking distances longer than the distance available to stop safely.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reaction Time">
  <data key="d0">Reaction Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The time a driver needs to react, estimated at 1.8 to 2.5 seconds and generally at 2 seconds in France. Fatigue, alcohol, drugs and distraction affect it, and it is a human factor in stopping distance.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node 12 with linguistic values Too Long, Long, Normal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visibility">
  <data key="d0">Visibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A human-related factor in stopping distance. Fog, rain, snow, hail and night reduce visibility and influence reaction time and the distance travelled.&lt;SEP&gt;Variable with states Good, Medium, Bad; parent node of Driver reaction time.&lt;SEP&gt;Input variable describing visibility conditions (e.g., medium) used to condition the probability of driver fatigue.&lt;SEP&gt;Parent node of Driver Reaction-Time with values Good, Medium and Bad.&lt;SEP&gt;Parent node of Driver's reaction in the network. In the Axiom 2 verification (Table IX), raising Visibility from its 50% prior to 70% increased the driver reaction-time node from 77.7% to 80%, and lowering it to 40% decreased that node to 65%. In the Axiom 3 verification (Table X), raising Visibility alone to 100% changed driver reaction time by 14.5%, from 50.5% to 65.0%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Adhesion">
  <data key="d0">Road Adhesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Grip of the road surface, which depends on the surface type (paving stones, sand, earth, draining or non-draining tarmac). Together with the weather, road adhesion forms the tire-road coefficient of friction.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node 9 with linguistic values Good, Medium, Bad.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient Of Friction">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient Of Friction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Friction between tires and road, determined by road surface type and weather, which influences stopping distance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Grade">
  <data key="d0">Road Grade</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Slope of the road. Uphill slopes improve braking distance and steering grip, and downhill slopes penalise them.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Braking Distance">
  <data key="d0">Braking Distance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Distance needed to stop a vehicle; an intermediate effect in the BN influenced by kinetic energy.&lt;SEP&gt;The distance a vehicle travels from the moment the driver applies the brakes until the vehicle comes to a complete stop. Accidents often occur when braking distance exceeds the distance available to stop safely.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node 10 with linguistic values Short, Normal, Long.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vehicle Kinetic Energy">
  <data key="d0">Vehicle Kinetic Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Energy stored by a moving vehicle that the braking system must absorb to stop the vehicle; a technical factor in stopping distance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stopping Distance Estimation Rule">
  <data key="d0">Stopping Distance Estimation Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">An approximate rule that estimates stopping distance by multiplying the tens figure of the vehicle speed by itself, for example 12 x 12 = 144 m at 120 km/h. The results are approximate because various factors affect braking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peak Hour">
  <data key="d0">Peak Hour</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Period of highest traffic, such as the morning peak between 6am and 9am. Home-to-work trips, home-school trips and urban deliveries cause congestion during peak hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Accident Occurrence">
  <data key="d0">Road Accident Occurrence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The occurrence of road/vehicle accidents, the target phenomenon modeled by the Bayesian Network.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kinetic Energy">
  <data key="d0">Kinetic Energy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Energy stored by a moving vehicle; higher kinetic energy implies longer braking distance. An intermediate effect in the BN.&lt;SEP&gt;Bayesian network node 2 with linguistic values Low, Medium, High.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
