  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref [72]">
  <data key="d0">Ref [72]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [72] for fuzzy logic reducing expert questions and probability table generation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Braking system transforms kinetic energy into heat">
  <data key="d0">Braking system transforms kinetic energy into heat</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The overall function of the braking system is therefore to transform kinetic energy into heat energy [64], [65].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tire pressure influences braking distance">
  <data key="d0">Tire pressure influences braking distance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Tire's pressure have a remarkable influence on the braking distance. [66]</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brake wear and system condition increase stopping distance">
  <data key="d0">Brake wear and system condition increase stopping distance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The wear of the brake, as well as the age of the hydraulic fluid, which is sensitive to heat, the temperature of the discs, pads, drums and linings after heavy use, will increase the stopping distance. [67]</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Higher vehicle weight increases stopping distance">
  <data key="d0">Higher vehicle weight increases stopping distance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The higher is the weight, the longer the stopping distances [68], [69], [70].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Too many conditional probabilities make expert elicitation impractical">
  <data key="d0">Too many conditional probabilities make expert elicitation impractical</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Moreover, the extensive number of conditional probabilities in this developed network that equal to 56.739.144, made it impractical to rely on expert knowledge for an evaluation [71].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy logic reduces expert questions and probability table generation">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy logic reduces expert questions and probability table generation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy logic permits to minimize the number of questions asked to experts while also to reduce the generation of probability tables [72].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weight Of The Vehicle">
  <data key="d0">Weight Of The Vehicle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An intermediate effect in the Bayesian Network. Heavier vehicles have longer stopping distances, as cited from [68], [69] and [70].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tire State And Condition">
  <data key="d0">Tire State And Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An input node in the Bayesian Network. Tire pressure has a remarkable influence on braking distance, as cited from [66].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="State And Condition Of The Braking System">
  <data key="d0">State And Condition Of The Braking System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An input node in the Bayesian Network. It covers brake wear, the age of the heat-sensitive hydraulic fluid, and the temperature of discs, pads, drums and linings after heavy use. These factors increase stopping distance.&lt;SEP&gt;Model variable with states Good, Medium and Bad.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian Network Input Nodes">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Network Input Nodes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">First level of the Bayesian Network, listed in Table II(a). It has 67 nodes that indirectly influence accident occurrence, such as fatigue, speed, weather condition, road lighting, alcohol or drug consumption and tire state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian Network Intermediate Effects">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Network Intermediate Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Second level of the Bayesian Network, listed in Table II(b). It covers kinetic energy, planning parameters, road adhesion, braking distance, reaction time, delivery period, distraction, occurrence of traffic-blocking events, visibility and weight of the vehicle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bayesian Network Final Impacts">
  <data key="d0">Bayesian Network Final Impacts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Third level of the Bayesian Network, listed in Table II(c). Its factors directly and adversely contribute to accidents: driver style, physical vehicle condition, road design, road surface condition, traffic flow, driver condition and stopping distance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Higher kinetic energy produces longer braking distance">
  <data key="d0">Higher kinetic energy produces longer braking distance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Relationship stated in the text without a citation: "the higher the kinetic energy, the longer the braking distance, because a higher kinetic energy requires more work to be done by the brakes to bring the vehicle to a stop."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy VIKOR">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy VIKOR</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fuzzy multi-criteria method used within a three-phase model for managing supply chain sustainability risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference [72]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [72]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited work showing that fuzzy logic minimizes the number of expert questions and reduces probability table generation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference [73]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [73]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited work on fuzzy logic in control systems for industrial processes, consumer products and vehicles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference [74]">
  <data key="d0">Reference [74]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited article proposing coordinated fuzzy-logic control of multiple Photovoltaic Static Compensator systems. It shows the fuzzy controller improves voltage profile dynamics.</data>
