<node id="Fuzzy Inference Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Inference Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Inference mechanism of the fuzzy system. In the worked example, it determines the state of the "Driver reaction time" node from the states of its parent nodes Driver Condition, Distracted? and Visibility in order to generate CPTs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fatigue">
  <data key="d0">Fatigue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Model variable with states No-Fatigue, Mild-Fatigue, Moderate-Fatigue, Severe-Fatigue and Exhausted.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Speed">
  <data key="d0">Speed</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Model variable with states Low, Medium and High.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weather Condition">
  <data key="d0">Weather Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Model variable with states Clear, Rainy, Foggy and Snowy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Truck Fill Rate">
  <data key="d0">Truck Fill Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Model variable with states Minimal, Low, Moderate and Full.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Lighting">
  <data key="d0">Road Lighting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Model variable with states Bright, Dim, Low Light and Obscurity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Max Aggregation Technique">
  <data key="d0">Max Aggregation Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Technique used to combine the conclusions of activated rules into an aggregate value per state; e.g. Too Long = 0.995, Long = 0.008, Normal = 0.015.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Reaction-Time">
  <data key="d0">Driver Reaction-Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Output variable/node with states Too Long, Long and Normal, whose value depends on parent nodes Driver Condition, Distracted and Visibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Distraction">
  <data key="d0">Driver Distraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input variable indicating whether the driver is distracted; in the computed example the driver is not distracted.&lt;SEP&gt;Parent node "Distracted" of Driver Reaction-Time with values Distracted, Half-Distracted and Not-Distracted.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Degree Of Membership">
  <data key="d0">Degree Of Membership</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Membership degree of each fuzzy subset of the output variable Driver Reaction-Time for the activated rules (Table V), e.g. R23 yields 0.995 for Too Long.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Probability Normalization">
  <data key="d0">Probability Normalization</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedure that divides each state's aggregated value by the sum of the values of all states to obtain conditional probabilities. For example, P(Drivers' Reaction-time = Too Long | Driver Condition = medium, not distracted, Visibility = medium) = 0.995/(0.995+0.008+0.015) = 0.98.&lt;SEP&gt;Procedure that divides each state's rule conclusion by the sum of the conclusions over all states so that the probabilities of a variable's states sum to 1. For example, P(congestion = high | density = high, flooding attacks = many) = 0.607/(0.001 + 0.044 + 0.607) = 0.931. A minimal value of 0.001 replaces zero possibilities because every state must remain possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7716e61d153c4d281900275dc536a626-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf&lt;SEP&gt;Symbiotic_Combination_of_a_Bayesian_Network_and_Fu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501552</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rule Activation">
  <data key="d0">Rule Activation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Triggering of the fuzzy rules that match the given input. For the input Driver Condition = medium, not distracted and Visibility = medium, 18 of the 27 rules are activated, and their conclusions are consolidated in Table V.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuzzy Subsets Of Driver Reaction-Time">
  <data key="d0">Fuzzy Subsets Of Driver Reaction-Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Linguistic states of the output variable Driver Reaction-Time: Too Long (0.2), Long (0.5) and Normal (0.8). The aggregated values for the example input are 0.995, 0.008 and 0.015 respectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Drivers' Fatigue">
  <data key="d0">Drivers' Fatigue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variable representing a driver's fatigue state, taking values such as Long, Tool long and Normal, with associated weights (0.5, 0.2, 0.8) in the rule table.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditional Probability Computation">
  <data key="d0">Conditional Probability Computation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedure computing P(Drivers' Fatigue | Driver Condition = medium, Driver is not distracted, Visibility = medium) by normalizing rule values, e.g., 0.008/(0.995+0.008+0.015) = 0.01 for Long and 0.015/(0.995+0.008+0.015) = 0.01 for Normal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fatigue Rule Base">
  <data key="d0">Fatigue Rule Base</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Set of rules (R19–R27) that map input conditions to Drivers' Fatigue outcomes. Each outcome carries a weight: Long (0.5), Tool long (0.2) and Normal (0.8). Each rule also has a value: Long rules (R19, R22, R25) have 0.008, Tool long rules (R20, R21, R24) have 0.015, R23 (Tool long) has 0.995, and Normal rules (R26, R27) have 0.008.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Axiom Validation Approach">
  <data key="d0">Three-Axiom Validation Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Model validation approach based on three axioms: a change in a parent node must affect the child node's probability; alterations in parent probability distributions must consistently affect the child; the combined effect of all parent nodes must exceed the influence of any single parent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Occurrence of Accident">
  <data key="d0">Occurrence of Accident</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Final outcome variable of the BN with states no accident, possible injury and fatal injury; e.g. 98% no accident in S1, 98% fatal injury in S2, 92.30% no accident in S3, 88.20% possible injury in S4.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Road Accident Risk Assessment">
  <data key="d0">Road Accident Risk Assessment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problem of assessing the likelihood of accident occurrence based on road and traffic, driver, delivery planning and vehicle condition parameters.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref [100]">
  <data key="d0">Ref [100]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [100]; originally proposed the three axioms used for validating Bayesian Networks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-a503621d914a716d7d3a5232c23b6bc6-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Paper_101-Enhancing_Predictive_Analysis_of_Vehicle_Accident_Risk.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501134</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
