<node id="TVAE">
  <data key="d0">TVAE</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Existing generative model for tabular data that the framework complements rather than competes with.&lt;SEP&gt;VAE-based alternative to CTGAN for tabular data generation.&lt;SEP&gt;Learned generative model for tabular data mentioned alongside CTGAN.&lt;SEP&gt;Tabular variational autoencoder used for synthetic tabular data generation, evaluated alongside CTGAN.&lt;SEP&gt;Tabular variational autoencoder synthetic data generator. Kiran et al. (2025) analyze its challenges and limitations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Modern IT infrastructures generate large volumes of operational data">
  <data key="d0">Modern IT infrastructures generate large volumes of operational data</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Modern IT infrastructures generate large volumes of operational data (Bautista et al., 2019), yet high-quality datasets for analysis and predictive maintenance remain difficult to obtain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generative models for tabular data have recently advanced but offer limited control over distributions and rules">
  <data key="d0">Generative models for tabular data have recently advanced but offer limited control over distributions and rules</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Despite recent advances in generative models for tabular data (Stoian et al., 2025), many approaches offer limited control over explicit distributions and domain rules.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Existing generative models include CTGAN and TVAE">
  <data key="d0">Existing generative models include CTGAN and TVAE</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">This framework is not intended to compete with existing generative models such as CTGAN (Xu et al., 2019) or TVAE (Kiran et al., 2025), but rather to complement them...</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parameter File">
  <data key="d0">Parameter File</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Spreadsheet (Excel or CSV) configuration defining the tabular schema, admissible values, and target marginal distribution percentages per variable; percentages for each variable must sum to 100%.&lt;SEP&gt;Input file of the framework that defines variables, admissible values or intervals, and target percentages or distributions.&lt;SEP&gt;An input file that defines variables, their values, and their percentage distributions. It implicitly constrains admissible ranges, and these bounds act as hard constraints during sampling.&lt;SEP&gt;Input specification file declaring per-variable target marginals (percentages and numeric intervals) that candidate rows must respect.&lt;SEP&gt;File defining the target distributions for each variable used by the generation framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditions File">
  <data key="d0">Conditions File</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Configuration input encoding logical exclusion rules that prevent invalid combinations of values across variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Input file of the framework that encodes forbidden combinations of variable values as logical exclusion rules.&lt;SEP&gt;A CSV file with a single column named conditions. It specifies row-level exclusion rules as conjunctions of predicates of the form Variable:operation:value, joined with #. Each row defines one forbidden configuration.&lt;SEP&gt;Input file of rule predicates that is transformed into GPU-compatible numeric encodings (variable identifier, operator code, one or two values) for parallel rule evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Input file with a single "conditions" column. It is compiled into conjunctions of atomic predicates with well-defined operators. Categorical terms are mapped to encodings for fast lookup, and numeric comparisons use tolerances to avoid floating-point artifacts.&lt;SEP&gt;File (Table 5) in which each row encodes a forbidden configuration as a conjunction of predicates, e.g., excluding active low-memory instances in EU-Central, active instances with CPU usage below 2%, and dev workloads with CPU usage below 15%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Evaluation Pipeline">
  <data key="d0">Evaluation Pipeline</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Validation pipeline that checks distribution fidelity, constraint satisfaction, null rows, and generation time for both generation strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distribution Fidelity">
  <data key="d0">Distribution Fidelity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Degree to which generated synthetic data match the user-defined target distributions. Experiments show minimal deviations for both methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspect measuring whether generated data matches target distributions; both methods match target distributions for all variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation aspect measuring whether generated data match target distributions for all variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constraint Satisfaction">
  <data key="d0">Constraint Satisfaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adherence of generated data to logical exclusion rules and defined intervals.&lt;SEP&gt;Requirement that generated data satisfy all logical exclusion rules. Both generation methods satisfy all exclusion rules in the experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IT Infrastructure Case Study">
  <data key="d0">IT Infrastructure Case Study</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Case study dataset on IT infrastructure used to demonstrate and evaluate the framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Case study of synthetic IT infrastructure data with five parameters, including flexible memory-usage intervals, used to evaluate the framework on 1,000,000 generated rows.&lt;SEP&gt;Case study with 5 features, 3 conditions, 1,000,000 rows generated; sequential 8990 s vs parallel 20 s.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smart Building Case Study">
  <data key="d0">Smart Building Case Study</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Case study dataset on smart building data used to demonstrate the framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Case study with 31 features, 14 conditions, 1,000,000 rows; sequential 20101 s vs parallel 42 s.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generative Models for Tabular Data">
  <data key="d0">Generative Models for Tabular Data</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class of models for generating tabular data. These models have advanced recently but often offer limited control over explicit distributions and domain rules.</data>
