<node id="VAE">
  <data key="d0">VAE</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Variational autoencoders, cited as a generative method against which accelerated diffusion models offer a favorable quality-diversity trade-off.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High-Energy Physics">
  <data key="d0">High-Energy Physics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application domain in which Cardoso et al. parallelized GAN training.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Explainability and Auditability">
  <data key="d0">Explainability and Auditability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transparency properties of the specification-driven framework that make the data generation process easy to understand and justify, supporting accountability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strict Rule Enforcement">
  <data key="d0">Strict Rule Enforcement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property of the specification-driven framework that ensures logical constraints and operational dependencies are respected throughout data generation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Survey reviews diffusion approaches for tabular data from 2015 to 2024">
  <data key="d0">Survey reviews diffusion approaches for tabular data from 2015 to 2024</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Surveys and recent reviews (Z. Li et al., 2025) have detailed current progress, challenges, and future directions in diffusion approaches tailored to tabular data, which systematically reviews nearly all relevant works from 2015 to 2024, and organizes them by application (augmentation, imputation, privacy, anomaly detection).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-device parallelization retains generative fidelity while improving efficiency">
  <data key="d0">Multi-device parallelization retains generative fidelity while improving efficiency</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Similarly, “Accelerating GAN training using highly parallel hardware on public cloud” confirms that parallelization over multiple devices retains generative fidelity while improving efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sequential Generation Approach">
  <data key="d0">Sequential Generation Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Baseline data generation method providing transparency and ease of verification, establishing baseline performance and correctness in reproducing distributions and constraints.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU-Accelerated Parallel Generation Approach">
  <data key="d0">GPU-Accelerated Parallel Generation Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Parallel strategy that leverages GPU-based parallel computing to accelerate sampling and constraint checking, enabling generation of millions of records within seconds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU Acceleration">
  <data key="d0">GPU Acceleration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Use of GPUs' massive parallelism to decompose complex computational problems into smaller tasks, suitable for high-throughput ML and DL workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical Fidelity">
  <data key="d0">Statistical Fidelity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Degree to which generated data preserves user-defined statistical distributions; hypothesized to be comparable across sequential and parallel approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPUs are ideal for growing computational requirements of data-driven applications">
  <data key="d0">GPUs are ideal for growing computational requirements of data-driven applications</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement attributed to reviewed authors: "this capability makes GPUs an ideal choice for handling the growing computational requirements of modern data-driven applications, especially as datasets and model complexity continue to increase."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU acceleration reduces computation time without affecting constraint adherence">
  <data key="d0">GPU acceleration reduces computation time without affecting constraint adherence</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothesis stated in the text: "GPU acceleration reduces computation time without affecting constraint adherence".</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lack of explicit distribution control and constraint enforcement in prior approaches">
  <data key="d0">Lack of explicit distribution control and constraint enforcement in prior approaches</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "The limitations identified in these approaches -particularly the lack of explicit distribution control and constraint enforcement -motivate the specification-driven framework."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IT Infrastructures">
  <data key="d0">IT Infrastructures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application field where large-scale datasets are required for model training, simulation, and benchmarking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Other hypotheses: constraint complexity increases execution time more slowly in parallel strategy">
  <data key="d0">Other hypotheses: constraint complexity increases execution time more slowly in parallel strategy</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothesis stated in the text: "constraint complexity increases execution time more slowly in the parallel strategy".</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Both approaches maintain comparable statistical fidelity when definitions are well specified">
  <data key="d0">Both approaches maintain comparable statistical fidelity when definitions are well specified</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothesis stated in the text: "both approaches maintain comparable statistical fidelity when definitions are well specified."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exclusion Rules">
  <data key="d0">Exclusion Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Logical rules encoded in the conditions file that prevent invalid combinations of values across variables during synthetic data generation.&lt;SEP&gt;Constraints encoded in the conditions file that forbid certain configurations; all were satisfied by both generation methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marginal Distributions">
  <data key="d0">Marginal Distributions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Target per-variable distributions specified in the parameter file, expressed as admissible values with associated percentages summing to 100%; supports categorical labels, fixed numeric values, and numeric intervals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Data Structure and Validation Workflow">
  <data key="d0">Input Data Structure and Validation Workflow</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 1 of the paper illustrating the structure of the input data (parameter and conditions files) and their validation workflow.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Target Percentage Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Target Percentage Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specification of admissible values for a variable together with their associated target percentages; the sum of all percentages for each variable is constrained to equal 100%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Categorical Labels">
  <data key="d0">Categorical Labels</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supported specification type used for discrete variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fixed Numeric Values">
  <data key="d0">Fixed Numeric Values</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supported specification type allowing fixed numeric values for a variable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Numeric Intervals">
  <data key="d0">Numeric Intervals</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supported specification type for continuous ranges of variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Percentage Range Resolution">
  <data key="d0">Percentage Range Resolution</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedure by which intervals expressed with percentage ranges (e.g., 10_15) are resolved during generation to ensure the final distribution is statistically consistent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reserved Tokens">
  <data key="d0">Reserved Tokens</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Special characters reserved for structural encoding that cannot be used in raw labels, avoiding parsing ambiguity and ensuring compatibility across processing stages; summarized in Table 2.&lt;SEP&gt;Structural symbols used in the parameter and conditions files. ':' separates a value from its percentage, '-' represents numeric intervals, and '#' denotes logical conjunction. These tokens cannot appear in variable names, labels, or values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical Consistency">
  <data key="d0">Statistical Consistency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property ensuring the final generated distribution is consistent with the specified percentages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parsing Ambiguity">
  <data key="d0">Parsing Ambiguity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problem in which special characters in raw labels could be confused with structural encoding. Reserving certain special characters as tokens avoids this problem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Encoding">
  <data key="d0">Structural Encoding</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Encoding scheme that uses reserved special characters to represent structure in variable specifications. It ensures compatibility across processing stages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Percentage Sum Constraint">
  <data key="d0">Percentage Sum Constraint</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Constraint that the sum of all target percentages for each variable must equal 100%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Del Gobbo, 2025">
  <data key="d0">Del Gobbo, 2025</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work by Del Gobbo (2025) cited in support of Python as a suitable language for data science and configurable synthetic data generation systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Work by Del Gobbo (2025) cited regarding Python's suitability for developing configurable and extensible synthetic data generation systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Joshi &amp; Tiwari, 2023">
  <data key="d0">Joshi &amp; Tiwari, 2023</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work by Joshi and Tiwari (2023) cited in support of Python as a suitable language for data science and configurable synthetic data generation systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Work by Joshi and Tiwari (2023) cited regarding Python's libraries, readability and modularity for synthetic data generation systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Python is an ideal choice for developing configurable and extensible synthetic data generation systems">
  <data key="d0">Python is an ideal choice for developing configurable and extensible synthetic data generation systems</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Python offers extensive libraries for data manipulation and numerical analysis while maintaining readability and modularity, which makes it an ideal choice for developing configurable and extensible synthetic data generation systems (Del Gobbo, 2025; Joshi &amp; Tiwari, 2023)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Step Validation Process">
  <data key="d0">Two-Step Validation Process</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Validation applied to each candidate row. The row is first checked against the percentage constraints and then evaluated against the logical rules. It is accepted only if both checks pass; otherwise it is discarded.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Data Validation Step">
  <data key="d0">Input Data Validation Step</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Pre-generation validation of the input files. It checks syntax, use of reserved tokens, type consistency, percentage feasibility, and well-formed, non-overlapping, non-contiguous intervals. For the conditions file, it also checks predicate syntax, variable references, operations, and value domains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Percentage Feasibility">
  <data key="d0">Percentage Feasibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Requirement on the percentages. Fixed percentages must sum to 100%. When intervals are present, the fixed percentages plus the interval lower bounds must be at most 100%, and the fixed percentages plus the upper bounds must be at least 100%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parallel Generation">
  <data key="d0">Parallel Generation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A generation mode in which conditions are enforced at scale. The conditions are parsed once at initialization and converted into numeric and categorical checks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Predicate">
  <data key="d0">Predicate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The basic unit of a rule in the conditions file. Each predicate has the form Variable:operation:value. The operation comes from a predefined set that includes equality, inequality, order relations and interval membership. When predicates are joined with '#', all of them must hold at the same time for the rule to apply.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sequential Generation">
  <data key="d0">Sequential Generation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A generation mode, alongside parallel generation, in which the conditions are enforced at scale after being parsed once at initialization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tolerance Thresholds">
  <data key="d0">Tolerance Thresholds</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Thresholds used to evaluate condition operations on continuous variables. Categorical operations are instead resolved through encoded lookups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interval Validation">
  <data key="d0">Interval Validation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A check that numeric intervals in the parameter file are well-formed, non-overlapping and non-contiguous, because connected ranges would make sampling ambiguous.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hard Constraints">
  <data key="d0">Hard Constraints</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bounds implicitly defined by the parameter file, which omits values outside the defined domains. During sampling these bounds keep the generated data consistent with the intended distributions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Garland et al., 2008">
  <data key="d0">Garland et al., 2008</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work by Garland et al. (2008) on GPU parallel computing with CUDA, cited for scalable parallelism and near-native GPU execution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oden, 2020">
  <data key="d0">Oden, 2020</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work by Oden (2020) cited regarding Numba enabling Python computational kernels with near-native GPU execution speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Python is ideal for configurable and extensible synthetic data generation systems">
  <data key="d0">Python is ideal for configurable and extensible synthetic data generation systems</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "offers extensive libraries for data manipulation and numerical analysis while maintaining readability and modularity, which makes it an ideal choice for developing configurable and extensible synthetic data generation systems (Del Gobbo, 2025; Joshi &amp; Tiwari, 2023)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CUDA parallelism enables scaling to millions of tabular records without compromising performance">
  <data key="d0">CUDA parallelism enables scaling to millions of tabular records without compromising performance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "This parallelism allows the system to scale efficiently from small datasets to millions of tabular records without compromising performance (Garland et al., 2008)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Numba allows Python kernels to achieve near-native GPU execution speed">
  <data key="d0">Numba allows Python kernels to achieve near-native GPU execution speed</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "This makes it possible to write computational kernels in Python while achieving near-native GPU execution speed (Garland et al., 2008; Oden, 2020)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Just-In-Time Compilation">
  <data key="d0">Just-In-Time Compilation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Compilation technique used by Numba to turn Python functions into highly optimized machine code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sequential Data Generation Baseline">
  <data key="d0">Sequential Data Generation Baseline</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Single-threaded rejection-sampling workflow producing one record at a time, enforcing a statistical contract (target marginals) and a logical contract (row-level exclusion rules) via percentage verification and conditions validation; fully reproducible reference implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rejection Sampling">
  <data key="d0">Rejection Sampling</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Sampling approach in which candidate rows drawn from declared marginals are rejected and resampled if they match any forbidden pattern.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parallel Data Generation">
  <data key="d0">Parallel Data Generation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed GPU-accelerated method embedding the entire generation process into a GPU kernel, with each thread generating a fixed batch of rows (e.g., 1,000 rows per thread), parallelizing sampling and constraint enforcement while preserving correctness.&lt;SEP&gt;Large-scale concurrent generation of data rows across GPU threads, including data preparation, thread-level field generation, percentage verification and conditions validation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical Contract">
  <data key="d0">Statistical Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specification defined by per-variable target marginals (percentages and numeric intervals).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logical Contract">
  <data key="d0">Logical Contract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specification defined by row-level exclusion rules that forbid certain value combinations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marginal Gaps">
  <data key="d0">Marginal Gaps</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Verification metric reporting observed minus target percentages during and after generation, alongside rule-hit frequencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CUDA accelerates random sampling, distribution enforcement, and constraint validation via thousands of concurrent threads">
  <data key="d0">CUDA accelerates random sampling, distribution enforcement, and constraint validation via thousands of concurrent threads</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "By enabling thousands of threads to run concurrently, CUDA significantly accelerates core operations such as random sampling, distribution enforcement, and constraint validation. This parallelism allows the system to scale efficiently from small datasets to millions of tabular records without compromising performance (Garland et al., 2008)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU Kernel">
  <data key="d0">GPU Kernel</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Kernel function executed on the GPU over a dynamically configured grid of blocks and threads; each thread generates 1,000 rows, threads are grouped in blocks of 10, and both sampling and rule checking run entirely on the GPU.&lt;SEP&gt;Computational kernel executed in parallel on the GPU; the proposed acceleration embeds the entire generation process into a GPU kernel, and Numba allows such kernels to be written in Python.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Schema">
  <data key="d0">Internal Schema</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Structure established by parsing the specification once before generation, comprising variable names, types (categorical, numeric-point, numeric-interval), admissible domains, and normalized target percentages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Percentage Verification">
  <data key="d0">Percentage Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Step in the parallel generation workflow verifying that generated values match target percentage weights.&lt;SEP&gt;First validation step in the sequential baseline: each candidate row is proposed from per-variable target marginals, which guarantees it is permissible under the percentage specifications of the parameter file.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conditions Validation">
  <data key="d0">Conditions Validation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Step in the parallel generation workflow that checks generated rows against encoded rules, executed concurrently on GPU threads.&lt;SEP&gt;Second validation step in the sequential baseline: each candidate is evaluated against compiled predicates from the conditions file. If any rule is fully satisfied, the candidate is rejected and resampled.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rule-Hit Frequencies">
  <data key="d0">Rule-Hit Frequencies</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Verification statistic reported by the baseline that identifies which constraints most often trigger rejections.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nested List Structure">
  <data key="d0">Nested List Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input specification format adapted for GPU processing, organized as a list of lists of lists so that every thread can access variables and their parameters in a uniform way.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fixed Random Seed">
  <data key="d0">Fixed Random Seed</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Reproducibility mechanism that, together with a configuration snapshot, makes the single-threaded baseline fully reproducible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parameter Record Encoding">
  <data key="d0">Parameter Record Encoding</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Data preparation where each variable's parameters are stored as five-field records (coded variable name, value, upper bound or epsilon placeholder, floating-point flag, target percentage weight), padded with zero-records to satisfy GPU array alignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Condition Numerical Encoding">
  <data key="d0">Condition Numerical Encoding</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Transformation of the conditions file into GPU-compatible numeric arrays: predicates become variable identifier, operator code and one or two values; strings mapped to integer codes; interval checks as paired bounds; rules padded to uniform size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU Array Alignment">
  <data key="d0">GPU Array Alignment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Requirement that device arrays be regular, motivating padding of parameter sub-lists and rules to uniform sizes to avoid irregular memory access.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fig. 3 Parallel Data Generation Workflow">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 3 Parallel Data Generation Workflow</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 3 of the article showing the workflow of parallel data generation: data preparation, thread-level field generation, percentage verification, and conditions validation executed concurrently across GPU threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread-Level Field Generation">
  <data key="d0">Thread-Level Field Generation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Stage of the parallel generation workflow in which each GPU thread generates field values; each thread is responsible for generating 1,000 rows.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread and Block Grid Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Thread and Block Grid Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Dynamic configuration of the GPU kernel grid according to the number of rows n to generate: the total number of threads is n/1000, threads are grouped into blocks of 10, and the number of blocks is computed by a formula.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU-Resident Sampling and Rule Checking">
  <data key="d0">GPU-Resident Sampling and Rule Checking</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "By relying only on numerical representations, the system ensures that both sampling and rule checking can be executed entirely on the GPU, eliminating costly data transfers and enabling efficient large-scale parallel generation."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Haramoto et al., 2022">
  <data key="d0">Haramoto et al., 2022</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Work by Haramoto et al. (2022) cited as the reference for the XOROSHIRO128+ random number generator.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XOROSHIRO128+">
  <data key="d0">XOROSHIRO128+</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Pseudo-random number generator used from Numba's CUDA random library to produce random values with statistical independence between GPU threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Numba CUDA Random Library">
  <data key="d0">Numba CUDA Random Library</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Numba's CUDA random library providing the XOROSHIRO128+ generator used in the GPU kernel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XOROSHIRO128+ ensures statistical independence between GPU threads">
  <data key="d0">XOROSHIRO128+ ensures statistical independence between GPU threads</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Random values are produced using the XOROSHIRO128+ generator (Haramoto et al., 2022) from Numba's CUDA random library, ensuring statistical independence between threads."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adaptive GPU Grid Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Adaptive GPU Grid Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Computation of blocksPerGrid as (numThreads + threadsPerBlock - 1) / threadsPerBlock, distributing workload evenly across the GPU and scaling automatically with dataset size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="In-Kernel Constraint Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">In-Kernel Constraint Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation of compiled rules directly inside the GPU kernel, rejecting candidate rows that violate any rule immediately, minimizing host-device communication; includes an iteration cap with row reset to avoid infinite rejection loops.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Numerical Encoding and Decoding">
  <data key="d0">Numerical Encoding and Decoding</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Encoding of variables and labels as numerical codes during GPU execution, then decoding back to variable names, categorical labels and original numeric formats to produce an interpretable tabular dataset.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Marginal Percentage Allocation Check">
  <data key="d0">Marginal Percentage Allocation Check</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Local check guaranteeing that percentage allocation for each variable does not exceed declared limits, preventing drift in the marginal distributions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IT Infrastructure Parameters File">
  <data key="d0">IT Infrastructure Parameters File</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Parameter file (Table 4) emulating a realistic cloud and data-center environment with five variables-cpu_usage, region, status, memory_usage and workload-with admissible values or intervals and target percentages; used to demonstrate the generation framework and test constraint enforcement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constraint Enforcement">
  <data key="d0">Constraint Enforcement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Enforcement of logical dependencies between variables during data generation, tested using the IT infrastructure parameters file.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cloud and Data-Center Resource Management">
  <data key="d0">Cloud and Data-Center Resource Management</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Domain of virtualized computing systems, resource management and workload distribution emulated by the IT infrastructure case study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Discrete Sampling">
  <data key="d0">Discrete Sampling</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Generation mechanism for categorical variables in the framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Generation mechanism for categorical variables used by the framework, selecting categorical labels according to target percentages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501852</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Interval Generation">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Interval Generation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Generation mechanism for interval-based variables, handling fixed-percentage bins and flexible intervals with admissible percentage ranges.&lt;SEP&gt;Generation mechanism producing numeric values within declared intervals (e.g., cpu_usage and memory_usage ranges) according to target percentages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mixed-Type Processing">
  <data key="d0">Mixed-Type Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Generation mechanism handling mixed variable types in the framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Generation mechanism handling datasets combining categorical, numeric and interval-based variables in the same framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rejection Iteration Cap">
  <data key="d0">Rejection Iteration Cap</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Kernel mechanism that limits rejection attempts when constraints are tight; rows are reset after the cap if no feasible combination is found, avoiding infinite rejection loops.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cpu_usage">
  <data key="d0">Cpu_usage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variable in the IT infrastructure parameters file modeling the distribution of virtual CPU consumption (vCPUs) across active instances, a continuous indicator of computational load, with intervals 0.1-2.0 (20.5%), 2.1-50 (49.5%), 50.1-80 (10%) and 81-100 (20%).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Region">
  <data key="d0">Region</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Categorical variable denoting deployment zone or data-center location (EU-West 25%, US-East 10.5%, APAC 34.5%, EU-Central 30%), reflecting geographical heterogeneity influencing latency and capacity allocation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Status">
  <data key="d0">Status</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Categorical variable encoding the lifecycle state of each instance (Active 50%, Paused 50%), capturing dynamic behavior and resource transitions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Memory_usage">
  <data key="d0">Memory_usage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Numeric interval variable representing the distribution of RAM consumption (intervals from 0.1-4.0 up to 64.1-128.0), complementing cpu_usage to characterize workload intensity and efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Specification-Driven Generation Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Specification-Driven Generation Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Generation mechanism enabling users to precisely define and enforce statistical distributions and logical constraints, giving control that learned generative models do not guarantee.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exclusion Constraints">
  <data key="d0">Exclusion Constraints</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Formalization of domain rules as explicit exclusion constraints (conjunctions of predicates in the format Variable:operation:value joined by #) that ensure logical consistency and reproducibility of generated datasets.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IT Infrastructure Dataset">
  <data key="d0">IT Infrastructure Dataset</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Synthetic dataset (Table 4) with categorical, numeric and interval-based variables such as region, status, workload, cpu_usage and memory_usage; 1,000,000 rows generated to demonstrate the completeness of the framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sequential Generation Method">
  <data key="d0">Sequential Generation Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Sequential generation approach that took 8990 seconds to generate 1,000,000 rows; matches target distributions and satisfies all exclusion rules.&lt;SEP&gt;Baseline generation method of the framework; matches target distributions, satisfies all exclusion rules, took 8990 s to generate 1,000,000 rows and produced 119 null rows.&lt;SEP&gt;Baseline sequential implementation of the framework; required 8990 s to generate one million rows for the IT infrastructure case.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parallel Generation Method">
  <data key="d0">Parallel Generation Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">GPU-parallelized generation approach that generated 1,000,000 rows in 20 seconds, a 449.5x speedup over the sequential method, while preserving distributions and constraints.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU Parallelization">
  <data key="d0">GPU Parallelization</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Parallel computing on GPUs used to achieve scalability and a 449.5x speedup in synthetic data generation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Distribution Verification">
  <data key="d0">Distribution Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Data quality check ensuring parameter frequencies match target specifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Component of the framework that checks generated variables match target distributions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Condition Verification">
  <data key="d0">Condition Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Data quality check (condition checker) confirming that all exclusion rules and dependencies are respected and no forbidden configurations appear.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System Stress Testing">
  <data key="d0">System Stress Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reproducible stress testing under controlled conditions enabling large-scale benchmarking and validation of system performance, supported by the framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learned Generative Models">
  <data key="d0">Learned Generative Models</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Generative models that learn distributions from data and do not guarantee precise control over distributions and logical constraints.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Region Variable">
  <data key="d0">Region Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Categorical variable in the IT infrastructure dataset (e.g., EU-Central) whose empirical proportions match the specified percentages in both the parallel and sequential generation methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Status Variable">
  <data key="d0">Status Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Categorical variable (e.g., Active) in the IT infrastructure dataset; used in exclusion rules, e.g., excluding Active instances with CPU usage below 2%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload Variable">
  <data key="d0">Workload Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Categorical variable (e.g., dev) in the IT infrastructure dataset; the third exclusion rule removes development workloads with CPU usage below 15%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU Usage Variable">
  <data key="d0">CPU Usage Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interval-based variable (cpu_usage) in the IT infrastructure dataset; constrained by rules excluding Active instances below 2% and dev workloads below 15%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Memory Usage Variable">
  <data key="d0">Memory Usage Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interval-based variable (memory_usage) with fixed-percentage bins generated at the expected 15% and flexible intervals (4.1–16.0 GB, 16.1–24.0 GB, 64.1–128.0 GB) falling within admissible percentage ranges; the first exclusion rule removes Active records with 0.1–4.0 GB memory in EU-Central.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flexible Intervals">
  <data key="d0">Flexible Intervals</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interval bins with admissible percentage ranges rather than fixed percentages; both sequential and parallel samplers keep them within their prescribed bounds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logical Consistency">
  <data key="d0">Logical Consistency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Consistency between attributes in generated data, ensured by exclusion constraints and verified by condition verification so that no forbidden configurations appear.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parallel Method Is 449.5x Faster Than Sequential Method">
  <data key="d0">Parallel Method Is 449.5x Faster Than Sequential Method</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The sequential method took 8990 seconds, while the parallel method completed in 20 seconds, achieving a 449.5× speedup."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generated Dataset Respects Distributions And Logical Constraints">
  <data key="d0">Generated Dataset Respects Distributions And Logical Constraints</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: the parallel and sequential methods both reproduce the target distributions and satisfy all exclusion rules, with no forbidden configurations observed in the one-million-row dataset.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synthetic Tabular Data Generation Framework">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Tabular Data Generation Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed framework that generates large tabular datasets consistent with specifications encoded in parameter and conditions files, combining distribution verification and condition checking to control generator behavior without manual post-filtering.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GPU-Based Parallel Generation Method">
  <data key="d0">GPU-Based Parallel Generation Method</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">GPU-based parallel generation method of the framework; matches target distributions, satisfies all exclusion rules, took 20 s to generate 1,000,000 rows and produced 77 null rows, reducing generation time by several orders of magnitude.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Condition Checking">
  <data key="d0">Condition Checking</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Component of the framework that enforces exclusion rules so that no forbidden configurations are generated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Null Rows">
  <data key="d0">Null Rows</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Number of null rows generated, used as a robustness indicator: 119 for the sequential method and 77 for the parallel method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synthetic Data">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Data</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Large volumes of synthetic IT infrastructure data needed for stress testing, benchmarking, or machine learning experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parameter And Conditions Files">
  <data key="d0">Parameter And Conditions Files</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specification files encoding the parameters (variables and target distributions) and the conditions (exclusion rules) that the framework's generated tabular data must remain consistent with.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Manual Post-Filtering">
  <data key="d0">Manual Post-Filtering</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Manual filtering of generated data after generation, made unnecessary by the framework's combination of distribution verification and condition checking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generation Time">
  <data key="d0">Generation Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Performance measure for generating 1,000,000 rows: 8990 s for the sequential method and 20 s for the parallel method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-8a68f6918fb713b374b6980cb85c4169-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Vol.13.No.7.13.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791501853</data>
