<node id="CRYSTALS-Dilithium">
  <data key="d0">CRYSTALS-Dilithium</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Lattice-based post-quantum signature scheme from which ML-DSA is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;Lattice-based signature scheme from which ML-DSA is derived.&lt;SEP&gt;Prominent lattice-based digital signature scheme selected in the NIST post-quantum cryptography standardization process.&lt;SEP&gt;Lattice-based digital signature scheme introduced in reference [13].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-039</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502187</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shor's Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Shor's Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Quantum algorithm that enables large-scale attacks on classical public-key cryptography such as ECDSA.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantum algorithm that solves integer factorization and discrete logarithm problems in polynomial time.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantum algorithm that can break the ECDLP on a sufficiently powerful quantum computer.&lt;SEP&gt;Quantum algorithms for discrete logarithms and factoring, presented by P. W. Shor in 1994.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502187</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Signature Architecture">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Signature Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Architecture proposed by the paper. It stores ML-DSA-65 signatures and public keys on-ledger as application-level cryptographic evidence through an external post-quantum signing microservice, without modifying Fabric core binaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502187</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaincode-as-a-Service">
  <data key="d0">Chaincode-as-a-Service</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Deployment mechanism used to deploy the Go chaincode in the implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Deployment mode used for the Go chaincode in the evaluated network.&lt;SEP&gt;CCAAS deployment model in which chaincode runs in a dedicated container outside the peer processes. This decouples chaincode lifecycle management from the peer runtime.&lt;SEP&gt;Deployment model used for the Go chaincode in the evaluated network.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502192</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quantum-Safe Migration">
  <data key="d0">Quantum-Safe Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The critical challenge of migrating enterprise blockchain systems from classical to post-quantum signatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Transition of enterprise blockchain infrastructures to quantum-resistant cryptography, requiring joint evaluation of security, compatibility, throughput, latency, storage and cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ledger Storage Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Ledger Storage Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The main measured cost of the hybrid approach: active blocks are about 1.43x larger than the ECDSA P-256 baseline.&lt;SEP&gt;Growth in on-ledger block size, identified as the primary operational cost of adopting hybrid signatures. PQC hybrid blocks are 1.48x larger than ECDSA baseline blocks (p &lt; 0.05).&lt;SEP&gt;Growth in block and ledger size caused by adding the PQC layer. Application-level ML-DSA-65 signature and public-key material is about 20.9x larger than ECDSA P-256 artifacts. Complete Fabric blocks grow only about 1.43x relative to the functionally equivalent ECDSA baseline. The measured block-size ratio relative to the original Fabric baseline is 1.48x for the signing-intensive SignDocument workload. Shared protocol structures dilute the larger post-quantum artifacts, and aggregate overhead in mixed workloads depends on the proportion of signing transactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Growth of ledger storage caused by post-quantum signatures, highlighted as the principal operational trade-off. Proposed mitigations include compact encoding, signature aggregation, off-chain storage with on-chain commitments, and selective audit trails.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FIPS 204">
  <data key="d0">FIPS 204</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [4]. NIST standard that specifies ML-DSA for post-quantum digital signatures and results from the NIST PQC standardization process.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [4]. A NIST standard that defines the ML-DSA parameter sets ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 for security-strength categories 2, 3 and 5.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [4]. NIST standard that defines ML-DSA and its three parameter sets, ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87, corresponding to NIST security-strength categories 2, 3 and 5.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard specifying the ML-DSA post-quantum signature algorithm.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard defining the ML-DSA parameter sets, which fix signature and public-key sizes.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard defining the ML-DSA security categories and parameter sets.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ref [1] Hyperledger Fabric">
  <data key="d0">Ref [1] Hyperledger Fabric</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [1]. Reference describing the architecture of Hyperledger Fabric.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [1]; describes Hyperledger Fabric's modular architecture, identity model, endorsement policies, ordering service and execute-order-validate architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
