<node id="NIST">
  <data key="d0">NIST</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Organization that ran the post-quantum cryptography standardization process.&lt;SEP&gt;Standards body whose security-strength categories 2, 3 and 5 correspond to the ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 parameter sets defined in FIPS 204.&lt;SEP&gt;National Institute of Standards and Technology. It defines the security-strength categories used for the ML-DSA parameter sets and ran the post-quantum cryptography standardization process that produced ML-DSA from CRYSTALS-Dilithium.&lt;SEP&gt;Organization whose security categories (2, 3 and 5) classify the ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 parameter sets.&lt;SEP&gt;Standards body that standardized ML-DSA in FIPS 204.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperledger Fabric has a modular architecture separating execution, ordering, and validation">
  <data key="d0">Hyperledger Fabric has a modular architecture separating execution, ordering, and validation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Among these platforms, Hyperledger Fabric is widely adopted because of its modular architecture, membership-based identity model, configurable endorsement policies, and separation between transaction execution, ordering, and validation [1].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Classical ECDSA mechanisms are vulnerable to quantum attacks based on Shor's algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Classical ECDSA mechanisms are vulnerable to quantum attacks based on Shor's algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">While these mechanisms are efficient and mature, they are vulnerable to large-scale quantum attacks based on Shor's algorithm [2], [3].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NIST standardization produced ML-DSA in FIPS 204">
  <data key="d0">NIST standardization produced ML-DSA in FIPS 204</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The NIST post-quantum cryptography standardization process has resulted in standardized algorithms such as ML-DSA, specified in FIPS 204, for post-quantum digital signatures [4].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
