</node>
<node id="ECDSA P-256">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA P-256</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Elliptic-curve digital signature configuration on the P-256 curve. It serves as the functionally equivalent application-layer baseline. Active blocks in the ML-DSA-65 configuration are about 1.43x larger than ECDSA P-256 blocks for signing-intensive transactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Classical signature scheme used as a functionally equivalent experimental baseline; produces 72-byte DER signatures and 178-byte PEM public keys (96 and 240 Base64 characters).&lt;SEP&gt;Functionally equivalent application-layer ECDSA control used specifically for the storage analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Classical elliptic-curve digital signature algorithm on curve P-256. In the study, it serves as the comparison algorithm for ML-DSA-65, both as a latency-matched baseline and as an application-layer control that produces actual signatures and public keys.&lt;SEP&gt;Classical elliptic-curve signature scheme used as the functionally equivalent application-layer baseline; 72-byte DER signature and 178-byte PEM public key (336 Base64 characters total per transaction), mean active-block size 30,479 bytes and estimated storage of about 15,240 bytes/transaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Classical signature scheme used as the original Fabric baseline. Its baseline block size is 29,328 bytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Classical elliptic-curve signature scheme used as the application-layer baseline; its artifacts are about 20.9x smaller than the encoded ML-DSA-65 artifacts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Post-Quantum Signing Microservice">
  <data key="d0">Post-Quantum Signing Microservice</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">External microservice that the hybrid architecture uses to produce ML-DSA-65 signatures at the application layer, without modifying Fabric core binaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502194</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">ML-DSA (Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Algorithm) is a post-quantum digital signature algorithm standardized by NIST in FIPS 204. It is the standardized version of CRYSTALS-Dilithium and is often referred to simply as Dilithium. Its security rests on module lattices, making it a lattice-based scheme intended to resist attacks from quantum computers.

FIPS 204 specifies three parameter sets for ML-DSA: ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87, which correspond to increasing security categories. Each parameter set produces signatures of a fixed size, which makes artifact sizes predictable. By default, ML-DSA uses a hedged signing variant, and the standard also specifies a deterministic variant.

ML-DSA has been benchmarked for use in blockchain systems, where it has been compared with the classical ECDSA scheme and with hybrid signature approaches. In a targeted experiment, the three parameter sets were compared with one another under a SignDocument workload. Because their artifact sizes are fixed, their on-ledger storage requirements grow predictably as the security category increases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502197</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSP/X.509 Identities">
  <data key="d0">MSP/X.509 Identities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric's native membership service provider identities based on X.509 certificates. In the evaluated deployment they remain based on classical cryptography and are outside the scope of the post-quantum protection.</data>
