<node id="PQFabric">
  <data key="d0">PQFabric</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [8]. Prior work that integrates hybrid post-quantum mechanisms within Hyperledger Fabric's cryptographic and credential-management stack.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [8]; one of the closest works on post-quantum Hyperledger Fabric integration, modifying Fabric cryptographic components to support post-quantum mechanisms and quantum-resistant identities.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [8]. A post-quantum Hyperledger Fabric integration with PQC and hybrid signatures. According to Table 1 it partially addresses NIST alignment and ECDSA comparison. It has a real deployment that requires modifications to Fabric's cryptographic layer and only partially reports TPS/latency. It does not address CPU/memory, block size, payload variation or statistical rigor.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [8]. A work that integrates post-quantum cryptography deeply into Hyperledger Fabric by modifying Fabric's cryptographic and credential-processing components, including the BCCSP and MSP.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [8]. Approach that integrates post-quantum mechanisms deeply into Hyperledger Fabric's cryptographic and credential-processing stack, including BCCSP and MSP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abbas et al. [9]">
  <data key="d0">Abbas et al. [9]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [9]. Prior work that extends Hyperledger Fabric's identity-provisioning process to incorporate post-quantum key material into Fabric credentials.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [9]. A post-quantum blockchain study on Hyperledger Fabric that covers both KEMs and digital-signature schemes. The study reports monitoring with limited experimental details and presents block-size results mainly graphically.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [9]. A work that introduces post-quantum key material by modifying Fabric's certificate-generation tool (Cryptogen) and the X.509 credential-generation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [9]. Work adopting identity-oriented integration of post-quantum key material through modifications to the credential-generation/X.509 process in Hyperledger Fabric.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Migration References [5]-[7]">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Migration References [5]-[7]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Works cited as [5]–[7]. They discuss hybrid approaches that combine classical and post-quantum primitives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid approaches combine classical and post-quantum primitives for backward compatibility and quantum resistance">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid approaches combine classical and post-quantum primitives for backward compatibility and quantum resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Instead of immediately replacing all classical cryptographic mechanisms, hybrid approaches combine classical and post-quantum primitives to preserve backward compatibility while introducing resistance against future quantum adversaries [5]–[7]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Architecture">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-invasive application-layer hybrid signature architecture. It adds ML-DSA-65 signatures through an external signing microservice and stores the signature and public key on-ledger, while preserving Fabric's native ECDSA pipeline.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Membership Service Provider">
  <data key="d0">Membership Service Provider</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric identity component (MSP). It is left unchanged by the proposed architecture.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperledger Fabric component (MSP) through which ECDSA identity authentication is performed.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric MSP providing authenticated organization identifiers, obtained in chaincode via ctx.GetClientIdentity().GetMSPID().</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BCCSP">
  <data key="d0">BCCSP</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric cryptographic service provider. It is left unchanged by the proposed architecture.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric's cryptographic service provider component. PQFabric modifies it, and modifying it would be required for full quantum resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric's native Blockchain Crypto Service Provider; the application-layer PQC key binding is achieved without modifying it.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric's blockchain cryptographic service provider component, left unmodified by the proposed architecture but modified by deeper approaches such as PQFabric.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric cryptographic service provider layer requiring integration for full quantum resistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FastAPI">
  <data key="d0">FastAPI</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Framework used to build the ML-DSA-65 signing microservice.&lt;SEP&gt;Python web framework used to implement the PQC signing microservice.&lt;SEP&gt;Web framework, served with uvicorn, used to implement the external PQC signing microservice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="liboqs-python">
  <data key="d0">liboqs-python</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Python library used by the signing microservice for ML-DSA-65 operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Python binding of the liboqs library (version 0.14.1) used to implement the PQC signing microservice.&lt;SEP&gt;Python library (version 0.14.1) providing post-quantum cryptographic algorithms used by the PQC microservice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Controlled Benchmarking Methodology">
  <data key="d0">Controlled Benchmarking Methodology</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Compares a latency-matched ECDSA baseline with the PQC hybrid configuration. Topology, endorsement policy, ordering configuration, workloads, and payloads are kept identical across six primary scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Post-Quantum Migration">
  <data key="d0">Post-Quantum Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transition of blockchain infrastructures from classical to post-quantum cryptography. It is affected by larger signature sizes, payloads, block sizes, ledger growth, resource consumption, and latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of transitioning systems to quantum-resistant cryptography. The text states it is more complex in Fabric than in simpler transaction-signing models because cryptography is involved in many Fabric components.&lt;SEP&gt;Transition of enterprise blockchain systems to post-quantum cryptography; the paper discusses its implications for organizations.&lt;SEP&gt;Transition of blockchain infrastructure to quantum-safe cryptography; full-stack migration would require protecting identity, endorsements, TLS, ordering-service cryptography and client-side signing.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of migrating Hyperledger Fabric deployments to quantum-safe cryptography. The proposed scheme is described as an incremental application-layer step in this process.&lt;SEP&gt;Incremental transition of enterprise permissioned blockchain systems to post-quantum cryptography. The paper's findings support its feasibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Migration">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transition strategy that combines classical and post-quantum primitives during the migration period.&lt;SEP&gt;Migration strategy in which classical and post-quantum mechanisms coexist during the transition period, preserving compatibility while adding quantum protection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Post-Quantum Transaction Authentication">
  <data key="d0">Post-Quantum Transaction Authentication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-term authentication of blockchain transactions resistant to quantum adversaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Goal of migrating blockchain transaction authentication toward quantum-resistant signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Go Chaincode">
  <data key="d0">Go Chaincode</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Smart contract written in Go. It is deployed through Chaincode-as-a-Service in the implementation of the hybrid architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA-65 Signing Microservice">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA-65 Signing Microservice</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">External FastAPI-based post-quantum signing service built with liboqs-python. It produces ML-DSA-65 signatures at the application layer without modifying Fabric core binaries.&lt;SEP&gt;External signing service that integrates post-quantum signatures at the application layer. Two instances were run concurrently with round-robin request distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X.509 Certificates">
  <data key="d0">X.509 Certificates</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Certificate standard that Hyperledger Fabric uses as one of the components integrating cryptography.&lt;SEP&gt;Certificate format deeply integrated into the Hyperledger Fabric architecture. It is a constraint for post-quantum migration.&lt;SEP&gt;Certificates used in Fabric for client authentication and identity. They are part of the cryptographic surface affected by post-quantum migration.&lt;SEP&gt;Certificate format used by Fabric identities. It remains unchanged in the hybrid model and is modified in the credential-generation process by Abbas et al.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TLS">
  <data key="d0">TLS</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Transport security protocol used in Hyperledger Fabric channels. It is left unchanged by the proposed architecture.&lt;SEP&gt;Secure channel protocol used between peers, orderers, and clients. It remains classical in the proposed design.&lt;SEP&gt;Transport Layer Security, enabled in the Hyperledger Fabric network configuration.&lt;SEP&gt;Transport layer security used for Fabric inter-node communication; its certificates remain classical in the hybrid architecture.&lt;SEP&gt;Transport security layer in Hyperledger Fabric. It remains ECDSA-based in the evaluated design and would need integration for full quantum resistance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsement Signatures">
  <data key="d0">Endorsement Signatures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Signatures produced in Fabric's endorsement mechanism. They remain natively ECDSA-based in the proposed architecture.&lt;SEP&gt;Signatures in Hyperledger Fabric that become part of transaction envelopes, endorsement payloads and ledger records.&lt;SEP&gt;Signatures that Fabric peers produce. They remain ECDSA-only in the hybrid model, so peer-side Fabric signature verification is unchanged.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Signature Size Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Signature Size Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Post-quantum signatures are substantially larger than ECDSA signatures. This size may affect transaction payloads, endorsement messages, block sizes, ledger growth, resource consumption, and transaction latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Effect of larger signatures, which according to the text directly increase the size of the transaction envelope, the block payload, ledger storage and network propagation cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block-Size Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Block-Size Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Analysis of on-ledger block-size growth. It complements the six primary experimental scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;On-ledger analysis of active blocks (&gt;15 KB) for SignDocument transactions; mean block size 43,551±4,293 bytes (PQC, n=478) vs. 29,328±640 bytes (ECDSA, n=576), Welch t=6.422, p&lt;0.05, ratio 1.48x.&lt;SEP&gt;Supplementary analysis showing that active ML-DSA-65 blocks are about 1.43x larger than the application-layer ECDSA P-256 baseline for signing-intensive SignDocument transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptographic Primitive Benchmarking">
  <data key="d0">Cryptographic Primitive Benchmarking</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Benchmarking of the signature primitives themselves. It complements the system-level experimental scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Supplementary evaluation measuring isolated ML-DSA-65 signing (0.226 ms average) and verification (0.087 ms average over 1,000 iterations) times with liboqs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Statistical Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical evaluation of the results of repeated benchmark runs. It is used to provide evidence about the hybrid configuration's performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quantum Adversaries">
  <data key="d0">Quantum Adversaries</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Future attackers equipped with quantum computers. Hybrid approaches introduce resistance against such attackers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperledger Fabric Is Not Fully Quantum-Safe Under The Proposed Architecture">
  <data key="d0">Hyperledger Fabric Is Not Fully Quantum-Safe Under The Proposed Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Accordingly, the proposed architecture should be interpreted as an incremental application-layer PQC migration strategy rather than a fully quantum-safe Hyperledger Fabric implementation."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Scheme">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Scheme</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid dual-signature scheme combining ML-DSA-65 and ECDSA. It adds no statistically significant transaction-level overhead, and active blocks are about 1.43x larger than an ECDSA P-256 baseline.&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid post-quantum signature scheme combining ML-DSA-65 and ECDSA; it introduced no additional fragility and no statistically significant throughput degradation compared to the ECDSA baseline.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDSA P-256 Baseline">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA P-256 Baseline</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Functionally equivalent application-layer ECDSA P-256 baseline against which the hybrid scheme is compared.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lattice-Based Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Lattice-Based Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Leading post-quantum direction that balances security assumptions, efficiency and deployability. It relies on hard problems such as LWE and SIS.&lt;SEP&gt;Family of post-quantum schemes (e.g., Kyber, Dilithium/ML-DSA) used for transaction authentication and confidential transactions in blockchain research.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryptography built on hard lattice problems. Addressed by references [11], [12] and [13].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-039</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning With Errors">
  <data key="d0">Learning With Errors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hard lattice problem (LWE) underlying lattice-based cryptography.&lt;SEP&gt;Lattice-based hardness problem studied in Regev's "On lattices, learning with errors, random linear codes, and cryptography" [11].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-039</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Short Integer Solution">
  <data key="d0">Short Integer Solution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hard lattice problem (SIS) underlying lattice-based cryptography.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage Growth">
  <data key="d0">Storage Growth</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principal measured trade-off of the hybrid scheme: larger blocks due to larger post-quantum signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NIST FIPS 204">
  <data key="d0">NIST FIPS 204</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [4]. NIST standard specifying ML-DSA, a module-lattice-based digital signature standard.&lt;SEP&gt;Cited as [4]. NIST standard that specifies the ML-DSA post-quantum digital signature algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
