  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shor's algorithm threatens ECDSA and classical public-key schemes">
  <data key="d0">Shor's algorithm threatens ECDSA and classical public-key schemes</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">However, Shor's quantum algorithm demonstrated that these problems can be solved in polynomial time on a sufficiently powerful quantum computer, thereby threatening the long-term security of widely deployed public-key schemes, including ECDSA [2].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDSA vulnerability motivates post-quantum transition in blockchains">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA vulnerability motivates post-quantum transition in blockchains</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Since ECDSA remains a common classical signature baseline in many blockchain architectures, including permissioned and permissionless systems, its vulnerability under the quantum threat model motivates the transition toward post-quantum alternatives [3].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC includes lattice, hash, code, multivariate and isogeny families">
  <data key="d0">PQC includes lattice, hash, code, multivariate and isogeny families</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Several families of PQC have been explored, including lattice-based, hash-based, code-based, multivariate, and isogeny-based cryptography [10].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lattice-based security relies on LWE, SIS and module variants">
  <data key="d0">Lattice-based security relies on LWE, SIS and module variants</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Its security foundations rely on hard lattice problems such as Learning With Errors (LWE), Short Integer Solution (SIS), and their structured module variants [11], [12].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CRYSTALS-Dilithium was selected in NIST PQC standardization">
  <data key="d0">CRYSTALS-Dilithium was selected in NIST PQC standardization</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">CRYSTALS-Dilithium is one of the most prominent lattice-based digital signature schemes and was selected during the NIST post-quantum cryptography standardization process [13].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptographic transition must begin before quantum computers arrive">
  <data key="d0">Cryptographic transition must begin before quantum computers arrive</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">This need for proactive migration is reinforced by quantum-risk analyses emphasizing that cryptographic transition must begin before the availability of cryptographically relevant quantum computers [14].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid frameworks combine ECDSA with Dilithium for dual signatures">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid frameworks combine ECDSA with Dilithium for dual signatures</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Recent surveys also highlight hybrid cryptographic frameworks combining classical and post-quantum primitives, including ECDSA with Dilithium for dual-signature authentication [15].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid migration strategies are important during PQC transition">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid migration strategies are important during PQC transition</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">For this reason, several studies have emphasized the importance of hybrid migration strategies, where classical and post-quantum mechanisms coexist during the transition period [5]–[7].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Post-quantum signatures are a key migration direction for blockchains">
  <data key="d0">Post-quantum signatures are a key migration direction for blockchains</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Earlier surveys also reviewed the impact of quantum computing on blockchain cryptography and identified post-quantum signatures as a key migration direction for blockchain systems [16].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA is specified in NIST FIPS 204 as a module-lattice signature standard">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA is specified in NIST FIPS 204 as a module-lattice signature standard</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Its standardized version, ML-DSA, is specified in NIST FIPS 204 and provides a module-lattice-based digital signature standard for post-quantum authentication [4].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DSA">
  <data key="d0">DSA</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Classical public-key signature mechanism whose security relies on the hardness of the discrete logarithm problem. It is vulnerable to Shor's algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elliptic-Curve Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Elliptic-Curve Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Classical public-key approach whose security relies on discrete logarithm hardness. It underlies ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integer Factorization">
  <data key="d0">Integer Factorization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Computational hardness problem underlying classical public-key schemes such as RSA. Shor's algorithm solves it in polynomial time on a sufficiently powerful quantum computer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Discrete Logarithm Problem">
  <data key="d0">Discrete Logarithm Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Computational hardness problem underlying DSA and elliptic-curve cryptography. Shor's algorithm solves it in polynomial time on a sufficiently powerful quantum computer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quantum Threat">
  <data key="d0">Quantum Threat</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Risk that sufficiently powerful quantum computers break widely deployed public-key schemes. The risk is especially relevant to blockchain digital signatures used for transaction authorization, participant authentication and non-repudiation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hash-Based Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Hash-Based Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family of post-quantum cryptography.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code-Based Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Code-Based Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family of post-quantum cryptography.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multivariate Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Multivariate Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family of post-quantum cryptography.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isogeny-Based Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Isogeny-Based Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family of post-quantum cryptography.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Membership Service Providers">
  <data key="d0">Membership Service Providers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric identity components that are deeply integrated into the system architecture and affected by signature migration.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric component that implements the membership-based identity model. It is one of the places where cryptographic mechanisms are involved.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operational Impact of Post-Quantum Signatures">
  <data key="d0">Operational Impact of Post-Quantum Signatures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Effects of larger post-quantum public keys and signatures on communication overhead, certificate size, transaction size, block propagation, storage growth and validation cost. These effects are amplified in blockchain environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Performance Benchmarking">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Performance Benchmarking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Topic reviewed in the related work of "Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric", concerning performance evaluation of Hyperledger Fabric.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation of Fabric throughput, latency and resource use. It requires control of configuration parameters such as block size, batch timeout, endorsement policy and peer count.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502198</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yang et al. Survey of Post-Quantum and Quantum Blockchains">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al. Survey of Post-Quantum and Quantum Blockchains</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [19]. Yang et al. provide a comprehensive survey and comparison of post-quantum and quantum blockchains, highlighting cryptographic challenges of quantum-vulnerable primitives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buser et al. Survey of Post-Quantum Exotic Signatures for Blockchain">
  <data key="d0">Buser et al. Survey of Post-Quantum Exotic Signatures for Blockchain</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [20]. Buser et al. survey post-quantum exotic signatures for blockchain applications, including multi-, aggregate, threshold, adaptor, blind and ring signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Schemitt et al. Impact of Post-Quantum Signatures on Blockchains">
  <data key="d0">Schemitt et al. Impact of Post-Quantum Signatures on Blockchains</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [21]. Schemitt et al. assess the impact of post-quantum digital signature algorithms on blockchains by benchmarking ML-DSA, SLH-DSA, Falcon and related candidates, using cryptographic benchmarking and blockchain simulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Algar-Fernandez et al. PQC Benchmarking on Commodity CPUs">
  <data key="d0">Algar-Fernandez et al. PQC Benchmarking on Commodity CPUs</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [22]. Compares multiple post-quantum KEMs and digital signatures on commodity CPUs and reports favorable performance for lattice-based schemes such as Kyber and Dilithium/ML-DSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Xu et al. Post-Quantum Blockchain Transaction Model Based on Dilithium">
  <data key="d0">Xu et al. Post-Quantum Blockchain Transaction Model Based on Dilithium</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [23]. Proposes a post-quantum blockchain transaction model based on Dilithium, focusing on lattice-based signatures for transaction authentication and scalability analysis; does not evaluate Hyperledger Fabric.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shahid and Khan Smart Digital Signatures">
  <data key="d0">Shahid and Khan Smart Digital Signatures</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
