<node id="Signatures authorize transfers and bind transactions to keys in public blockchains">
  <data key="d0">Signatures authorize transfers and bind transactions to keys in public blockchains</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In Bitcoin, Ethereum, and other public blockchains, signatures authorize asset transfers and bind transactions to the corresponding signing keys [17], [18].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Signatures support authentication and policy enforcement in permissioned blockchains">
  <data key="d0">Signatures support authentication and policy enforcement in permissioned blockchains</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">In permissioned blockchains, signatures additionally support participant authentication, access control, endorsement collection, and policy enforcement [1].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric's architecture makes it attractive for consortium deployments">
  <data key="d0">Fabric's architecture makes it attractive for consortium deployments</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Its modular architecture, membership-based identity model, endorsement policies, and configurable ordering service make it attractive for consortium and institutional deployments [1].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Some post-quantum signatures are feasible for blockchain verification">
  <data key="d0">Some post-quantum signatures are feasible for blockchain verification</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"Their results suggest that some post-quantum signatures can be computationally feasible for blockchain verification workloads." (Schemitt et al. [21])</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
