  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Post-quantum migration in blockchain goes beyond replacing ECDSA">
  <data key="d0">Post-quantum migration in blockchain goes beyond replacing ECDSA</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"Their work demonstrates that post-quantum migration in blockchain is not limited to replacing ECDSA with an ordinary post-quantum signature. Instead, it also affects advanced blockchain functionalities such as account management, consensus efficiency, privacy-preserving transactions, atomic swaps, payment channels, and scriptless blockchain protocols." (Buser et al. [20])</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lattice-based signatures can support blockchain transaction authentication">
  <data key="d0">Lattice-based signatures can support blockchain transaction authentication</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Xu et al. proposed a post-quantum blockchain transaction model based on Dilithium, focusing on the use of lattice-based signatures for transaction authentication and scalability analysis [23].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502202</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
