<node id="Execute-Order-Validate Architecture">
  <data key="d0">Execute-Order-Validate Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric transaction flow. Clients submit proposals to endorsing peers, the ordering service orders endorsed transactions into blocks, and peers validate and commit the blocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Transaction processing architecture of Hyperledger Fabric. Fabric performance depends on it, along with endorsement policies, ordering configuration, block size and other parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperledger Fabric transaction pipeline that serializes cryptographic overhead with network-bound endorsement, Raft consensus and block propagation, masking PQC signing latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Key Encapsulation Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Key Encapsulation Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cryptographic primitive for key establishment, distinct from digital signatures used for transaction authentication. Kyber is an example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EVM-Compatible Blockchain">
  <data key="d0">EVM-Compatible Blockchain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Blockchain context compatible with the Ethereum Virtual Machine. Allende et al. evaluated hybrid Falcon-ECDSA signatures in this context, and the findings cannot be directly generalized to Fabric.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Healthcare Data Management">
  <data key="d0">Healthcare Data Management</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application domain of sensitive healthcare and EHR data. Fabric-based and blockchain-based post-quantum architectures have been proposed for this domain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Post-Quantum Migration Is Complex">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Post-Quantum Migration Is Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "However, these same characteristics also make post-quantum migration more complex than in simpler transaction-signing models." The statement follows from the Fabric characteristics cited to [1].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
