<node id="Hyperledger Caliper">
  <data key="d0">Hyperledger Caliper</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Caliper is a blockchain benchmarking framework, used here in version 0.6.0, that generates workloads, submits transactions through parallel workers, and collects performance metrics such as throughput, latency, and success rate. It appears among the paper's index terms. In this context it is used to obtain reproducible, system-level measurements of Hyperledger Fabric. Caliper submits transaction proposals and measures transaction latency from initial submission to commit confirmation. The tool also has precedent in related research: Abbas et al. used it to evaluate post-quantum cryptographic schemes in Fabric.

Within the experimental setup, Caliper's worker index plays a functional role beyond measurement. It drives a deterministic round-robin distribution of signing requests across multiple signing-service instances, which balances load among them.

In the analysis of results, some secondary differences between configurations are attributed to Caliper itself. Metrics such as send rate and minimum latency showed statistically significant differences between configurations. For example, a small but statistically significant send-rate difference of 20.190 versus 20.393 TPS was observed. These minor variations are explained by Caliper's workload scheduling behavior rather than by substantive differences in the configurations under test.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502203</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Post-Quantum Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Post-Quantum Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) refers to cryptographic primitives designed to remain secure against both classical and quantum adversaries. Its main families include lattice-based, hash-based, code-based, multivariate, and isogeny-based cryptography, and it covers both post-quantum digital signatures and key encapsulation mechanisms (KEMs). PQC algorithms have been standardized through the NIST process. Because quantum computers threaten existing public-key schemes, PQC creates a necessary migration path for any system that depends on public-key primitives.

In blockchain, the implications of PQC have been surveyed, and several cited works have applied it to blockchain systems. Integrating it into platforms such as Hyperledger Fabric is an active research direction. Such integration affects authentication, consensus, and performance. Post-quantum signatures in particular can increase transaction size and validation cost. Hybrid PQC deployments are one approach: they pair off-chain cryptographic services with deterministic on-chain execution.

In the studied work, PQC is applied to transaction signing and document signing on Hyperledger Fabric. It is added as a quantum-resistant hybrid layer using client-side ML-DSA-65 signing, implemented through a dedicated PQC signing microservice. The resulting hybrid signature configuration, which combines ML-DSA-65 with ECDSA and is abbreviated as PQC in the paper's figures, is evaluated against an ECDSA-only baseline. The paper aims to isolate the performance impact of PQC signatures on Fabric. It concludes that the main barrier to adopting post-quantum signatures in Fabric is not computational cost. Instead, the barriers are the complexity of architectural integration and the overhead of storing larger signatures on the ledger.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-040</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502204</data>
