<node id="Hybrid Signature Migration Model">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Signature Migration Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid ECDSA plus ML-DSA signature configuration integrated at the application layer for Fabric.&lt;SEP&gt;Design in which Fabric keeps native ECDSA and ML-DSA-65 signatures are added at the application layer through a PQC signing microservice, rather than replacing the native cryptographic layer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Execute-Order-Validate Pipeline">
  <data key="d0">Execute-Order-Validate Pipeline</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Three-phase transaction pipeline of Hyperledger Fabric: execution (endorsement), ordering, and validation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block-Size Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Block-Size Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Increase in block size caused by larger post-quantum signatures, measured as a blockchain-specific metric.&lt;SEP&gt;On-ledger block-size overhead analyzed in Section VI-G.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary trade-off of the PQC hybrid configuration: mean block size rises from 29,328 to 43,551 bytes (1.48x versus the original baseline; about 1.43x versus an application-layer ECDSA P-256 workflow); workload-dependent.&lt;SEP&gt;Ledger growth effect of 1.48x for signing-intensive workloads. The overhead is deterministic and directly proportional to the number of SignDocument-type transactions per block. It is smaller for read-heavy or CreateDocument-only workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Throughput And Latency">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Throughput And Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Core performance metrics of Fabric evaluated alongside success rate, CPU and memory usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Controlled Empirical Benchmark">
  <data key="d0">Controlled Empirical Benchmark</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The evaluation approach of "Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric". It keeps Fabric topology, endorsement policy, ordering configuration, workload parameters and payload scenarios consistent across experiments, so the performance impact of the cryptographic transition is isolated. It measures both performance and resource indicators.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Primitive-Level PQC Benchmarks">
  <data key="d0">Primitive-Level PQC Benchmarks</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Existing PQC benchmarks that evaluate cryptographic primitives in isolation, TLS settings, or blockchain simulations. They do not capture the complete Fabric transaction pipeline, including endorsement, ordering, validation, block formation and ledger commit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gap Between Post-Quantum Signatures And Fabric Performance Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Gap Between Post-Quantum Signatures And Fabric Performance Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A research gap at the intersection of post-quantum digital signatures and Hyperledger Fabric performance evaluation. PQC benchmarks ignore the full Fabric pipeline, and Fabric performance studies do not evaluate post-quantum or hybrid signatures. The few Fabric-oriented PQC studies modify Fabric internals, focus on certificate generation, rely on simulation, or analyze too little.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Performance Benchmarking Studies">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Performance Benchmarking Studies</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Existing Hyperledger Fabric performance studies that give detailed insights into configuration tuning and benchmarking methodology. They do not evaluate post-quantum or hybrid signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload-Size Sensitivity">
  <data key="d0">Payload-Size Sensitivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Blockchain-specific evaluation metric that captures how performance varies with transaction payload size under post-quantum and hybrid signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU And Memory Consumption">
  <data key="d0">CPU And Memory Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource indicators measured for peers and orderers to determine whether PQC overhead comes from cryptographic computation, transaction size, block growth, validation cost or resource consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Read-Write Set">
  <data key="d0">Read-Write Set</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Output of chaincode execution that represents the proposed state changes. Each endorser signs the read-write set with its own ECDSA key.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Proposal">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Proposal</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Message that a client application constructs in the execution phase. It contains the function name, arguments and a digital signature that authenticates the client identity, and it is sent to the endorsing peers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsing Peers">
  <data key="d0">Endorsing Peers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric peers selected by the channel's endorsement policy. They execute chaincode, produce read-write sets, sign results with ECDSA and return endorsed responses to the client, which assembles them into a transaction envelope.&lt;SEP&gt;Four endorsing peers in the Fabric network, split across two organizations: peer1-org1 and peer2-org1 (Org1MSP), and peer1-org2 and peer2-org2 (Org2MSP). Each peer keeps a local copy of the ledger and a CouchDB state database instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
