<node id="Schemitt et al. [21]">
  <data key="d0">Schemitt et al. [21]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [21]. A PQC digital-signature benchmarking study using a blockchain simulation based on Bitcoin/Ethereum-oriented models, with statistical rigor.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Algar-Fernandez et al. [22]">
  <data key="d0">Algar-Fernandez et al. [22]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [22]. A PQC digital-signature benchmarking study using a TCP client-server testbed rather than a blockchain deployment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shahid and Khan [24]">
  <data key="d0">Shahid and Khan [24]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [24]. A PQC digital-signature and distributed-ledger study. According to Table 1 it addresses PQC signatures and partially addresses ECDSA comparison, but has no real deployment or performance metrics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wang et al. [34]">
  <data key="d0">Wang et al. [34]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [34]. A Hyperledger Fabric performance benchmarking study without PQC. According to Table 1 it uses a real Fabric deployment and measures TPS/latency and CPU/memory. It does not address block size, payload variation or statistical rigor.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Melo et al. [35]">
  <data key="d0">Melo et al. [35]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [35]. A Hyperledger Fabric performance study without PQC that uses a Stochastic Petri Net model of Fabric rather than a real deployment. According to Table 1 it addresses TPS/latency and block size and partially addresses CPU/memory, because resource utilization is modeled rather than measured from real Fabric components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lechhab Ouadrassi et al. [36]">
  <data key="d0">Lechhab Ouadrassi et al. [36]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [36]. A Hyperledger Fabric performance benchmarking study without PQC that uses a real deployment and covers TPS/latency and block size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raft [32]">
  <data key="d0">Raft [32]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [32] as the reference for the Raft consensus protocol.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hyperledger Besu">
  <data key="d0">Hyperledger Besu</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">EVM-compatible blockchain platform used by Allende et al. instead of Hyperledger Fabric.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA-44">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA-44</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">ML-DSA parameter set for NIST security-strength category 2, defined in FIPS 204.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA parameter set for NIST category 2. It has a 2,420-byte signature and a 1,312-byte public key, the smallest artifacts of the three parameter sets.&lt;SEP&gt;Lower-security ML-DSA parameter set with a mean signing time of 0.137 ms. It contributes about 4,980 Base64 characters of signature and public-key material, giving an estimated active-block size of about 39,288 bytes.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA parameter set with NIST security category 2, signature size 2,420 B, public key 1,312 B, mean signing time 0.137 ms, throughput 13.24±1.53 TPS, model-estimated active block size ~39,288 B (~1.34× ECDSA baseline).&lt;SEP&gt;FIPS 204 ML-DSA parameter set compared in a targeted SignDocument experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA parameter set. It is compared in a targeted SignDocument evaluation, and future work should extend it to the full six-scenario benchmark suite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA-87">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA-87</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">ML-DSA parameter set for NIST security-strength category 5, defined in FIPS 204.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA parameter set for NIST category 5. It has a 4,627-byte signature and a 2,592-byte public key, the largest artifacts of the three parameter sets.&lt;SEP&gt;Highest-security ML-DSA parameter set with a mean signing time of 0.280 ms. It contributes about 9,628 Base64 characters of signature and public-key material, giving an estimated active-block size of about 48,584 bytes.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA parameter set with NIST security category 5, signature size 4,627 B, public key 2,592 B, mean signing time 0.280 ms, throughput 12.02±0.39 TPS, model-estimated active block size ~48,584 B (~1.66× ECDSA baseline).&lt;SEP&gt;FIPS 204 ML-DSA parameter set compared in a targeted SignDocument experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA parameter set. It is compared in a targeted SignDocument evaluation, and future work should extend it to the full six-scenario benchmark suite.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Base64 Encoding">
  <data key="d0">Base64 Encoding</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Encoding applied to ML-DSA-65 signatures and public keys before on-ledger storage.&lt;SEP&gt;Encoding used by the PQC signing microservice to return signatures and public keys; ECDSA P-256 signature and public key correspond to 96 and 240 Base64 characters respectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Encoding applied to signatures and public keys passed as transaction arguments, contributing to larger arguments processed by the chaincode.&lt;SEP&gt;Encoding used for signature and public-key artifacts; ECDSA's 72-byte DER signature and 178-byte PEM public key correspond to 96 and 240 Base64 characters.&lt;SEP&gt;Encoding applied to ML-DSA signature and public-key material before it is stored. After encoding, ML-DSA-44 contributes about 4,980 characters, ML-DSA-65 about 7,016 and ML-DSA-87 about 9,628 per transaction.&lt;SEP&gt;Encoding applied to ML-DSA-65 signature and public-key artifacts before on-ledger storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MVCC Validation">
  <data key="d0">MVCC Validation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Validation step in which each peer checks read-write set conflicts during Fabric's validation phase.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stochastic Petri Net">
  <data key="d0">Stochastic Petri Net</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Modeling formalism that Melo et al. use to model Fabric.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System-Level Post-Quantum Signature Overhead">
  <data key="d0">System-Level Post-Quantum Signature Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation of PQC signature cost across the end-to-end transaction pipeline, including endorsement, ordering, block formation, MVCC validation and commit, rather than through isolated cryptographic benchmarks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
