</node>
<node id="ECDSA (P-256)">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA (P-256)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm over the P-256 curve and the default signature mechanism in Hyperledger Fabric. Its signatures are about 70–72 bytes in DER encoding. It is not quantum-safe because it relies on the ECDLP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CRYSTALS-Dilithium 3">
  <data key="d0">CRYSTALS-Dilithium 3</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Signature scheme from the NIST post-quantum cryptography standardization process; ML-DSA-65 is its standardized successor.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blockchain Crypto Service Provider">
  <data key="d0">Blockchain Crypto Service Provider</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric cryptographic component (BCCSP) used for ECDSA operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDLP">
  <data key="d0">ECDLP</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem, the hardness assumption underlying ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Module-LWE">
  <data key="d0">Module-LWE</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Module Learning With Errors, a module-lattice hardness assumption underlying ML-DSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Module-SIS">
  <data key="d0">Module-SIS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Module Short Integer Solution, a module-lattice hardness assumption underlying ML-DSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Signing Microservice">
  <data key="d0">PQC Signing Microservice</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Dedicated microservice that the client application invokes before transaction submission to produce ML-DSA-65 signatures. It can be scaled and updated independently.&lt;SEP&gt;Application-layer microservice that provides ML-DSA-65 cryptographic operations. It generates signatures over document hashes before transaction submission.&lt;SEP&gt;Dedicated microservice that the client application invokes before transaction submission to produce ML-DSA-65 signatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Python FastAPI service external to the Fabric network exposing POST /pqc/sign, which signs content hashes with a persistent ML-DSA-65 private key and returns Base64 signature, public key and signing time; also configured to produce ECDSA P-256 baseline signatures.&lt;SEP&gt;External FastAPI/uvicorn ML-DSA-65 signing microservice whose CPU utilization and resident memory are monitored independently at the host-process level and reported as arithmetic means. It may introduce additional variance.&lt;SEP&gt;External post-quantum signing microservice present only in the hybrid PQC configuration; reported as arithmetic means across 417 host-process monitoring samples (about 0.10% CPU and 64.7 MB memory).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ledger Growth">
  <data key="d0">Ledger Growth</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Increase in ledger storage, which is affected by large post-quantum signature artifacts persisted on-ledger.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FIPS 204 defines three ML-DSA parameter sets for NIST categories 2, 3 and 5">
  <data key="d0">FIPS 204 defines three ML-DSA parameter sets for NIST categories 2, 3 and 5</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">FIPS 204 defines three ML-DSA parameter sets corresponding to NIST security-strength categories 2, 3, and 5: ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65, and ML-DSA-87, respectively [4].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDSA is used for identity authentication, endorsement signing and transaction validation in Fabric">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA is used for identity authentication, endorsement signing and transaction validation in Fabric</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">It is used for client identity authentication, endorsement signing, and transaction envelope validation through Fabric's Membership Service Provider (MSP) and Blockchain Crypto Service Provider (BCCSP) [3].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDSA is vulnerable to Shor's algorithm">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA is vulnerable to Shor's algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">However, ECDSA relies on the hardness of the elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem (ECDLP), which is vulnerable to Shor's algorithm on a sufficiently powerful quantum computer [2].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA-65 is the standardized successor of CRYSTALS-Dilithium 3">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA-65 is the standardized successor of CRYSTALS-Dilithium 3</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">It is the standardized successor of CRYSTALS-Dilithium 3 from the NIST post-quantum cryptography standardization process [13].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA relies on Module-LWE and Module-SIS">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA relies on Module-LWE and Module-SIS</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">ML-DSA relies on module-lattice hardness assumptions, mainly Module-LWE and Module-SIS [11].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA-65 corresponds to NIST security-strength category 3 in FIPS 204">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA-65 corresponds to NIST security-strength category 3 in FIPS 204</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">ML-DSA-65 is the Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Algorithm parameter set corresponding to NIST security-strength category 3 in FIPS 204 [4].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NIST Security-Strength Categories">
  <data key="d0">NIST Security-Strength Categories</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">NIST classification of security levels. ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 correspond to categories 2, 3 and 5. Higher categories require progressively larger cryptographic artifacts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security-Storage Trade-off">
  <data key="d0">Security-Storage Trade-off</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Trade-off between NIST security category and the size of signature and public-key artifacts persisted on-ledger. ML-DSA-65 was chosen as an intermediate point, and Section VI quantifies the trade-off across ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hedged Signing Variant">
  <data key="d0">Hedged Signing Variant</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Default FIPS 204 ML-DSA signing procedure, which uses fresh randomness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deterministic Signing Variant">
  <data key="d0">Deterministic Signing Variant</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Alternative ML-DSA signing procedure that is also specified in FIPS 204.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument Workflow">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument Workflow</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode workflow in which the ML-DSA-65 signature is passed as a transaction argument and persisted through the chaincode data model.&lt;SEP&gt;Workflow through which an organization submits an ML-DSA-65 signature. It requires the organization's post-quantum public key to be registered beforehand.&lt;SEP&gt;Application workflow in which the application-level ML-DSA-65 signature is persisted through the chaincode data model, while Fabric-native ECDSA signing is retained.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaincode Data Model">
  <data key="d0">Chaincode Data Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric chaincode data structure through which application-level ML-DSA-65 signatures are persisted on-ledger in the SignDocument workflow.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Signature Model">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Signature Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">An application-layer migration strategy that combines Fabric-native ECDSA with application-level ML-DSA-65 signatures. It is motivated by backward compatibility, defense in depth, and separation of concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Application-layer approach that adds post-quantum transaction authentication without modifying the Fabric infrastructure, MSP configuration, or endorsement policies. The approach provides an incremental migration path.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSP">
  <data key="d0">MSP</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric's Membership Service Provider, which handles identity certificates and enrollment. In the proposed design it remains protected only by ECDSA.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric Membership Service Provider identity infrastructure; MSP identities remain classical and the authenticated MSP ID is used to bind organizations to ML-DSA-65 keys.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric Membership Service Provider, which remains ECDSA-based in the evaluated design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptogen">
  <data key="d0">Cryptogen</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric's certificate-generation tool, which Abbas et al. modify.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Post-Quantum Cryptography Migration">
  <data key="d0">Post-Quantum Cryptography Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The transition of blockchain cryptography to quantum-resistant schemes. Approaches range from incremental application-layer migration to full-stack migration.&lt;SEP&gt;Transition of systems to post-quantum cryptography. The paper recommends aligning this transition with NIST-standardized algorithms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Defense in Depth">
  <data key="d0">Defense in Depth</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rationale that combining classical ECDSA and post-quantum ML-DSA protection keeps the system secure if either scheme is broken.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Backward Compatibility">
  <data key="d0">Backward Compatibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rationale that existing Fabric infrastructure stays unchanged, which reduces deployment risk and allows incremental migration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Separation of Concerns">
  <data key="d0">Separation of Concerns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rationale that isolating post-quantum signing logic in a microservice decouples the Fabric upgrade cycle from the PQC migration timeline.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Payload Size Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Payload Size Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The main Fabric-side overhead of the hybrid model, caused by the larger ML-DSA-65 signature and public key. It may affect block size, ledger growth, endorsement payloads, and network propagation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Internals Modification for PQC">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Internals Modification for PQC</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Prior Hyperledger Fabric post-quantum approaches modify Fabric internals, while the proposed approach does not. Statement in the text: "This approach differs fundamentally from works such as PQFabric [8], which modify Fabric's internal cryptographic components (BCCSP/MSP), and from Abbas et al. [9], which modify the certificate-generation tool (Cryptogen)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQFabric modifies Fabric's internal cryptographic components">
  <data key="d0">PQFabric modifies Fabric's internal cryptographic components</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "PQFabric [8] adopts a deeper integration point by modifying Fabric cryptographic and credential-processing components, including the BCCSP and MSP."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abbas et al. modify Fabric's credential-generation process">
  <data key="d0">Abbas et al. modify Fabric's credential-generation process</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "Abbas et al. [9] introduce post-quantum key material through modifications to the credential-generation/X.509 process, requiring changes to organizational credential provisioning."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application-Layer Migration">
  <data key="d0">Application-Layer Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An incremental PQC migration strategy that confines PQC integration to the application signing service, PQ-key registration, and the chaincode workflow. Fabric core binaries, MSP configuration, TLS, and endorsement mechanisms stay unchanged. Its quantum-resistance scope is narrower than that of approaches that modify Fabric internals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Full-Stack Migration">
  <data key="d0">Full-Stack Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Complete post-quantum migration of Fabric. It would require post-quantum protection of identity and credential management, peer endorsements, TLS and inter-node communication, ordering-service cryptography, and client-side authentication and signing. The authors identify it as future research.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protection-Versus-Integration Spectrum">
  <data key="d0">Protection-Versus-Integration Spectrum</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The framing that PQC approaches for Fabric are different points on a trade-off between protection depth and integration depth, rather than functionally equivalent alternatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quantum Resistance">
  <data key="d0">Quantum Resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security against attacks by quantum computers. For Fabric infrastructure, full quantum resistance would require modifying the BCCSP, MSP, and TLS layers.&lt;SEP&gt;Full quantum resistance would require deeper integration at the BCCSP, MSP and TLS layers of Fabric's cryptographic infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Signing Latency">
  <data key="d0">PQC Signing Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Client-side latency of ML-DSA-65 signing during transaction preparation. The paper characterizes it separately from Fabric commit latency unless the benchmark workload explicitly includes it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blockchain Benchmarking Tools">
  <data key="d0">Blockchain Benchmarking Tools</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Standard tools the study uses to measure the overhead of hybrid post-quantum signatures at the application and transaction level.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Post-Quantum Signature System">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Post-Quantum Signature System</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The proposed system combines Fabric-native ECDSA operations with application-level ML-DSA-65 signing. It has three layers: the Fabric network, a PQC signing microservice, and client applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raft Ordering Service">
  <data key="d0">Raft Ordering Service</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Ordering service made up of three Raft-based orderers (orderer1, orderer2, orderer3) that provides crash fault-tolerant consensus.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MAJORITY Endorsement Policy">
  <data key="d0">MAJORITY Endorsement Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Endorsement policy that requires endorsement from a majority of channel member organizations. In the two-organization setup, this means endorsements from both Org1MSP and Org2MSP.&lt;SEP&gt;Endorsement policy of the Fabric network that requires endorsement from both organizations (Org1MSP and Org2MSP).&lt;SEP&gt;Endorsement policy of the evaluated Fabric network requiring endorsement from peers across both organizations; satisfied by enabling service discovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric endorsement policy requiring endorsement from both organizations in the evaluated network.&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric endorsement policy used in the targeted parameter-set experiment on the Fabric 2.5.13 deployment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document_pqc Chaincode">
  <data key="d0">Document_pqc Chaincode</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Go chaincode built with the Fabric Contract API and deployed as a CCAAS container. It implements multi-party document management with hybrid signature support. It also enforces authorization checks and binding between MSP identities and PQC keys.&lt;SEP&gt;Chaincode deployed with the Chaincode-as-a-Service (CCAAS) model in a dedicated container outside the peer processes. In the CreateDocument workflow it registers document metadata and initializes an empty signature collection. It does not persist the generated ML-DSA-65 signature in the ledger state at creation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CreateDocument Workflow">
  <data key="d0">CreateDocument Workflow</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Workflow in which a client hashes the document content and obtains an ML-DSA-65 signature over the hash from the PQC microservice. The chaincode then registers the document metadata with an empty signature collection, and the transaction is endorsed, ordered and committed with ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Key Registration">
  <data key="d0">PQC Key Registration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prerequisite step in which an organization's post-quantum public key is registered before the organization can submit ML-DSA-65 signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Org1MSP">
  <data key="d0">Org1MSP</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric organization that hosts the endorsing peers peer1-org1 and peer2-org1.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the two organizations in the experimental Fabric network. It operates 2 peers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Org2MSP">
  <data key="d0">Org2MSP</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric organization that hosts the endorsing peers peer1-org2 and peer2-org2.&lt;SEP&gt;One of the two organizations in the experimental Fabric network. It operates 2 peers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Payload Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Payload Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Increase in transaction payload size caused by storing ML-DSA-65 signatures and public keys on-ledger. It may affect block size, ledger growth, endorsement payloads and network propagation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client Applications">
  <data key="d0">Client Applications</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Third layer of the proposed system. Client applications orchestrate the hybrid signature workflow: they compute document hashes, invoke the PQC microservice and submit transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client-Side And Fabric-Side Overhead Separation">
  <data key="d0">Client-Side And Fabric-Side Overhead Separation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design property of the hybrid approach. Client-side PQC signing overhead can be distinguished from Fabric-side system overhead, which is harder to isolate in approaches that replace Fabric's native cryptographic layer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Organization Consortium">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Organization Consortium</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Configuration of the Fabric network with two organizations, Org1MSP and Org2MSP, reflecting realistic multi-party governance scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Signature Workflow">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Signature Workflow</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Workflow for the SignDocument transaction. The client obtains an ML-DSA-65 signature from the PQC microservice, then submits the transaction to Fabric peers for ECDSA-based endorsement, ordering, and commit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Microservice">
  <data key="d0">PQC Microservice</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Off-chain service that computes ML-DSA-65 signatures over document content hashes on behalf of clients.&lt;SEP&gt;Service that performs ML-DSA-65 signing and reports signing duration in milliseconds.&lt;SEP&gt;HTTP microservice invoked by workloads to generate ML-DSA-65 signatures; measured average latency of 2.44 ms at unit concurrency (n=100, range 1-30 ms), payload-size invariant at 2.1-2.8 ms.&lt;SEP&gt;Signing service whose cryptographic primitive performance is reported; evaluated also in a two-instance ML-DSA-65 signing-service setup.&lt;SEP&gt;Post-quantum signing microservice whose response time remains below 22 ms even at 20 concurrent requests.&lt;SEP&gt;Microservice that performs PQC signing over HTTP. Its average latency at unit concurrency was measured over n=100 runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQCKeyRecord">
  <data key="d0">PQCKeyRecord</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data structure that maintains the application-layer binding between a Fabric MSP identity and its authorized ML-DSA-65 public key. It is stored in the CouchDB world state.&lt;SEP&gt;Chaincode registry record binding an organization's MSP identifier to its registered ML-DSA-65 public key, key hash, registration timestamp and ACTIVE/REVOKED status.&lt;SEP&gt;On-chain record of an organization's registered ML-DSA-65 public key, its hash, registration metadata and status (e.g. ACTIVE, revoked).&lt;SEP&gt;On-chain record in the PQC key registry holding an organization's ML-DSA-65 public key and its status (e.g. ACTIVE, revoked). An ACTIVE record is required for signing transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSP-to-PQC Key Binding">
  <data key="d0">MSP-to-PQC Key Binding</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application-layer association that chains X.509 identity, MSP authentication, MSP ID, PQCKeyRecord, and ML-DSA-65 key. The chaincode checks that the submitted public key matches the active key registered for the authenticated organization. This check is distinct from cryptographic signature verification.&lt;SEP&gt;Enforcement that the authenticated MSP organization has an ACTIVE PQCKeyRecord and that the submitted ML-DSA-65 public key matches the registered key.&lt;SEP&gt;Binding between MSP-authenticated organizations and their registered ML-DSA-65 public keys. The binding is enforced at the application layer by the chaincode rather than within Fabric's credential infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Membership Service Provider (MSP)">
  <data key="d0">Membership Service Provider (MSP)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric identity component that authenticates X.509 identities and provides the organization identifier through ctx.GetClientIdentity().GetMSPID().&lt;SEP&gt;Fabric's native identity infrastructure based on X.509 credentials. It authenticates organizations and remains based on classical cryptography in the evaluated deployment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raft Consensus">
  <data key="d0">Raft Consensus</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Consensus protocol used by the 3 orderers in the Fabric network configuration.&lt;SEP&gt;Consensus protocol used in Hyperledger Fabric ordering; one of the network-bound operations dominating the transaction lifecycle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaincode-as-a-Service (CCAAS)">
  <data key="d0">Chaincode-as-a-Service (CCAAS)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Deployment model in which the chaincode runs as an external container.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Party Document Management">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Party Document Management</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">System in which documents require signatures from authorized parties. A document's status transitions to SIGNED once the signature threshold is reached.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument Transaction">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument Transaction</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Transaction in which a party signs an existing document. The client retrieves the document from the ledger, computes an ML-DSA-65 signature over the content hash using the PQC microservice, and submits a transaction that appends a hybrid signature entry to the document's signature list. The chaincode checks that the MSP ID matches the declared orgID, and that the organization is an authorized party that has not already signed. It also checks that an ACTIVE PQCKeyRecord exists and that the submitted public key matches the registered key.&lt;SEP&gt;Chaincode transaction that updates document state with signatures. The benchmark preserves its semantics while avoiding concurrent updates to the same document.&lt;SEP&gt;Transaction path used by both ML-DSA-65 and ECDSA P-256 control configurations to store on-ledger signature records.&lt;SEP&gt;Transaction type for which on-ledger block-size overhead was measured.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document">
  <data key="d0">Document</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode data structure representing a multi-party document awaiting signatures. Its fields include docID, docType (e.g., CONTRACT), initiatorOrg, parties, contentHash, signatures, and signThreshold. Its status transitions to SIGNED once the number of accepted signatures reaches the threshold.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HybridSignature">
  <data key="d0">HybridSignature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode data structure representing an individual application-level ML-DSA-65 signature associated with a document. It records the signature, the associated public-key information, and the document content hash as cryptographic evidence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X.509 Identity">
  <data key="d0">X.509 Identity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Certificate-based identity that Fabric MSP authentication uses as the starting point of the application-layer key-binding chain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Off-Chain ML-DSA-65 Signature Verification">
  <data key="d0">Off-Chain ML-DSA-65 Signature Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cryptographic verification of stored ML-DSA-65 signatures, performed off-chain by clients, auditors, or external verification services using the registered public key. The chaincode itself does not execute the ML-DSA-65 verification algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Contract API">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Contract API</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric API used to write the document_pqc chaincode in Go.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Go">
  <data key="d0">Go</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Programming language in which the document_pqc chaincode is written.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application-Layer PQC Key Registry">
  <data key="d0">Application-Layer PQC Key Registry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Registry in which ML-DSA-65 public keys are registered. During registration, the chaincode derives the organization ID from the transaction context with ctx.GetClientIdentity().GetMSPID() rather than accepting it as a caller-supplied parameter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Signature">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Signature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Signature entry combining signer ID, organization MSP ID, ML-DSA-65 signature, public key, signature size and signing time, appended to documents alongside Fabric-native ECDSA signatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Combination of Fabric's native MSP-authenticated transaction identity with an application-level ML-DSA-65 post-quantum signature appended to a document by SignDocument.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document Structure">
  <data key="d0">Document Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode data structure with docID, docType, initiatorOrg, parties, contentHash, signatures, signThreshold, status (PENDING or SIGNED) and deterministic createdAt timestamp.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application-Layer Key Binding">
  <data key="d0">Application-Layer Key Binding</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Association chain X.509 → MSP ID → PQCKeyRecord → ML-DSA-65 key that links MSP-authenticated organizations to authorized post-quantum public keys without modifying Fabric internals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X.509 Certificate">
  <data key="d0">X.509 Certificate</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Certificate structure underlying Fabric identities; the first link in the application-layer binding chain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502212</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RegisterPQCKey">
  <data key="d0">RegisterPQCKey</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode function registering an ML-DSA-65 public key for the Fabric-authenticated organization, storing it with hash, timestamp and ACTIVE status in a PQCKeyRecord.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RevokePQCKey">
  <data key="d0">RevokePQCKey</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode function revoking the caller organization's registered ML-DSA-65 key by setting the PQCKeyRecord status to REVOKED.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetPQCKeyRecord">
  <data key="d0">GetPQCKeyRecord</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Read-only chaincode function retrieving a PQCKeyRecord so clients, auditors and relying parties can inspect registered keys.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric is an article by Lechhab Ouadrassi et al., accepted for publication in IEEE Access in 2026 (DOI 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3735938). The author's version carries the IEEE template page header "VOLUME 4, 2016", and the article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License. It addresses gaps in system-level benchmarking by providing a controlled empirical evaluation of ECDSA, ML-DSA and hybrid post-quantum digital signatures in a deployed, permissioned Hyperledger Fabric network. Its reference list covers post-quantum cryptography, lattice-based cryptography and blockchain.

The article proposes a non-invasive, application-layer hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA signature architecture. ML-DSA-65 signatures are generated by an external, client-side post-quantum cryptography (PQC) signing microservice and recorded through chaincode (document_pqc) as application-level cryptographic evidence. Fabric's native mechanisms remain unchanged, including the MSP, BCCSP, TLS, ECDSA-based endorsement and validation pipeline. Fabric-native ECDSA signatures stay in transaction envelopes and endorsement metadata. The chaincode binds MSP-authenticated Fabric identities to ML-DSA-65 public keys through a PQCKeyRecord registry and supports hybrid document signatures. The authors frame the approach as an incremental migration step toward post-quantum security.

The evaluation uses Hyperledger Caliper on a single-host testbed, with four workload modules and six primary experimental scenarios. These scenarios cover:
- baseline performance (throughput/TPS, latency and success rate)
- stress behavior
- payload-size sensitivity and robustness
- CPU and memory consumption
- on-ledger block-size overhead

The hybrid configuration is compared against a latency-matched ECDSA baseline, and a supplemental application-layer ECDSA P-256 control is used for storage analysis. Statistical rigor is ensured through confidence intervals, Welch's t-test, Cohen's d and an exploratory TOST equivalence analysis. The article also compares the ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 parameter sets to quantify trade-offs between security, signing performance and storage. It also evaluates a multi-instance external signing service and discusses threats to validity.

The study finds no statistically significant differences in transaction-level throughput or latency across the baseline, stress and payload robustness tests, and no significant resource overhead. The main cost is storage. Blocks are roughly 1.43x larger than with the application-layer ECDSA P-256 baseline, and a 1.48x block-size overhead is reported for signing-intensive SignDocument workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-041</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deterministic Timestamp">
  <data key="d0">Deterministic Timestamp</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Timestamp derived from the transaction timestamp to ensure identical results across all endorsing peers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TLS Configuration">
  <data key="d0">TLS Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric's native TLS configuration, left unmodified by the application-layer post-quantum binding.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsement Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Endorsement Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric's native endorsement mechanism, left unmodified by the scheme; endorsement metadata stores Fabric-native ECDSA signatures, and endorsing peers must produce identical results.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="World State">
  <data key="d0">World State</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric chaincode state where documents (created with status PENDING) and PQCKeyRecords are stored; ECDSA signatures are not duplicated as application-level fields in it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Key Revocation">
  <data key="d0">Key Revocation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Process by which a registered ML-DSA-65 public key is marked REVOKED in its PQCKeyRecord, preventing its acceptance in subsequent document-signing transactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document Lifecycle">
  <data key="d0">Document Lifecycle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Document status progression from PENDING to SIGNED, governed by the signThreshold number of required signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetDocument">
  <data key="d0">GetDocument</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Query-only chaincode function retrieving a document by identifier without modifying world state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Off-Chain Signature Verification">
  <data key="d0">Off-Chain Signature Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design choice in which ML-DSA-65 signature validity is checked by clients, auditors or relying parties outside the chaincode runtime; chaincode-side verification is left for future work.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="liboqs">
  <data key="d0">liboqs</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Open quantum-safe cryptography library whose isolated ML-DSA-65 verification primitive was benchmarked at 0.087 ms.&lt;SEP&gt;C-optimized post-quantum cryptography library; achieves 522x signing speedup, 140x key generation speedup and 175x verification speedup over dilithium-py; selected as backend implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryptographic library selected as the backend implementation for ML-DSA-65, showing improvements for key generation (140x) and verification (175x).&lt;SEP&gt;Optimized cryptographic library backend used for ML-DSA signing and verification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetTxTimestamp">
  <data key="d0">GetTxTimestamp</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric API used to derive deterministic transaction timestamps for chaincode instead of local wall-clock time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deterministic Execution">
  <data key="d0">Deterministic Execution</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Requirement that endorsing peers produce identical chaincode results, motivating use of transaction timestamps.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Peers">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Peers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Endorsing nodes of the Hyperledger Fabric network that execute chaincode authorization and key-registry checks during endorsement; no ML-DSA-65 verification primitive is invoked within the chaincode runtime.&lt;SEP&gt;Peer nodes peer1-org1, peer2-org1, peer1-org2 and peer2-org2; average CPU 12.0±1.6% (PQC) vs 12.5±1.4% (ECDSA) and memory 263±5 MB vs 258±5 MB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Orderers">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Orderers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ordering nodes of the Hyperledger Fabric network from which the external PQC signing microservice can be updated, scaled or replaced independently.&lt;SEP&gt;Orderer nodes fabric-orderer1, fabric-orderer2 and fabric-orderer3; average CPU 2.9±0.7% (PQC) vs 3.3±0.8% (ECDSA) and memory 95±3 MB vs 96±2 MB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document Status Lifecycle">
  <data key="d0">Document Status Lifecycle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Document states managed by chaincode: a document must be in PENDING status to accept signatures and transitions to SIGNED once the number of accepted signatures reaches the required threshold.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Signature Threshold">
  <data key="d0">Signature Threshold</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Required number of accepted signatures after which SignDocument updates a document's status to SIGNED.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application-Layer Integration Strategy">
  <data key="d0">Application-Layer Integration Strategy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Integration approach in which the PQC signature is produced before transaction submission and passed to the chaincode as part of the transaction arguments, rather than replacing Fabric's native cryptography.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage Cost">
  <data key="d0">Storage Cost</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">On-chain storage overhead associated with the application-layer signature scheme, isolated experimentally by comparing ML-DSA-65 against the ECDSA P-256 baseline.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency Conflicts">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency Conflicts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transaction conflicts in a multi-organization Fabric network that implementation safeguards were designed to avoid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zenodo Experimental Package [38]">
  <data key="d0">Zenodo Experimental Package [38]</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited as [38]. Reproducibility package deposited on Zenodo that accompanies "Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric". It contains the Hyperledger Fabric network configuration, Caliper benchmark configurations and workloads, execution scripts, the document_pqc chaincode, the FastAPI ML-DSA-65 signing microservice, dependency specifications, and run-level experimental data for scenarios S1–S6 and supplementary experiments. To preserve data provenance, it distinguishes original run-level measurements, values extracted from Caliper logs, ledger-recovered block measurements and analytically derived quantities. It also includes PQC microservice resource monitoring artifacts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Signatures">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Signatures</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid post-quantum signature scheme combining ML-DSA-65 and ECDSA, whose performance impact on Hyperledger Fabric is evaluated.&lt;SEP&gt;Proposed hybrid architecture combining ML-DSA-65 application-layer signatures with Fabric's native ECDSA mechanisms. It is an incremental migration step toward post-quantum transaction authentication without modifying Fabric core binaries. Active ML-DSA-65 blocks are about 1.43x larger for SignDocument transactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Proposed hybrid signature mechanism that provides application-layer authenticity and integrity for Fabric transactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid signature approach that combines post-quantum ML-DSA-65 with classical ECDSA. It is integrated into Hyperledger Fabric at the application layer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FastAPI ML-DSA-65 Signing Microservice">
  <data key="d0">FastAPI ML-DSA-65 Signing Microservice</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Off-chain Python cryptographic service that generates ML-DSA-65 signatures for the hybrid workflow.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deterministic Transaction Timestamp">
  <data key="d0">Deterministic Transaction Timestamp</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Use of Fabric's GetTxTimestamp() instead of local wall-clock time in chaincode to avoid divergent read-write sets and endorsement mismatches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MVCC Conflicts">
  <data key="d0">MVCC Conflicts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Multi-version concurrency control conflicts avoided by assigning each Caliper worker a dedicated set of document identifiers.&lt;SEP&gt;Multi-version concurrency control conflicts in Hyperledger Fabric, listed as one cause of transaction failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Service Discovery">
  <data key="d0">Service Discovery</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Caliper/Fabric feature (discover: true) enabled to route transaction proposals to endorsing peers across both organizations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="document_pqc Chaincode">
  <data key="d0">document_pqc Chaincode</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Go chaincode implementing CreateDocument and SignDocument transactions for the hybrid PQC workflow.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502213</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Experimental package enables reproducibility of the hybrid PQC Fabric experiments">
  <data key="d0">Experimental package enables reproducibility of the hybrid PQC Fabric experiments</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "To support reproducibility, the accompanying experimental package has been deposited on Zenodo [38]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CreateDocument Transaction">
  <data key="d0">CreateDocument Transaction</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">One of the two chaincode transaction types evaluated under the ECDSA baseline and the hybrid PQC configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Success Rate">
  <data key="d0">Success Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Aggregate performance metric reported by Hyperledger Caliper alongside throughput and latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endorsement Mismatches">
  <data key="d0">Endorsement Mismatches</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problem in which endorsing peers produce divergent read-write sets because of non-deterministic chaincode values, such as local wall-clock time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single-Host Testbed">
  <data key="d0">Single-Host Testbed</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Single virtualized host with an Intel Xeon E5-2695 v4 processor (34 vCPUs), 143 GiB RAM, a 2 TB disk and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS. The setup eliminates external network variability and ensures reproducible, identical conditions for the ECDSA and hybrid configurations, although it does not capture wide-area network latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload Modules">
  <data key="d0">Workload Modules</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Four benchmark workload modules (CreateDocument-PQC, CreateDocument-ECDSA, SignDocument-PQC, SignDocument-ECDSA). They generate unique document identifiers from the worker index, round index and a run-specific timestamp to prevent cross-worker contention and to keep ledger state independent across runs.&lt;SEP&gt;Four Caliper workload modules that evaluate the CreateDocument and SignDocument transactions under the ECDSA baseline and hybrid PQC configurations. Each Caliper worker is assigned a dedicated set of document identifiers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid PQC Scheme">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid PQC Scheme</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid post-quantum signature workflow in which the client calls the PQC microservice before submitting transactions to Fabric.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synthetic Delay Calibration">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Delay Calibration</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Injection of a calibrated 2.44 ms synthetic delay into ECDSA workloads to equalize the HTTP round-trip overhead of the PQC microservice, enabling a fair latency-matched comparison.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MVCC_READ_CONFLICT">
  <data key="d0">MVCC_READ_CONFLICT</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric transaction conflict error avoided by ensuring each document receives at most one signature.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baseline Performance Scenarios S1-S2">
  <data key="d0">Baseline Performance Scenarios S1-S2</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">30 independent benchmark runs per transaction type and configuration, totaling 120 runs; n=30 supports normal-approximation inference for confidence intervals and hypothesis tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tests S3-S4">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tests S3-S4</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress scenarios at 10 TPS (nominal) and 50 TPS (moderate stress) with 10 parallel workers over 60 seconds, five runs per configuration, assessing degradation and failure rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Sensitivity">
  <data key="d0">Payload Sensitivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation dimension where contentHash is used as a controlled hexadecimal payload carrier to test payload robustness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ledger Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Ledger Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation dimension assessing storage impact of the hybrid PQC scheme on the ledger.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Consumption">
  <data key="d0">Resource Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation dimension assessed across the six primary experimental scenarios, measuring the resource usage of the hybrid PQC scheme.&lt;SEP&gt;Average CPU utilization and memory consumption of Fabric infrastructure components across five benchmark runs per configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Stress Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation dimension assessing how the hybrid PQC scheme behaves under increasing transaction submission rates and sustained load, including failure rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baseline Performance">
  <data key="d0">Baseline Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation dimension measuring nominal performance of CreateDocument and SignDocument transactions under PQC hybrid and ECDSA configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Normal-Approximation Inference">
  <data key="d0">Normal-Approximation Inference</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical inference applied to the sample mean, justified by n=30 runs per configuration, improving the reliability of confidence intervals and hypothesis tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP Round-Trip Overhead">
  <data key="d0">HTTP Round-Trip Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Additional client-side latency from calling the PQC microservice before submitting a transaction to Fabric. The PQC microservice latency is payload-size invariant, averaging 2.1-2.8 ms at unit concurrency. Without control, this overhead could confound the cryptographic cost of ML-DSA-65.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid PQC Signature Scheme">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid PQC Signature Scheme</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Configuration combining post-quantum ML-DSA-65 signatures with Fabric's signing path, compared against ECDSA under load, payload and resource scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FastAPI/Uvicorn ML-DSA-65 Signing Service">
  <data key="d0">FastAPI/Uvicorn ML-DSA-65 Signing Service</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">External microservice performing ML-DSA-65 signing, monitored at the host-process level with 417 samples at 10 and 50 TPS offered loads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Round-Robin Request Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Round-Robin Request Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Deterministic assignment of signing requests between two signing-service instances based on Caliper worker index.&lt;SEP&gt;Deterministic request-distribution scheme used in a supplementary experiment. Requests were spread across two concurrently deployed ML-DSA-65 signing-service instances that shared the same persistent key material.&lt;SEP&gt;Load distribution method used across two concurrent ML-DSA-65 signing-service instances in the revised evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Testing Scenarios S3-S4">
  <data key="d0">Stress Testing Scenarios S3-S4</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenarios testing CreateDocument and SignDocument at 10 TPS and 50 TPS with 10 workers over 60 seconds, five runs each, to assess degradation under sustained load.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Robustness Scenario S5">
  <data key="d0">Payload Robustness Scenario S5</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario varying transaction payload size (64-character baseline, 1 KB, 10 KB) via the contentHash argument at 10 TPS with 5 workers over 60 s, 9-10 runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Monitoring Scenario S6">
  <data key="d0">Resource Monitoring Scenario S6</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario measuring CPU and memory consumption of peers, orderers, CouchDB and chaincode containers over five runs, filtering values above 500% CPU as outliers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Instance Signing Scalability">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Instance Signing Scalability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplementary experiment assessing a two-instance ML-DSA-65 signing-service configuration for request distribution, throughput, latency and run-to-run stability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="On-Chain PQC Key Registry">
  <data key="d0">On-Chain PQC Key Registry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Registry storing the ML-DSA-65 public key corresponding to the persistent key material loaded by signing-service instances.&lt;SEP&gt;On-chain registry through which persistent ML-DSA-65 key material shared by signing-service instances was registered.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Baseline Scenarios S1-S2">
  <data key="d0">Baseline Scenarios S1-S2</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Primary throughput and latency comparison scenarios: S1 runs CreateDocument at 50 TPS with 5 workers for 1,000 transactions, and S2 runs SignDocument at 20 TPS with 5 workers for 500 transactions. Each scenario has 30 runs and is executed for both the ECDSA baseline and the PQC hybrid configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ContentHash Field">
  <data key="d0">ContentHash Field</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode transaction argument that holds a 64-character hex SHA-256 hash of the document content in scenarios S1-S4. In S5 it serves as a controlled payload carrier for 1 KB (1,024-character) and 10 KB (10,240-character) random hex data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Payload Size">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Payload Size</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Size of application-level transaction arguments, studied in Scenario S5 to isolate its effect on Fabric throughput, latency and the relative overhead of PQC signature artifacts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDSA P-256 Application-Layer Control">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA P-256 Application-Layer Control</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Functionally equivalent storage baseline that generates actual ECDSA P-256 signatures and public keys through the same external signing service and SignDocument path as ML-DSA-65; completed 505 SignDocument transactions with zero failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency-Matched ECDSA Baseline">
  <data key="d0">Latency-Matched ECDSA Baseline</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Baseline used in Scenarios S1–S6 that replaces the PQC microservice call with a calibrated synthetic delay and does not store application-layer ECDSA signatures; 576 blocks analyzed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External Signing Service">
  <data key="d0">External Signing Service</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">External FastAPI/uvicorn PQC signing microservice whose CPU and memory are monitored at the host-process level; 417 process-level samples were collected at 10 and 50 TPS.&lt;SEP&gt;External signing architecture shown to support concurrent multi-instance deployment and deterministic request distribution while using the same registered ML-DSA-65 key material.&lt;SEP&gt;Architecture component that hosts ML-DSA-65 signing. It can be replicated without modifying the Fabric network or the chaincode. It was evaluated with two instances using round-robin request distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block Size Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Block Size Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation of on-ledger storage overhead by fetching blocks generated during benchmark runs with the Fabric CLI (peer channel fetch) and measuring serialized protobuf block size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Average Latency">
  <data key="d0">Average Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric: arithmetic mean of per-transaction latency from submission to commit confirmation.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary performance metric compared between ECDSA and PQC configurations using Welch's t-test, Cohen's d and TOST.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance metric measured in seconds. Reported values of 6.38 s and 6.24 s, with a non-significant difference (t=0.233, p=0.816).&lt;SEP&gt;The primary performance metric, measured in seconds, used to compare transaction-level performance between configurations.&lt;SEP&gt;Transaction latency metric in seconds compared between configurations; no significant differences were found.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance metric in seconds. It was 7.11, 5.84 and 9.31 s for ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87. The two-instance configuration recorded 1.42 s at 10 TPS and 7.47 s at 50 TPS.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="On-Ledger Storage Overhead">
  <data key="d0">On-Ledger Storage Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Storage cost imposed on the ledger by signature artifacts, assessed for ML-DSA-65 relative to ECDSA references.&lt;SEP&gt;Increase in block size induced by the PQC hybrid configuration; a 1.48x ratio that is both statistically significant and practically relevant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric CLI">
  <data key="d0">Fabric CLI</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric command-line interface. The study uses its 'peer channel fetch' command to retrieve blocks generated during the SignDocument benchmark runs for block-size analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FastAPI/Uvicorn">
  <data key="d0">FastAPI/Uvicorn</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Python web framework and ASGI server stack used to implement the external PQC signing microservice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum and Minimum Latency">
  <data key="d0">Maximum and Minimum Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric (s): the extreme latency values observed across all transactions in a run. It gives information about tail behavior and best-case performance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Success and Failure Counts">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Success and Failure Counts</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric: the number of transactions that committed successfully versus those that failed due to endorsement errors, MVCC conflicts, timeouts, or other Fabric-level rejections.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Send Rate">
  <data key="d0">Send Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric (TPS): the actual rate at which Caliper submitted transaction proposals. It may differ from the configured target rate under high load because of backpressure.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance metric in TPS. It differs statistically between configurations (SignDocument: 20.190 vs. 20.393 TPS, t=28.065, p&lt;0.001). The difference is attributed to Caliper's workload scheduling and does not indicate degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rate at which transactions are submitted to Fabric. It differs statistically between configurations (20.190 vs. 20.393 TPS, p &lt; 0.001), but the difference is small in absolute terms and reflects Caliper's workload scheduling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU Utilization">
  <data key="d0">CPU Utilization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric (%): CPU usage of the Fabric Docker containers, measured with docker stats and averaged over the sampling intervals. For the PQC signing microservice, it is monitored at the host-process level.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource metric sampled at the host-process level for the external FastAPI/uvicorn ML-DSA-65 signing microservice during the supplementary resource-monitoring experiment, summarized using arithmetic means.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource metric for Fabric peers and orderers. Peer CPU utilization averaged 12.0% under PQC and 12.5% under ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Memory Consumption">
  <data key="d0">Memory Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric (MB): resident memory usage of the Fabric Docker containers, measured with docker stats. For the PQC signing microservice, it is monitored at the host-process level and reported as the mean across samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Resident memory consumption of the external PQC signing microservice, monitored independently at the host-process level and reported as the mean across collected monitoring samples, separately from the container-level statistics.&lt;SEP&gt;Resource metric for Fabric infrastructure components. No statistically significant difference was observed between the PQC and ECDSA configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protocol Buffers">
  <data key="d0">Protocol Buffers</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Serialization format for ledger blocks (.pb files); the block size metric measures the serialized size of protobuf-encoded blocks, covering transaction payloads, endorsement signatures and block metadata.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="95% Confidence Interval">
  <data key="d0">95% Confidence Interval</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Interval computed per metric and configuration as the sample mean ± t(0.975,n−1)·s/√n using the two-tailed Student's t critical value.&lt;SEP&gt;The statistical interval shown by the error bars in the transaction-level performance comparison, based on n = 30 runs.&lt;SEP&gt;Interval estimate reported with the mean values in the stress test and payload robustness tables (Tables 10–12).&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical interval shown as error bars in the stress-test throughput figure. The 0.5 percentage-point peer CPU difference falls within it.&lt;SEP&gt;Error measure reported for block sizes and Fabric-container CPU/memory values across n=5 benchmark runs.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical interval used to report mean throughput and latency over five independent runs in Tables 16 and 17.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Student's t-Distribution">
  <data key="d0">Student's t-Distribution</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Distribution used for confidence interval construction for individual configuration means; it provides a more conservative interval for small sample sizes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch's t-Test">
  <data key="d0">Welch's t-Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Unequal-variance t-test used for all between-configuration comparisons of ECDSA baseline and PQC hybrid configurations, at significance level α = 0.05. It is preferred over Student's t-test because it does not assume equal variances.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch–Satterthwaite Equation">
  <data key="d0">Welch–Satterthwaite Equation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation used to approximate the degrees of freedom for Welch's t-test.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Student's t-Test">
  <data key="d0">Student's t-Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Equal-variance t-test, which the paper does not use for between-configuration comparisons because of its equal-variance assumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cohen's d">
  <data key="d0">Cohen's d</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Effect size metric reported for primary metrics such as throughput and average latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Standardized effect size computed as the absolute difference of PQC and ECDSA means divided by the pooled standard deviation, applied to primary throughput and average-latency comparisons.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two One-Sided Tests (TOST)">
  <data key="d0">Two One-Sided Tests (TOST)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Equivalence-testing procedure applied as an exploratory sensitivity analysis with a symmetric ±10% margin of the ECDSA baseline mean, using a 90% confidence interval for the PQC–ECDSA mean difference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical Equivalence">
  <data key="d0">Statistical Equivalence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The notion that statistical non-significance does not by itself demonstrate equivalence; formal equivalence testing requires a prespecified margin and power requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Formal equivalence under a ±10% margin, which the revised analysis distinguishes from mere absence of a statistically significant difference. Throughput comparisons satisfy the margin. Latency comparisons do not, because of their wider confidence intervals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uvicorn">
  <data key="d0">Uvicorn</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">ASGI server used together with FastAPI to host the external ML-DSA-65 PQC signing microservice.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supplementary Resource-Monitoring Experiment">
  <data key="d0">Supplementary Resource-Monitoring Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Experiment described in Section V-C5 in which the external ML-DSA-65 signing microservice is monitored at the host-process level. Its measurements are reported separately from the container-level S6 statistics because they do not constitute five independent benchmark-run means.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Hybrid Configuration">
  <data key="d0">PQC Hybrid Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid ECDSA plus post-quantum signature configuration compared against the ECDSA baseline; achieved 31.55 TPS throughput versus 30.52 TPS for ECDSA.&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid post-quantum signature configuration of Hyperledger Fabric that relies on an external ML-DSA-65 signing microservice, which may introduce additional variance. It is compared against the ECDSA baseline.&lt;SEP&gt;Hybrid post-quantum cryptographic signature configuration for Hyperledger Fabric, compared against the ECDSA baseline. It shows statistically indistinguishable throughput and latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Post-quantum cryptography (PQC) hybrid configurations evaluated in Figure 3. They are compared against the ECDSA baseline for the CreateDocument and SignDocument transactions and show no statistically significant throughput differences.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pooled Standard Deviation">
  <data key="d0">Pooled Standard Deviation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Combined standard deviation of the PQC and ECDSA configurations, used as the denominator of Cohen's d.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalence Margin">
  <data key="d0">Equivalence Margin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Margin for equivalence testing that was not prospectively specified; future confirmatory evaluations should define it before data collection.&lt;SEP&gt;Symmetric exploratory margin δ = 0.10 times the ECDSA baseline mean (±10%). It was not prespecified before data collection, so TOST results are treated as exploratory rather than confirmatory.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="90% Confidence Interval">
  <data key="d0">90% Confidence Interval</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Interval for the PQC–ECDSA mean difference. In TOST, equivalence is supported only when this interval lies entirely within [−δ, +δ].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical Significance Level">
  <data key="d0">Statistical Significance Level</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Significance level α = 0.05 at which all hypothesis tests are conducted. A result is significant if |t| exceeds the critical value at the corresponding degrees of freedom; otherwise the null hypothesis of equal means is not rejected.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical Power">
  <data key="d0">Statistical Power</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Power requirements that a future confirmatory evaluation should define before data collection, alongside equivalence margins.&lt;SEP&gt;Power requirements that a future confirmatory evaluation should define, together with equivalence margins, before experimental data are collected.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sample Size">
  <data key="d0">Sample Size</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Number of independent runs n, chosen as a trade-off between statistical rigor and experimental cost. For n = 30 the t critical value approaches the normal approximation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="dilithium-py">
  <data key="d0">dilithium-py</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Pure-Python implementation of Dilithium/ML-DSA; signing takes 117.936 ms per operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TOST Analysis">
  <data key="d0">TOST Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Two one-sided tests equivalence analysis interpreted as exploratory because the equivalence margin was not prospectively specified.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch's t-test">
  <data key="d0">Welch's t-test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Unequal-variance t-test used for between-configuration comparisons and payload robustness analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Tier Sampling Strategy">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Tier Sampling Strategy</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Sampling strategy using n=30 for primary benchmarks, n=5 for stress tests and resource monitoring, n=5 per condition for supplementary experiments, and n=9-10 for payload robustness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Latency">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Latency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Primary performance metric measured in seconds, including average, maximum and minimum latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary performance metric (average, maximum, minimum) reported in seconds.&lt;SEP&gt;Average, maximum and minimum latency metrics. These were nearly identical between configurations, but strict 10% equivalence could not be established.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="liboqs achieves 522x signing speedup over dilithium-py">
  <data key="d0">liboqs achieves 522x signing speedup over dilithium-py</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "The liboqs library achieves a 522× speedup on the signing operation relative to dilithium-py, reducing per-signature latency from 117.936 ms to 0.226 ms."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC hybrid shows no significant throughput difference from ECDSA">
  <data key="d0">PQC hybrid shows no significant throughput difference from ECDSA</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from Table 8: CreateDocument throughput 30.52 TPS (ECDSA) vs 31.55 TPS (PQC hybrid), t=0.911, p=0.366, d=0.24.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CreateDocument Workload">
  <data key="d0">CreateDocument Workload</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transaction workload whose throughput and latency are compared between the ECDSA baseline and the PQC hybrid over n=30 runs in Table 8; send rate was 50.18 TPS (ECDSA) and 50.45 TPS (PQC hybrid).&lt;SEP&gt;Scenario S1 benchmark workload: 50 submitted TPS, 5 workers, 1,000 transactions, n=30 runs; throughput 30.520 vs. 31.550 TPS (ECDSA vs. PQC hybrid).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument Workload">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument Workload</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Transaction workload whose primary throughput and latency results are presented in Section VI-C.&lt;SEP&gt;Scenario S2 benchmark workload: 20 submitted TPS, 5 workers, 500 transactions, n=30 runs; throughput 18.507 vs. 18.757 TPS.&lt;SEP&gt;Benchmark transaction workload used for block-size analysis, comprising 478 blocks for the PQC configuration and 576 blocks for ECDSA, restricted to active blocks larger than 15 KB.&lt;SEP&gt;Signing-intensive workload evaluated at 20 TPS with a 100-ms batching timeout, for which a 1.48× block-size overhead was observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted workload under which ML-DSA parameter sets were compared over five independent runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Two-Instance Signing-Service Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Two-Instance Signing-Service Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplementary experiment evaluating two ML-DSA-65 signing-service instances, with five independent runs at each target submission rate (10 and 50 TPS) to estimate mean performance, 95% confidence intervals and run-to-run variability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA Parameter-Set Comparison">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA Parameter-Set Comparison</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplementary targeted experiment comparing ML-DSA parameter sets, with n=5 runs per condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Tests">
  <data key="d0">Stress Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Evaluation scenarios S3 and S4 under increasing transaction load, each run with n=5 because of substantial setup time (document pool creation) and execution time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Robustness Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Payload Robustness Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario S5 evaluating payload-size sensitivity with n=9-10 runs, sufficient to apply the Welch t-test with reasonable statistical power.&lt;SEP&gt;Evaluation with 1 KB and 10 KB transaction arguments showing no significant throughput difference (e.g., 10 KB: 9.410 vs 9.780 TPS, p=0.153).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Monitoring">
  <data key="d0">Resource Monitoring</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario S6 measuring resource consumption, run with n=5.&lt;SEP&gt;Secondary experimental scenario that measures resource consumption, run with n=5 runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="95% Confidence Intervals">
  <data key="d0">95% Confidence Intervals</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Interval estimates reported with mean values for the benchmarks and supplementary experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Shown as error bars in Figure 3 to represent uncertainty in the performance metrics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDSA and PQC hybrid show no significant average latency difference">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA and PQC hybrid show no significant average latency difference</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from Table 8: average CreateDocument latency 6.38 s (ECDSA) vs 6.24 s (PQC hybrid), t=-0.233, p=0.816, d=0.06.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC hybrid shows significantly higher minimum latency than ECDSA">
  <data key="d0">PQC hybrid shows significantly higher minimum latency than ECDSA</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding from Table 8: minimum latency 0.513 s (ECDSA) vs 0.595 s (PQC hybrid), t=3.905, p&lt;0.001.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="liboqs backend prevents ML-DSA-65 signing from becoming the dominant bottleneck">
  <data key="d0">liboqs backend prevents ML-DSA-65 signing from becoming the dominant bottleneck</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement: "These results justify the selection of liboqs as the backend implementation, ensuring that ML-DSA-65 signing does not become the dominant bottleneck in the Fabric transaction pipeline." The comparison is framed as an implementation-level backend selection rationale rather than an algorithmic contribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-019</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Hybrid Scheme">
  <data key="d0">PQC Hybrid Scheme</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid post-quantum signature configuration compared against the ECDSA baseline; shows no statistically significant degradation in primary performance metrics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch's T-Test">
  <data key="d0">Welch's T-Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Unequal-variance t-test used for between-configuration comparisons of throughput and latency.&lt;SEP&gt;A statistical test used at α = 0.05 to compare the ECDSA and PQC hybrid configurations. It found all differences in the plotted primary metrics to be non-significant.&lt;SEP&gt;A statistical test applied at alpha = 0.05 to assess whether performance differences between the configurations are significant.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical test used to assess significance at alpha = 0.05 across the experimental scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cohen's D">
  <data key="d0">Cohen's D</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Standardized effect-size metric reported for the primary metrics. For CreateDocument, d=0.24 for throughput (small) and d=0.06 for average latency (negligible). For SignDocument, d=0.13 for throughput and d=0.001 for average latency.&lt;SEP&gt;Effect-size metric showing small-to-negligible effects for the primary throughput and average-latency comparisons.&lt;SEP&gt;Effect size metric reported in the revised study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TOST Equivalence Analysis">
  <data key="d0">TOST Equivalence Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Exploratory equivalence test with a ±10% sensitivity margin; supported equivalence for throughput but not for average latency in CreateDocument.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploratory two one-sided tests sensitivity analysis. It supports the ±10% equivalence criterion for the primary throughput comparisons but not for the latency comparisons.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Transparency">
  <data key="d0">Performance Transparency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Property that the hybrid scheme introduces no observable performance overhead; a necessary condition is that cryptographic computation is not the source of overhead.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA-65 cryptographic overhead is negligible relative to Fabric latency">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA-65 cryptographic overhead is negligible relative to Fabric latency</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">At 0.226 ms per signing operation, the ML-DSA-65 cryptographic overhead is negligible relative to Fabric transaction latency (typically 0.5–6 s under the tested configurations).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC hybrid shows no significant degradation in primary metrics">
  <data key="d0">PQC hybrid shows no significant degradation in primary metrics</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">These results show no statistically significant degradation in the primary CreateDocument performance metrics under the evaluated conditions, with small-to-negligible standardized effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Size">
  <data key="d0">Payload Size</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">System-level factor named as a possible source of any performance difference between the ECDSA and hybrid configurations, since cryptographic computation cannot account for observable overhead.&lt;SEP&gt;Size of the transaction payload (1 KB and 10 KB), varied in the payload robustness test. At 1 KB, ECDSA reached 9.930 TPS and PQC 9.722 TPS (p = 0.509). At 10 KB, ECDSA reached 9.410 TPS and PQC 9.780 TPS (p = 0.153).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Network Effects">
  <data key="d0">Network Effects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">System-level factor named as a possible source of performance differences between the ECDSA and hybrid configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="liboqs backend selection is an implementation-level rationale">
  <data key="d0">liboqs backend selection is an implementation-level rationale</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"The comparison should be interpreted as an implementation-level backend selection rationale rather than as an algorithmic contribution." liboqs showed improvements for key generation (140x) and verification (175x), ensuring that ML-DSA-65 signing does not become the dominant bottleneck in the Fabric transaction pipeline.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument shows no significant difference between ECDSA and PQC hybrid">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument shows no significant difference between ECDSA and PQC hybrid</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"Welch's t-test confirms no statistically significant difference between the ECDSA baseline and the PQC hybrid configuration for the primary SignDocument performance metrics." Throughput was 18.507 vs. 18.757 TPS (p=0.620) and average latency was 1.593 vs. 1.591 s (p=0.997).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Throughput satisfies 10% exploratory equivalence criterion">
  <data key="d0">Throughput satisfies 10% exploratory equivalence criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">The exploratory equivalence analysis supports a 10% equivalence criterion for throughput, but not for average latency because of the comparatively wide latency confidence interval.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Minimum latency and send rate differences do not indicate degradation">
  <data key="d0">Minimum latency and send rate differences do not indicate degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">"Although minimum latency and send rate differ statistically, these metrics do not indicate a degradation of the PQC hybrid scheme." The minimum latency differs by only 0.082 s and reflects best-case transaction behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch's Unequal-Variance T-Test">
  <data key="d0">Welch's Unequal-Variance T-Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical test used for between-configuration comparisons of throughput and latency. It was applied at significance level α = 0.05.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TOST Equivalence Testing">
  <data key="d0">TOST Equivalence Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Two one-sided tests procedure, applied with an exploratory ±10% sensitivity margin and 90% confidence intervals. It establishes throughput equivalence but not latency equivalence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cohen's d Effect Size">
  <data key="d0">Cohen's d Effect Size</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Standardized effect size measure. It was d = 0.13 for throughput and d ≈ 0.001 for average latency, indicating negligible differences.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Test">
  <data key="d0">Stress Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental procedure (S3–S4, n = 5) evaluating sustained 10 TPS and 50 TPS submission rates for CreateDocument and SignDocument.&lt;SEP&gt;Secondary experimental scenario run with n=5 runs. Its smaller sample size gives it lower statistical power than the primary benchmarks, which use n=30 runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Robustness Test">
  <data key="d0">Payload Robustness Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Experiment measuring throughput for 1 KB and 10 KB payloads at a 10 TPS submission rate (n = 9–10). No significant differences were found between ECDSA and the PQC hybrid configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC hybrid signatures cause no significant throughput degradation in Fabric">
  <data key="d0">PQC hybrid signatures cause no significant throughput degradation in Fabric</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Throughput shows no statistically significant difference between the two configurations (18.507 vs. 18.757 TPS, t= 0.498, p= 0.620)".</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency equivalence at 10% margin cannot be established">
  <data key="d0">Latency equivalence at 10% margin cannot be established</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Consequently, a strict 10% latency-equivalence conclusion cannot be established from the present sample despite the near-zero observed effect size."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Throughput satisfies the exploratory 10% equivalence criterion">
  <data key="d0">Throughput satisfies the exploratory 10% equivalence criterion</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Under the exploratory ±10% TOST sensitivity margin, the 90% confidence interval for the throughput difference is approximately [−0.59,1.09] TPS, which lies within the corresponding equivalence bounds of approximately [−1.85,1.85] TPS. The throughput comparison therefore satisfies the exploratory 10% equivalence criterion."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Send-rate difference reflects Caliper scheduling, not Fabric degradation">
  <data key="d0">Send-rate difference reflects Caliper scheduling, not Fabric degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The send rate differs statistically (p &lt;0.001), but this difference is small in absolute terms (20.190 vs. 20.393 TPS) and reflects Caliper's workload scheduling rather than a Fabric-level performance degradation."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Both configurations reach Fabric saturation at 50 TPS">
  <data key="d0">Both configurations reach Fabric saturation at 50 TPS</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "At 50 TPS, both configurations reach the saturation region of the Fabric pipeline, with no statistically significant throughput difference detected (33.52 vs. 31.12 TPS, t=−1.751, p= 0.118)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch T-Test">
  <data key="d0">Welch T-Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical test used to compare ECDSA and PQC hybrid throughput; all tests were non-significant at alpha = 0.05 for the SignDocument stress workload.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical test used to compare configurations; all tests were non-significant at alpha = 0.05 for throughput and resource consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical test used to compare PQC and ECDSA configurations over five run-level means; p&gt;0.05 for resource metrics and t=6.422, p&lt;0.05 for block size.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical test used to compare PQC and ECDSA configurations for block size and resource usage.&lt;SEP&gt;Statistical test used to compare the PQC and ECDSA configurations in resource-usage results.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument Stress Workload">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument Stress Workload</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Stress workload tested at 10 TPS and 50 TPS; zero transaction failures were recorded for both configurations across all five runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Robustness Evaluation (Scenario S5)">
  <data key="d0">Payload Robustness Evaluation (Scenario S5)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario S5 evaluates throughput at 1 KB and 10 KB payload sizes at 10 TPS with 5 workers over 9–10 runs, using randomly generated hexadecimal strings as the contentHash field to simulate variable-length application data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid scheme causes no significant throughput degradation">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid scheme causes no significant throughput degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding reported in the text: no statistically significant throughput difference was observed between ECDSA and PQC hybrid configurations for the SignDocument stress workload at 10 TPS (10.08 vs. 10.68 TPS, t=1.469, p=0.215) or 50 TPS (33.14 vs. 36.00 TPS, t=−1.136, p=0.289). The same held for payloads of 1 KB (9.930 vs. 9.722 TPS, t=−0.688, p=0.509) and 10 KB (9.410 vs. 9.780 TPS, t=1.499, p=0.153). "These results indicate that no statistically significant throughput degradation was detected as transaction" argument size increases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid scheme introduces no additional fragility">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid scheme introduces no additional fragility</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Importantly, zero transaction failures were recorded for both configurations at all load levels across all five runs. This confirms that the hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA scheme does not introduce additional fragility under sustained SignDocument load compared to the ECDSA baseline."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric throughput saturates at approximately 33–36 TPS under sustained load">
  <data key="d0">Fabric throughput saturates at approximately 33–36 TPS under sustained load</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Observation stated in the text: "At 50 TPS, both configurations saturate at approximately 33–36 TPS, consistent with the Fabric network's ordering and commit capacity under sustained load."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Size Sensitivity">
  <data key="d0">Payload Size Sensitivity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sensitivity of Fabric throughput to increasing transaction argument size. It is assessed by testing 1 KB and 10 KB payloads at a fixed 10 TPS submission rate, which isolates the effect of payload size from the submission rate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transactions Per Second (TPS)">
  <data key="d0">Transactions Per Second (TPS)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Throughput metric used to report submission rates and achieved throughput for the ECDSA and PQC hybrid configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-022</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC">
  <data key="d0">PQC</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Post-quantum cryptography configuration. One of three signature configurations whose throughput and average latency are compared in the figure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid signature configuration, presumably combining classical and post-quantum schemes. One of three configurations compared on throughput and average latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="T-Test">
  <data key="d0">T-Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical significance test used to compare the signature configurations. The figure reports t=0.233, p=0.816 for average latency and t=0.498, p=0.620 for throughput. Both results are marked n.s. (not significant).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No Significant Performance Difference Between Signature Configurations">
  <data key="d0">No Significant Performance Difference Between Signature Configurations</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding shown in the figure: differences in average latency (6.38 vs 6.24 s; t=0.233, p=0.816) and throughput (18.51 vs 18.76 TPS; t=0.498, p=0.620) are marked "n.s." (not significant).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECDSA Baseline">
  <data key="d0">ECDSA Baseline</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">The baseline signature configuration using ECDSA. It is compared against the PQC hybrid configuration in transaction-level performance experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Hybrid (ML-DSA-65)">
  <data key="d0">PQC Hybrid (ML-DSA-65)</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A post-quantum cryptography hybrid signature configuration that uses ML-DSA-65. It is evaluated against the ECDSA baseline. For SignDocument, both configurations show an average latency of about 1.59 s, and the difference is non-significant (t = 0.003, p = 0.997).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction-Level Performance">
  <data key="d0">Transaction-Level Performance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Performance measured per transaction, such as CreateDocument and SignDocument, for comparing signature configurations.&lt;SEP&gt;The performance of individual transactions, such as CreateDocument and SignDocument, measured as achieved throughput. It is evaluated under the ECDSA baseline and the PQC hybrid cryptographic configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC hybrid signatures add no significant transaction latency over ECDSA">
  <data key="d0">PQC hybrid signatures add no significant transaction latency over ECDSA</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">A finding stated in the text: "For the plotted primary metrics, all differences are non-significant according to Welch's t-test at α = 0.05." It applies to the CreateDocument and SignDocument transactions when the ECDSA baseline is compared with the PQC hybrid configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Achieved Throughput">
  <data key="d0">Achieved Throughput</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">A performance metric measured in transactions per second (TPS). It was plotted against submission rates of 10 TPS and 50 TPS in stress tests with n=5 runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC hybrid configurations show no significant throughput difference from ECDSA baseline">
  <data key="d0">PQC hybrid configurations show no significant throughput difference from ECDSA baseline</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "For the plotted primary metrics, all differences are non-significant according to Welch's t-test at α = 0.05."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Submission Rate">
  <data key="d0">Submission Rate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The rate at which transactions are submitted during stress testing, set to 10 TPS and 50 TPS in the Figure 3 experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Hybrid">
  <data key="d0">PQC Hybrid</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Hybrid post-quantum signing configuration (ML-DSA-65) compared with the ECDSA baseline; peer CPU utilization averaged 12.0%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FastAPI/Uvicorn Signing Microservice">
  <data key="d0">FastAPI/Uvicorn Signing Microservice</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">External ML-DSA-65 signing microservice monitored independently at the host-process level, yielding 417 monitoring samples.&lt;SEP&gt;External ML-DSA-65 signing microservice that exhibited a small host-resource footprint; at 10 TPS offered load the system achieved 10.2 TPS with 1.16 s mean latency, and at 50 TPS achieved 25.0 TPS with 39.88 s mean latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress-Test Throughput">
  <data key="d0">Stress-Test Throughput</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Throughput measured under 10 TPS and 50 TPS submission rates for CreateDocument and SignDocument operations, with 95% confidence intervals and zero failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No significant resource overhead from PQC signing">
  <data key="d0">No significant resource overhead from PQC signing</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "No statistically significant difference is observed in CPU utilization or memory consumption for peers or orderers (Welch t-test, p&gt;0.05 for all components)."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No significant throughput difference between PQC hybrid and ECDSA">
  <data key="d0">No significant throughput difference between PQC hybrid and ECDSA</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "All Welch t-tests are non-significant at α = 0.05 across the evaluated load levels." The stress test (n = 5, 95% CI) reported zero failures for both configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Post-Quantum Signature Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Post-Quantum Signature Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Application-layer hybrid PQC signature mechanism that does not introduce a statistically significant resource overhead on Fabric infrastructure but increases on-ledger block size by 1.48x (43,551±4,293 bytes vs. 29,328±640 bytes).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document-Pqc-Chaincode">
  <data key="d0">Document-Pqc-Chaincode</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode container that showed a non-significant tendency toward higher CPU utilization under PQC (20.0% vs. 16.4%, t=1.698, p&gt;0.05), consistent with processing larger Base64-encoded signatures and public keys deserialized into Go structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Resource Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CPU and memory consumption of Fabric peers, orderers and chaincode; no statistically significant difference observed between PQC and ECDSA configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Peer">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Peer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric infrastructure component whose CPU utilization averaged 12.0% under PQC vs. 12.5% under ECDSA, a 0.5 percentage-point difference that is not statistically significant (Welch t-test, p&gt;0.05).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fabric Orderer">
  <data key="d0">Fabric Orderer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric infrastructure component whose CPU utilization averaged 2.9% under PQC vs. 3.3% under ECDSA, with no statistically significant difference (p&gt;0.05).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Microservice Resource-Monitoring Experiment">
  <data key="d0">PQC Microservice Resource-Monitoring Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplementary monitored execution yielding 417 monitoring samples, in which the external ML-DSA-65 signing microservice consumed an average of 0.10% CPU and 64.7 MB of memory; offered loads of 10 TPS and 50 TPS achieved 10.2 TPS (610 committed transactions, 1.16 s mean latency) and 25.0 TPS (1,318 committed transactions, 39.88 s mean latency).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Confidence Interval">
  <data key="d0">Confidence Interval</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical measure computed over five run-level means, after container-level samples were averaged over the active benchmark interval for each run.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid PQC Signatures Do Not Introduce Significant Resource Overhead On Fabric">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid PQC Signatures Do Not Introduce Significant Resource Overhead On Fabric</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "These results indicate that the application-layer hybrid post-quantum signature mechanism does not introduce a statistically significant resource overhead on the Fabric infrastructure under the evaluated workload conditions."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Hybrid Configuration Increases Block Size By 1.48x">
  <data key="d0">PQC Hybrid Configuration Increases Block Size By 1.48x</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: block size is 43,551±4,293 bytes for PQC vs. 29,328±640 bytes for ECDSA (ratio 1.48x, p&lt;0.05); "Unlike the transaction-level performance metrics, this difference is both statistically significant and practically relevant."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Post-Quantum Cryptography">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Post-Quantum Cryptography</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">PQC hybrid configuration combining post-quantum signatures with the Fabric signing workflow; yields an average block size of 43,551 bytes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="On-Ledger Block-Size Overhead">
  <data key="d0">On-Ledger Block-Size Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Storage overhead measured for SignDocument transactions; PQC hybrid blocks are 1.48x larger than ECDSA blocks, a statistically significant difference (t=6.422, p&lt;0.05).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External PQC Signing Microservice">
  <data key="d0">External PQC Signing Microservice</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Separate signing service for PQC operations, monitored across 417 host-process samples during scenario S6.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU Utilization and Memory Consumption">
  <data key="d0">CPU Utilization and Memory Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource metrics measured during scenario S6; e.g., peer1-org1 used 13.4% CPU and 257 MB memory under PQC versus 14.5% CPU and 252 MB under ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="peer1-org1">
  <data key="d0">peer1-org1</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperledger Fabric peer container whose CPU and memory were measured during scenario S6: 13.4±3.7% CPU and 257±6 MB memory under PQC, versus 14.5±3.2% CPU and 252±5 MB under ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scenario S6">
  <data key="d0">Scenario S6</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Benchmark scenario during which CPU utilization and memory consumption were measured. Fabric-container values are reported as mean±95% CI across n=5 benchmark runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document-PQC-Chaincode">
  <data key="d0">Document-PQC-Chaincode</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Chaincode component using about 20.0% CPU and 19 MB memory under PQC versus 16.4% CPU and 17 MB under ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Storage Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Increase in block and per-transaction storage caused by larger PQC signature and public-key artifacts; estimated incremental ML-DSA-65 overhead of about 6,536 bytes/transaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block-Size Ratio Of 1.48x">
  <data key="d0">Block-Size Ratio Of 1.48x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Total block expansion observed when the PQC layer is added relative to the original latency-matched ECDSA Fabric control; the only practically meaningful statistically significant difference in the original evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block-Size Ratio Of 1.43x">
  <data key="d0">Block-Size Ratio Of 1.43x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Block-size ratio between ML-DSA-65 and the functionally equivalent application-layer ECDSA P-256 configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artifact-Size Ratio Of 20.9x">
  <data key="d0">Artifact-Size Ratio Of 20.9x</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">ML-DSA-65 signature and public-key material is approximately 20.9 times larger than the corresponding ECDSA P-256 artifacts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Latency-Matched ECDSA Control">
  <data key="d0">Latency-Matched ECDSA Control</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Original Fabric baseline configuration used in the original block-level analysis; it does not store application-layer ECDSA signature artifacts and yields the 1.48× block-size ratio when compared with the PQC configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid PQC Configuration">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid PQC Configuration</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">System configuration combining Hyperledger Fabric with an application-layer ML-DSA-65 PQC layer and an external PQC signing microservice; compared against ECDSA configurations for CPU, memory and storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Serialization And Transaction-Level Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Serialization And Transaction-Level Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Overhead beyond raw encoded cryptographic artifacts explaining the gap between the estimated 6,536 bytes/transaction storage overhead and the independently measured 6,680 characters/transaction encoded-artifact difference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch One-Way ANOVA">
  <data key="d0">Welch One-Way ANOVA</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical test applied to the parameter-set comparison. It gave F=3.974, p=0.083 for throughput and F=4.784, p=0.057 for average latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No significant Fabric-level performance difference among ML-DSA parameter sets">
  <data key="d0">No significant Fabric-level performance difference among ML-DSA parameter sets</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "At the Fabric level, no statistically significant overall parameter-set effect was detected for either throughput or average latency at α = 0.05."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Block-Size Model">
  <data key="d0">Block-Size Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Estimation model applied to the original ECDSA baseline of 29,328 bytes. It assumes about two signing transactions per active block and estimates active-block sizes of about 39,288, 43,360 and 48,584 bytes for ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage overhead is diluted at the complete block level">
  <data key="d0">Storage overhead is diluted at the complete block level</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "This dilution occurs because endorsement signatures, MSP credentials, transaction metadata, block headers, read-write sets, and other protocol structures are common to both configurations and constitute a substantial fraction of the serialized block size."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA signing time remains sub-millisecond across parameter sets">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA signing time remains sub-millisecond across parameter sets</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "All three measurements remain sub-millisecond and are small relative to the multi-second end-to-end transaction latencies observed in the targeted Fabric workload."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage overhead depends on workload composition">
  <data key="d0">Storage overhead depends on workload composition</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding: "For mixed workloads, the aggregate ledger-growth overhead depends on the proportion and serialized composition of such signing transactions and is expected to decrease as their frequency decreases."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage Model">
  <data key="d0">Storage Model</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Analytical model estimating active-block sizes from fixed artifact sizes and ~two signing transactions per active block; validated empirically for ML-DSA-65 within ~0.4%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security–Storage Trade-Off">
  <data key="d0">Security–Storage Trade-Off</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding that higher ML-DSA security categories yield predictably larger on-ledger storage requirements from larger fixed artifact sizes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Round-Robin Assignment">
  <data key="d0">Round-Robin Assignment</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Deterministic request distribution method used to split signing requests between two signing-service instances.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coefficient of Variation">
  <data key="d0">Coefficient of Variation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Metric of run-to-run throughput variability; 4.2% at 10 TPS and 2.0% at 50 TPS for the two-instance configuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Instance Signing-Service Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Instance Signing-Service Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Supplementary scalability experiment with two concurrent ML-DSA-65 signing-service instances at 10 and 50 TPS targets, yielding 9.84±0.51 and 22.78±0.56 TPS with estimated per-instance utilization of ~0.11% and ~0.26%.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NIST Security Category">
  <data key="d0">NIST Security Category</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security-strength classification of the ML-DSA parameter sets: category 2 for ML-DSA-44, category 3 for ML-DSA-65 and category 5 for ML-DSA-87. Higher categories bring larger signature and public-key material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Signing-Capacity Utilization Estimate">
  <data key="d0">Signing-Capacity Utilization Estimate</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Analytical estimate of signing demand as a fraction of primitive-level capacity. It is based on the 0.226 ms ML-DSA-65 signing time (about 4,425 operations per second on a single thread) and roughly equal round-robin distribution. It gives about 0.11% per instance at 10 TPS and about 0.26% at 50 TPS. The values are not direct CPU-utilization measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Availability">
  <data key="d0">High Availability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Includes automatic failover, health-aware load balancing, instance-loss recovery, and shared-storage resilience; these were not evaluated.&lt;SEP&gt;Deployment property that the multi-instance experiment explicitly should not be interpreted as fully demonstrating.&lt;SEP&gt;Future work direction covering automatic failover after instance loss, health-aware load balancing and shared storage for the signing service.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Signature Scheme">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA Signature Scheme</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Proposed hybrid signature scheme combining ML-DSA-65 and ECDSA. Under the tested workloads it introduces no statistically significant overhead on primary transaction-level performance metrics but produces blocks 1.48x larger.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External Signing Architecture">
  <data key="d0">External Signing Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Signing-service architecture supporting concurrent multi-instance deployment and deterministic request distribution while preserving use of the same registered ML-DSA-65 key material. High-availability features were not evaluated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Scheme Shows No Significant Transaction-Level Degradation">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Scheme Shows No Significant Transaction-Level Degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The results show that, under the tested single-host Fabric deployment and workload conditions, no statistically significant degradation was detected in the primary transaction-level performance metrics of the hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA configuration relative to the ECDSA baseline."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Hybrid Blocks Are 1.48x Larger Than ECDSA Blocks">
  <data key="d0">PQC Hybrid Blocks Are 1.48x Larger Than ECDSA Blocks</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "PQC hybrid blocks are 1.48× larger than ECDSA baseline blocks (p &lt; 0.05), which is a direct and expected consequence of storing the larger ML-DSA-65 signature and public-key artifacts in the Fabric ledger."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ledger Storage Growth Is the Primary Cost of Hybrid PQC Adoption">
  <data key="d0">Ledger Storage Growth Is the Primary Cost of Hybrid PQC Adoption</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "for organizations planning post-quantum migration in enterprise Hyperledger Fabric deployments, the primary operational cost of adopting hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA signatures is ledger storage growth rather than transaction processing overhead."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Signing-Service Cryptographic Capacity Was Not Saturated">
  <data key="d0">Signing-Service Cryptographic Capacity Was Not Saturated</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: analytical utilization estimates "indicate that the signing-service cryptographic capacity was not saturated under the tested workloads." These are estimates rather than direct CPU-utilization measurements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CreateDocument Scenario">
  <data key="d0">CreateDocument Scenario</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario S1 (n=30). Throughput was 30.520±1.579 TPS for ECDSA and 31.550±1.689 TPS for PQC hybrid (p=0.366). Average latency was 6.381 s for ECDSA and 6.244 s for PQC hybrid (p=0.816). Neither difference was significant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument Scenario">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument Scenario</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario S2 (n=30). Throughput was 18.507 TPS for ECDSA and 18.757 TPS for PQC hybrid (p=0.620). Average latency was 1.593 s and 1.591 s respectively (p=0.997). Neither difference was significant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stress Test Scenarios">
  <data key="d0">Stress Test Scenarios</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenarios S3 (Stress Create) and S4 (Stress Sign), each with n=5, run at a 50 TPS submission rate. S3 achieved 33.52 TPS for ECDSA and 31.12 TPS for PQC hybrid (p=0.118). S4 achieved 36.00 TPS and 33.14 TPS respectively (p=0.289). Neither difference was significant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Size Scenario">
  <data key="d0">Payload Size Scenario</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario S5 with 1 KB payloads (n=9; p=0.509) and 10 KB payloads (n=10; p=0.153). Throughput did not differ significantly between ECDSA and PQC hybrid.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Liboqs">
  <data key="d0">Liboqs</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">C-optimized post-quantum cryptography library that reduces ML-DSA-65 per-signature latency to 0.226 ms, 522x faster than dilithium-py.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dilithium-py">
  <data key="d0">Dilithium-py</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Python implementation of Dilithium signatures, 522x slower than liboqs for signing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No measurable transaction-level overhead from PQC signing in Fabric">
  <data key="d0">No measurable transaction-level overhead from PQC signing in Fabric</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The absence of measurable transaction-level overhead can be explained by the interplay between three factors." Signing takes under 0.05% of average Fabric commit latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource Utilization Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Resource Utilization Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Scenario S6 measuring peer resource usage (n=5): average peer CPU 12.5±1.4% vs 12.0±1.6% (t=−0.486) and average peer memory 258±5 MB vs 263±5 MB, with no significant difference between configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Instance Operation">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Instance Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Two-instance operation evaluation (n=5) reaching 9.84±0.51 TPS at a 10 TPS target load and 22.78±0.56 TPS at a 50 TPS target load.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workload Dependence">
  <data key="d0">Workload Dependence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ledger-growth overhead is transaction-type dependent: SignDocument persists ML-DSA-65 signature and public-key artifacts, whereas non-signing operations such as CreateDocument do not, so the 1.48x ratio is not a uniform storage multiplier for all transaction types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptographic-Artifact Expansion vs Block Expansion">
  <data key="d0">Cryptographic-Artifact Expansion vs Block Expansion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Distinction between the roughly 20.9x larger ML-DSA-65 artifacts and the roughly 1.43x active-block size increase, because endorsement information, MSP credentials, transaction metadata, read-write sets and block headers dilute the effect of larger signatures at the block level.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="X.509 Credential Process">
  <data key="d0">X.509 Credential Process</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Fabric credential-generation process modified by Abbas et al. to incorporate post-quantum key material; not modified by the proposed architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC Key Registry">
  <data key="d0">PQC Key Registry</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">On-chain application-layer registry associating each MSP-authenticated organization with its authorized ML-DSA-65 public key via ACTIVE PQCKeyRecord entries; organization IDs are derived from ctx.GetClientIdentity().GetMSPID().</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MSP-Authenticated Application-Layer Key Binding">
  <data key="d0">MSP-Authenticated Application-Layer Key Binding</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mechanism enforcing that SignDocument() accepts an ML-DSA-65 public key only if it matches the active registered key of the authenticated organization, preventing unregistered, revoked or mismatched keys.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Off-Chain ML-DSA Verification">
  <data key="d0">Off-Chain ML-DSA Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Trade-off whereby chaincode checks key matching but does not execute the ML-DSA-65 verification algorithm during transaction processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Design trade-off in which cryptographic verification of the stored ML-DSA-65 signatures happens outside chaincode execution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PQC-Induced Ledger Growth">
  <data key="d0">PQC-Induced Ledger Growth</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Storage overhead caused by storing PQC signatures and artifacts on the ledger; depends on proportion and composition of transaction types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Migration Intrusiveness Versus Quantum-Resistance Coverage Trade-Off">
  <data key="d0">Migration Intrusiveness Versus Quantum-Resistance Coverage Trade-Off</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Architectural trade-off in which application-layer integration reduces migration depth but provides a narrower post-quantum protection boundary.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application-layer PQC overhead is a lower bound on full quantum-safe Fabric overhead">
  <data key="d0">Application-layer PQC overhead is a lower bound on full quantum-safe Fabric overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "The present evaluation characterizes only the application-layer contribution, which should be understood as a lower bound on the total storage overhead of a fully quantum-safe Fabric deployment."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deep Fabric-internal PQC integration extends protection beyond the application layer">
  <data key="d0">Deep Fabric-internal PQC integration extends protection beyond the application layer</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "Approaches such as PQFabric [8] integrate post-quantum mechanisms more deeply into Fabric's cryptographic and credential-processing stack, including components such as the BCCSP and MSP."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Credential-level PQC integration requires changes to credential provisioning">
  <data key="d0">Credential-level PQC integration requires changes to credential provisioning</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "Abbas et al. [9] adopt a deeper identity-oriented integration by incorporating post-quantum key material through modifications to the credential-generation/X.509 process."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Full-Stack Post-Quantum Migration">
  <data key="d0">Full-Stack Post-Quantum Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Complete quantum-safe reconstruction of Fabric. It would require protecting identity and credential management, peer endorsements, TLS and inter-node communications, ordering-service cryptography, and client-side authentication and signing. The proposed architecture is not a substitute for it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application-layer PQC integration provides a narrower protection boundary">
  <data key="d0">Application-layer PQC integration provides a narrower protection boundary</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "By limiting PQC integration to the application workflow, the architecture avoids modifications to Fabric's native cryptographic infrastructure, but consequently provides a narrower post-quantum protection boundary than approaches that modify the credential or cryptographic layers."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Quantum Resistance">
  <data key="d0">Partial Quantum Resistance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Primary limitation of the architecture: post-quantum protection is confined to the application-level signature evidence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Confidentiality">
  <data key="d0">Payload Confidentiality</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security objective that is distinct from and complementary to signature authenticity. It could be addressed through content-level encryption.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multimedia Encryption">
  <data key="d0">Multimedia Encryption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Image and video encryption mechanisms that could complement the hybrid signature architecture for confidential multimedia handling. These mechanisms are not experimentally evaluated in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaos-Based Video Encryption">
  <data key="d0">Chaos-Based Video Encryption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class of video encryption techniques that is systematized in a recent review.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three-Dimensional Logistic Map Image Encryption">
  <data key="d0">Three-Dimensional Logistic Map Image Encryption</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Image-encryption approach based on a three-dimensional Logistic map, cited as [39].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
