<node id="Ref [42] Review of Chaos-Based Video Encryption">
  <data key="d0">Ref [42] Review of Chaos-Based Video Encryption</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited recent review [42] that systematizes chaos-based video-encryption techniques and their security and performance considerations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recent image-encryption approaches can protect image payloads">
  <data key="d0">Recent image-encryption approaches can protect image payloads</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Recent image-encryption approaches based on a three-dimensional Logistic map [39] and a three-dimensional memristive cubic map with dual discrete memristors [40] illustrate potential mechanisms for protecting image payloads."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaos-based and memristive methods enable video protection">
  <data key="d0">Chaos-based and memristive methods enable video protection</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement made in the text: "Related work on video protection includes segment-selective encryption based on a discrete sinusoidal memristive Rulkov neuron [41], while a recent review systematizes chaos-based video-encryption techniques and their security and performance considerations [42]."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Instance Scalability">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Instance Scalability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Demonstrated capability of the signing-service layer to support concurrent multi-instance deployment. Estimated signing demand stayed below 0.3% of per-instance capacity. Full high-availability behavior was not validated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage Capacity Planning">
  <data key="d0">Storage Capacity Planning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The primary operational concern identified by the paper, due to a 1.48x block-size overhead from SignDocument transactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary operational concern for migration, driven by the 1.48x block-size overhead in signing-intensive workloads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid ML-DSA-65 signatures impose no significant transaction-level performance penalty">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid ML-DSA-65 signatures impose no significant transaction-level performance penalty</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding of the article: "The absence of statistically significant differences across the primary transaction-level metrics-throughput, average latency, stress behavior, payload sensitivity, and resource consumption-indicates that adopting hybrid ML-DSA-65 signatures does not require changes to Fabric performance tuning, workload configuration, or hardware provisioning under workloads comparable to those evaluated in this study."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Signing-intensive workloads incur a 1.48x block-size overhead">
  <data key="d0">Signing-intensive workloads incur a 1.48x block-size overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "The 1.48×block-size overhead for signing-intensive workloads implies that storage capacity planning must account for increased ledger growth."&lt;SEP&gt;Finding of the article: "The 1.48× block-size overhead for signing-intensive workloads implies that storage capacity planning must account for increased ledger growth. This overhead is deterministic and directly proportional to the number of" signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High-Availability Validation">
  <data key="d0">High-Availability Validation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Characterization of availability and resilience that was not evaluated in the study. It covers automatic failover, health-aware load balancing, instance-loss recovery, shared-storage failure and restart/recovery behavior. It is left as future work.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="On-Chain ML-DSA-65 Verification">
  <data key="d0">On-Chain ML-DSA-65 Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Future-work extension that would integrate ML-DSA-65 verification directly into the Fabric transaction path. It would provide stronger on-chain enforcement but would add post-quantum computation to chaincode execution and endorsement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptographically Relevant Quantum Adversary">
  <data key="d0">Cryptographically Relevant Quantum Adversary</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Threat actor capable of attacking Fabric's classical infrastructure components, such as MSP/X.509 identities, endorsement signatures, TLS, block metadata and ordering-service communications. Such an adversary could do so even though the stored ML-DSA-65 signature artifacts stay quantum-resistant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application-Layer Authenticity and Integrity">
  <data key="d0">Application-Layer Authenticity and Integrity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security scope evaluated in the article, which is provided through hybrid ML-DSA-65/ECDSA signatures. It is distinct from payload confidentiality.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Content-Level Encryption">
  <data key="d0">Content-Level Encryption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Encryption of sensitive multimedia content. It could be combined with the proposed signature architecture to extend protection to confidentiality.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Welch Test">
  <data key="d0">Welch Test</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistical difference test used in the original experimental protocol. Failure to reject its null hypothesis does not establish equivalence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TOST">
  <data key="d0">TOST</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Two one-sided tests equivalence procedure. The paper applies it with a ±10% margin only as an exploratory sensitivity analysis. Throughput comparisons satisfy the bounds; latency comparisons do not.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Docker Bridge Network">
  <data key="d0">Docker Bridge Network</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Container network over which all Fabric components communicated on a single physical machine.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Document Management Workload">
  <data key="d0">Document Management Workload</data>
  <data key="d1">dataset</data>
  <data key="d2">Signing-intensive benchmark workload of CreateDocument and SignDocument transactions that carry application-level PQC signature material.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid ML-DSA-65 signatures do not require Fabric tuning changes">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid ML-DSA-65 signatures do not require Fabric tuning changes</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "adopting hybrid ML-DSA-65 signatures does not require changes to Fabric performance tuning, workload configuration, or hardware provisioning under workloads comparable to those evaluated in this study."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptographic signing cost is unlikely to dominate Fabric latency">
  <data key="d0">Cryptographic signing cost is unlikely to dominate Fabric latency</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "While the results suggest that the cryptographic signing cost is unlikely to dominate Fabric latency, they should not be interpreted as a direct prediction of performance in geographically distributed production networks."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Throughput comparisons satisfy exploratory equivalence bounds">
  <data key="d0">Throughput comparisons satisfy exploratory equivalence bounds</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Finding stated in the text: "Under this criterion, the primary throughput comparisons satisfy the exploratory equivalence bounds, whereas the latency comparisons do not because their 90% confidence intervals remain wider than the corresponding margins despite negligible observed effect sizes."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Organizations should align PQC migration roadmaps with NIST-standardized algorithms">
  <data key="d0">Organizations should align PQC migration roadmaps with NIST-standardized algorithms</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Recommendation stated in the text: "Organizations should align their PQC migration roadmaps with NIST-standardized algorithms to ensure long-term compliance and interoperability with regulatory requirements and industry standards that reference FIPS 204." The recommendation is linked to the citation of NIST FIPS 204 [4].</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Payload Robustness Scenarios">
  <data key="d0">Payload Robustness Scenarios</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Secondary experimental scenarios run with n=9 to 10 runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Equivalence Testing">
  <data key="d0">Equivalence Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Exploratory sensitivity analysis with equivalence margins. Throughput comparisons met the bounds; latency comparisons did not because their 90% confidence intervals were wider than the margins.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument Experiment">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Targeted experiment comparing ML-DSA-44, -65 and -87 with five independent runs per parameter set. It measured signing performance, throughput, latency and storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ML-DSA-65 signing overhead is absorbed by Fabric pipeline">
  <data key="d0">ML-DSA-65 signing overhead is absorbed by Fabric pipeline</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement from the conclusion: "These findings indicate that, under the evaluated conditions, the computational cost of ML-DSA-65 signing is effectively absorbed by Fabric's endorsement, ordering, and commit pipeline."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Storage overhead is the main practical cost of the hybrid approach">
  <data key="d0">Storage overhead is the main practical cost of the hybrid approach</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement from the conclusion: "The main practical cost of the proposed hybrid approach is storage-related... active ML-DSA-65 blocks are approximately 1.43× larger for signing-intensive SignDocument transactions."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Off-Chain ML-DSA-65 Verification">
  <data key="d0">Off-Chain ML-DSA-65 Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">Design choice in which ML-DSA-65 cryptographic verification is performed off-chain rather than within the chaincode during endorsement. As a result, the system-level experiments do not measure the throughput, latency or resource overhead of chaincode-side verification. The isolated 0.087 ms verification time should not be read as chaincode-side overhead.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chaincode-Side ML-DSA-65 Verification">
  <data key="d0">Chaincode-Side ML-DSA-65 Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">On-chain verification of ML-DSA-65 signatures inside the chaincode. It would add signature and public-key decoding, key reconstruction, transaction-argument processing, chaincode runtime overhead and concurrent execution by endorsing peers. Its performance impact lies outside the validated scope and is left to a dedicated future control experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;On-chain cryptographic verification of ML-DSA-65 signatures inside chaincode. Future work should quantify its impact on endorsement latency, end-to-end transaction throughput, chaincode-container CPU and memory consumption, and behavior under increasing concurrency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Host Geographically Distributed Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Host Geographically Distributed Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Future work direction: evaluating the architecture in multi-host and geographically distributed Fabric deployments to measure how PQC payload overhead interacts with real network latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No statistically significant transaction-level overhead from ML-DSA-65">
  <data key="d0">No statistically significant transaction-level overhead from ML-DSA-65</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement from the conclusion: "At the transaction level, no statistically significant overhead was observed for the primary performance metrics under the tested workloads."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Throughput comparisons satisfy exploratory equivalence bounds but latency comparisons do not">
  <data key="d0">Throughput comparisons satisfy exploratory equivalence bounds but latency comparisons do not</data>
  <data key="d1">claim</data>
  <data key="d2">Statement in the text: "Under this criterion, the primary throughput comparisons satisfy the exploratory equivalence bounds, whereas the latency comparisons do not because their 90% confidence intervals remain wider than the corresponding margins despite negligible observed effect sizes."</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-037</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Algorithms for Quantum Computation: Discrete Logarithms and Factoring">
  <data key="d0">Algorithms for Quantum Computation: Discrete Logarithms and Factoring</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [2]. P. W. Shor, Proceedings of the 35th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science, IEEE, 1994, pp. 124–134. Presents quantum algorithms for factoring and discrete logarithms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA)">
  <data key="d0">The Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA)</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [3]. Johnson, Menezes and Vanstone, International Journal of Information Security, vol. 1, no. 1, pp. 36–63, 2001. Describes ECDSA.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-038</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FIPS 204: Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Standard">
  <data key="d0">FIPS 204: Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Standard</data>
  <data key="d1">paper</data>
  <data key="d2">Reference [4]. National Institute of Standards and Technology, Federal Information Processing Standard 204, 2024. Standardizes the module-lattice-based digital signature (ML-DSA).</data>
