<node id="Multi-Objective Optimization">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Objective Optimization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Research interest of Khalid Jebari Hassani.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CreateDocument">
  <data key="d0">CreateDocument</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">CreateDocument is a chaincode transaction function that creates a new document record on the ledger. Each record starts in a PENDING state and holds the document's content hash, the participating organizations, and a signature threshold. The function takes its timestamp from GetTxTimestamp(), so the timestamp is deterministic. CreateDocument registers document metadata and initializes an empty signature collection.

CreateDocument is a non-signing operation. It does not store signature artifacts or post-quantum cryptography (PQC) artifacts, such as an ML-DSA-65 signature or public key. Workloads with a higher share of CreateDocument transactions therefore cause less PQC-induced ledger growth than signing-heavy workloads.

CreateDocument appears in several evaluation scenarios (S1, S3, S5 and S6) to create document records. In scenario S1, it was evaluated at 50 TPS with 5 workers and 1,000 transactions per run. In the baseline and stress tests (S3), it was benchmarked at submission rates of 10 TPS and 50 TPS.

Its performance was compared between the ECDSA baseline and the PQC hybrid configuration. Its average latency was also benchmarked across the ECDSA, PQC and Hybrid configurations. The throughput differences between the ECDSA baseline and the PQC hybrid configuration were not statistically significant (p=0.126 and p=0.118). This indicates that PQC integration has no meaningful throughput impact on this transaction type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502226</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SignDocument">
  <data key="d0">SignDocument</data>
  <data key="d1">methodology</data>
  <data key="d2">SignDocument is a chaincode transaction function in a blockchain-based document-signing architecture. It appends a hybrid signature to a document in PENDING status. Before appending, it verifies that the signer is an authorized party that has not already signed. It also validates the signer's MSP identity, checks the binding between the MSP identity and the post-quantum (PQC) key, and requires an ACTIVE PQCKeyRecord for the authenticated organization. A submitted ML-DSA-65 public key is accepted only if it exactly matches the organization's registered key. Once the required signature threshold is reached, the document's status changes to SIGNED. SignDocument does not itself verify whether the ML-DSA-65 signature is cryptographically valid. Each document receives at most one signature per authorized signer.

SignDocument is a signing-intensive operation because it persists ML-DSA-65 signature and public-key artifacts on-ledger. This makes it the main driver of PQC-induced ledger growth. Block-size measurements showed a 1.48x block-size overhead relative to the baseline (t = 6.422, p &lt; 0.05). The transaction was also used in the block-size ratio analyses and in the parameter-set experiment.

As the primary benchmarked transaction type, SignDocument was evaluated across ECDSA, PQC and Hybrid configurations, in scenarios S2 and S4. In scenario S2 it ran at 20 TPS with 5 workers and 500 transactions per run. The stress tests (S4) used submission rates of 10 TPS and 50 TPS under both the ECDSA baseline and the PQC hybrid configuration.

In the baseline comparison, throughput was 18.507 vs. 18.757 TPS (p = 0.620). Latency was 1.593 vs. 1.591 s. Other throughput comparisons between ECDSA and the PQC hybrid configuration were also non-significant (p = 0.215 and p = 0.289). Figure 3 compares average latency using 95% confidence intervals from n = 30 runs. Both configurations sit at about 1.59 s, with no significant difference (t = 0.003, p = 0.997). Overall, the ML-DSA-65 hybrid scheme added no measurable throughput or latency penalty to SignDocument, though it increased on-ledger storage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d4">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1791502228</data>
