<edge source="Random Forest" target="Decision Tree">
  <data key="d7">8.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relationship between Decision Tree (DT) and Random Forest (RF) is that of a base learner and the ensemble method built on it. Random Forest generates many decision trees, each trained on a random subset of attributes, and aggregates their outputs, typically by averaging, to produce more robust predictions. Because the individual trees classify in complementary ways, combining them strengthens the overall result. The performance of a Random Forest also depends on how many trees it contains.

In comparative evaluations, Random Forest outperformed Decision Tree among the tested models, reaching 93% accuracy against the Decision Tree's 87%. However, the two models respond differently to more data: unlike Random Forest, the Decision Tree keeps improving as training data is added. In a specific prediction case, both models correctly predicted actual class 3, with the Decision Tree assigning that class the highest probability.</data>
  <data key="d9">compares_with,ensemble,ensemble component,extends,related_to</data>
