<edge source="Fuzzy Logic" target="This Paper">
  <data key="d7">12.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between Fuzzy Logic (FL) and This Paper is central to the paper's methodology. Fuzzy Logic is a core component of the proposed model. The paper selects it as the method for calculating the conditional probabilities of the nodes, or variables, in its Bayesian Networks (BNs). Specifically, the paper uses an FL system to generate the conditional probabilities of the causal graph systematically.

The paper justifies this choice by noting the lack of suitable databases and the impracticality of eliciting conditional probabilities directly from experts. Fuzzy Logic offers a structured alternative. Expert evaluations are expressed as fuzzy rules, which allows qualitative expert knowledge to be turned into quantitative probability values.

To apply Fuzzy Logic, the paper defines linguistic values, specifies membership functions, and builds fuzzy rules that together make up the fuzzy inference process. Beyond its current use, the paper also proposes using fuzzy logic for pairwise comparisons in future research, suggesting the technique could be extended to other parts of the modeling approach.</data>
  <data key="d9">CP generation,conditional probability generation,expert elicitation,future work,probability generation,related_to,uses_method</data>
