<edge source="Throughput" target="This Paper">
  <data key="d7">16.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The relation between This Paper and Throughput centers on throughput as a core performance metric in the paper's experimental evaluation. This Paper measures throughput in transactions per second (tx/s) and investigates how different configuration parameters affect it. It measures throughput for each evaluated configuration, including each combination of block size and timeout settings and each peer configuration.

Throughput appears throughout the paper's experimental figures and is reported specifically in Fig. 9 and Table 5 for the various peer configurations. The paper also uses throughput to rank configurations, with the highest observed value reaching 448.5 tx/s. One of the paper's stated aims is to preserve transaction capacity, which it assesses by measuring throughput.&lt;SEP&gt;Scalability under load measured via throughput.&lt;SEP&gt;Throughput is a primary performance metric.&lt;SEP&gt;The paper measures throughput in TPS for the SignDocument operation.</data>
  <data key="d9">addresses,evaluation metric,metric,metrics,performance evaluation,performance metric,uses_method</data>
