<edge source="ECDSA P-256" target="ML-DSA-65">
  <data key="d7">5.0</data>
  <data key="d8">ECDSA P-256 signatures over the same document content hash serve as a functionally equivalent classical baseline to ML-DSA-65.&lt;SEP&gt;The principal experimental difference between the configurations is the application-layer signature algorithm (ML-DSA-65 versus ECDSA P-256) and the size of the resulting cryptographic artifacts.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA-65 artifacts are about 20.9× larger and blocks about 1.43× larger than with ECDSA P-256.&lt;SEP&gt;ML-DSA-65 artifacts are about 20.9x larger than ECDSA P-256 artifacts, but full blocks are only about 1.43x larger.&lt;SEP&gt;Encoded ML-DSA-65 artifacts are about 20.9x larger than ECDSA P-256 artifacts, yet active-block size increases only about 1.43x.</data>
  <data key="d9">artifact size,classical vs post-quantum,compares_with,signature algorithm,storage comparison</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d11">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1791502266</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
