<edge source="Abbas et al. [9]" target="Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric">
  <data key="d7">7.0</data>
  <data key="d8">PQFabric [8] integrates hybrid post-quantum mechanisms within Fabric's cryptographic and credential-management stack, whereas Abbas et al. [9] extend the identity-provisioning process to incorporate post-quantum key material into Fabric credentials.&lt;SEP&gt;The paper contrasts its application-layer approach with the credential-level post-quantum key provisioning in Abbas et al.&lt;SEP&gt;Listed in Table 1 as related work on post-quantum Hyperledger Fabric integration covering KEMs and signatures.&lt;SEP&gt;Abbas et al. [9] introduce post-quantum key material through modifications to the credential-generation/X.509 process, requiring changes to organizational credential provisioning.&lt;SEP&gt;The paper compares its approach with the credential-level modifications made by Abbas et al.&lt;SEP&gt;Abbas et al. [9] adopt a deeper identity-oriented integration by incorporating post-quantum key material through modifications to the credential-generation/X.509 process.&lt;SEP&gt;The paper contrasts its approach, which leaves X.509/MSP unchanged, with the credential-level modification of Abbas et al.</data>
  <data key="d9">cites,compares_with,comparison,contrasting approach,credential-level,integration point,migration depth,related work</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034</data>
  <data key="d11">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1791502241</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
