<edge source="Post-Quantum Migration" target="Hybrid Post-Quantum Signatures for Hyperledger Fabric">
  <data key="d7">4.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The paper addresses the gap in empirical evaluations of hybrid post-quantum signatures in deployed Fabric networks.&lt;SEP&gt;The paper examines implications for post-quantum migration in enterprise Hyperledger Fabric deployments.&lt;SEP&gt;The architecture is presented as an incremental migration step rather than a full-stack reconstruction.&lt;SEP&gt;The proposed scheme is described as an incremental application-layer PQC migration step. The paper also discusses implications for migration planning.</data>
  <data key="d9">addresses,enterprise blockchain,incremental migration,migration planning</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d11">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1791502260</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Post-Quantum Migration" target="Signature Size Overhead">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Migration from ECDSA to post-quantum signatures is not a simple algorithm replacement, because the larger signatures affect payloads, blocks, ledger growth, and latency.</data>
  <data key="d9">challenge,related_to</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-3cdf7983d59d3aabda65b170e48426b0-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">Toward_Quantum-Safe_Permissioned_Blockchains_Evalu.pdf</data>
  <data key="d12">1791502260</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
